data: {"stage": "done", "message": "{\"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-16T13:46:54.568452-04:00\", \"title_query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\", \"results\": {\"legislation\": [{\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1729: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1729 En cas de vente par un vendeur professionnel, l’existence d’un vice au moment de la vente est présumée, lorsque le mauvais fonctionnement du bien ou sa détérioration survient prématurément par rapport à des biens identiques ou de même espèce; cette présomption est repoussée si le défaut est dû à une mauvaise utilisation du bien par l’acheteur. 1991, c. 64, a. 1729 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1729 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1721. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 588 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : suspendu Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 30 (5 décembre 1991),  p. 1244-1245 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1729 (LQ 1991, c. 64) En cas de vente par un vendeur professionnel, l'existence d'un vice au moment de la vente est présumée, lorsque le mauvais fonctionnement du bien ou sa détérioration survient prématurément par rapport à des biens identiques ou de même espèce ; cette présomption est repoussée si le défaut est dû à une mauvaise utilisation du bien par l'acheteur. Article 1729 (SQ 1991, c. 64) A defect is presumed to have existed at the time of a sale by a professional seller if the property malfunctions or deteriorates prematurely in comparison with identical items of property or items of the same type; such a presumption is not made, however, where the defect is due to improper use of the property by the buyer. Sources Loi sur la protection du consommateur, L.R.Q., chap. P-40.1 : article 38 Commentaires Cet article, de droit nouveau, complète la garantie de qualité à laquelle est tenu un vendeur professionnel, c'est-à-dire la personne qui a pour occupation habituelle la vente de biens, en présumant l'existence d'un vice lorsque le mauvais fonctionnement du bien ou sa détérioration survient prématurément. Cette garantie de bon fonctionnement s'inspire de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur (L.R.Q., chapitre P-40.1, art. 38). Ainsi, la garantie de qualité que doit offrir le vendeur professionnel reçoit, par le jeu de la présomption, une certaine extension. Il sera, par voie de conséquence, plus facile d'établir la preuve du vice, d'autant que le vendeur sera tenu de garantir l'acheteur contre le fonctionnement anormal ou la détérioration prématurée du bien vendu, par rapport à des biens identiques ou de même espèce. Cette garantie cesse cependant si le défaut résulte d'une mauvaise utilisation du bien par l'acheteur. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03306011110544205, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1729: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"@search.score\": 0.03306011110544205, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\"}, \"token_count\": 859, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1726: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1726 Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l’acheteur que le bien et ses accessoires sont, lors de la vente, exempts de vices cachés qui le rendent impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acheté, ou n’aurait pas donné si haut prix, s’il les avait connus. Il n’est, cependant, pas tenu de garantir le vice caché connu de l’acheteur ni le vice apparent; est apparent le vice qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert. 1991, c. 64, a. 1726 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1726 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1719. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 588 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (Projet de loi 125). Livre V - Des obligations (De l'Affrètement, du Transport et de l'Assurance maritime) , p. 7 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1726 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l'acheteur que le bien et ses accessoires sont, lors de la vente, exempts de vices cachés qui le rendent impropre à l'usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l'acheteur ne l'aurait pas acheté, ou n'aurait pas donné si haut prix, s'il les avait connus. Il n'est, cependant, pas tenu de garantir le vice caché connu de l'acheteur ni le vice apparent; est apparent le vice qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert Article 1726 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1522, 1523 O.R.C.C. : L. V, articles 373, 374 Commentaires Cet article reprend en substance le droit antérieur qu'énonçaient les articles 1522 et 1523 C.C.B.C., quant à la garantie du vendeur contre les vices cachés, mais en y apportant certaines précisions. Ainsi, le premier alinéa précise, conformément à la doctrine et à la jurisprudence, que le vice doit, pour être garanti, exister lors de la vente. De même, le second alinéa, conformément à la doctrine et à la jurisprudence, crée une exception pour le vice caché connu de l'acheteur et pour le vice apparent. Il met également un terme à une longue controverse, en rejetant expressément l'obligation pour l'acheteur d'avoir recours à un expert pour examiner le bien avant l'achat. Il consacre, en fait, une règle qui se dégage de la jurisprudence majoritaire en matière mobilière et l'étend au domaine immobilier. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03333333507180214, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1726: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"@search.score\": 0.03333333507180214, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\"}, \"token_count\": 988, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1728: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1728 Si le vendeur connaissait le vice caché ou ne pouvait l’ignorer, il est tenu, outre la restitution du prix, de réparer le préjudice subi par l’acheteur. 1991, c. 64, a. 1728 ; N.I. 2014-05-01 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1728 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1720.1. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 587-588 Amendement : ajout | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1728 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Si le vendeur connaissait le vice caché ou ne pouvait l'ignorer, il est tenu, outre la restitution du prix, de tous les dommages-intérêts soufferts par l'acheteur. Article 1728 (SQ 1991, c. 64) If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1527, 1528 Commentaires Cet article s'inspire des articles 1527 et 1528 C.C.B.C. et complète l'article 1727; il accorde explicitement à l'acheteur le droit de réclamer des dommages-intérêts du vendeur de mauvaise foi qui connaissait le vice caché ou ne pouvait l'ignorer. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03229166567325592, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1728: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"@search.score\": 0.03229166567325592, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 5, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\"}, \"token_count\": 514, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1740: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1740 Le vendeur d’un bien meuble peut, lorsque l’acheteur n’en paie pas le prix et n’en prend pas délivrance, considérer la vente comme résolue si l’acheteur est en demeure de plein droit d’exécuter ses obligations ou s’il ne les a pas exécutées dans le délai fixé par la mise en demeure. Il peut aussi, lorsqu’il apparaît que l’acheteur n’exécutera pas une partie substantielle de ses obligations, arrêter la livraison du bien en cours de transport. 1991, c. 64, a. 1740 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1740 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1731. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 593 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 20 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1740 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur d'un bien meuble peut, lorsque l'acheteur n'en paie pas le prix et n'en prend pas délivrance, considérer la vente comme résolue si l'acheteur est en demeure de plein droit d'exécuter ses obligations ou s'il ne les a pas exécutées dans le délai fixé par la mise en demeure. Il peut aussi, lorsqu'il apparaît que l'acheteur n'exécutera pas une partie substantielle de ses obligations, arrêter la livraison du bien en cours de transport. Article 1740 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller of movable property may, if the buyer fails to pay the sale price and to accept delivery of it, consider the sale resolved if the buyer is in default by operation of law or if he fails to perform his obligations within the time allowed in the notice of default. The seller may also, where it appears that the buyer will not perform a substantial part of his obligations, stop delivery of the property in transit. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1544 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 388 Convention sur la vente internationale de marchandises : article 71 Commentaires Le premier alinéa de cet article reprend l'article 1544 C.C.B.C. qui donne au vendeur le droit de considérer la vente d'un meuble résolue de plein droit, lorsque l'acheteur n'en paie pas le prix et n'en prend pas livraison. Il y apporte certaines adaptations en fonction des règles générales introduites par l'article 1605, en matière de résolution de plein droit. Ainsi, le vendeur pourra, dans ce cas, considérer la vente comme résolue si l'acheteur est en demeure de plein droit d'exécuter ses obligations ou s'il ne les a pas exécutées dans le délai fixé par la mise en demeure. L'article 1597 prévoit les cas où le débiteur est considéré en demeure de plein droit. Le second alinéa ajoute au droit antérieur, en ce qu'il complète le droit de suspendre l'exécution qui résulte des règles générales applicables en matière d'obligations. Il permet au vendeur d'arrêter la livraison du bien en cours de transport, lorsqu'il apparaît que l'acheteur n'exécutera pas une partie substantielle de ses obligations. Ce nouveau principe comble une lacune du droit antérieur, en protégeant les droits du vendeur impayé qui a remis le bien vendu à un transporteur. Cette règle s'inspire de la Convention des Nations Unies sur la vente internationale de marchandises (Convention de Vienne, 1980). Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.032522473484277725, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1740: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"@search.score\": 0.032522473484277725, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 5, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\"}, \"token_count\": 998, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1741: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1741 Lorsque la vente d’un bien meuble a été faite sans terme, le vendeur peut, dans les 30 jours de la délivrance, considérer la vente comme résolue et revendiquer le bien, si l’acheteur, alors qu’il est en demeure, fait défaut de payer le prix et si le meuble est encore entier et dans le même état, sans être passé entre les mains d’un tiers qui en a payé le prix ou d’un créancier hypothécaire qui a obtenu le délaissement du bien. La saisie par un tiers, alors que l’acheteur est en demeure de payer le prix et que le bien est dans les conditions prescrites pour la résolution, ne fait pas obstacle au droit du vendeur. 1991, c. 64, a. 1741 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1741 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1732. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 592-593 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1741 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Lorsque la vente d'un bien meuble a été faite sans terme, le vendeur peut, dans les trente jours de la délivrance, considérer la vente comme résolue et revendiquer le bien, si l'acheteur, alors qu'il est en demeure, fait défaut de payer le prix et si le meuble est encore entier et dans le même état, sans être passé entre les mains d'un tiers qui en a payé le prix ou d'un créancier hypothécaire qui a obtenu le délaissement du bien. La saisie par un tiers, alors que l'acheteur est en demeure de payer le prix et que le bien est dans les conditions prescrites pour la résolution, ne fait pas obstacle au droit du vendeur Article 1741 (SQ 1991, c. 64) Except in the case of a sale with a term, the seller of movable property may, within thirty days of delivery, consider the sale resolved and revendicate the property if the buyer, being in default, has failed to pay the price and if the property is still entire and in the same condition and has not passed into the hands of a third person who has paid the price thereof, or of a hypothecary creditor who has obtained surrender thereof. Where the buyer is in default to pay the price and the property meets the conditions prescribed for resolution of the sale, the seizure of the property by a third person in no hindrance to the rights of the seller. Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1998, 1999, 2000 O.R.C.C. : L. IV, articles 288, 289 Commentaires Cet article qui associe directement le droit de revendication du bien, auparavant accordé au vendeur par les articles 1998 et 2000 C.C.B.C., à la résolution de plein droit de la vente, modifie quelque peu les recours antérieurement accordés au vendeur d'un bien meuble, lorsque la vente est faite sans terme et que l'acheteur fait défaut de payer le prix. L'article demeure cependant proche du droit antérieur, en ce que les conditions d'exercice du droit de résolution sont les mêmes que celles qui étaient exigées pour l'exercice du droit de revendication. Ainsi, la vente devra être faite sans terme, le meuble devra être encore entier et dans le même état et il ne devra pas être passé entre les mains d'un tiers qui en a payé le prix ou d'un créancier hypothécaire qui a obtenu le délaissement du bien. En ce dernier cas, le vendeur ne peut plus avoir droit à la résolution de la vente et à la revendication du bien; il peut, cependant, se prévaloir de la priorité que lui confère l'article 2651-2° s'il remplit les conditions qui y sont édictées. Quant au délai pour exercer ce recours, il est fixé à 30 jours : en effet, rien ne justifie que l'on conserve la distinction du droit antérieur qui fixait ce délai à 30 jours, en cas de faillite, et à 8 jours dans les autres cas. L'article 1741 modifie aussi le droit antérieur, car conformément aux conditions générales de la résolution de plein droit introduites par l'article 1605, il exige une mise en demeure préalable; la demeure peut être, dans certains cas, de plein droit (article 1597). Le second alinéa modifie lui aussi le droit antérieur, en ce qu'il conserve au vendeur le droit à la résolution de plein droit de la vente en cas de saisie du bien par un tiers. L'acheteur doit toutefois être en demeure de payer le prix et le bien doit être dans les conditions prescrites pour la résolution. Si les conditions de l'article 1741 ne se rencontrent pas, la résolution de la vente, pour défaut de paiement du prix, peut alors s'obtenir en vertu des règles générales du droit des obligations. En effet, les restrictions antérieures qu'énonçait l'article 1543 C.C.B.C., selon lesquelles le bien devait être demeuré entre les mains de l'acheteur et, qu'en cas de faillite, ce droit devait être exercé dans les 30 jours de la délivrance, ne sont pas reprises. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03229166567325592, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1741: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"@search.score\": 0.03229166567325592, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 4, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\"}, \"token_count\": 1279, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1738\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1738\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1738: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1738 L’acheteur qui découvre un risque d’atteinte à son droit de propriété doit, par écrit et dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte, dénoncer au vendeur le droit ou la prétention du tiers, en précisant la nature de ce droit ou de cette prétention. Le vendeur qui connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer ce droit ou cette prétention ne peut, toutefois, se prévaloir d’une dénonciation tardive de l’acheteur. 1991, c. 64, a. 1738 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1738 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1729. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 589-592 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1738 (LQ 1991, c. 64) L'acheteur qui découvre un risque d'atteinte à son droit de propriété doit, par écrit et dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte, dénoncer au vendeur le droit ou la prétention du tiers, en précisant la nature de ce droit ou de cette prétention. Le vendeur qui connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer ce droit ou cette prétention ne peut, toutefois, se prévaloir d'une dénonciation tardive de l'acheteur. Article 1738 (SQ 1991, c. 64) A buyer who discovers a risk of infringement of his right of ownership shall, within a reasonable time after discovering it, give notice to the seller, in writing, of the right or claim of the third person, specifying its nature. The seller may not invoke tardy notice from the buyer if he was aware of the right or claim or could not have been unaware of it. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1520 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 377, al.2 Convention sur la vente internationale de marchandises : article 43 Commentaires Cet article s'inspire de l'article 43 de la Convention des Nations Unies sur la vente internationale de marchandises (Convention de Vienne, 1980). Ainsi, il oblige l'acheteur qui désire se prévaloir de la garantie du droit de propriété prévue aux articles 1723 à 1725, à dénoncer au vendeur, par écrit et dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte, le droit ou la prétention du tiers, en en précisant la nature. Le second alinéa précise toutefois, comme le fait d'ailleurs la Convention internationale, que le vendeur qui connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer ce droit ou cette prétention, ne pourra se prévaloir du caractère tardif de la dénonciation faite par l'acheteur, ce qui ne l'empêchera pas d'invoquer, le cas échéant, les délais de prescription prévus aux articles 2923 et 2925 du nouveau code. Cet article est de nature à inciter les parties en cause à régler leur différend à l'amiable et non en justice. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1738\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.024705883115530014, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1738\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1738: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1738\", \"@search.score\": 0.024705883115530014, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 5, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1738\"}, \"token_count\": 872, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1738\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1733: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1733 Le vendeur ne peut exclure ni limiter sa responsabilité s’il n’a pas révélé les vices qu’il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer et qui affectent le droit de propriété ou la qualité du bien. Cette règle reçoit exception lorsque l’acheteur achète à ses risques et périls d’un vendeur non professionnel. 1991, c. 64, a. 1733 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1733 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1724. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 588-589 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : suspendu Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 599 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 34 (11 décembre 1991),  p. 1371-1373 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1733 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur ne peut exclure ni limiter sa responsabilité s'il n'a pas révélé les vices qu'il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer et qui affectent le droit de propriété ou la qualité du bien. Cette règle reçoit exception lorsque l'acheteur achète à ses risques et périls d'un vendeur non professionnel. Article 1733 (SQ 1991, c. 64) A seller may not exclude or limit his liability unless he has disclosed the defects of which he was aware or could not have been unaware and which affect the right of ownership or the quality of the property. An exception may be made to this rule where a buyer buys property at his own risk from a seller who is not a professional seller. Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1510, 1524 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 361 Commentaires Cet article, qui s'inspire des articles 1510 et 1524 C.C.B.C., introduit un régime identique pour les clauses limitatives ou exclusives de garantie, plutôt que de retenir les régimes différents établis par le droit antérieur, selon qu'il s'agissait de la garantie d'éviction ou de la garantie des vices cachés. Il précise que le vendeur ne peut, par garantie conventionnelle, se soustraire aux obligations de garantie du droit de propriété et de garantie de qualité, énoncées aux articles 1723 à 1731 dans l'hypothèse où il n'a pas dénoncé les vices qu'il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer. De plus, il protège l'acheteur contre le vendeur professionnel qui pourrait exclure sa responsabilité en mentionnant simplement au contrat que l'acheteur achète à ses risques et périls. Une telle exclusion ne sera possible que si le vendeur n'est pas professionnel. Le caractère impératif de cette règle s'infère du texte de l'article 1733, sans qu'il ait paru nécessaire de l'énoncer expressément. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.0314980149269104, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1733: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"@search.score\": 0.0314980149269104, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 4, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\"}, \"token_count\": 883, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-c-67.01-fr\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-c-67.01-fr\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Loi concernant la convention des Nations Unies sur les contrats de vente internationale de marchandises, RLRQ c C-67.01 - Article 3\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... a lieu, à remettre les documents s’y rapportant. SECTION I LIVRAISON DES MARCHANDISES ET REMISE DES DOCUMENTS Article 31 Si le vendeur n’est pas tenu de livrer les marchandises en un autre lieu particulier, son obligation de livraison consiste: a ) lorsque le contrat de vente implique un transport des marchandises, à remettre les marchandises au premier transporteur pour transmission à l’acheteur; b ) lorsque, dans les cas non visés au précédent alinéa, le contrat porte sur un corps certain ou sur une chose de genre qui doit être prélevée sur une masse déterminée ou qui doit être fabriquée ou produite et lorsque, au moment de la conclusion du contrat, les parties savaient que les marchandises se trouvaient ou devaient être fabriquées ou produites en un lieu particulier, à mettre les marchandises à la disposition de l’acheteur en ce lieu; c ) dans les autres cas, à mettre les marchandises à la disposition de l’acheteur au lieu où le vendeur avait son établissement au moment de la conclusion du contrat. Article 32 1) Si, conformément au contrat ou à la présente Convention, le vendeur remet les marchandises à un transporteur et si les marchandises ne sont pas clairement identifiées aux fins du contrat par l’apposition d’un signe distinctif sur les marchandises, par des documents de transport ou par tout autre moyen, le vendeur doit donner à l’acheteur avis de l’expédition en désignant spécifiquement les marchandises. 2) Si le vendeur est tenu de prendre des dispositions pour le transport des marchandises, il doit conclure les contrats nécessaires pour que le transport soit effectué jusqu’au lieu prévu, par les moyens de transport appropriés aux circonstances et selon les conditions usuelles pour un tel transport. 3) Si le vendeur n’est pas tenu de souscrire lui-même une assurance de transport, il doit fournir à l’acheteur, à la demande de celui-ci, tous renseignements dont il dispose qui sont nécessaires à la conclusion de cette assurance. Article 33 Le vendeur doit livrer les marchandises: a ) si une date est fixée par le contrat ou déterminable par référence au contrat, à cette date; b ) si une période de temps est fixée par le contrat ou déterminable par référence au contrat, à un moment quelconque au cours de cette période, à moins qu’il ne résulte des circonstances que c’est à l’acheteur de choisir une date; ou c ) dans tous les autres cas, dans un délai raisonnable à partir de la conclusion du contrat. Article 34 Si le vendeur est tenu de remettre les documents se rapportant aux marchandises, il doit s’acquitter de cette obligation au moment, au lieu et dans la forme prévus au contrat. En cas de remise anticipée, le vendeur conserve, jusqu’au moment prévu pour la remise, le droit de réparer tout défaut de conformité des documents, à condition que l’exercice de ce droit ne cause à l’acheteur ni inconvénients ni frais déraisonnables. Toutefois, l’acheteur conserve le droit de demander des dommages-intérêts conformément à la présente Convention. SECTION II CONFORMITÉ DES MARCHANDISES ET DROITS OU PRÉTENTIONS DE TIERS Article 35 1) Le vendeur doit livrer des marchandises dont la quantité, la qualité et le type répondent à ceux qui sont prévus au contrat, et dont l’emballage ou le conditionnement correspond à celui qui est prévu au contrat. 2) À moins que les parties n’en soient convenues autrement, les marchandises ne sont conformes au contrat que si: a ) elles sont propres aux usages auxquels serviraient habituellement des marchandises du même type; b ) elles sont propres à tout usage spécial qui a été porté expressément ou tacitement à la connaissance du vendeur au moment de la conclusion du contrat, sauf s’il résulte des circonstances que l’acheteur ne s’en est pas remis à la compétence ou à l’appréciation du vendeur ou qu’il n’était pas raisonnable de sa part de le faire; c ) elles possèdent les qualités d’une marchandise que le vendeur a présentée à l’acheteur comme échantillon ou modèle; d ) elles sont emballées ou conditionnées selon le mode habituel pour les marchandises du même type ou, à défaut de mode habituel, d’une manière propre à les conserver et à les protéger. 3) Le vendeur n’est pas responsable, au regard des alinéas a) à d) du paragraphe précédent, d’un défaut de conformité que l’acheteur connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer au moment de la conclusion du contrat. Article 36 1) Le vendeur est responsable, conformément au contrat et à la présente Convention, de tout défaut de conformité qui existe au moment du transfert des risques à l’acheteur, même si ce défaut n’apparaît qu’ultérieurement. 2) Le vendeur est également responsable de tout défaut de conformité qui survient après le moment indiqué au paragraphe précédent et qui est imputable à l’inexécution de l’une quelconque de ses obligations, y compris à un manquement à une garantie que, pendant une certaine période, les marchandises resteront propres à leur usage normal ou à un usage spécial ou conserveront des qualités ou caractéristiques spécifiées. Article 37 En cas de livraison anticipée, le vendeur a le droit, jusqu’à la date prévue pour la livraison, soit de livrer une partie ou une quantité manquante, ou des marchandises nouvelles en remplacement des marchandises non conformes au contrat, soit de réparer tout défaut de conformité des marchandises, à condition que l’exercice de ce droit ne cause à l’acheteur ni inconvénients ni frais déraisonnables. Toutefois, l’acheteur conserve le droit de demander des dommages-intérêts conformément à la présente Convention. Article 38 1) L’acheteur doit examiner les marchandises ou les faire examiner dans un délai aussi bref que possible eu égard aux circonstances. 2) Si le contrat implique un transport des marchandises, l’examen peut être différé jusqu’à leur arrivée à destination. 3) Si les marchandises sont déroutées ou réexpédiées par l’acheteur sans que celui-ci ait eu raisonnablement la possibilité de les examiner et si, au moment de la conclusion du contrat, le vendeur connaissait ou aurait dû connaître la possibilité de ce déroutage ou de cette réexpédition, l’examen peut être différé jusqu’à l’arrivée des marchandises à leur nouvelle destination. Article 39 1) L’acheteur est déchu du droit de se prévaloir d’un défaut de conformité s’il ne le dénonce pas au vendeur, en précisant la nature de ce défaut, dans un délai raisonnable à partir du moment où il l’a constaté ou aurait dû le constater. 2) Dans tous les cas, l’acheteur est déchu du droit de se prévaloir d’un défaut de conformité, s’il ne le dénonce pas au plus tard dans un délai de deux ans à compter de la date à laquelle les marchandises lui ont été effectivement remises, à moins que ce délai ne soit incompatible avec la durée d’une garantie contractuelle. Article 40 Le vendeur ne peut pas se prévaloir des dispositions des articles 38 et 39 lorsque le défaut de conformité porte sur des faits qu’il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer et qu’il n’a pas révélés à l’acheteur. Article 41 Le vendeur doit livrer les marchandises libres de tout droit ou prétention d’un tiers, à moins que l’acheteur n’accepte de prendre les marchandises dans ces conditions. Toutefois, si ce droit ou cette prétention est fondé sur la propriété industrielle ou autre propriété intellectuelle, l’obligation du vendeur est régie par l’article 42. Article 42 1) Le vendeur doit livrer les marchandises libres de tout droit ou prétention d’un tiers fondé sur la propriété industrielle ou autre propriété intellectuelle, qu’il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer au moment de la conclusion du contrat, à condition que ce droit ou cette prétention soit fondé sur la propriété industrielle ou autre propriété intellectuelle: a ) en vertu de la loi de l’État où les marchandises doivent être revendues ou utilisées, si les parties ont envisagé au moment de la conclusion du contrat que les marchandises seraient revendues ou utilisées dans cet État; ou b ) dans tous les autres cas, en vertu de la loi de l’État où l’acheteur a son établissement. 2) Dans les cas suivants, le vendeur n’est pas tenu de l’obligation prévue au paragraphe précédent: a ) au moment de la conclusion du contrat, l’acheteur connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer l’existence du droit ou de la prétention; ou b ) le droit ou la prétention résulte de ce que le vendeur s’est conformé aux plans techniques, dessins, formules ou autres spécifications analogues fournis par l’acheteur. Article 43 1) L’acheteur perd le droit de se prévaloir des dispositions des articles 41 et 42 s’il ne dénonce pas au vendeur le droit ou la prétention du tiers, en précisant la nature de ce droit ou de cette prétention, dans un délai raisonnable à partir du moment où il en a eu connaissance ou aurait dû en avoir connaissance. 2) Le vendeur ne peut pas se prévaloir des dispositions du paragraphe précédent s’il connaissait le droit ou la prétention du tiers et sa nature. Article 44 Nonobstant les dispositions du paragraphe 1 de l’article 39 et du paragraphe 1 de l’article 43, l’acheteur peut réduire le prix conformément à l’article 50 ou demander des dommages-intérêts, sauf pour le gain manqué, s’il a une excuse raisonnable pour n’avoir pas procédé à la dénonciation requise. SECTION III MOYENS DONT DISPOSE L’ACHETEUR EN CAS DE CONTRAVENTION AU CONTRAT PAR LE VENDEUR Article 45 1) Si le vendeur n’a pas exécuté l’une quelconque des obligations résultant pour lui du contrat de vente ou de la présente Convention, l’acheteur est fondé à: a ) exercer les droits prévus aux articles 46 à 52; b ) demander les dommages-intérêts prévus aux articles 74 à 77. 2) L’acheteur ne perd pas le droit de demander des dommages-intérêts lorsqu’il exerce son droit de recourir à un autre moyen. 3) Aucun délai de grâce ne peut être accordé au vendeur par un juge ou par un arbitre lorsque l’acheteur se prévaut d’un des moyens dont il dispose en cas de contravention au contrat. Article 46 1) L’acheteur peut exiger du vendeur l’exécution de ses obligations, à moins qu’il ne se soit prévalu d’un moyen incompatible avec cette exigence. 2) Si les marchandises ne sont pas conformes au contrat, l’acheteur ne peut exiger du vendeur la livraison de marchandises de remplacement que si le défaut de conformité constitue une contravention essentielle au contrat et si cette livraison est demandée au moment de la dénonciation du défaut de conformité faite conformément à l’article 39 ou dans un délai raisonnable à compter de cette dénonciation. 3) Si les marchandises ne sont pas conformes au contrat, l’acheteur peut exiger du vendeur qu’il répare le défaut de conformité, à moins que cela ne soit déraisonnable compte tenu de toutes les circonstances. La réparation doit être demandée au moment de la dénonciation du défaut de conformité faite conformément à l’article 39 ou dans un délai raisonnable à compter de cette dénonciation. Article 47 1) L’acheteur peut impartir au vendeur un délai supplémentaire de durée raisonnable pour l’exécution de ses obligations. 2) À moins qu’il n’ait reçu du vendeur une notification l’informant que celui-ci n’exécuterait pas ses obligations dans le délai ainsi imparti, l’acheteur ne peut, avant l’expiration de ce délai, se prévaloir d’aucun des moyens dont il dispose en cas de contravention au contrat. Toutefois, l’acheteur ne perd pas, de ce fait, le droit de demander des dommages-intérêts pour retard dans l’exécution. Article 48 1) Sous réserve de l’article 49, le vendeur peut, même après la date de la livraison, réparer à ses frais tout manquement à ses obligations, à condition que cela n’entraîne pas un retard déraisonnable et ne cause à l’acheteur ni inconvénients déraisonnables ni incertitude quant au remboursement par le vendeur des frais faits par l’acheteur. Toutefois, l’acheteur conserve le droit de demander des dommages-intérêts conformément à la présente Convention. 2) Si le vendeur demande à l’acheteur de lui faire savoir s’il accepte l’exécution et si l’acheteur ne lui répond pas dans un délai raisonnable, le vendeur peut exécuter ses obligations dans le délai qu’il a indiqué dans sa demande. L’acheteur ne peut, avant l’expiration de ce délai, se prévaloir d’un moyen incompatible avec l’exécution par le vendeur de ses obligations. 3) Lorsque le vendeur notifie à l’acheteur son intention d’exécuter ses obligations dans un délai déterminé, il est présumé demander à l’acheteur de lui faire connaître sa décision conformément au paragraphe précédent. 4) Une demande ou une notification faite par le vendeur en vertu des paragraphes 2 ou 3 du présent article n’a effet que si elle est reçue par l’acheteur. Article 49 1) L’acheteur peut déclarer le contrat résolu: a ) si l’inexécution par le vendeur de l’une quelconque des obligations résultant pour lui du contrat ou de la présente Convention constitue une contravention essentielle au contrat; ou b ) en cas de défaut de livraison, si le vendeur ne livre pas les marchandises dans le délai supplémentaire imparti par l’acheteur conformément au paragraphe 1 de l’article 47 ou s’il déclare qu’il ne les livrera pas dans le délai ainsi imparti. 2) Cependant, lorsque le vendeur a livré les marchandises, l’acheteur est déchu du droit de déclarer le contrat résolu s’il ne l’a pas fait: a ) en cas de livraison tardive, dans un délai raisonnable à partir du moment où il a su que la livraison avait été effectuée; b ) en cas de contravention autre que la livraison tardive, dans un délai raisonnable: i) à partir du moment où il a eu connaissance ou aurait dû avoir connaissance de cette contravention; ii) après l’expiration de tout délai supplémentaire imparti par l’acheteur conformément au paragraphe 1 de l’article 47 ou après que le vendeur a déclaré qu’il n’exécuterait pas ses obligations dans ce délai supplémentaire; ou iii) après l’expiration de tout délai supplémentaire indiqué par le vendeur conformément au paragraphe 2 de l’article 48 ou après que l’acheteur a déclaré qu’il n’accepterait pas l’exécution. Article 50 En cas de défaut de conformité des marchandises au contrat, que le prix ait été ou non déjà payé, l’acheteur peut réduire le prix proportionnellement à la différence entre la valeur que les marchandises effectivement livrées avaient au moment de la livraison et la valeur que des marchandises conformes auraient eue à ce moment. Cependant...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... ’accepterait pas l’exécution. Article 50 En cas de défaut de conformité des marchandises au contrat, que le prix ait été ou non déjà payé, l’acheteur peut réduire le prix proportionnellement à la différence entre la valeur que les marchandises effectivement livrées avaient au moment de la livraison et la valeur que des marchandises conformes auraient eue à ce moment. Cependant, si le vendeur répare tout manquement à ses obligations conformément à l’article 37 ou à l’article 48 ou si l’acheteur refuse d’accepter l’exécution par le vendeur conformément à ces articles, l’acheteur ne peut réduire le prix. Article 51 1) Si le vendeur ne livre qu’une partie des marchandises ou si une partie seulement des marchandises livrées est conforme au contrat, les articles 46 à 50 s’appliquent en ce qui concerne la partie manquante ou non conforme. 2) L’acheteur ne peut déclarer le contrat résolu dans sa totalité que si l’inexécution partielle ou le défaut de conformité constitue une contravention essentielle au contrat. Article 52 1) Si le vendeur livre les marchandises avant la date fixée, l’acheteur a la faculté d’en prendre livraison ou de refuser d’en prendre livraison. 2) Si le vendeur livre une quantité supérieure à celle prévue au contrat, l’acheteur peut accepter ou refuser de prendre livraison de la quantité excédentaire. Si l’acheteur accepte d’en prendre livraison en tout ou en partie, il doit la payer au tarif du contrat. CHAPITRE III OBLIGATIONS DE L’ACHETEUR Article 53 L’acheteur s’oblige, dans les conditions prévues au contrat et par la présente Convention, à payer le prix et à prendre livraison des marchandises. SECTION I PAIEMENT DU PRIX Article 54 L’obligation qu’a l’acheteur de payer le prix comprend celle de prendre les mesures et d’accomplir les formalités destinées à permettre le paiement du prix qui sont prévues par le contrat ou par les lois et les règlements. Article 55 Si la vente est valablement conclue sans que le prix des marchandises vendues ait été fixé dans le contrat expressément ou implicitement ou par une disposition permettant de le déterminer, les parties sont réputées, sauf indications contraires, s’être tacitement référées au prix habituellement pratiqué au moment de la conclusion du contrat, dans la branche commerciale considérée, pour les mêmes marchandises vendues dans des circonstances comparables. Article 56 Si le prix est fixé d’après le poids des marchandises, c’est le poids net qui, en cas de doute, détermine ce prix. Article 57 1) Si l’acheteur n’est pas tenu de payer le prix en un autre lieu particulier, il doit payer le vendeur: a ) à l’établissement de celui-ci; ou b ) si le paiement doit être fait contre la remise des marchandises ou des documents, au lieu de cette remise. 2) Le vendeur doit supporter toute augmentation des frais accessoires au paiement qui résultent de son changement d’établissement après la conclusion du contrat. Article 58 1) Si l’acheteur n’est pas tenu de payer le prix à un autre moment déterminé, il doit le payer lorsque, conformément au contrat et à la présente Convention, le vendeur met à sa disposition soit les marchandises soit des documents représentatifs des marchandises. Le vendeur peut faire du paiement une condition de la remise des marchandises ou des documents. 2) Si le contrat implique un transport des marchandises, le vendeur peut en faire l’expédition sous condition que celles-ci ou les documents représentatifs ne seront remis à l’acheteur que contre paiement du prix. 3) L’acheteur n’est pas tenu de payer le prix avant d’avoir eu la possibilité d’examiner les marchandises, à moins que les modalités de livraison ou de paiement dont sont convenues les parties ne lui en laissent pas la possibilité. Article 59 L’acheteur doit payer le prix à la date fixée au contrat ou résultant du contrat et de la présente Convention, sans qu’il soit besoin d’aucune demande ou autre formalité de la part du vendeur. SECTION II PRISE DE LIVRAISON Article 60 L’obligation de l’acheteur de prendre livraison consiste: a ) à accomplir tout acte qu’on peut raisonnablement attendre de lui pour permettre au vendeur d’effectuer la livraison; et b ) à retirer les marchandises. SECTION III MOYENS DONT DISPOSE LE VENDEUR EN CAS DE CONTRAVENTION AU CONTRAT PAR L’ACHETEUR Article 61 1) Si l’acheteur n’a pas exécuté l’une quelconque des obligations résultant pour lui du contrat de vente ou de la présente Convention, le vendeur est fondé à: a ) exercer les droits prévus aux articles 62 à 65; b ) demander les dommages-intérêts prévus aux articles 74 à 77. 2) Le vendeur ne perd pas le droit de demander des dommages-intérêts lorsqu’il exerce son droit de recourir à un autre moyen. 3) Aucun délai de grâce ne peut être accordé à l’acheteur par un juge ou par un arbitre lorsque le vendeur se prévaut d’un des moyens dont il dispose en cas de contravention au contrat. Article 62 Le vendeur peut exiger de l’acheteur le paiement du prix, la prise de livraison des marchandises ou l’exécution des autres obligations de l’acheteur, à moins qu’il ne se soit prévalu d’un moyen incompatible avec ces exigences. Article 63 1) Le vendeur peut impartir à l’acheteur un délai supplémentaire de durée raisonnable pour l’exécution de ses obligations. 2) À moins qu’il n’ait reçu de l’acheteur une notification l’informant que celui-ci n’exécuterait pas ses obligations dans le délai ainsi imparti, le vendeur ne peut, avant l’expiration de ce délai, se prévaloir d’aucun des moyens dont il dispose en cas de contravention au contrat. Toutefois, le vendeur ne perd pas, de ce fait, le droit de demander des dommages-intérêts pour retard dans l’exécution. Article 64 1) Le vendeur peut déclarer le contrat résolu: a ) si l’inexécution par l’acheteur de l’une quelconque des obligations résultant pour lui du contrat ou de la présente Convention constitue une contravention essentielle au contrat; ou b ) si l’acheteur n’exécute pas son obligation de payer le prix ou ne prend pas livraison des marchandises dans le délai supplémentaire imparti par le vendeur conformément au paragraphe 1 de l’article 63 ou s’il déclare qu’il ne le fera pas dans le délai ainsi imparti. 2) Cependant, lorsque l’acheteur a payé le prix, le vendeur est déchu du droit de déclarer le contrat résolu s’il ne l’a pas fait: a ) en cas d’exécution tardive par l’acheteur, avant d’avoir su qu’il y avait eu exécution; ou b ) en cas de contravention par l’acheteur autre que l’exécution tardive, dans un délai raisonnable: i) à partir du moment où le vendeur a eu connaissance ou aurait dû avoir connaissance de cette contravention; ou ii) après l’expiration de tout délai supplémentaire imparti par le vendeur conformément au paragraphe 1 de l’article 63 ou après que l’acheteur a déclaré qu’il n’exécuterait pas ses obligations dans ce délai supplémentaire. Article 65 1) Si le contrat prévoit que l’acheteur doit spécifier la forme, la mesure ou d’autres caractéristiques des marchandises et si l’acheteur n’effectue pas cette spécification à la date convenue ou dans un délai raisonnable à compter de la réception d’une demande du vendeur, celui-ci peut, sans préjudice de tous autres droits qu’il peut avoir, effectuer lui-même cette spécification d’après les besoins de l’acheteur dont il peut avoir connaissance. 2) Si le vendeur effectue lui-même la spécification, il doit en faire connaître les modalités à l’acheteur et lui impartir un délai raisonnable pour une spécification différente. Si, après réception de la communication du vendeur, l’acheteur n’utilise pas cette possibilité dans le délai ainsi imparti, la spécification effectuée par le vendeur est définitive. CHAPITRE IV TRANSFERT DES RISQUES Article 66 La perte ou la détérioration des marchandises survenue après le transfert des risques à l’acheteur ne libère pas celui-ci de son obligation de payer le prix, à moins que ces événements ne soient dus à un fait du vendeur. Article 67 1) Lorsque le contrat de vente implique un transport des marchandises et que le vendeur n’est pas tenu de les remettre en un lieu déterminé, les risques sont transférés à l’acheteur à partir de la remise des marchandises au premier transporteur pour transmission à l’acheteur conformément au contrat de vente. Lorsque le vendeur est tenu de remettre les marchandises à un transporteur en un lieu déterminé, les risques ne sont pas transférés à l’acheteur tant que les marchandises n’ont pas été remises au transporteur en ce lieu. Le fait que le vendeur soit autorisé à conserver les documents représentatifs des marchandises n’affecte pas le transfert des risques. 2) Cependant, les risques ne sont pas transférés à l’acheteur tant que les marchandises n’ont pas été clairement identifiées aux fins du contrat, que ce soit par l’apposition d’un signe distinctif sur les marchandises, par des documents de transport, par un avis donné à l’acheteur ou par tout autre moyen. Article 68 En ce qui concerne les marchandises vendues en cours de transport, les risques sont transférés à l’acheteur à partir du moment où le contrat est conclu. Toutefois, si les circonstances l’impliquent, les risques sont à la charge de l’acheteur à compter du moment où les marchandises ont été remises au transporteur qui a émis les documents constatant le contrat de transport. Néanmoins, si, au moment de la conclusion du contrat de vente, le vendeur avait connaissance ou aurait dû avoir connaissance du fait que les marchandises avaient péri ou avaient été détériorées et qu’il n’en a pas informé l’acheteur, la perte ou la détérioration est à la charge du vendeur. Article 69 1) Dans les cas non visés par les articles 67 et 68, les risques sont transférés à l’acheteur lorsqu’il retire les marchandises ou, s’il ne le fait pas en temps voulu, à partir du moment où les marchandises sont mises à sa disposition et où il commet une contravention au contrat en n’en prenant pas livraison. 2) Cependant, si l’acheteur est tenu de retirer les marchandises en un lieu autre qu’un établissement du vendeur, les risques sont transférés lorsque la livraison est due et que l’acheteur sait que les marchandises sont mises à sa disposition en ce lieu. 3) Si la vente porte sur des marchandises non encore individualisées, les marchandises ne sont réputées avoir été mises à la disposition de l’acheteur que lorsqu’elles ont été clairement identifiées aux fins du contrat. Article 70 Si le vendeur a commis une contravention essentielle au contrat, les dispositions des articles 67, 68 et 69 ne portent pas atteinte aux moyens dont l’acheteur dispose en raison de cette contravention. CHAPITRE V DISPOSITIONS COMMUNES AUX OBLIGATIONS DU VENDEUR ET DE L’ACHETEUR SECTION I CONTRAVENTION ANTICIPÉE ET CONTRATS À LIVRAISONS SUCCESSIVES Article 71 1) Une partie peut différer l’exécution de ses obligations lorsqu’il apparaît, après la conclusion du contrat, que l’autre partie n’exécutera pas une partie essentielle de ses obligations du fait: a ) d’une grave insuffisance dans la capacité d’exécution de cette partie ou sa solvabilité; ou b ) de la manière dont elle s’apprête à exécuter ou exécute le contrat. 2) Si le vendeur a déjà expédié les marchandises lorsque se révèlent les raisons prévues au paragraphe précédent, il peut s’opposer à ce que les marchandises soient remises à l’acheteur, même si celui-ci détient un document lui permettant de les obtenir. Le présent paragraphe ne concerne que les droits respectifs du vendeur et de l’acheteur sur les marchandises. 3) La partie qui diffère l’exécution, avant ou après l’expédition des marchandises, doit adresser immédiatement une notification à cet effet à l’autre partie, et elle doit procéder à l’exécution si l’autre partie donne des assurances suffisantes de la bonne exécution de ses obligations. Article 72 1) Si, avant la date de l’exécution du contrat, il est manifeste qu’une partie commettra une contravention essentielle au contrat, l’autre partie peut déclarer celui-ci résolu. 2) Si elle dispose du temps nécessaire, la partie qui a l’intention de déclarer le contrat résolu doit le notifier à l’autre partie dans des conditions raisonnables pour lui permettre de donner des assurances suffisantes de la bonne exécution de ses obligations. 3) Les dispositions du paragraphe précédent ne s’appliquent pas si l’autre partie a déclaré qu’elle n’exécuterait pas ses obligations. Article 73 1) Dans les contrats à livraisons successives, si l’inexécution par l’une des parties d’une obligation relative à une livraison constitue une contravention essentielle au contrat en ce qui concerne cette livraison, l’autre partie peut déclarer le contrat résolu pour ladite livraison. 2) Si l’inexécution par l’une des parties d’une obligation relative à une livraison donne à l’autre partie de sérieuses raisons de penser qu’il y aura contravention essentielle au contrat en ce qui concerne des obligations futures, elle peut déclarer le contrat résolu pour l’avenir, à condition de le faire dans un délai raisonnable. 3) L’acheteur qui déclare le contrat résolu pour une livraison peut, en même temps, le déclarer résolu pour les livraisons déjà reçues ou pour les livraisons futures si, en raison de leur connexité, ces livraisons ne peuvent être utilisées aux fins envisagées par les parties au moment de la conclusion du contrat. SECTION II DOMMAGES-INTÉRÊTS Article 74 Les dommages-intérêts pour une contravention au contrat commise par une partie sont égaux à la perte subie et au gain manqué par l’autre partie par suite de la contravention. Ces dommages-intérêts ne peuvent être supérieurs à la perte subie et au gain manqué que la partie en défaut avait prévus ou aurait dû prévoir au moment de la conclusion du contrat, en considérant les faits dont elle avait connaissance ou aurait dû avoir connaissance, comme étant des conséquences possibles de la contravention au contrat. Article 75 Lorsque le contrat est résolu et que, d’une manière raisonnable et dans un délai raisonnable après la résolution, l’acheteur a procédé à un achat de remplacement ou le vendeur à une vente compensatoire, la partie qui demande des dommages-intérêts peut obtenir la différence entre le prix du contrat et le prix de l’achat de remplacement ou de la vente compensatoire ainsi que tous autres dommages-intérêts qui peuvent être dus en vertu de l’article 74. Article 76 1) Lorsque le contrat est résolu et que les marchandises ont un prix courant, la partie qui...\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-c-67.01-fr\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03067915514111519, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-c-67.01-fr\", \"title\": \"Loi concernant la convention des Nations Unies sur les contrats de vente internationale de marchandises, RLRQ c C-67.01 - Article 3\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-c-67.01-fr\", \"@search.score\": 0.03067915514111519, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 4, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-c-67.01-fr\"}, \"token_count\": 7195, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-c-67.01-fr\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1765\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1765\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1765: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1765 Le défaut de l’acheteur de payer le prix, selon les conditions de la vente, permet à l’encanteur, outre les recours ordinaires du vendeur, de revendre le bien à la folle enchère, selon l’usage et après un avis suffisant. Le fol enchérisseur ne peut, alors, enchérir de nouveau et il est tenu, le cas échéant, de payer la différence entre le prix de son adjudication et le prix moindre de la revente, sans qu’il puisse réclamer l’excédent. Il est aussi, en cas de vente forcée, responsable envers le vendeur, le saisi et les créanciers qui ont obtenu un jugement, des intérêts, des frais et des dommages-intérêts résultant de son défaut. 1991, c. 64, a. 1765 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1765 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1755. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 600 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1765 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le défaut de l'acheteur de payer le prix, selon les conditions de la vente, permet à l'encanteur, outre les recours ordinaires du vendeur, de revendre le bien à la folle enchère, selon l'usage et après un avis suffisant. Le fol enchérisseur ne peut, alors, enchérir de nouveau et il est tenu, le cas échéant, de payer la différence entre le prix de son adjudication et le prix moindre de la revente, sans qu'il puisse réclamer l'excédent. Il est aussi, en cas de vente forcée, responsable envers le vendeur, le saisi et les créanciers qui ont obtenu un jugement, des intérêts, des frais et des dommages-intérêts résultant de son défaut. Article 1765 (SQ 1991, c. 64) If the buyer fails to pay the price in compliance with the conditions of the sale, the auctioneer may, in addition to the ordinary remedies of a seller, resell the property for false bidding, according to usage and after sufficient notice. A false bidder may not bid again at a resale on default. He is bound to pay the difference between the price at which the property was sold to him and the resale price, if lesser, but is not entitled to claim any excess amount. He is also, in the case of a forced sale, liable towards the seller, the person from whom the property was seized and the creditors having obtained the judgment, for all interest, costs and damages arising from his default. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1568 Code de procédure civile : articles 686 d ), 694 O.R.C.C. : L. V, articles 403, 404, 405 Commentaires Cet article reprend en substance l'article 1568 C.C.B.C. et les articles 686 d ) et 694 C.P.C. Le premier alinéa prévoit la revente du bien à la folle enchère, dans le cas où l'acheteur fait défaut de payer le prix selon les conditions de la vente, tout en réservant cependant au vendeur le droit d'exercer ses recours ordinaires. Le second alinéa reprend l'interdiction faite auparavant au fol enchérisseur d'enchérir de nouveau et lui conserve l'obligation de payer, le cas échéant, la différence entre le prix de son adjudication et le prix moindre de la revente. De plus, comme auparavant en cas de vente forcée, cet alinéa rend le fol enchérisseur responsable à l'égard du vendeur, comme il l'était à l'égard du saisi et des créanciers qui ont obtenu un jugement, quant aux intérêts, aux frais et aux dommages-intérêts résultant de son défaut de payer le prix. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1765\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.031159421429038048, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1765\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1765: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1765\", \"@search.score\": 0.031159421429038048, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 4, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1765\"}, \"token_count\": 942, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1765\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1739: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1739 L’acheteur qui constate que le bien est atteint d’un vice doit, par écrit, le dénoncer au vendeur dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte. Ce délai commence à courir, lorsque le vice apparaît graduellement, du jour où l’acheteur a pu en soupçonner la gravité et l’étendue. Le vendeur ne peut se prévaloir d’une dénonciation tardive de l’acheteur s’il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer le vice. 1991, c. 64, a. 1739 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1739 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1730. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 589-592 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1739 (LQ 1991, c. 64) L'acheteur qui constate que le bien est atteint d'un vice doit, par écrit, le dénoncer au vendeur dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte. Ce délai commence à courir, lorsque le vice apparaît graduellement, du jour où l'acheteur a pu en soupçonner la gravité et l'étendue. Le vendeur ne peut se prévaloir d'une dénonciation tardive de l'acheteur s'il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer le vice. Article 1739 (SQ 1991, c. 64) A buyer who ascertains that the property is defective may give notice in writing of the defect to the seller only within a reasonable time after discovering it. The time begins to run, where the defect appears gradually, on the day that the buyer could have suspected the seriousness and extent of the defect. The seller may not invoke tardy notice from the buyer if he was aware of the defect or could not have been unaware of it. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1530 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 377 Convention sur la vente internationale de marchandises : articles 39,40 Commentaires Cet article s'inspire de l'article 39 de la Convention des Nations Unies sur la vente internationale de marchandises (Convention de Vienne, 1980). Il complète l'article précédent. Ainsi, il substitue à l'obligation qu'avait l'acheteur d'intenter une action rédhibitoire pour vice caché dans un délai raisonnable en vertu de l'article 1530 C.C.B.C., celle de dénoncer le vice au vendeur, par écrit. Plutôt que de déterminer un délai fixe, il a semblé préférable de retenir un délai raisonnable, afin de mieux tenir compte des situations diverses qui peuvent se présenter, tant en matière mobilière qu'en matière immobilière. L'exigence d'un délai raisonnable est par ailleurs conforme à la Convention. Afin de ne pas imposer à l'acheteur l'obligation de donner l'avis requis dès la première apparition du vice lorsque le vice apparaît graduellement, le délai raisonnable pour remettre l'avis ne commencera à courir que du jour où l'acheteur aura pu soupçonner la gravité et l'étendue du vice. Enfin, le second alinéa précise, comme le fait d'ailleurs la Convention de Vienne, que le vendeur qui connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer le vice, ne peut se prévaloir du caractère tardif de la dénonciation faite par l'acheteur, ce qui ne l'empêche pas d'invoquer le cas échéant, les délais de prescription prévus aux articles 2923 et 2925 du nouveau code. Comme l'article 1738, cet article est de nature à inciter les parties à régler leur différend à l'amiable. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.029857397079467773, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1739: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"@search.score\": 0.029857397079467773, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 4, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\"}, \"token_count\": 1022, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1723: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1723 Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l’acheteur que le bien est libre de tous droits, à l’exception de ceux qu’il a déclarés lors de la vente. Il est tenu de purger le bien des hypothèques qui le grèvent, même déclarées ou inscrites, à moins que l’acheteur n’ait assumé la dette ainsi garantie. 1991, c. 64, a. 1723 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1723 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1716. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 586-587 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 9 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1723 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l'acheteur que le bien est libre de tous droits, à l'exception de ceux qu'il a déclarés lors de la vente. Il est tenu de purger le bien des hypothèques qui le grèvent, même déclarées ou inscrites, à moins que l'acheteur n'ait assumé la dette ainsi garantie. Article 1723 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property is free of all rights except those he has declared at the time of the sale. The seller is bound to discharge the property of all hypothecs, even declared or registered, unless the buyer has assumed the debt so secured. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1508 O.R.C.C. : L. V, articles 363, 364 Commentaires Cet article modifie le droit antérieur qu'exprimait l'article 1508 C.C.B.C., en ce qu'il remplace la garantie contre l'éviction par une garantie plus étendue, la garantie du droit de propriété. Cette garantie oblige le vendeur à fournir non seulement une possession utile et paisible du bien vendu, mais aussi à fournir un bon titre au moment de la vente. L'acheteur n'aura donc plus à attendre d'être menacé d'éviction avant de pouvoir intenter ses recours. Cette garantie est complétée par celles instituées en matière de publicité des droits réels. On pense notamment à la nouvelle présomption de connaissance des droits inscrits aux registres, introduite par l'article 2943, de même qu'à la nouvelle règle énoncée à l'article 2962, visant à protéger les tiers de bonne foi qui se sont fiés aux registres. Cet article, de même que les articles 1724 et 1725, sont complétés par l'article 1738 qui impose à l'acheteur l'obligation de dénoncer au vendeur le risque d'éviction qu'il découvre. Ni l'article 1511 sur les recours particuliers de l'acheteur évincé ni les articles 1512 ss. sur les restrictions à l'exercice de ces recours que l'on retrouvait au Code civil du Bas Canada n'ont été retenus et l'on appliquera plutôt que ces distinctions, les règles générales du droit des obligations, notamment celles prévues au chapitre sixième De l'exécution de l'obligation . Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.027984343469142914, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1723: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"@search.score\": 0.027984343469142914, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 4, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\"}, \"token_count\": 891, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1727: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1727 Lorsque le bien périt en raison d’un vice caché qui existait lors de la vente, la perte échoit au vendeur, lequel est tenu à la restitution du prix; si la perte résulte d’une force majeure ou est due à la faute de l’acheteur, ce dernier doit déduire, du montant de sa réclamation, la valeur du bien, dans l’état où il se trouvait lors de la perte. 1991, c. 64, a. 1727 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1727 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1720. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 588 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1727 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Lorsque le bien périt en raison d'un vice caché qui existait lors de la vente, la perte échoit au vendeur, lequel est tenu à la restitution du prix; si la perte résulte d'une force majeure ou est due à la faute de l'acheteur, ce dernier doit déduire, du montant de sa réclamation, la valeur du bien, dans l'état où il se trouvait lors de la perte. Article 1727 (SQ 1991, c. 64) If the property perishes by reason of a latent defect that existed at the time of the sale, the loss is borne by the seller, who is bound to restore the price; if the loss results from superior force or is due to the fault of the buyer, the buyer shall deduct from his claim the value of the property in the state it was in at the time of the loss. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1529 Commentaires Cet article est en substance conforme au droit antérieur édicté à l'article 1529 C.C.B.C. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.028588563203811646, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1727: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"@search.score\": 0.028588563203811646, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 4, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\"}, \"token_count\": 607, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1716: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1716 Le vendeur est tenu de délivrer le bien, et d’en garantir le droit de propriété et la qualité. Ces garanties existent de plein droit, sans qu’il soit nécessaire de les stipuler dans le contrat de vente. 1991, c. 64, a. 1716 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1716 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1709. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 585 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 8 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1716 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur est tenu de délivrer le bien, et d'en garantir le droit de propriété et la qualité. Ces garanties existent de plein droit, sans qu'il soit nécessaire de les stipuler dans le contrat de vente. Article 1716 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller is bound to deliver the property and to warrant the ownership and quality of the property. These warranties exist of right whether or not they are stipulated in the contract of sale. Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1491, 1506, 1507 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 359 Commentaires Cet article énonce les obligations du vendeur. Il reprend en premier lieu l'obligation de délivrance à laquelle le vendeur est tenu, précisée aux articles 1717 à 1722. Il modifie cependant le droit antérieur, en remplaçant la garantie contre l'éviction par une garantie du droit de propriété, de même qu'en remplaçant la garantie contre les défauts cachés à laquelle le vendeur est tenu, par une garantie de qualité. Enfin, il reprend la règle du droit antérieur, selon laquelle ces garanties existent de plein droit, sans qu'il soit nécessaire de les stipuler dans le contrat de vente. La portée des garanties légales du vendeur est énoncée aux articles 1723 à 1731. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.032258063554763794, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1716: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"@search.score\": 0.032258063554763794, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\"}, \"token_count\": 650, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1748\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1748\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1748: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1748 Lorsque l’acheteur fait défaut de payer le prix de vente selon les modalités du contrat, le vendeur peut exiger le paiement immédiat des versements échus ou reprendre le bien vendu; si le contrat contient une clause de déchéance du terme, il peut plutôt exiger le paiement du solde du prix de vente. 1991, c. 64, a. 1748 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1748 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1738. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 595 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : aucun Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 597 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 21 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1748 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Lorsque l'acheteur fait défaut de payer le prix de vente selon les modalités du contrat, le vendeur peut exiger le paiement immédiat des versements échus ou reprendre le bien vendu; si le contrat contient une clause de déchéance du terme, il peut plutôt exiger le paiement du solde du prix de vente. Article 1748 (SQ 1991, c. 64) Where the buyer fails to pay the sale price in accordance with the terms and conditions of the contract, the seller may exact immediate payment of the instalments due or take back the sold property; if the contract contains a clause of forfeiture of benefit of the term, the seller may instead exact payment of the balance of the sale price. Sources Loi sur la protection du consommateur, L.R.Q., chap. P.40.1 : article 138 Commentaires Cet article nouveau s'inspire de l'article 138 de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur pour déterminer les recours ouverts au vendeur, en cas de défaut par l'acheteur de payer le prix de vente selon les modalités du contrat. Il énonce que le vendeur peut soit exiger le paiement des versements échus, soit reprendre le bien vendu. Dans l'hypothèse où le contrat contient une clause de déchéance du terme, le vendeur peut choisir d'exiger le paiement du solde du prix de vente. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1748\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.0297619067132473, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1748\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1748: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1748\", \"@search.score\": 0.0297619067132473, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1748\"}, \"token_count\": 723, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1748\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCBC - 1539: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... Remplacé le 1er janvier 1994 par le Code civil du Québec (RLRQ, c. C-1991) Article 1539 Le vendeur ne peut rentrer en possession de la chose vendue, sur résolution de la vente faute de paiement du prix, avant d’avoir remboursé à l’acheteur ce qu’il a reçu de lui sur le prix, avec les frais de toutes les réparations nécessaires et des améliorations qui ont augmenté la valeur de la chose, et jusqu’à concurrence de cette valeur. Si ces améliorations sont de nature à être enlevées, il a le choix de les laisser enlever par l’acheteur. 1866 a. 1539 Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.027973394840955734, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"title\": \"CCBC - 1539: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"@search.score\": 0.027973394840955734, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\"}, \"token_count\": 168, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1524\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1524\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCBC - 1524: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... Remplacé le 1er janvier 1994 par le Code civil du Québec (RLRQ, c. C-1991) Article 1524 Le vendeur est tenu des vices cachés, quand même il ne les aurait pas connus, à moins qu’il n’ait stipulé qu’il ne serait obligé à aucune garantie. 1866 a. 1524 Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1524\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.027364110574126244, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1524\", \"title\": \"CCBC - 1524: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1524\", \"@search.score\": 0.027364110574126244, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1524\"}, \"token_count\": 93, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1524\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/ca/regl-dors-2005-103-fr\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/legislation/ca/regl-dors-2005-103-fr\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Ordonnance sur les redevances à payer pour la recherche, le développement des marchés et la promotion des bovins de boucherie, DORS/2005-103 - Article 10\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... Responsabilité 10. (1) Le vendeur n’est pas redevable à l’Office du montant de la redevance déduite conformément à la présente ordonnance de la somme qui lui est due. Preuve (2) À la demande de l’Office, le vendeur lui démontre que la redevance due en vertu de la présente ordonnance a été déduite. Défaut d’effectuer la déduction (3) Si l’acheteur ou le négociant fait défaut d’effectuer la déduction de la redevance conformément aux paragraphes 6(1) et (2) , le vendeur est tenu d’en remettre le montant à l’Office dans les quatre-vingt-dix jours suivant la vente. DORS/2008-17, art. 3(A)....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/ca/regl-dors-2005-103-fr\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.030477948486804962, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/ca/regl-dors-2005-103-fr\", \"title\": \"Ordonnance sur les redevances à payer pour la recherche, le développement des marchés et la promotion des bovins de boucherie, DORS/2005-103 - Article 10\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/ca/regl-dors-2005-103-fr\", \"@search.score\": 0.030477948486804962, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 2, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/legislation/ca/regl-dors-2005-103-fr\"}, \"token_count\": 153, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/ca/regl-dors-2005-103-fr\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1793\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1793\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1793: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1793 Lorsque la vente d’un immeuble à usage d’habitation n’est pas précédée du contrat préliminaire ou de la note d’information, l’acheteur peut, s’il en subit un préjudice sérieux, demander la nullité de la vente et des dommages-intérêts. Si l’acheteur préfère que le contrat soit maintenu, il peut demander une réduction de son obligation équivalente aux dommages-intérêts qu’il eût été justifié de réclamer. Cette action doit être intentée soit dans les 90 jours de la vente, soit dans les 90 jours suivant l’assemblée extraordinaire prévue à l’article 1104. Il en est de même lorsque le contrat préliminaire ou la note d’information comportent des erreurs ou des lacunes. 1991, c. 64, a. 1793 ; 2019, c. 28, a. 67 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1793 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1783. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 608 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 25 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. 2. Loi visant principalement l’encadrement des inspections en bâtiment et de la copropriété divise, le remplacement de la dénomination de la Régie du logement et l’amélioration de ses règles de fonctionnement et modifiant la Loi sur la Société d’habitation du Québec et diverses dispositions législatives concernant le domaine municipal, LQ 2019, c. 28, a. 67 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 16 ,  1re sess, 42e lég, Québec, 2019, a. 61. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Commission de l'aménagement du territoire, vol. 45, no 27 (22 août 2019),  p. 33-40 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : aucun vote Commission de l'aménagement du territoire, vol. 45, no 28 (29 août 2019),  p. 1-4 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1793 (LQ 1991, c. 64) La vente d'un immeuble à usage d'habitation qui n'est pas précédée du contrat préliminaire peut être annulée à la demande de l'acheteur, si celui-ci démontre qu'il en subit un préjudice sérieux. Article 1793 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The sale of a residential immovable that is not preceded by the preliminary contract may be annulled on the application of the buyer if he shows that he suffers serious prejudice therefrom. Sources Loi sur la protection du consommateur, L.R.Q., chap. P-40.1 : article 271 Commentaires Cet article est nouveau. Il sanctionne de nullité relative toute vente d'immeuble à usage d'habitation qui n'est pas précédée du contrat préliminaire, lequel doit comprendre dans certains cas la note d'information, si l'acheteur démontre qu'il en subit un préjudice sérieux. Ce principe s'applique indépendamment du nombre de logements que peut comprendre l'immeuble. Cet article s'inspire de l'article 271 de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur , lequel permet au consommateur de demander la nullité du contrat lorsque certaines règles de formation n'ont pas été respectées ou lorsque le contrat ne respecte pas une exigence de forme. Il s'en distingue toutefois puisque l'article 271 n'exige pas que le consommateur prouve que le défaut lui cause un préjudice. De la même manière, il déroge à la règle générale énoncée aux articles 1414, 1416 et 1420; ces articles sanctionnent par la nullité du contrat le non-respect d'une forme particulière exigée comme condition nécessaire à la formation du contrat, sans égard au fait que la personne en faveur de qui la nullité est établie ait subi ou non un préjudice en raison du manquement. Toutefois, dans le contexte de la vente d'immeuble à usage d'habitation, il a paru souhaitable de limiter le recours en nullité aux seuls cas où l'acheteur prouve qu'il subit un préjudice sérieux en raison du défaut du vendeur de ne pas lui avoir remis les documents prescrits. Si ceux-ci sont essentiels pour que l'acheteur reçoive toute l'information nécessaire pour prendre une décision éclairée et pour qu'il puisse bénéficier d'un délai de réflexion, l'absence de préjudice sérieux rendrait la sanction de nullité disproportionnée eu égard à la gravité du manquement. Si le contrat préliminaire et la note d'information étaient remis au futur acheteur, mais contenaient des informations fausses, trompeuses ou incomplètes sur un élément substantiel, ou encore si le vendeur passait sous silence un fait important, l'acheteur bénéficierait des recours ordinaires prévus au livre Des obligations , en cas d'erreur ou de dol, en vertu des articles 1400, 1401, 1407 et 2927. Par ailleurs, depuis juin 1988, certaines pratiques de commerce prévues par la Loi sur la protection du consommateur sont interdites en matière de construction et de vente d'immeuble (art. 6.1, 215 et suivants). Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1793\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.028258707374334335, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1793\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1793: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1793\", \"@search.score\": 0.028258707374334335, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 2, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1793\"}, \"token_count\": 1380, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1793\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Loi sur la régie du logement, RLRQ c R-8.1 (QUÉBEC) - Article 54.11\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 54.11 . L’acheteur d’une fraction dans un immeuble locatif converti en copropriété divise peut, dans les trois ans de la signature du contrat de vente, réclamer du vendeur la réduction de ses obligations si le rapport d’expert, la circulaire d’information ou le contrat de vente contiennent des informations fausses, trompeuses ou incomplètes sur un élément substantiel, ou si le vendeur n’a pas remis à l’acheteur le rapport d’expert ou la circulaire d’information. Le tribunal rejette la demande si le vendeur démontre que l’acheteur n’en a subi aucun préjudice. 1987, c. 77, a. 2 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.0317540317773819, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"title\": \"Loi sur la régie du logement, RLRQ c R-8.1 (QUÉBEC) - Article 54.11\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"@search.score\": 0.0317540317773819, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 2, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\"}, \"token_count\": 152, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-t-15.01-fr\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-t-15.01-fr\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Loi sur le Tribunal administratif du logement, RLRQ c T-15.01 - Article 54.11\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 54.11 . L’acheteur d’une fraction dans un immeuble locatif converti en copropriété divise peut, dans les trois ans de la signature du contrat de vente, réclamer du vendeur la réduction de ses obligations si le rapport d’expert, la circulaire d’information ou le contrat de vente contiennent des informations fausses, trompeuses ou incomplètes sur un élément substantiel, ou si le vendeur n’a pas remis à l’acheteur le rapport d’expert ou la circulaire d’information. Le tribunal rejette la demande si le vendeur démontre que l’acheteur n’en a subi aucun préjudice. 1987, c. 77, a. 2 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-t-15.01-fr\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03102453239262104, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-t-15.01-fr\", \"title\": \"Loi sur le Tribunal administratif du logement, RLRQ c T-15.01 - Article 54.11\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-t-15.01-fr\", \"@search.score\": 0.03102453239262104, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 2, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent la chose impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquise, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, si ces vices avaient été connus. L’acheteur conserve ses recours lorsque le défaut se manifeste dans un délai raisonnable après la découverte. Le vendeur répond aussi, selon les circonstances, des qualités promises ou de celles auxquelles l’acheteur peut légitimement s’attendre.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-t-15.01-fr\"}, \"token_count\": 152, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-t-15.01-fr\"}], \"jurisprudence\": [{\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"--- BEGIN PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 0\\n2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) | ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. | CanLII Date: 2007-11-22 File number: 31176; 31177; 31174 Other citations: 287 DLR (4th) 385 — 369 NR 152 — 162 ACWS (3d) 1050 — AZ-50459657 — 36 BLR (4th) 1 — EYB 2007-126361 — JE 2007-2243 — [2007] SCJ No 50 (QL) — [2007] ACS no 50 Citation: ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII), [2007] 3 SCR 461 SUPREME COURT OF CANADA Citation: ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461, 2007 SCC 50 Date: 20071122 Docket: 31176, 31177, 31174 Between: ABB Inc. and Alstom Canada Inc. Appellants and Domtar Inc. Respondent and between: Chubb Insurance Company of Canada Appellant and Domtar Inc. Respondent and between: Domtar Inc. Appellant and Arkwright Mutual Insurance Company Respondent Official English Translation Coram: McLachlin C.J. and Bastarache, Binnie, LeBel, Deschamps, Fish, Abella, Charron and Rothstein JJ. Joint Reasons for Judgment: (paras. 1 to 121) LeBel and Deschamps JJ. (McLachlin C.J. and Bastarache, Binnie, Fish, Abella, Charron and Rothstein JJ. concurring) ______________________________ ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461, 2007 SCC 50 ABB Inc. and Alstom Canada Inc. Appellants v. Domtar Inc. Respondent ‑ and ‑ Chubb Insurance Company of Canada Appellant v. Domtar Inc. Respondent ‑ and ‑ Domtar Inc. Appellant v. Arkwright Mutual Insurance Company Respondent Indexed as:  ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. Neutral citation:  2007 SCC 50. File Nos.:  31176, 31177, 31174. 2006: November 8; 2007: November 22. Present:  McLachlin C.J. and Bastarache, Binnie, LeBel, Deschamps, Fish, Abella, Charron and Rothstein JJ. on appeal from the court of appeal for quebec Sale — Latent defect — Limitation of liability — Damages — Purchase of recovery boiler by paper manufacturer — Leaks and cracks in boiler’s superheater caused by tie welds connecting its tubes together — Superheater repaired but subsequently replaced by paper manufacturer with product supplied by another boiler manufacturer — Action in damages for latent defects and for breach of duty to inform buyer — Whether conditions for existence of latent defect are met — Whether clause limiting seller’s liability is applicable where seller knew or is presumed to have known about latent defect — Whether seller breached its duty to inform or buyer its duty to inquire — Whether buyer is entitled to be reimbursed for losses sustained with respect to repairs and to recover costs incurred to replace superheater — Whether insurer was subrogated to buyer’s rights in respect of payment made to buyer, and whether damages awarded to buyer should be reduced proportionally to that payment — Civil Code of Lower Canada, art. 1527. Sale — Duty to inform — Latent defect — Inclusion of seller’s duty to inform in warranty against latent defects. Domtar built a new pulp and paper mill in Windsor, Quebec.  In December 1984, it purchased a recovery boiler manufactured by C.E. (now ABB and Alstom) for $13,500,000.  The contract of sale included a clause limiting the seller’s liability.  The boiler’s design included a superheater with “H‑style” tie welds even though, at the time, C.E. was aware of problems resulting from the use of such welds and knew about the technology of hinge‑pin attachments.  In March 1989, 18 months after the boiler was put into service, tests by Domtar revealed some leaks and hundreds of cracks in the superheater’s tubes.  C.E. replaced a number of H‑style tie welds with hinge‑pin attachments, but there was disagreement as to a permanent solution to the problem.  In October 1989, at Domtar’s request, a competitor of C.E. replaced the three banks of the superheater with elements installed using hinge‑pin attachments.  Domtar brought an action in damages against C.E. that was based on the warranty against latent defects and the duty to inform.  It also sued a number of insurers, including Chubb and Arkwright, the former on the basis of a performance bond granted to C.E. for latent defects, and the latter under an “all risk” insurance policy held by Domtar.  However, Domtar discontinued its action against the insurer Lloyd’s pursuant to an agreement that included the payment of $1,578,900. The Superior Court rejected the latent defect claim, but found that C.E. had not discharged its duty to inform as regards the risks associated with tie welds.  It condemned C.E. to pay $13,366,583 in damages, less the $1,578,900 paid by Lloyd’s, on the basis that Lloyd’s was, as a result of that payment, subrogated to Domtar’s rights.  Given that there was no latent defect, the court dismissed the action against Chubb; it also dismissed the claim against Arkwright on the basis that the cost of replacing the superheater was not insured.  The Court of Appeal ruled that C.E. was liable on the basis of both the legal warranty against latent defects and the duty to inform.  It held that the amount paid by Lloyd’s should not be deducted.  It condemned the insurer Chubb, solidarily with C.E., to pay Domtar the amount provided for in the performance bond.  It affirmed the decision to dismiss Domtar’s action against Arkwright. Held :  The appeals should be dismissed. Given that all the facts alleged in support of Domtar’s action occurred before 1994, and in light of ss. 83 and 85 of the Act respecting the implementation of the reform of the Civil Code , the issues relating to the warranty against latent defects must be resolved by applying the Civil Code of Lower Canada (“C.C.L.C.”).  [30] In intervening, the Court of Appeal did not reassess the evidence in the record, but relied on the trial judge’s findings of fact to arrive at a different conclusion of law regarding the nature of the defect.  That conclusion is therefore not inconsistent with the principle of deference for a trial judge’s assessment of the facts.  Rather, the issue here is one of legal characterization.  [37] Article 1527 C.C.L.C. provides that any seller who knows or is legally presumed to know about the defects of the thing is obliged to pay for all damage suffered by the buyer.  In Quebec civil law, manufacturers are considered to be the ultimate experts with respect to goods because they have control over the labour and materials used to produce them.  Consequently, they are subject to the strongest presumption of knowledge and to the most exacting obligation to disclose latent defects.  The buyer’s expertise is also relevant as it serves to assess whether the defect is latent or apparent.  The more knowledge a buyer has of a good being purchased, the more likely it is that a defect in that good will be considered apparent.  Buyers therefore have an obligation to inform themselves by carrying out a reasonable inspection of the good.  The test is whether a reasonable buyer in the same circumstances could have detected the defect at the time of the sale.  However, the buyer’s expertise does not nullify the presumption applicable to the manufacturer.  The manufacturer will be unable to rely on a limitation of liability clause unless it can rebut the presumption of knowledge of the defect. [39] [41‑42] [44] Regardless of whether the defect is a material defect, a functional defect or a conventional defect, it must have four characteristics, all of which are essential to the warranty:  it must be latent, must be sufficiently serious, must have existed at the time of the sale and must have been unknown to the buyer.  All these conditions are met in the case at bar.  The trial judge erred in concluding that the cracking of the tubes was a design feature rather than a design defect.  First, he limited the definition of a defect to a problem preventing the good from being used at all and, second, he confused the sale of a lower‑performance version of a good with the sale of a defective one.  A defect will be considered to be serious if it renders the good unfit for its intended use or so diminishes its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it at the price paid.  Domtar would not have bought a boiler with H‑style tie welds if it had been informed of the risks associated with this type of attachment.  C.E. used these attachments without having independent analyses conducted to determine whether they could withstand the stress placed on the tubes by the circulation of steam at high temperatures.  It chose to rely on its customers’ assessments and delayed the adoption of hinge‑pin attachments in order to maintain its competitive position.  Domtar was unaware of the defect at the time of the sale because C.E. had not shared the information it possessed on this subject with Domtar.  Even though Domtar was described as a sophisticated operator, and however expert it was at using boilers, it cannot be characterized as a professional “of identical expertise” to C.E.  Nor is the defect apparent simply because Domtar was assisted by an expert.  The cause of the excessive cracking was unknown to both Domtar and its expert. [45] [50] [86‑88] [91] [93] [97] [99] [101] To rebut the presumption of knowledge of the defect provided for in art. 1527 C.C.L.C., it is never open to a manufacturer to rely on its ignorance of the defect as its sole defence.  The manufacturer must show that it did not know about the defect and that its lack of knowledge was justified, that is, that it could not have discovered the defect even if it had taken every precaution that the buyer would be entitled to expect a reasonable seller to take in the same circumstances. To absolve a manufacturer from liability will be justified only if the manufacturer shows that it had full knowledge of the technology in its field at the time the good was designed and that the defect in question cannot be attributed to it.  In light of the strength of the presumption of knowledge applicable to it, the manufacturer must meet a high standard of diligence, and the range of defences available to it to rebut the presumption is very narrow.  C.E.’s argument is based not on fault on the part of Domtar or a third party, or on superior force or development risk, but on its own good faith throughout its business relations with Domtar.  In this case, it can be seen from the evidence that C.E. had known about the problems associated with tie welds since the early 1980s and that a better technology was available to it as of that time.  [69‑72] [102] [104] The duty to inform and the warranty against latent defects are two concepts that overlap, but the former derives from the general principle of good faith, whereas the latter is expressly provided for in the C.C.L.C. and the Civil Code of Québec .   Furthermore, the scope of the general duty to inform is much broader than that of the disclosure of a latent defect.  The duty to inform is subsumed in the analysis of the seller’s liability for latent defects, and there is no need for the court to conduct a separate analysis on the duty.  [107‑109] Because a clause in the insurance contract between Lloyd’s and Domtar expressly excluded damage resulting from a breakdown of or defect in the recovery boiler, the payment made by Lloyd’s could not have been related to the damages being claimed by Domtar.  Lloyd’s was not, therefore, subrogated to Domtar’s rights and the damages claimed by Domtar should not be reduced by the amount of the payment. [113] Chubb agreed to guarantee the performance of C.E.’s obligations under the contract of sale for the recovery boiler. Since there was a latent defect in the superheater, Chubb is solidarily liable for the amount of its guarantee. [114] The “all risk” insurance policy issued by Arkwright for Domtar’s property contained a clause excluding damage resulting from latent defects.  To accept Domtar’s contention that the welds can be dissociated from the superheater would render the exclusion clause meaningless. [115] [119] Cases Cited Applied: Desgagné v. Fabrique de St‑Philippe d’Arvida , 1984 CanLII 129 (SCC) , [1984] 1 S.C.R. 19; Placement Jacpar Inc. v. Benzakour , 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , [1989] R.J.Q. 2309; Samson & Filion v. Davie Shipbuilding & Repairing Co. , 1924 CanLII 57 (SCC) , [1925] S.C.R. 202; Touchette v. Pizzagalli , 1938 CanLII 39 (SCC) , [1938] S.C.R. 433; Manac inc./Nortex v. Boiler Inspection and Insurance Co. of Canada , 2006 QCCA 1395 (CanLII) , [2006] R.R.A. 879; referred to: Marquis v. Saltsman , 2002 CanLII 41228 (QC CA) , J.E. 2002‑1729, SOQUIJ AZ‑50143509; Rousseau v. 2732‑1678 Québec inc. , [1999] R.D.I. 565 ; Société en commandite A.C. enr. v. Wadieh , 1997 CanLII 9977 (QC CA) , [1997] R.D.I. 345; Bertrand v. Pelletier , [1997] R.D.I. 321 ; Poirier v. Martucelli , [1995] R.D.I. 319 ; Trottier v. Robitaille , [1994] R.D.I. 537 ; Cloutier v. Létourneau , 1992 CanLII 3716 (QC CA) , [1993] R.L. 530; Rousseau v. Gagnon , 1986 CanLII 3706 (QC CA) , [1987] R.J.Q. 40; Auto Peliss ltée v. Proulx Pontiac Buick ltée , 2001 CanLII 5911 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 856; Garage Robert inc. v. 2426‑9888 Québec inc. , 2001 CanLII 9967 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 865; Trois Diamants Autos (1987) ltée v. M.G.B. Auto inc. , 2001 CanLII 39923 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 860; General Motors Products of Canada v. Kravitz , 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , [1979] 1 S.C.R. 790; Oakwood Construction Inc. v. Ratthé , [1993] R.D.I. 181 ; Blandino v. Colagiacomo , 1988 CanLII 1193 (QC CA) , [1989] R.D.I. 148; Oppenheim v. Forestiers R.P.G.M. inc. , J.E. 2002‑1197, SOQUIJ AZ‑50133145 ; London & Lancashire Guarantee & Accident Co. of Canada v. Cie F.X. Drolet , 1944 CanLII 46 (SCC) , [1944] S.C.R. 82; Civ. 1 re , November 24, 1954, J.C.P. 1955.II.8565, obs. H. B.; Civ. 1 re , January 19, 1965, D. 1965.389, obs. Cornu; Com., November 6, 1978, J.C.P. 1979.II.19178, obs. Ghestin; Com., October 8, 1973, J.C.P. 1975.II.17927, obs. Ghestin; Civ. 3 e , October 30, 1978, J.C.P. 1979.II.19178; Tony’s Broadloom & Floor Covering Ltd. v. NMC Canada Inc. (1995), 1995 CanLII 7153 (ON SC) , 22 O.R. (3d) 244, aff’d (1996), 1996 CanLII 680 (ON CA) , 31 O.R. (3d) 481; Jenkins v. Foley (2002), 215 Nfld. & P.E.I.R. 257 , 2002 NFCA 46; Parlby Construction Ltd. v. Stewart Equipment Co. , 1971 CanLII 1426 (BC SC) , [1972] 1 W.W.R. 503; Guarantee Co. of North America v. Gordon Capital Corp. , 1999 CanLII 664 (SCC) , [1999] 3 S.C.R. 423; Hunter Engineering Co. v. Syncrude Canada Ltd. , 1989 CanLII 129 (SCC) , [1989] 1 S.C.R. 426; R. G. McLean Ltd. v. Canadian Vickers Ltd. , 1970 CanLII 386 (ON CA) , [1971] 1 O.R. 207; Bank of\\n\\n\\n--- END PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 3\\n... will be considered apparent.  An apparent defect is one that the buyer either detected at the time of the sale or could have detected given his or her knowledge (art. 1523 C.C.L.C. and art. 1726, para. 2 C.C.Q.).  Buyers therefore have an obligation to inform themselves by carrying out a reasonable inspection of the good.  In all cases, the test is whether a reasonable buyer in the same circumstances could have detected the defect at the time of the sale. 43 We will conclude the discussion in this section with a few comments on the specific situation raised by the parties in the case at bar, that is, where the seller and the buyer are characterized as [ translation ] “professionals of identical expertise”, in which case they would be able to contract out of the warranty against latent defects.  This argument is based on three judgments of the Quebec Court of Appeal: Auto Peliss ltée v. Proulx Pontiac Buick ltée , 2001 CanLII 5911 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 856; Garage Robert inc. v. 2426‑9888 Québec inc. , 2001 CanLII 9967 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 865; Trois Diamants Autos (1987) ltée v. M.G.B. Auto inc. , 2001 CanLII 39923 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 860. 44 The position taken by the Court of Appeal in this trilogy of cases does not support the appellants’ argument.  The court’s decisions did not relate to the situation of a manufacturer.  Nor did the court recognize a presumption of knowledge on the buyer’s part that would be incompatible with the warranty against latent defects.  Furthermore, the position taken by this Court on the manufacturer’s warranty in General Motors Products of Canada v. Kravitz , 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , [1979] 1 S.C.R. 790, is clear.  In that case, at pp. 798‑99, the Court confirmed that, for the purposes of art. 1527 C.C.L.C., a manufacturer and a professional seller are always presumed to be in bad faith and that the fraudulence associated with the manufacturer’s actual or presumed knowledge of a defect is unaffected by the professional status of a buyer who is a dealer.  The implication of this line of reasoning is that the buyer’s expertise does not nullify the presumption applicable to the manufacturer. Even if a professional buyer is personally in the business of selling a manufacturer’s automobiles, he or she cannot be considered to have the same expertise as the manufacturer.  Professional buyers, too, are protected by the warranty against latent defects.  If a defect is latent, the manufacturer will be unable to rely on a limitation of liability clause unless it can rebut the presumption of knowledge of the defect. 45 At any rate, in the case at bar, C.E. is a manufacturer of recovery boilers, and Domtar bought one of C.E.’s boilers for use in the production of pulp and paper.  However expert Domtar may be at using boilers, it cannot be characterized as a professional “of identical expertise” to C.E.  As a manufacturer, C.E. had greater expertise than Domtar as regards the characteristics of the recovery boiler in question.  The warranty against latent defects is applicable in this case. 3.6 Manufacturer’s Liability for Defects in the Item Sold 46 When enforcing a legal warranty, a court must first determine whether there was a latent defect in the good sold.  At this stage, the analysis relates essentially to the good and to the buyer’s conduct.  Second, the court will determine whether the seller is liable; this part of the analysis consists in establishing whether the seller knew or is legally presumed to have known about the alleged defect.  This determination will enable the court to decide whether a clause limiting the seller’s liability can be set up against the buyer.  It is therefore important to distinguish the conditions of the seller’s liability for a latent defect from the scope of his or her liability. 3.6.1 Conditions of Liability: Existence of a Latent Defect 3.6.1.1 Different Types of Latent Defects 47 The legislature has not expressly defined what constitutes a “defect”. Article 1522 C.C.L.C. does, however, contain some useful information.  For example, the first criterion for determining whether a latent defect exists is the loss of use it causes.  The purpose of the warranty against latent defects is thus to ensure that the buyer of a good will be able to make practical and economical use of it. 48 There are three main types of latent defects: the material defect, which relates to a specific good; the functional defect, which relates to the good’s design; and the conventional defect, which arises where the buyer has disclosed that the good is to be put to a particular use.  Material and functional defects are assessed in light of the normal use to which buyers put the good, whereas a conventional defect is assessed in light of the particular use indicated by the buyer to the seller.  However, it is necessary, in discussing this classification, to briefly consider the problem of technological change. 49 Technological change is a modern‑day reality that is characterized by the rapid pace at which improvements are made to products.  The trial judge rightly noted that manufacturers are constantly redesigning their products: 2003 CanLII 52306 (QC CS) , [2003] R.J.Q. 2194, at para. 161 .  He was wary, and rightly so, of a tendency to condemn a manufacturer simply because a different version of the original product has since emerged on the market.  Selling an improved or better performing version of a product does not render the previous version defective.  Differences in quality and possible use between these two versions of the product cannot be characterized as a latent defect.  The key factor in the analysis resides in the loss of use, as assessed in light of the buyer’s reasonable expectations. 50 The categories of defects can sometimes overlap.  In the case at bar, Domtar complains that the tie welds, which were integral to the superheater, compromised the normal operation of the boiler by causing cracks and unforeseeable shutdowns.  According to Domtar, the argument that it should not have to accept untimely shutdowns flows from the very nature of the equipment purchased and from the fact that this equipment operates continuously.  In this sense, the defect of which Domtar complains is both functional and conventional. However, regardless of how the defect is characterized, it must have four characteristics, all of which are essential to the warranty:  it must be latent, must be sufficiently serious, must have existed at the time of the sale and must have been unknown to the buyer. 3.6.1.2 Latency of the Defect 51 The latency of the defect is assessed objectively, that is, by reviewing the buyer’s examination of the good in light of what a prudent and diligent buyer of identical expertise would have done:  P.‑G. Jobin, “Précis on sale”, in Reform of the Civil Code (1993), vol. 3A, at p. 61; M. Pourcelet, La vente (5th ed. 1987), at p. 149. In other words, the issue is not limited to ignorance of the defect; it must also be determined whether a reasonable buyer in the same circumstances would have realized that there was a defect. 3.6.1.3 Seriousness of the Defect 52 Loss of use does not in itself suffice to support the conclusion that a latent defect exists.  The loss must also be serious, that is, it must render the good unfit for its intended use or must so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have purchased it at the price paid.  This second criterion, the seriousness of the defect, flows from the words of art. 1522 C.C.L.C. However, the defect does not have to render the good completely unusable but simply has to reduce its usefulness significantly in relation to the legitimate expectations of a prudent and diligent buyer. 3.6.1.4 Existence of the Defect at the Time of the Sale 53 The defect must also have existed at the time of the sale.  This third criterion was developed by the courts and commentators before being codified in art. 1726, para. 1 C.C.Q.: Commentaires du ministre de la Justice (1993), vol. I, at p. 1078. What this basically means is that a seller cannot be held liable for a defect caused by abnormal use of the good by the buyer. 3.6.1.5 Defect Unknown to the Buyer 54 It is not enough for the defect to be latent.  It must also be unknown to the buyer, as is required by the concluding words of art. 1522 in fine C.C.L.C.  This criterion is assessed subjectively.  Unlike the seller, the buyer is not subject to a presumption of knowledge, since the buyer is always presumed to be in good faith.  As a result, the burden of proving actual knowledge of the defect always rests on the seller:  Jobin, at p. 60; Pourcelet, at p. 149; T. Rousseau‑Houle, Précis du droit de la vente et du louage (2nd ed. 1986), at p. 134. 55 The seller’s liability is not the same in every latent defect case.  It will accordingly be necessary to review the conditions that determine the scope and applicability of the seller’s liability. 3.6.2 Scope of the Seller’s Liability 3.6.2.1 Presumption of Knowledge of Latent Defects on the Seller’s Part 56 According to art. 1524 C.C.L.C., when a buyer has shown that a latent defect exists, the seller will be held liable “unless it is stipulated that he shall not be obliged to any warranty”.  However, even if the contract contains a limitation of liability clause, the seller will not always be able to invoke it.  Presumed or actual knowledge of the defect can, in certain circumstances, bar the seller from relying on such a clause.  Since actual or presumed knowledge of a defect on the seller’s part is an indication of bad faith, the seller, in such a case, has not only to repay the sale price, but also to compensate the buyer for any damage caused by the latent defect.  A limitation of liability clause may not be set up against the buyer if the seller knew or is legally presumed to have known about the defect:  J. Deslauriers, “La vente 1”, in Cours de la formation professionnelle du Barreau du Québec 1988‑1989, vol. 3, 51, at p. 82; Pourcelet, at p. 160; Rousseau‑Houle, at pp. 135 and 156.  The presumption of knowledge is thus determinative not only of the issue of whether a seller may limit the warranty against latent defects, but above all of that of the scope of the seller’s liability. 57 Although the warranty against latent defects originated in Ancient Rome, the presumption of knowledge made its first appearance only in old French law, in particular in the works of DuMoulin, Domat and Pothier:  Edwards, at p. 275.  Before then, a seller had to have actual knowledge of a defect to be liable for it since all sellers were presumed to be in good faith.  The new presumption came into being as a result of the work of certain authors, who were of the opinion that manufacturers and specialized sellers, by reason of their occupations, should know about any defects in their goods.  According to Pothier, manufacturers should be considered to be masters of their craft and specialized sellers should be presumed to have a detailed knowledge of the products they sell, and both should accordingly be deprived of the protection afforded sellers in good faith: [ translation ] There is one case in which the seller, despite having no knowledge whatsoever of the defect in the thing sold, is nevertheless obliged to make reparation for the wrong this defect has caused to the buyer in respect of the buyer’s other property:  where the seller is an artisan, or a merchant who sells works of his craft, or of the trade that is his profession. Even if this artisan or merchant should claim not to know about the defect, he is obligated to make reparation for any damage the buyer has sustained because of the defect in using the thing for its intended purpose. . . . The reason is that an artisan, by profession of his craft, spondet peritiam artis .  He becomes liable to all who contract with him for the quality of his works, when they are put to the use for which they are by nature intended. His incompetence or lack of knowledge in any aspect of his craft is a fault on his part, since no one should publicly profess a craft if he does not possess all the knowledge needed to exercise it properly: Imperitia culpae annumeratur ; l. 132, ff. Reg. J .  The same is true of the merchant, whether or not he is also the manufacturer.  By reason of his public profession of his trade, he becomes liable for the quality of the wares he sells, when they are put to their intended use.  If he is a manufacturer, he must employ, to make his wares, only good artisans, and he is answerable for this.  If he is not a manufacturer, he must display only good wares for sale; he must have expert knowledge of his wares and sell only good ones.  [Emphasis added.] ( Œuvres de Pothier (new ed. 1823), vol. II, No. 214) 58 This passage, sometimes referred to as [ translation ] “Pothier’s rule”, was regarded as the main source of art. 1527, para. 2 C.C.L.C.: Edwards, at pp. 280‑81; Traité de droit civil du Québec , vol. 11, by L. Faribault, 1961, at p. 295; P.-B. Mignault, Le droit civil canadien (1906), vol. VII, 1906, at p. 112.  Inspired by the principles enunciated by Pothier, the commissioners who drafted the C.C.L.C. introduced presumed knowledge into Quebec civil law:  Edwards, at p. 276.  This attributed knowledge is provided for in arts. 1527 and 1528 C.C.L.C.  In principle, sellers are presumed to be in good faith (art. 2202 C.C.L.C.), but they will be held liable if they had actual knowledge of defects (art. 1527, para. 1 C.C.L.C.). Moreover, art. 1527, para. 2 C.C.L.C. relieves the buyer of the burden of proving bad faith on the seller’s part if the seller is legally presumed to have known about the defects.  What remains is to identify the sellers to whom this presumption applies. 3.6.2.2 Sellers to Whom the Presumption of Knowledge Applies 59 The authors agree that the presumption of knowledge under the C.C.L.C. applies to manufacturers and to specialized professional sellers.  Thus, they would not apply it to non‑specialized professional sellers or non‑professional sellers, since such sellers lack technical expertise or commercial experience with respect to the good, which makes it impossible for them to personally verify the quality of the good:  Pourcelet, at p. 158; Rousseau‑Houle, at pp. 139‑52; F. Langelier, Cours de droit civil de la province de Québec , vol. V, 1909, at p. 77; Mignault, at p. 118. 60 It should be noted, however, that since the enactment of art. 1733 C.C.Q., the presumption of knowledge has applied to all professional sellers without distinction.  Only non‑professional sellers are now exempt from the presumption. 3.6.2.3 Rebuttable Nature of the Presumption of Knowledge 61 The nature of the presumption must now be examined to determine its effect, that is, whether it can ultimately be rebutted and, if so, under what conditions.  This Court has considered this question in a number of judgments. Samson & Filion v. Davie Shipbuilding & Repairing Co. , 1924 CanLII 57 (SCC) , [1925] S.C.R. 202, was one of the first leading decisions in which the presumption of knowledge on the seller’s part was addressed.  That case concerned the sale of used pipes.  The contract of sale did not contain a limitation of liability clause.  This Court held that the presumption of knowledge does not apply to a non‑professional seller with no expertise.  Such a seller is not liable for damage caused by the good unless he or she had actual knowledge of the defect.  Manufacturers and professional sellers ([ translation] “who [sell] works of [their] craft, or of the trade that is [their] profession”), on the other hand, are subject to a presumption of knowledge, which is rebuttable. 62 A few years later, this Court rendered its decision in Touchette v. Pizzagalli , 1938 CanLII 39 (SCC) , [1938] S.C.R. 433, which concerned the sale of a new car by a professional seller to a non‑professional buyer without the manufacturer’s involvement.  Under the contract, the warranty was limited to the one provided by the manufacturer.  The Court noted that professional sellers are presumed to know about any defects in the goods they sell, although it implicitly acknowledged that a manufacturer or a specialized professional seller can rebut the presumption of knowledge: It is now settled that the seller is responsible in respect of all damages sustained by the purchaser by reason of latent defect where the...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 4\\n... .C.R. 433, which concerned the sale of a new car by a professional seller to a non‑professional buyer without the manufacturer’s involvement.  Under the contract, the warranty was limited to the one provided by the manufacturer.  The Court noted that professional sellers are presumed to know about any defects in the goods they sell, although it implicitly acknowledged that a manufacturer or a specialized professional seller can rebut the presumption of knowledge: It is now settled that the seller is responsible in respect of all damages sustained by the purchaser by reason of latent defect where the seller is either a manufacturer or a person who deals in, as merchant, articles of the same kind as that which was the subject of the sale. Unless he can establish that the defect was such that it could not have been discovered by the most competent and diligent person in his position , his ignorance is no excuse, because it is conclusively presumed (in the absence of such proof) to be the result of negligence or of incompetence in the calling which he publicly practises and in respect of which he thereby professes himself to be competent.  The principle is spondet peritiam artis . [Emphasis added; p. 439 ( per Duff C.J.).] 63 As mentioned above, this Court noted in Kravitz that, in the context of the warranty against latent defects, the manufacturer and professional seller of a defective good are presumed to be in bad faith (p. 798).  In that case, the Court went even further, however, refusing to allow the manufacturer to transfer its liability to the dealer despite the dealer’s professional knowledge (at p. 798): What is the situation when, as in the case at bar, the manufacturer has sold a new thing to a dealer who is himself a professional seller?  If the latter is presumed to be aware of the defects when he resells the thing, does it not follow that he is also presumed to be aware of them when he buys it from the manufacturer?  While this reasoning could have some appeal in certain circumstances, it cannot serve to exempt the manufacturer from his liability for latent defects in the thing he has manufactured when he sells it to a dealer who is responsible for reselling it. The manufacturer of a defective thing must assume the ultimate responsibility for his incompetence, actual or presumed. The bad faith of the professional seller toward the non‑professional buyer does not convert the “dol” of the manufacturer toward his dealer into an act of good faith. [Emphasis added.] 64 The Court then analysed the sub‑purchaser’s action against the dealer and noted that in France , limitation of liability clauses do not relieve manufacturers of liability since the presumption of knowledge applicable to them is not rebuttable (pp. 799‑801).  The Court did not rule on the nature of the presumption of knowledge applicable to manufacturers (p. 802).  It simply concluded that neither the limitation of liability clause included by the dealer in the contract of sale nor the manufacturer’s limited warranty could be a bar to the sub‑purchaser’s extra‑contractual remedy against the manufacturer based on the warranty against latent defects. 65 Some authors have cited the principles enunciated by Pothier or the reference in Kravitz to French law as a basis for stating that the presumption of knowledge is irrebuttable, where manufacturers and specialized professional sellers are concerned, because of their duty to be masters of their craft.  But these authors concede that the presumption remains rebuttable for non‑specialized professional sellers who have no control over the manufacture of the product and no specific knowledge of the product:  Edwards, at pp. 289‑94; Pourcelet, at pp. 158‑59; Mignault, at p. 113. 66 However, the majority view in Quebec law is that the presumption of knowledge provided for in art. 1527 C.C.L.C. is rebuttable, even for manufacturers: Manac inc./Nortex v. Boiler Inspection and Insurance Co. of Canada , 2006 QCCA 1395 (CanLII) , [2006] R.R.A. 879 (C.A.); also Oakwood Construction Inc. v. Ratthé , [1993] R.D.I. 181 (C.A.), at p. 183 ; Blandino v. Colagiacomo , 1988 CanLII 1193 (QC CA) , [1989] R.D.I. 148 (C.A.), at pp. 151 and 153; Oppenheim v. Forestiers R.P.G.M. inc. , J.E. 2002‑1197 (C.A.), SOQUIJ AZ‑50133145, at para. 24 .  Several authors have observed that it is very difficult for a manufacturer to rebut this presumption by showing the court that it was impossible to know about the defect:  D.‑C. Lamontagne, Droit de la vente (1995), at pp. 103‑4; Jobin, at p. 62; Deslauriers, at p. 82; Faribault, at pp. 295‑96.  Indeed, it has been pointed out that there are no known cases in which a manufacturer has in fact succeeded in rebutting the presumption:  Rousseau‑Houle, at pp. 138‑52; Langelier, at p. 77. 67 The new wording of the C.C.Q.’s provisions, which codify the case law under the C.C.L.C., confirms the majority view.  In art. 1729 C.C.Q., the legislature kept the word “presumed” rather than using “deemed” ( réputé ). A presumption “concerning presumed facts is simple and may be rebutted by proof to the contrary” (art. 2847, para. 2 C.C.Q.).  When the first and second paragraphs of art. 1733 C.C.Q. are read together, it is apparent that the legislature has provided that a professional seller who “was aware or could not have been unaware” of a defect cannot exclude his or her liability. In our opinion, if the legislature took care to mention two situations in which professional sellers cannot exclude their liability, this was because it realized that situations arise in which they might raise as a defence that they were not or could not have been aware of the defect in the thing.  Otherwise, the legislature would simply have stated that professional sellers may not exclude their liability by contract. 3.6.2.4 Means of Rebutting the Presumption of Knowledge on the Manufacturer’s Part 68 It can be seen from the case law and the academic commentaries that a single standard governs the defences available to all sellers to rebut the presumption of knowledge applicable to them.  As this Court held in Samson & Filion , it must be determined whether a reasonable seller in the same circumstances would have been able to detect the defect at the time of the sale. 69 Contrary to C.E.’s position, it is never open to a manufacturer to rely on its ignorance of the defect as its sole defence: Samson & Filion , Touchette and Kravitz .  The  manufacturer may rebut the presumption only by showing that it did not know about the defect and that its lack of knowledge was justified , that is, that it could not have discovered the defect even if it had taken every precaution that the buyer would be entitled to expect a reasonable seller to take in the same circumstances. 70 The principle underlying this rule is that to absolve a manufacturer from liability will be justified if the manufacturer shows that it had full knowledge of the technology in its field at the time the good was designed and that the defect in question cannot be attributed to it.  This principle is consistent with the idea that gave rise to the presumption of knowledge, as set out by Pothier, according to which manufacturers must be masters of their craft. 71 Although the standard for a rebuttal of the presumption of knowledge will always be an objective one, the strength of the presumption will vary depending on the seller’s expertise.  A non‑specialized professional seller will be able to rebut the presumption more easily than a specialized professional seller.  Manufacturers will have difficulty rebutting it because they have special knowledge and because they are responsible for manufacturing the goods. 72 Even though the possibility of manufacturers rebutting the presumption is accepted, the high standard of diligence they are required to meet means that the range of defences available to them remains very narrow.  Only two defences have been recognized so far, and C.E. has invoked neither of them. Under the first of these defences, a manufacturer can rebut the presumption by proving causal fault on the part of the buyer or a third person, or superior force: Manac inc./Nortex , at para. 138 ; Commentaires du ministre de la Justice , vol. 1, at p. 902.  The second defence is that of development risk, but it is still subject to debate in contract matters.  This defence enables the manufacturer to avoid liability if it would have been impossible to detect the defect given the state of scientific and technical knowledge at the time the good was put on the market.  In such a case, only scientific or technological discoveries made after the good was put on the market will have permitted the defect to be detected.  This defence originated in London & Lancashire Guarantee & Accident Co. of Canada v. Cie F.X. Drolet , 1944 CanLII 46 (SCC) , [1944] S.C.R. 82, and is now partially codified in respect of extra‑contractual matters in art. 1473 C.C.Q. 3.6.2.5 Application of the Limitation of Liability Clause 73 The rules discussed above mean that a professional seller who was aware of a defect or who has not rebutted the presumption of knowledge cannot avoid liability.  Such a seller cannot rely on a limitation of liability clause, but is obliged to reimburse the buyer for the sale price and provide compensation for any damage resulting from the latent defect (art. 1527 C.C.L.C.). 3.6.3 Comparative Aspects 74 Following this survey of the rules relating to latent defects in Quebec civil law, it may be of interest to consider the extent to which these rules resemble the rules of French law or those that are applied in the rest of Canada.  In fact, every provision of the C.C.L.C. respecting the warranty against latent defects is a restatement of the rules of the French Civil Code (“F.C.C.”).  It will therefore be interesting to see how the issues before the Court have been analysed in French law.  Moreover, a brief review of the common law rules will show that they differ significantly from the applicable rules of Quebec law and French law. 3.6.3.1 French Law 75 In French law, the conditions for enforcing the warranty against latent defects are essentially the same as in Quebec law: the defect must be serious, must have existed before ownership was transferred, and must be latent (arts. 1641 and 1642 F.C.C.). 76 For the determination as to whether a defect is latent, the professional buyer is subject to a rebuttable presumption of knowledge of the defect:  A. Bénabent, Droit civil:  Les contrats spéciaux civils et commerciaux (5th ed. 2001), No. 226; see also O. Barret, “Vente”, in Répertoire de droit civil (2nd ed. (loose‑leaf)), vol. X, Nos. 563 and 565, and J. Huet, Les principaux contrats spéciaux (2nd ed. 2001), at pp. 329‑30.  This presumption is stronger where the buyer is from the same field of expertise as the seller.  However,  professional buyers can rebut the presumption at any time by showing that it would have been impossible for them to detect the defect:  Barret, Nos. 569 and 571, and Bénabent, No. 226. 77 When the conditions for enforcing the warranty are met, the buyer has a redhibitory or an estimatory action against the seller (art. 1644 F.C.C.).  It is also open to the buyer to seek damages if the seller knew about the defect (art. 1645 F.C.C.) or is presumed to have known about it.  However, these actions may be subject to the application of limitation of liability clauses (art. 1643 F.C.C.). 78 A professional seller is subject to a presumption of knowledge of latent defects in the good sold (Civ. 1 re , November 24, 1954, J.C.P. 1955.II.8565, obs. H.B.; also Civ. 1 re , January 19, 1965, D. 1965.389, and obs. G. Cornu, Rev. trim. dr. civ. 1965.665).  This presumption is almost irrebuttable; the only exception is where it would have been impossible to detect the defect, and the parties are a professional seller and a professional buyer [ translation ] “from the same field of expertise”.  This exception has rarely been applied by the courts, which have interpreted it narrowly:  Com., November 6, 1978, J.C.P. 1979.II.19178, obs. J. Ghestin, and obs. G. Cornu, Rev. trim. dr. civ. 1979.392; also Huet, at pp. 348‑49, and P. le Tourneau, La responsabilité civile (3rd ed. 1982), at p. 375, No. 1183. 79 Developments in French law relating to the presumption of knowledge on the part of professional sellers have incorporated the rules governing the presumption into the legal framework applicable to limitation of liability clauses and have confirmed that the presumption is almost irrebuttable.  As a result, the analysis of the presumption of knowledge on the part of professional sellers also applies to the review of limitation of liability clauses.  This means that such clauses are valid in principle (art. 1645 F.C.C.), but that they are inapplicable in cases involving professional sellers.  On an exceptional basis, a professional seller will be able to invoke a limitation of liability clause if the buyer is a professional from the same area of expertise and if it would not have been impossible to detect the defect:  Com., October 8, 1973, J.C.P. 1975.II.17927, obs. J. Ghestin; Civ. 3 e , October 30, 1978, J.C.P. 1979.II.19178, obs. J. Ghestin, and obs. G. Cornu, Rev. trim. dr. civ. 1979.392; Com., November 6, 1978, J.C.P. 1979.II.19178, obs. J. Ghestin, and obs. G. Cornu, Rev. trim. dr. civ. 1979.392. Thus, a professional seller will always be liable where it would have been impossible for the buyer to detect the defect, regardless of the buyer’s status. 3.6.3.2 Common Law 80 In Canada, the common law rule is that a latent defect must affect an essential characteristic of the good and make that good unfit for its intended use: Tony’s Broadloom & Floor Covering Ltd. v. NMC Canada Inc. (1995), 1995 CanLII 7153 (ON SC) , 22 O.R. (3d) 244 (Gen. Div.), aff’d (1996), 1996 CanLII 680 (ON CA) , 31 O.R. (3d) 481 (C.A.); Jenkins v. Foley (2002), 215 Nfld. & P.E.I.R. 257 , 2002 NFCA 46.  The onus is on the buyer to prove that the latent defect was known to the seller or that the seller showed reckless disregard for what he or she should have known.  However, where it has been established that the seller could have obtained information about an essential characteristic of the good, the seller cannot simply allege an honest belief: Parlby Construction Ltd. v. Stewart Equipment Co. , 1971 CanLII 1426 (BC SC) , [1972] 1 W.W.R. 503 (B.C.S.C.), at pp. 507 and 509‑10. 81 With some exceptions, provincial and territorial statutes generally allow sellers to limit the warranty against latent defects by contract. Contrary to French and Quebec law, the common law has no specific rule for the special case of professional sellers and buyers of identical expertise.  In principle, a limitation of liability clause in a contract between two merchants will be valid unless it is declared to be unenforceable either for unconscionability or because failure to discharge the obligation to which it applies would amount to a fundamental breach of contract. 82 Under the doctrine of unconscionability, a limitation of liability clause will be unenforceable where one party to the contract has abused its negotiating power to take undue advantage of the other.  This doctrine is generally applied in the context of a consumer contract or contract of adhesion. 83 Under the doctrine of fundamental breach, parties with equal bargaining power can, in certain cases, apply to have an unreasonable clause declared unenforceable on the basis that it does not reflect the intent of the parties. For this purpose, the breaching party’s failure to perform its obligations under the contract must be such that it “deprives the non‑breaching party of substantially the whole benefit of the agreement”: Guarantee Co. of North America v. Gordon Capital Corp. , 1999 CanLII 664 (SCC) , [1999] 3 S.C.R. 423, at para. 50 . Thus, the existence of a latent defect does not automatically amount to a fundamental breach of contract:  G. H. L. Fridman, The Law of Contract in Canada (5th ed. 2006), at p. 592; the latent defect must be “ir...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 5\\nthe whole benefit of the agreement”: Guarantee Co. of North America v. Gordon Capital Corp. , 1999 CanLII 664 (SCC) , [1999] 3 S.C.R. 423, at para. 50 . Thus, the existence of a latent defect does not automatically amount to a fundamental breach of contract:  G. H. L. Fridman, The Law of Contract in Canada (5th ed. 2006), at p. 592; the latent defect must be “irreparable”, or the good must be unusable: Hunter Engineering Co. v. Syncrude Canada Ltd. , 1989 CanLII 129 (SCC) , [1989] 1 S.C.R. 426, at pp. 501‑2; Guarantee Co. of North America ; R. G. McLean Ltd. v. Canadian Vickers Ltd. , 1970 CanLII 386 (ON CA) , [1971] 1 O.R. 207 (C.A.), at pp. 211‑12. 84 Once the existence of a fundamental breach has been established, the court must still analyse the limitation of liability clause in light of the general rules of contract interpretation.  If the words can reasonably be interpreted in only one way, it will not be open to the court, even on grounds of equity or reasonableness, to declare the clause to be unenforceable since this would amount to rewriting the contract negotiated by the parties. 3.6.3.3 Conclusion on Comparative Law 85 In short, owing to certain of its characteristics, the common law, to an even greater extent than French law, cannot easily be grafted on to Quebec civil law.  Nor, despite the connections between Quebec civil law and French law, does it appear any more desirable to import the rules of the French system into Quebec law.  What now remains to be considered is how to apply the Quebec rules to the facts of the instant case. 3.7 Analysis of C.E.’s Liability 86 The trial judge made findings of fact.  The Court of Appeal saw no palpable errors in them, and the parties have not asked us to review them. Only the conclusions of law drawn from those facts are in issue.  As mentioned above, the trial judge concluded that the cracking of the tubes was a design feature rather than a design defect.  Domtar contests this conclusion and argues that the cracking constitutes a latent defect.  It must therefore be determined whether there was in fact a latent defect in C.E.’s recovery boiler and, if so, whether C.E. knew or is presumed to have known that there was such a defect. 3.7.1 Existence of a Latent Defect 3.7.1.1 Seriousness of the Loss of Use 87 In the case at bar, the trial judge’s conclusion that the superheater did not have a latent defect was based on two errors he made in interpreting this first criterion.  The first was to limit the definition of a defect to a problem preventing the good from being used at all, while the second was to confuse the sale of a lower‑performance version of a good with the sale of a defective one. 88 A defect will be considered to be serious if it renders the good unfit for its intended use or so diminishes its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it at the price paid (arts. 1522 C.C.L.C. and 1726 C.C.Q.).  An example frequently cited by the authors is where a house is at risk of water damage owing to a crack in its foundation.  The crack does not actually have to cause such damage for a latent defect to exist; it is enough that the crack exists and that it is likely to lead to serious damage. 89 C.E. considers only the first aspect of the seriousness of the defect. According to its interpretation, the boiler could not be considered to be defective unless there were absolutely no possibility of it working.  The trial judge, too, failed to ask whether the usefulness of the good had been diminished, as he stressed that the recovery boiler could continue to operate despite the cracks. 90 In the instant case, the superheater was a new product at the time of delivery.  After 18 months of use, 6 leaks had to be repaired, 789 H‑style tie welds had to be replaced, and 764 cracks were detected in the superheater’s various compartments.  The cost of these repairs reached $445,483.57, and the boiler was out of service for 13 days.  Six months later, a full inspection revealed 3 new leaks and 859 new cracks, including some that C.E.’s expert described as being severe and likely to cause new leaks.  Domtar replaced the superheater two years after it was put into service.  However, the trial judge himself stated that this type of machine is designed for continuous use, apart from scheduled shutdowns, and generally has a lifespan, free of major problems, of at least 15 years.  The fact that Domtar noticed leaks by chance does not make the defect less serious, especially since so little time had elapsed since the equipment was put into service.  This clearly suggests abnormal wear and tear of the machine. Furthermore, an engineer who testified for C.E. acknowledged that it should normally take 15 to 20 years for cracks to occur in the superheater tubes. 91 For the defect to be considered serious, it was enough for there to be a problem with the boiler so serious that the buyer would not have purchased the boiler had it known about the problem.  The trial judge himself concluded that Domtar would undoubtedly have elected to use hinge‑pin attachments, just as any other paper manufacturer provided with the relevant information about the two types of attachments would have done.  He added that if Domtar had elected to use hinge‑pin attachments, it is probable that no leaks would have been discovered in the ITSH and that fewer cracks would have been found in the ITSH and HTSH.  In our opinion, these findings of fact support the Court of Appeal’s conclusion that Domtar would not have bought a boiler with H‑style tie welds if it had been informed of the risks associated with this type of attachment. 92 There is another problem with the trial judge’s characterization of the facts:  the confusion between performance and defectiveness of a product.  In the case at bar, C.E. submitted that the hinge‑pin attachments were simply of higher quality than the H‑style tie welds.  While it is true that a manufacturer may design goods of varying quality, each version of a good must be fit for its intended purpose for as long as a buyer might reasonably be entitled to expect.  Yet the technology of hinge‑pin attachments existed at the time the boiler was manufactured.  What are in issue here are not two types of attachments of different quality or different brands, but rather two types of attachments, one of which tends to weaken the tubes, and is as a result likely to cause unscheduled shutdowns of the boiler, even after only a short time in operation. 93 C.E. knew about the problems associated with the rigidity of both A‑style and H‑style tie welds.  Furthermore, it was the rigidity of H‑style tie welds that prompted C.E.’s parent company to decide to offer hinge‑pin attachments as the standard in superheater design.  Nevertheless, C.E. used A‑style and H‑style tie welds without having independent analyses conducted to determine whether they could withstand the stress placed on the tubes by the circulation of steam at high temperatures.  According to the trial judge, C.E. chose instead to rely on its customers’ experience in this respect to assess the real efficacy of its new product.  Moreover, it can be seen from a memorandum filed at trial that C.E. had delayed the adoption of hinge‑pin attachments in order to maintain its competitive position in the marketplace, and that the decision to adopt them as the standard had been imposed on the manufacturer gradually by market forces: . . . it is apparent that it was reluctantly responding to market forces that were to the effect that “the pulp and paper industry no longer considers the use of tie welds to be an acceptable superheater attachment . . . .” ( 2003 CanLII 52306 (QC CS) , [2003] R.J.Q. 2194, at para. 41 ) 94 Where there is a serious problem that affects the use of a good and the manufacturer is aware of the problem, the manufacturer must address the problem to avoid being held liable for it.  The manufacturer cannot simply put the product on the market and wait for reactions from users. 95 This being said, C.E. still uses H‑style tie welds in the design of some of its products, sometimes in combination with hinge‑pin attachments.  The present reasons for judgment should not be understood to imply that H‑style tie welds must never be used in the design of superheaters.  However, as a boiler manufacturer, C.E. is required to take its customers’ needs and objectives into account in designing its boilers.  This is the standard that Domtar was entitled to expect C.E. to meet. 3.7.1.2 Existence of the Defect at the Time of the Sale 96 In the case at bar, the cracking caused by the use of H‑style tie welds was not the result of abnormal use of the recovery boiler, as C.E. originally claimed.  Moreover, the trial judge found that the cause of the cracking was related to the rigidity of the attachments used.  C.E. decided, with good reason, not to pursue its original argument in this Court.  In all probability, the defect existed at the time of the sale. 3.7.1.3 Domtar Was Unaware of the Defect at the Time of the Sale 97 This criterion requires the application of a subjective standard.  The trial judge found that Domtar did not have actual knowledge of what he described as a design feature.  According to the evidence, C.E. did not share the information it possessed on this subject with Domtar. 3.7.1.4 Latency of the Defect Despite Domtar’s Expertise 98 The latency of a defect is analysed in relation to the buyer’s level of expertise.  In other words, should Domtar have known it was running a risk of excessive cracking because of the use of H‑style tie welds? 99 The trial judge found that Domtar’s “principal interlocutor” was aware of the two different opinions on the attachments since he had been a member of the Black Liquor Recovery Boiler Advisory Committee which met to discuss recent cases in which problems with recovery boilers had led to unscheduled shutdowns.  The trial judge also mentioned the expertise of Domtar’s engineering department and of Simons‑Sandwell as regards the operation of pulp and paper mills.  Although Domtar was characterized as a sophisticated operator of the equipment used in operating a pulp and paper mill, the issue here is the design of a custom‑made piece of industrial equipment.  C.E. is a company that specializes in the design of recovery boilers.  According to the trial judge, approximately 50 to 60 percent of C.E.’s revenue came from the design and sale of boilers.  The company enjoyed an 80 percent share of the Canadian market for these machines.  Expertise in operating such equipment does not automatically entail expertise in the actual design of the product.  The manufacturer inevitably has more complete knowledge of the product it has designed since it controls the materials and labour. 100 In discussing the professional qualifications of Domtar’s representatives, the trial judge stated that Simons‑Sandwell was knowledgeable to a certain extent about boilers and was aware of, among other things, the existence and potential advantages of hinge‑pin attachments. However, neither Simons‑Sandwell nor Domtar knew that the tie welds were in all probability the cause of the numerous cracks that had appeared. 101 C.E. submits that the trial judge did not attach sufficient importance to Simons‑Sandwell’s knowledge.  This argument must fail.  The defect is not apparent simply because Domtar was assisted by an expert.  Since the cause of the excessive cracking was unknown to both Domtar and Simons‑Sandwell, the defect in this case was indeed latent. 3.7.2 Applicability of the Limitation of Liability Clause 102 As mentioned above, the limitation of liability clause may be relied on only if C.E., as a manufacturer, can rebut the presumption of knowledge applicable to it.  C.E. had to prove that another manufacturer in the same circumstances would not have known of the defect: Samson & Filion . Its argument in this Court is based not on fault on the part of Domtar or a third party, or on superior force or development risk, but on its own good faith throughout its business relations with Domtar.  Although good faith has not yet been recognized as a means of rebutting the presumption, it will be helpful to review the facts as alleged by C.E. and found by the trial judge. 103 The trial judge reviewed in detail the evolution of the types of attachments used in recovery boilers.  In 1977, C.E. had decided to replace A‑style tie welds with H‑style tie welds because A‑style tie welds had been causing serious leakage problems over the previous five years.  C.E. decided that H‑style tie welds would be preferable, even though they had not been tested. 104 Manufacturers started using hinge‑pin attachments in the early 1980s.  It can be seen from internal C.E. memoranda that a number of customers in the United States had complained about problems with cracks and leaks linked to tie welds.  As a result, C.E.’s parent company adopted hinge‑pin attachments as its standard in the United States in 1984.  But a similar problem was occurring in Canada:  C.E. had received complaints about A‑style tie welds from Canadian customers in January 1983, May 1983 and December 1984.  In June 1985, C.E. recommended to one of its customers that it use hinge‑pin attachments to avoid cracks and leaks.  In 1988, C.E. decided to limit its use of hinge‑pin attachments in order to maintain its competitive position in the marketplace: An internal memorandum insisted on the use of tie welds because of the additional cost to manufacture hinge pins, and that in order to maintain the competitive position of C.E. in the marketplace, hinge pins should only be used when there was a “known failure, deterioration, misalignment, etc. of our present standard tangent tube flex ties”, or, “if specifically requested by the customer or when ‘latest state‑of‑the‑art’ design is noted.” ( 2003 CanLII 52306 (QC CS) , [2003] R.J.Q. 2194, at para. 39 ) C.E. did not finally adopt hinge‑pin attachments as its Canadian standard until the following year.  However, C.E. had known about the problems associated with tie welds since the early 1980s, that is, before Domtar submitted its purchase order in September 1985. 105 It can be seen from the evidence that the technical knowledge of the time had led some of C.E.’s competitors, and even its parent company, to change their standards.  To successfully rebut the presumption of knowledge, it matters little that C.E. believed in good faith that it had solved the problem.  Simply having an honest belief in the adequacy of its product is not enough to relieve a manufacturer of liability.  It must be concluded that, since C.E. has failed to raise a valid defence, it has not rebutted the presumption of knowledge applicable to it in accordance with the standard established in Samson & Filion . 106 It is therefore our view that the Court of Appeal correctly found that there was a latent defect in the superheater that C.E. knew or should have known about.  Since C.E. has failed to rebut the presumption of knowledge applicable to it, it cannot rely on the limitation of liability clause in its defence. 3.8 Duty to Inform 107 The trial judge found that C.E. had breached its duty to inform, whereas in the Court of Appeal’s view, the issue related to the warranty against latent defects.  The two concepts overlap, but it is important to distinguish them in order to identify the circumstances in which each rule will be applied. 108 Whereas the warranty against latent defects is expressly provided for in the C.C.L.C. and the C.C.Q., the duty to inform derives instead from the general principle of good faith ( Bank of Montreal v. Bail Ltée , 1992 CanLII 71 (SCC) , [1992] 2 S.C.R. 554, at p. 586; arts. 6, 7 and 1375 C.C.Q.) and the principle of free and informed consent.  Furthermore, the scope of the general duty to inform is much broader than that of the disclosure of a latent defect.  This duty encompasses any information that is of decisive importance for a party to a contract, as Gonthier J. stated in Bail (see pp. 586‑87). It is therefore easy to imagine a situation in which a seller would be in breach of the duty even though no latent defect exists. 109 Where a seller fails to discharge the duty to disclose a defect, on the other hand, it can probably be said at the same time that he or she has also breached the general duty to inform the buyer of a factor of decisive importance in respect of the good sold, namely the existence of a latent defect.  The instant case is one example of this.  If a party invokes the seller’s warranty against latent defects, the duty to inform is in a sense subsumed in the analysis of the seller’s liability for latent defects, and there is no need for the court to conduct a separate analysis on the seller’s duty to inform.  As a result, our analysis and conclusion regarding C.E.’s liability under the warranty against latent defects are sufficient to dispose of the case\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 6\\nin respect of the good sold, namely the existence of a latent defect.  The instant case is one example of this.  If a party invokes the seller’s warranty against latent defects, the duty to inform is in a sense subsumed in the analysis of the seller’s liability for latent defects, and there is no need for the court to conduct a separate analysis on the seller’s duty to inform.  As a result, our analysis and conclusion regarding C.E.’s liability under the warranty against latent defects are sufficient to dispose of the case before the Court. 110 What now remains is to consider C.E.’s argument concerning the reduction of the quantum awarded to Domtar in light of Domtar’s failure to mitigate its damages and of the payment it received from its insurer, Lloyd’s, as well as the actions against Chubb, based on the performance bond, and Arkwright, based on the all risk insurance policy. 3.9 Reduction of the Quantum of the Damages 111 In this Court, C.E. has submitted two arguments for reducing the quantum of the damages awarded to Domtar by the Court of Appeal.  First, C.E. contends that by replacing a superheater that was fully operational, Domtar failed to mitigate its damages and that Domtar is accordingly entitled to be reimbursed only for the repairs effected in the spring of 1989.  Second, C.E. submits that the determination a posteriori by a court that the damage was not covered by the insurance policy does not alter the compensatory nature of the payment that has already been made.  According to C.E., the payment made to Domtar by Lloyd’s was intended to settle certain claims, including the one related to the purchase of the boiler from C.E., and once that payment was made, Lloyd’s was subrogated to Domtar’s rights and Domtar’s claim for damages against C.E. had to be reduced accordingly. 112 The trial judge rejected C.E.’s suggestion that Domtar could have repaired the superheater instead of replacing it.  He noted that Domtar had had to make a decision in light of the available information and that its inability to adequately assess the alternatives was due to C.E.’s breach of the duty to inform.  Regarding the subrogation argument, the trial judge agreed with C.E. and held that the damages claimed by Domtar should be reduced by the amount it had received from Lloyd’s. 113 The Court of Appeal also rejected the repair scenarios proposed by C.E., citing Domtar’s need for reliable equipment.  However, it reversed the trial judge’s holding on the subrogation issue.  According to the Court of Appeal, for a payment to result in subrogation of an insurer to the rights of the insured, it must be made to the insured on account of an obligation arising from the insurance contract or by operation of law.  In the instant case, because a clause in the insurance contract between Lloyd’s and Domtar expressly excluded damage resulting from a breakdown of or defect in the recovery boiler, the payment made by Lloyd’s could not have been related to the damages being claimed by Domtar.  Lloyd’s was not, therefore, subrogated to Domtar’s rights. The Court of Appeal’s reasons are sound, and there is no need to intervene. 3.10 Chubb’s Appeal 114 This Court granted leave to appeal in this incidental case because its outcome depended directly on the outcome of the main case.  In this case, Domtar relies on a contract establishing a performance bond in its favour. Under the contract, Chubb agreed to guarantee the performance of C.E.’s obligations under the contract of sale for the recovery boiler.  Since there was a latent defect in the superheater, Chubb is solidarily liable for the amount of its guarantee as surety in favour of Domtar, as the Court of Appeal held. 3.11 Domtar’s Appeal Against Arkwright 115 This incidental case concerns an “all risk” insurance policy issued by Arkwright that covered Domtar’s property, including the recovery boiler, at the time of the events in question.  Domtar has already been indemnified under this policy for the damage it sustained as a result of the repairs effected in the spring of 1989.  However, the policy contained a clause excluding damage resulting from latent defects in the boiler. 116 In its appeal, Domtar seeks to demonstrate that the losses were nonetheless covered by the insurance policy.  More specifically, it submits that the lost profits it suffered in the fall of 1989 when C.E.’s superheater was replaced with one from B. & W. resulted from the faulty design of the H‑style tie welds.  In other words, the latent defect affected not the entire boiler or superheater, but strictly the H‑style tie welds, and the damage was accordingly covered by the policy. 117 The trial judge agreed with Arkwright.  In his view, replacing the superheater was not the only possible solution for Domtar, since at the time the machine was replaced, it could have continued operating indefinitely. Domtar could not, therefore, rely on its all risk insurance policy to recover disbursements made to prevent a hypothetical future loss. 118 Domtar appealed that judgment.  The Court of Appeal considered the replacement of the superheater to have been necessary.  In its view, however, the tie welds could not be dissociated from the superheater itself and the insurer could rely on the exclusion clause because the design defect affected the entire superheater and not just the tie welds.  According to the Court of Appeal, the narrow interpretation Domtar seeks to give the exclusion clause would deprive the clause of any effect, and the loss to which Domtar’s claim applied was clearly not covered by the insurance policy with Arkwright. 119 In the main case, we held that the superheater’s design was defective because of the excessive cracking of the tubes caused by the use of H‑style tie welds, and that C.E., as the manufacturer, was liable for, inter alia , the cost of replacing the superheater.  To accept Domtar’s contention that the welds can be dissociated from the superheater would render the exclusion clause meaningless, since it would in almost every case be possible to isolate one component as the cause of the defect. 120 To sum up, Domtar’s submissions in this appeal against Arkwright are unfounded in law.  For the reasons given by the Court of Appeal, we are therefore of the opinion that the cost of replacing the defective superheater was excluded under the insurance policy. 4. Disposition 121 For these reasons, we would dismiss the three appeals and affirm the judgments of the Court of Appeal, with costs throughout. APPENDIX Civil Code of Lower Canada 1522. The seller is obliged by law to warrant the buyer against such latent defects in the thing sold, and its accessories, as render it unfit for the use for which it was intended, or so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it, or would not have given so large a price, if he had known them. 1523. The seller is not bound for defects which are apparent and which the buyer might have known of himself. 1524. The seller is bound for latent defects even when they were not known to him, unless it is stipulated that he shall not be obliged to any warranty. 1527. If the seller knew the defect of the thing, he is obliged not only to restore the price of it, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer. He is obliged in like manner in all cases in which he is legally presumed to know the defects. 1528. If the seller did not know the defects, or is not legally presumed to have known them, he is obliged only to restore the price and to reimburse to the buyer the expenses caused by the sale. Civil Code of Québec 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer. 1733. A seller may not exclude or limit his liability unless he has disclosed the defects of which he was aware or could not have been unaware and which affect the right of ownership or the quality of the property. An exception may be made to this rule where a buyer buys property at his own risk from a seller who is not a professional seller. Appeals dismissed with costs . Solicitors for the appellants ABB Inc., Alstom Canada Inc. and Chubb Insurance Company of Canada:  Stikeman Elliott, Montréal. Solicitors for the appellant/respondent Domtar Inc.:  Ogilvy, Renault, Montréal. Solicitors for the respondent Arkwright Mutual Insurance Company:  Kugler, Kandestin, Montréal.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03333333507180214, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\", \"title\": \"ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.03333333507180214, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\"}, \"token_count\": 18307, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"De Castris v. Banque Nationale du Canada, 1999 CanLII 10304 (QC CQ)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 1999 CanLII 10304 (QC CQ) | De Castris v. Banque Nationale du Canada | CanLII Date: 1999-11-22 File number: 500-32-042779-993 Other citation: EYB 1999-15929 Citation: De Castris v. Banque Nationale du Canada, 1999 CanLII 10304 (QC CQ) REJB 1999-15929 - Texte intégral CITATION: De Castris c. Banque Nationale du Canada Cour du Québec (Division des petites créances) CANADA PROVINCE DE QUÉBEC DISTRICT DE Montréal NO : 500-32-042779-993 DATE :  1999-11-22 EN PRÉSENCE DE : Raoul-P. Barbe , J.C.Q. Linda De Castris & Thomas Silvestre Plaintiffs v. Banque Nationale du Canada Defendant Barbe J.C.Q.:- 1 By a motion served on April 6, 1999, the plaintiffs claim $3,000 from the defendant as a reduction in the purchase price of an immovable. The amount corresponds to the cost of repairing a latent defect in the fireplace chimney. The petition was contested. The hearing took place on 18 October 1999. Proof of the Plaintiffs 2 Ms. Linda De Castris represented herself and Mr. Thomas Silvestre as the plaintiffs. She testified as to the events surrounding the case. 3 On 29 September 1997, the plaintiffs signed a promise to purchase a house owned by the defendant. The price was $90,000 and the plaintiffs' promise was conditional upon the completion of a satisfactory building inspection. A deed of sale was concluded between the parties on 3 December 1997 at the agreed price. 4 A real-estate broker evaluated the house for the defendant on 18 September 1997, before the promise to purchase was signed. The broker was Ms. DeCastris' father, who testified that he did the evaluation before he knew that his daughter was considering purchasing the house. The report states that the roof and the chimney are in bad condition and suggests a price of $104,000. The report doesnot clarify the fact that there are two chimneys: one for the furnace and one for the fireplace. The chimney referred to in the broker's report is the furnace chimney and there is no mention of a second chimney. The plaintiffs testified that they were fully aware that these repairs were needed when purchasing the house. 5 The building inspection was done (see R-4) and refers to the same needed repairs as the broker's evaluation. It indicates that there are two chimneys in the house, but does not say that the fireplace chimney needed repairs. However, the building inspector advised that the fireplace chimney be cleaned before using it. 6 The plaintiffs followed the advice of the building inspector and had the fireplace chimney cleaned on 15 October 1998 by a certified chimney services company. It was necessary to use a special cleaning process because the fireplace chimney was caked with “glazed 3 rd degree creosote” which cannot be removed by the usual method. After removing the creosote, it wasdiscovered that some of the tiles in the chimney were cracked and completely broken. The chimney is unsafe in this condition and the plaintiffs were told not to use the fireplace until the chimney was repaired. The Fire Prevention Service of the Town of Pierrefonds confirmed this assessment after carrying out its own inspection of the chimney. The chimney cleaning technicians told theplaintiffs that such damage could only have been caused by a previous chimney fire. 7 The plaintiffs were told that they had two options for repairing the chimney: either it could be torn down and rebuilt for a cost of $4,500, or the existing chimney could be lined with a stainless steel liner for a cost of $3,000. The plaintiffs informed the defendant of the situation by a letter sent in November 1998, and gave it the choice to cover the cost of either option. The defendantdid not make a payment and so the plaintiffs have brought the matter to court. 8 The plaintiffs testified that while they were fully aware of the problems with the roof and the furnace chimney at the time of the sale, they would not have agreed to the purchase price had they known of the condition of the fireplace chimney. Proof of the Defendant 9 Ms. Nicole Parent is a hypothec agent and represented the defendant at the hearing. The defendant was the owner of the house at the time of the sale, having acquired it following a default on a hypothec. She agreed with the plaintiffs version of the events but stressed that the plaintiffs were aware of the roof and furnace problems. It is the defendant's position that the terms of the deedof sale exclude its liability for latent defects. The defendant did not seem aware that the plaintiffs were referring to a second, separate chimney in the house. 10 The defendant relied on several clauses contained in the deed of sale: the “Object of Contract” clause, the “Warranty” clause and the “Obligations” clause. 11 The “Object of Contract” clause states that: “ The vendor sells without any other warranty than those hereinafter mentioned to the purchaser, hereto present and accepting, the immovable hereinafter described .” 12 The “Warranty” clause provides that: “ This sale is made without any other warranty than that of the vendor's personal fault or acte [ sic .] with regard to the title of the property and without any warranty as to apparent or latent defects, the Purchaser buying the property at his own risks and perils .” [Emphasis added]. 13 The “Obligation” clause reads as follows: “ The purchaser obliges himself to: 1 ) to take the property “ as is ”, with the purchaser declaring that he has visited and inspected the same to his satisfaction and has himself verified with the competent authorities to ensure that his intended use of the property complieswith the statutes, regulations, and by-laws in force ”, 2 ) to refrain from requiring the Vendor to make any repairs whatsoever with regard to any apparent or latent defects .” [Emphasis added]. Reasons for Judgment 14 The facts of this case are not in dispute. The main issue is to determine whether plaintiff is still owed a legal guarantee of quality against latent defects despite the terms of the deed of sale. Relevant Provisions of the Civil Code of Quebec 15 This case comes within the domain of a contract of sale, which is regulated by articles 1708 and following of the Civil Code of Quebec . The plaintiff bases its claim on article 1726 of the CCQ. 16 The law imposes two legal obligations on the seller through article 1716 of the Civil Code of Quebec : the obligation to deliver the thing sold and the obligation to guarantee it with respect to ownership and quality. 17 Article 1716 CCQ provides that: The seller is bound to deliver the property and to warrant the ownership and quality of the property. These warranties exist of right whether or not they are stipulated in the contract of sale. 18 This case is concerned with the seller's legal guarantee of quality, which includes a legal guarantee against latent defects present in the thing sold. This guarantee is set out in article 1726 CCQ, which states that: The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against anylatent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. 19 However, the seller and buyer are permitted to vary the legal guarantee of quality as they wish in a contract of sale. This is expressed in article 1732 of the CCQ which provides that: The parties may, in their contract, add to the obligations of legal warranty diminish its effects or exclude it altogether but in no case may the seller exempt himself from his personal fault. 20 Article 1733 CCQ creates a restriction on this ability to freely determine the extent of the obligation of quality. Article 1733 CCQ states that: A seller may not exclude or limit his liability unless he has disclosed the defects of which he was aware or could not have been unaware and which affect the right of ownership or the quality of the property. An exception may be made to this rule where a buyer buys the property at his own risk from seller who is not a professional seller. 21 In order to settle the issue, it must be determined if the broken fireplace chimney is a latent defect in the sense of the law expressed in 1726 CCQ. Is the broken fireplace chimney a latent defect? 22 A defect must meet several criteria in order to be a latent defect. 1 To be a “latent defect” the problem must be subjectively and objectively unknown to the prudent and diligent buyer. 2 Such a buyer is one who has taken reasonable precautions before purchasing the property. While it is not necessary that the buyer have an expert examine the property to be purchased in order to be considered a reasonable buyer, 3 and a defect is necessarily considered latent if an expert could not discover it. 4 23 In this case, the plaintiffs had the house inspected before finalizing their promise to purchase. This meets the requirement that the buyer be reasonably prudent and diligent. The building inspector's report indicates that he looked at both the furnace and fireplace chimneys, but only made recommendations with respect to the furnace chimney. Moreover, the fact that the inspector did notnotice the true condition of the fireplace chimney means that it is incontestably a latent defect and indicates that it was objectively unknowable. 24 In addition, it is clear from both the plaintiffs and defendant's testimony that presence of the broken fireplace chimney was subjectively unknown. There is no indication that either party was aware of the broken chimney at the time of the sale or that either was ignoring it. As both parties were acting in good faith, there is no issue of fraud or deceit. 25 However, more is required for a defect to be latent. To be considered a latent defect the problem must also result in a serious reduction of the usefulness of the property and must have been present before the sale. These criteria are set out in the doctrine as: Le vice doit être grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l'acheteur et occulte. En revanche, tout fait provoquant un déficit d'usage constitue un vice au sens de la garantie. 5 26 In this case, these additional requirements are met. The plaintiff's testimony and reports by the chimney cleaners and fire department indicate that the fireplace is completely unusable in its present condition and that the damage could only have resulted from a “previous chimney fire”. Having established that the condition of the chimney does constitute a latent defect in thelegal sense, the effect of the clauses in the deed of sale can now be considered. Do the clauses in the deed of sale effectively exclude the legal guarantee against latent defects? 27 The material clauses of the deed of sale are those set out in the “proof of the defendant” section above. Clauses limiting or excluding the guarantee of quality are restrictively interpreted. 6 In order to have effect they must be drafted in clear and unambiguous language. It has been held that clauses specifying that the buyer accepts the property “as is” and after “having seen and examined the property” do not exclude the guarantee with respect to latent defects. 7 Rather, clear reference must be made to latent defects. 28 The deed excludes the defendant's obligation to guarantee against latent defects since the words “latent defects” are used in both the Warranty and Obligations clauses. I would note here the Obligations clause, point 2, refers to the request of “repairs” whereas in this case the plaintiff is requesting only reduction of the purchase price. In this case, the language ofthe Warranty clause still covers the situation and would operate to exclude the defendant's obligation to guarantee against latent defects. 29 However, the law prevents, by article 1733 CCQ, the limitation of the seller's liability if he was aware or could not have been unaware of defects. If the seller has awareness of the latent defect, such clauses will be of no force or effect. Although the evidence establishes that the defendant was not actually aware of the broken fireplace chimney, there are situations in which the law willpresume that the seller did have awareness of the defect. 30 This presumption operates with respect to “professional sellers” and is contained in article 1733 paragraph 1 CCQ (cited above) and 1728 CCQ which provides that: If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all the damages suffered by the buyer. 31 The term “professional seller” did not exist in the Civil Code of Lower Canada but was introduced into the CCQ. However, the idea of “professional sellers” is not new to the law and had been treated in several cases under the CCLC. 8 32 While the jurisprudence has not yet indicated precisely how this presumption of knowledge on the part of the professional sellers operates through articles 1733 and 1728 of the Civil Code of Quebec , the doctrine is clear that such a presumption exists, although some debate remains as to whether it is rebuttable. 9 33 The presumption is embodied in phrase “could not have been unaware” or “ne pouvait l'ignorer” in the French version of both articles 1728 and 1733 CCQ, which replaces the language used in articles 1527 and 1528 of the Civil Code of Lower Canada. The CCLC stated that the seller could be “legally presumed to know the defects” or “légalementprésumé connaître” in the French version. 34 The link between articles 1733 and 1728 can be made by noting that the use of the phrase “ not a professional seller” in the second paragraph of article 1733 implies that the first paragraph includes professional sellers. Since it is professional sellers who will be presumed to be unable to have been unaware of the defect, they cannot exclude their liability. 35 Therefore, lack of precision in the scope of this presumption arises mostly from the nature of concept of “professional seller” in the CCQ. According to Edwards, 10 the presumption is part of the Civil Code 's goal to protect economically weaker parties and derives from the writings of DuMoulin, Domat, and Pothier. 11 It is meant to express the idea that “ la profession publique du marchand a une certaine compétence dans les biens qu'il choisit de vendre .” 12 In addition, it has been expanded from including specialized sellers to also including general sellers of a diversity of products and remains for the courts to determine the precise scope of the presumption. 13 Can the defendant be considered a professional seller and so presumed to be aware of the defect? 36 The term “professional seller” is not defined in the CCQ, but the Commentaries of the Minister of Justice provide some guidance. They state that a professional seller is “ la personne qui a pour son occupation habituelle la vente des biens ”. 14 This definition has been used by the courts to determine whether a seller could be characterized as a professional seller. 15 37 Can it be said that the defendant comes within this definition? There are only a few cases dealing with this issue since the coming into the force of the CCQ in 1994. 38 In the case of Bergeron v. Société d'habitation et de développement de Montréal , 16 the Court of Appeal held that a developer and seller of immovables was a specialized seller to whom the presumption applied. The Société had purchased a college and converted it into apartments, which it then sold individually. In its decision the court provided several criteria for determining who is a specialized seller. The court did not address the issueof general sellers. 39 The case of Durand v. Jobidon 17 refered to jurisprudence existing before the CCQ in making its determination that the sellers were not professional sellers. The sellers were a divorcing couple who had extensively renovated a house built in approximately 1815. Judge Sheehan, referring to Ouellet v. Eymann , 18 stated that, La présomption de connaissance des vices de la chose repose essentiellement sur l'expertise que possède le vendeur parce qu'il est un “commerçant en semblables matières” dont les connaissances font présumer qu'il ne peut ignorer les vices cachés de la chose. Cette présomption ne s'applique pas au vendeur ordinaire de bonne foiqui ne possède pas de connaissance technique, ni d'expérience commerciale. 19 40 Judge Bossé treats the idea of professional sellers briefly in the case of Kack 20 . It involves the sale of a mobile home by a seller who worked as a truck driver in the transport industry and bought the mobile home solely to resell it. Commenting on the definition of professional seller provided by the Minister of Justice he notes that, Toute insuffisante cette définition puisse-t-elle être, en autant qu' elle ne fait pas la distinction entre le vendeur ordinaire et le vendeur spécialisé , elle contient, pour les fins de la présente affaire, les éléments essentiels. Dans le présent cas, le défendeur n'avait pas 'pour occupation habituelle la vente debiens'. La vente de roulottes fut très occasionnelle, son commerce étant le transport. [Emphasis added]. 21 41 Are there any principles which emerge from these cases? The decision of Bergeron does not assist much as...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... pas la distinction entre le vendeur ordinaire et le vendeur spécialisé , elle contient, pour les fins de la présente affaire, les éléments essentiels. Dans le présent cas, le défendeur n'avait pas 'pour occupation habituelle la vente debiens'. La vente de roulottes fut très occasionnelle, son commerce étant le transport. [Emphasis added]. 21 41 Are there any principles which emerge from these cases? The decision of Bergeron does not assist much as it is limited to specialized sellers. As discussed above the doctrine indicates that the presumption now extends to general sellers. 42 The case of Durand refers to sellers “ en semblables matières ” which translates as “dealing in similar goods”. This language was used in decisions made under the CCLC. The phrase is not specifically defined in the case law. It is not necessary to determine its scope in this case for the reason that the presumption is now considered to includegeneral sellers of a variety of products. In addition, the judge refers to other elements in reaching his conclusion: the ordinary seller who has neither technical knowledge or commercial experience. 43 Finally, the case of Kack makes the important observation that the Minister of Justice's definition does not distinguish between ordinary sellers and specialized sellers. This suggests that it is the Legislator's intention that there no longer be a difference in legal effect between general and specialized sellers with respect to latent defects and is in keeping with the doctrineciting an expansion of the presumption. 44 In Kack , Judge Bossé goes on to base his decision on the frequency of the seller's selling activity and the fact that his principle occupation was in a different field. He held that the seller in the case only sold mobile homes very occasionally and that his usual occupation was transport, not selling. 45 In the present case, the employee representing the defendant identified herself as a “hypothec agent”. This suggests that the defendant deals frequently with immovables if it has a specialized department and employees devoted to hypothecs. It can be assumed that this is not the first house that the defendant has sold. In addition, banks are engaged in the business of selling awide variety of products to the public so that it cannot be said that the defendant has no commercial experience or that selling is not one of its main activities. 46 Given the pervasiveness of banks into the day-to-day affairs of the public and the fact that one of the general objectives of the Civil Code of Quebec is to protect the economically weaker party, 22 it seems reasonable to classify them as professional sellers. 23 47 As such, the defendant is presumed to have been aware of the latent defect. The obligation to guarantee quality cannot be excluded in this case according to 1733 CCQ, despite the clear language of the deed of sale. Conclusion 48 Since the presumption has the effect of putting article 1726 CCQ back into operation, the existence of the broken fireplace chimney means that the defendant has failed in its obligation. The plaintiffs are entitled to a remedy and have requested, in effect, a reduction in the purchase price. The CCQ does not provide a specific remedy for latent defects and so we must resort to the generalrules of obligations. 49 The plaintiffs' claim falls within the remedy known as quantum minoris that is expressed in the second paragraph of article 1604 CCQ, 24 which states: However and notwithstanding any stipulation to the contrary, he is not entitled to resolution or resiliation of the contract if the default of his debtor is of minor importance, unless, in the case of an obligation of successive performance, the default occurs repeatedly, but he is then entitled to a proportional reduction of his correlative obligation . [Emphasisadded]. 50 The defendant had the obligation to guarantee against latent defects and, in return, the plaintiffs had the obligation to pay the purchase price of $90,000. Because the defendant has defaulted in its obligation, the plaintiffs are entitled to reduce their obligation and this is done by reducing the purchase price. 25 51 However, the reduction in the purchase price does not necessarily correspond to the cost of repair work required to fix the latent defect. In Bergeron , 26 Judge Forget recognizes this when stating that, “ La diminution du prix de vente, on le sait, correspond assez souvent -- bien que pas nécessairement -- au coût des réparations ....” 52 Judge Sheehan in Durand 27 discusses why this is so and some of the other factors which should be considered when determining how much to reduce the price of a sale when there are hidden defects: Il suffit de réaliser qu'une réparation peut non seulement éliminer un vice de la chose vendue, mais également lui donner une plus-value. Par ailleurs, un prix de vente résulte normalement de concessions de part et d'autre ... la Cour d'appel s'est prononcée sur la manière d'évaluer le montant que constitue la diminution du prix devente en raison d'un vice caché. Il y a plusieurs façons de s'y prendre et la méthode utilisée dépend des circonstances de chaque cas. Des critères tels que l'âge de la bâtisse, le prix de vente, l'emplacement de l'immeuble, la nature des vices cachés et, surtout, le résultat des travaux de réparation peuventêtre considerés pour pondérer le coût des réparations qui est réclamé. ....La Cour d'appel souligne que si les termes du Code civil sont assez larges pour viser le calcul du coût réel pour remédier au vice, cela ne veut pas dire que, en tout état de cause, ce sont les coûts des réparations quiconstituent la juste diminution du prix de vente . [Emphasis added]. 53 In this case, the plaintiffs purchased the house for $90,000, a price which is $14,000 less than the amount of $104,000 suggested by the real-estate broker. The house is a large house with eight rooms, and is situated on a relatively large piece of land, 8,505 square feet. In addition, the report indicates that it is in an excellent location as it is near schools, a shopping centre, acultural center, and a library. Given all these circumstances, the court considers $1,500 to be a fair reduction in the price. For these Reasons, The Court: 54 CONDEMNS the defendant to pay plaintiff $1,500 with interest from the date of service of the action and the additional indemnity and judicial costs of $69. Barbe J.C.Q. 1. see Placement Jacpar c. Benzakour [1989] R.J.Q. 2317 . 2. Droit de la vente , Denys-Claude Lamontagne, Les éditions Yvon Blais Inc, Cowansville Qc. (1995) at p. 100 paragraphs 229 and 230 [hereinafter Lamontagne]. 3. Placement Jacpar Inc. c. Benzakour 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , [1989] R.J.Q. 2309 C.A. . 4. see Laurier Vachon Ltée c. Girard [1968] B.R. 497 , cited in Lamontagne, supra note 2. 5. La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit Québécois , Jeffery Edwards, Wilson & Lafleur ltée, Montréal Qc. (1998) at 135 paragraphe 304 [hereinafter Edwards]. 6. Edwards, supra note 5 at 123 paragraph 283. 7. Edwards, supra note 5 at 124 paragraph 284; see also Maltais c. Tremblay [1990] R.D.I. 143 C.Q. . 8. see General Motors Products of Canada v. Leo Kravitz 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , [1979] 1 S.C.R. 790 , Caisse Populaire Desjardins de Saint-Nicholas c. Rouette (C.A.Q) , [1988], A.Q. No. 1842 , Ouellet c. Eymann (C.A.) 1988 CanLII 1013 (QC CA) , [1988] R.J.Q. 2448 . 9. LaMontagne, supra note 2, refers to “présomptions de connaissance” at 102 and in paragraph 232 states that “Les présomptions de connaissance s'appliquent au vendeur (ou à l'acheteur) professionnel” and refers to articles 1728 , 1729, and 1733 CCQ; see also the discussion in Edwards, supra note 5, at 62 paragraph 140, at 285-289 paragraphs 612-619 andat 289-300 paragraphs 620-636; see also Kack c. Sirois [1999] J.Q. no 2349 [hereinafter Kack ] . 10. Edwards, supra note 5 at 62 paragraph 142. 11. see discussion in Edwards, supra note 5, at 279 -281 paragraphs 598-604. 12. Ibid. at 287 paragraph 615. 13. Ibid. paragraphs 616-618. 14. Québec, Minister of Justice, Commentaries of the Minister of Justice: the Civil Code of Quebec , tome 1, Quebec, Publications du Quebec, 1993 at p. 1079 with respect to article 1729 CCQ. 15. See Kack , supra note 9. 16. 1996 CanLII 5767 (QC CA) , [1996] R.J.Q. 2088 [hereinafter Bergeron ]. 17. [1997] A.Q. no. 3680 [hereinafter Durand ] . 18. (C.A.) 1988 CanLII 1013 (QC CA) , [1988] R.J.Q. 2448 . 19. Supra note 17 at paragraph 37. 20. Supra note 9. 21. Ibid. at paragraph 11. 22. See Edwards, supra note 10. 23. The case of Caisse populaire Des jardins de Saint Nicolas c. Rouette (C.A.Q) [1988] A.Q. No. 1842 held that Caisse populaire was not a professional seller. It can be distinguished from the present case in that it was under the old Code which did not express the same general objective of the CCQ to protect economically weaker parties and was decided before the expansion of thepresumption. In addition, Caisse populaires are different entities from banks. 24. Lamontagne, supra note 2, at 107 paragraph 241. 25. Ibid. 26. Supra note 16 at paragraph 31. 27. Supra note 17 at paragraphs 29-30....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.0320020467042923, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\", \"title\": \"De Castris v. Banque Nationale du Canada, 1999 CanLII 10304 (QC CQ)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.0320020467042923, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\"}, \"token_count\": 6675, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc., 2015 QCCS 5514 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2015 QCCS 5514 (CanLII) | Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc. | CanLII Date: 2015-11-23 File number: 500-17-060283-101 Other citations: AZ-51234224 — [2015] CarswellQue 11288 — [2015] QJ 12923 Citation: Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc., 2015 QCCS 5514 (CanLII) Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc. 2015 QCCS 5514 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTRÉAL No: 500-17-060283-101 DATE: November 23, 2015 _____________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE Marie-Anne Paquette, S.C.J. _____________________________________________________________________ Transport Desgagnés Inc. DESGAGNÉS TRANSARCTIK INC. NAVIGATION DESGAGNÉS INC. Plaintiffs / Cross-Defendants Lloyds underwriters and institution of Lloyds underwriters (ILU) companies subscribing to Policy number B0856 09H0016 and Aim Insurance (Barbados) SCC Plaintiffs in continuance of suit v. Wärtsilä Canada Inc. Wärtsilä Nederland b.v. Defendants / Cross-Plaintiffs _____________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT _____________________________________________________________________ OVERVIEW [ 1 ] JP2056 The Plaintiffs/Cross-Defendants Transport Desgagnés inc., Desgagnés Tansarctik Inc. and Navigation Desgagnés Inc. ( TDI ) operate a fleet of vessels in the Canadian arctic waters, on the St-Lawrence river and on international waters. The present litigation pertains to one of its ships, the MV Camilla Desgagnés ( Camilla Desgagnés ). [ 2 ] The Defendants/Cross-Plaintiffs Wärtsilä Canada inc. and Wärtsilä Netherlands B.V. are part of the Wärtsilä Group ( Wärtsilä ) and carry business as manufacturer and supplier of marine engines and propulsion systems. [ 3 ] In February 2007, Wärtsilä delivered a new bedplate and reconditioned crankshaft to the Camilla Desgagnés, in the Port of Halifax. [ 4 ] Wärtsilä assembled and mounted the crankshaft onto the bedplate in its factory in Zwolle, Netherlands from November 13 to November 28, 2006. [ 5 ] The reconditioned crankshaft sustained a catastrophic failure on October 27, 2009, while the vessel was on route on the St-Lawrence River near Les Escoumins. [ 6 ] The Court holds that the crankshaft sold was affected by a latent defect at the time of the sale, that Wärtsilä was presumably aware of same and that as a result, it is liable for all the damages resulting from this failure, in accordance with the civil law of Quebec. [ 7 ] TDI’s claim is therefore granted and Wärtsilä’s Counter-Claim for the expenses which it incurred as a result of the failure shall be rejected. 1. ADMISSIONS AND UNCHALLENGED EVIDENCE [ 8 ] The parties admit, subject to all legal and factual arguments against TDI’s claim, that the damages resulting from this failure of the crankshaft amount to $ 5,661,830.33. [ 9 ] It is also undisputed that: 9.1. the failure results form an insufficient tightening of the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L; 9.2. the incident occurred after 13,653 running hours of the engine, which represents 217 000 000 (2.17x10 8 ) stress cycles on the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L; 9.3. The cracks that led to the failure of the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L commenced on the outer edge of the 3 rd thread root in the stud and propagated outwards across the diameter of the stud. 2. POSITION OF THE PARTIES [ 10 ] TDI claims that the improper tightening of the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L is a latent defect which dates back to the assembly of the crankshaft in Wärtsilä’s factory in Zwolle. Wärtsilä, as a manufacturer and professional seller, would thus be liable for the resulting damages. [ 11 ] Wärtsilä denies the presence of a latent defect.  The improper tightening would rather result from manipulations by TDI employees during maintenance work carried on board since the installation of the engine on the Camilla Desgagnés in February 2006. [ 12 ] Wärtsilä also claims that the warranty no longer applies, as the six-month-warranty-period provided in the contract had expired long before the failure occurred. [ 13 ] The applicability of such limitation clause raises a debate as to whether, as Wärtsilä submits, Canadian maritime law applies to the present dispute or whether, as TDI submits, Quebec law applies. [ 14 ] On the one hand, TDI, submits that such limitation clauses are not enforceable in Quebec civil law in the context of this case, which pertains to the liability of the professional seller and manufacturer for latent defects. [ 15 ] On the other hand, Wärtsilä pleads that such limitation is enforceable in Canadian maritime law. 3. ISSUES IN DISPUTE 1.         Does Canadian maritime law or Quebec civil law govern the current dispute? 2.         Based on such governing law, is Wärtsilä liable for the damages resulting from the engine breakdown? 2.1.        Are the opening conditions of the legal warranty against latent defects met? 2.2.        Are the contractual limitation clauses applicable? 4. ANALYSIS 4.1 Does Canadian maritime law or Quebec civil law govern the current dispute? [ 16 ] For the following reasons, the Quebec civil law applies. 4.1.1 What is Canadian maritime law? [ 17 ] Canadian maritime law is not the law of any province of Canada.  It is the product of international conventions. [1] [ 18 ] This body of federal law encompasses the principles of English maritime law as they were developed and applied in the Admiralty Court of England.  Such admiralty law was as incorporated into Canadian law in 1934. [2] It has been amended by the federal Parliament and developed through judicial precedents since then. [3] 4.1.2 When does Canadian maritime law apply? [ 19 ] The constitutional division of powers between the federal government and the legislatures of the provinces is at the root of this governing law issue. [4] [ 20 ] While the laws of the Parliament govern matters of “Navigation and Shipping” ( Constitution Act, 1867 , s. 91(10)), the laws of the relevant province apply to matters of “Property and Civil Rights” ( Constitution Act, 1867 , s. 92(13)). [ 21 ] Presumably, the need to achieve some uniformity and compliance with applicable international law in the regulation of the various aspects of maritime commerce and water traffic has prompted the Fathers of the Confederation to grant the legislative power over navigation and shipping to the Parliament. [5] Such power extends to inland waterwaysm provided that they are navigable. As the Supreme Court of Canada held in Whitbread , the legal rights and obligations of those engaged in navigation and shipping should not arbitrarily change as their vessels cross the point at which the water ceases or, as the case may be, commences to ebb and flow. [6] [ 22 ] Hence, matters relating to the framework of the legal relationships arising out of shipping activities or relating to the infrastructure of navigation and shipping activities fall within the pith and substance of the Parliament’s jurisdiction over navigation and shipping. [7] [ 23 ] For such matters, an encroachment of the provincial power over Property and Civil Rights is justified and federal laws, including Canadian maritime law, apply. [8] [ 24 ] The test uniformly applied to determine whether Canadian maritime law applies is the following: Is the activity at stake so integrally connected to maritime matters such that it is practically necessary for Parliament to have jurisdiction over same, in order to properly exercise its legislative power over navigation and shipping ? [Emphasis added] [ 25 ] Such a line drawing exercise requires an examination of factual context of the claim. [9] The single fact that a ship or maritime undertakings are involved does not automatically trigger the application of Canadian maritime law. [10] For Canadian maritime law to apply, the matter, in view of the factual context, must be integrally connected to maritime matters. 4.1.3 Application to the present case [ 26 ] The current matter relates to the sale of a marine engine. [ 27 ] Issues relating to the obligations arising from such a contract of sale are not integrally connected to the pith and substance of the Parliament’s jurisdiction over navigation and shipping, as defined above [11] . [ 28 ] More particularly, the contract for the sale of a marine engine is not integrally connected, for instance, to issues of safe carriage of goods over the sea, movement of goods on and off a ship (shipping), seaworthiness of a ship or good seamanship (navigation).  It is also not integrally connected to applicable admiralty law, rules, principles or practices or international maritime conventions. [12] [ 29 ] Moreover, there is no practical necessity for uniform federal law to prescribe, for instance, the rules governing the seller’s obligations to provide warranty regarding the quality of the product sold.  The fact that such rules may vary depending on the applicable provincial law of contracts does no hinder the efficient and coherent conduct of the activities of navigation and shipping. [ 30 ] Neither the extraterritorial use of the good sold nor the fact that the good sold will be used in connection with maritime activities drag such sale out of the ambit of the legislature’s power over Property and Civil Rights.  Although related to maritime activities, the current dispute is not integrally connected with same. [ 31 ] Wärtsilä is sued as the seller of the crankshaft and bedplate.  It is not sued as a party performing a contract that pertains to the pith and substance of navigation and shipping activities. [ 32 ] As the contract for the sale of the engine and of the bedplate was formed in Montreal, [13] the laws of Quebec therefore apply to the present dispute. [14] 4.2 Based on such governing law, is Wärtsilä liable for the damages resulting from the engine breakdown? 4.2.1 General principles [ 33 ] They relevant provisions of the Civil Code of Quebec on the legal warranty against latent defects read as follows : III - Warranty of quality 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. […] 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but also to make reparation for the injury suffered by the buyer . 1729. In a sale by a professional seller, a defect is presumed to have existed at the time of the sale if the property malfunctions or deteriorates prematurely in comparison with identical property or property of the same type; such a presumption is rebutted if the defect is due to improper use of the property by the buyer . 1730. The manufacturer, any person who distributes the property under his name or as his own, and any supplier of the property, in particular the wholesaler and the importer, are also bound to warrant the buyer in the same manner as the seller . […] IV – Conventional warranty […] 1733. A seller may not exclude or limit his liability unless he has disclosed the defects of which he was aware or could not have been unaware and which affect the right of ownership or the quality of the property. An exception may be made to this rule where a buyer buys property at his own risk from a seller who is not a professional seller. [Emphasis added] [ 34 ] The legal warranty against latent defects thus comes into play if the defect: 1- was present at the time of the sale; 2- is serious; 3- was hidden and unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale. [ 35 ] In 2007 in Domtar, [15] the Supreme Court of Canada also reviewed the content and contours of the Quebec law on the legal warranty against latent defects, particularly in the case of a sale by a professional seller or a manufacturer.  These principles were subsequently reiterated and applied by the Quebec Court of Appeal. [16] [ 36 ] In the case of sale by a professional seller, two presumptions come into play. These presumptions also apply against the manufacturer and distributor of a good. [ 37 ] Firstly, the defect is presumed to have existed at the time of the sale (article 1729, C.C.Q.).  The first condition stated above is therefore automatically met; unless the seller or the manufacturer proves on the balance of probabilities that the defect is due to improper use by the buyer. [ 38 ] Secondly, the professional seller is presumed to have known the existence of the defect at the time of the sale and is deemed to be of bad faith. [17] [ 39 ] Professional sellers, by definition, are indeed presumably aware of the characteristics of their merchandise.  Manufacturers are also considered to be the ultimate experts with respect to the good sold. They have control over the labour and materials used to produce it. [18] [ 40 ] As the Supreme Court ruled in ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. [19] , the presumption of knowledge and of bad faith against the professional seller and the manufacturer has a two-fold determinative impact. [20] [ 41 ] Firstly, it extends the scope of their liability beyond the price of the good sold.  In addition to their obligation to reimburse the price of the good sold, they are bound to pay for the damages which the buyer sustained as a result of the latent defect. [ 42 ] Secondly, the professional seller and the manufacturer cannot rely on a limitation of liability clause, unless they rebut the presumption of knowledge and bad faith. As they are presumed to be of bad faith and to be aware of the existence of the defect, allowing them to contractually exclude or limit their liability for such defect would amount to an endorsement of a fraudulent behaviour. [21] [ 43 ] Therefore, only a narrow range of defences are available to the professional seller and the manufacturer if they want to rebut the presumption of knowledge and bad faith against them, and avoid the resulting legal consequences of such presumption. [ 44 ] Evidence of their good faith, of their ignorance of the defect or of their honest belief in the adequacy of the product sold is not enough. [22] [ 45 ] The buyer’s expertise also does not nullify the presumption of knowledge and bad faith which applies against professional sellers and manufacturers.  Such expertise is only relevant to assess whether the defect was apparent or not. [23] [ 46 ] According to the Supreme Court of Canada, the professional seller and the manufacturer can only rebut the presumption of knowledge and bad faith in two ways. They may demonstrate, on a balance of probabilities, that (1) the buyer or a third person caused the defect or that (2) only scientific or technological discoveries made after the good was put on the market would have permitted the defect to be detected at the time the good was put on the market. [72] Even though the possibility of manufacturers rebutting the presumption [(of knowledge)] is accepted, the high standard of diligence they are required to meet means that the range of defences available to them remains very narrow. Only two defences have been recognized so far , and C.E. has invoked neither of them. Under the first of these defences, a manufacturer can rebut the presumption by proving causal fault on the part of the buyer or a third person, or superior force : Manac inc./Nortex, at para. 138; Commentaires du ministre de la Justice, vol. 1, at p. 902. The second defence is that of development risk , but it is still subject to debate in contract matters. This defence enables the manufacturer to avoid liability if it would have been impossible to detect the defect given the state of scientific and technical knowledge at the time the good was put on the market . In such a case, only scientific or technological discoveries made after the good was put on the market will have permitted the defect to be detected. This defence originated in London & Lancashire Guarantee & Accident Co. of Canada v. Cie F.X. Drolet, 1944 CanLII 46 (SCC) , [1944] S.C.R. 82, and is now partially codified in respect of extra-contractual matters in art. 1473 C.C.Q. [24] [Emphasis added] 4.2.2 Application to the present case 4.2.2.1 Are the opening conditions of the legal warranty against latent defects met? [ 47 ] As stated above, the legal warranty against latent defects applies if the defect: 1- was present at the time of the sale; 2- is serious; and 3- was hidden and unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale. [ 48 ] The seriousness and hidden nature of the defect are unchallenged. [ 49 ] The engine failure was a...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\nphasis added] 4.2.2 Application to the present case 4.2.2.1 Are the opening conditions of the legal warranty against latent defects met? [ 47 ] As stated above, the legal warranty against latent defects applies if the defect: 1- was present at the time of the sale; 2- is serious; and 3- was hidden and unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale. [ 48 ] The seriousness and hidden nature of the defect are unchallenged. [ 49 ] The engine failure was a catastrophic event. Replacement of the entire engine was required as a result. [ 50 ] Also, if the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L was insufficiently tightened at the time of the sale, it was indisputably impossible for TDI to notice it. [ 51 ] The debate rather lies with the first condition:  was the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L insufficiently tightened at the time of the sale? [ 52 ] For the following reasons, an affirmative response to this question is warranted. 4.2.2.1.1 Evidence offered to rebut the presumption of existence of the defect at the time of the sale [ 53 ] Pursuant to sections 1729 and 1739 C.C.Q., as the crankshaft and bedplate assembly were sold by a professional seller, there is a presumption that such defect existed at the time of the sale.  Hence, the insufficient tightening of the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L presumably occurred while Wärtsilä employees mounted the crankshaft assembly in Wärtsilä facility in Zwolle, in November 2006. [ 54 ] Wärtsilä claims that such improper tightening results from maintenance work performed by TDI employees after the delivery of the crankshaft assembly; and that the presumption of existence of the defect at the time of the sale is therefore rebutted. 1729. In a sale by a professional seller, a defect is presumed to have existed at the time of the sale if the property malfunctions or deteriorates prematurely in comparison with identical property or property of the same type; such a presumption is rebutted if the defect is due to improper use of the property by the buyer . [Emphasis added] [ 55 ] To show improper maintenance, which is tantamount to improper use within the meaning of section 1729 C.C.Q., Wärtsilä offered the following factual evidence: 55.1. Deformations on the holes of several nuts Deformations were found on the holes of several nuts, [25] showing that significant force was applied, using a hammer against a tool inserted in the holes of the nuts, in attempts to loosen, tighten or test the tension of the nuts. Such manipulations would show the lack of skill and experience of the TDI maintenance crew; 55.2. Incidents in the course of the overhaul of cylinder units 1L and 2R in June 2009 In June 2009, during the overhaul of cylinder units 1L and 2R, [26] the engine room crew would have displayed incompetence, thereby requiring the presence of a senior technician of Wärtisilä, Mr. Hans Wyss, to assist on board. More particularly: 55.2.1. They used the instructions for line engines [27] while they should have followed the instructions for V engines. [28] As a result, they applied 750 bar of pressure, instead of the required 900 bar, to loosen the nuts on the big end studs of the units and were unable to release the nuts; 55.2.2. A small connecting rod of unit 1L was accidentally bent in the course of the operation, when the top part of the bearing was put in place. The bent stud was replaced before the overhaul job was completed; and 55.2.3. In the course of performing the job, the crew found out that they only had one set of smaller bearing shells, while they needed two. They discovered that the second set which they had was of regular size, i.e. 1 mm bigger than needed.  As a result, a new set of bearing shells was ordered and the work was completed after the receipt of same. [29] [ 56 ] The cumulative effect of these errors would show the lack of skill and care of the maintenance team on the Camilla Desgagnés. It would support Wärtsilä’s theory that the improper tightening of the nut on the big end stud of unit 5L results from improper manipulations by the TDI employees. [ 57 ] Wärtsilä also offered expert evidence from Mr. John Poulson, marine engineer, [30] and Mr. Joseph P. Crosson, metallurgical engineer, [31] to rebut the presumption of existence of improper tightening at the time of the sale. [ 58 ] To that effect, Mr. Crosson highlights that the cracks that led to the failure of the 5L big end stud originated from the surface, not from the interior, of the stud.  He further states that fracture initiation sites at the surface are compatible with failure occurring within a few thousands of hours (1,000 – 2,000 hours) [32] of operation after the improper tightening.  In the present case, the failure occurred after 13,653 hours of operation from the initial tightening of the stud.  Hence, the improper torque which led to the fracture cannot date back to the assembly of the engine.  It results from a more recent event, and is thus related to manipulations by TDI employees. [ 59 ] Mr. Poulson adds that improper tightening of such stud causes failure to happen within a relatively short period of time (within hundreds to 2,000 hours of operation). Improper tightening at the Wärtsilä facility in November 2006 is therefore not compatible with his knowledge and experience, as the stud would then have sustained stress from improper tightening for a much longer period, i.e. 13,653 hours of operation. [ 60 ] Mr. Poulson also is of the opinion that the procedure which Wärtsilä followed for the tightening of the studs was very comprehensive and thorough and that Wärtsilä employees complied with same. 4.2.2.1.2 The evidence nevertheless supports the presumption that the defect existed at the time of the sale [ 61 ] Despite the foregoing, the evidence does not show on a balance of probabilities that the improper tightening of the big stud on unit 5L results from manipulations carried on board by TDI employees, after the assembly of the crankshaft at Wärtsilä facility in Zwolle in November 2006. [ 62 ] Rebutting the presumption of article 1729 C.C.Q. indeed requires more than raising doubts on the adequacy of the buyer’s use or maintenance of the good sold. Unless the professional seller or manufacturer shows, on a balance of probabilities, that the defect is due to improper maintenance by the buyer, the presumption that the defect existed at the time of the sale still applies. [33] Such a demonstration may be made either by direct evidence or by presumption. [ 63 ] The evidence on which Wärtsilä relies raises legitimate scientific questioning on the timing of the incident and shows that TDI’s interventions were not perfect at all times and in all respects. However, it falls short of demonstrating that TDI employees have or probably have loosened the nut on the big end stud 5L. [ 64 ] The following findings indeed stem from the contradictory evidence offered and should not be disregarded or underestimated. [ 65 ] Firstly, the deformations of the holes of several nuts, including the nut on the big stud of unit 5L, indicate that TDI maintenance crew has inserted a tool in the holes of several nuts and hit such tool with a hammer in an attempt to tighten, loosen or check the tightness of the nuts.  This is an improper and useless maintenance practice. [ 66 ] All TDI maintenance crew members who were questioned in this regard [34] indicate that they never heard of such a method and never witnessed it on board the Camilla Desgagnés.  This was not the manner in which TDI employees checked the tightness of the nuts.  In this regard, they followed the recommended Wärtsilä method, i.e. sound test when hitting the nut itself with a small hammer. [ 67 ] More importantly, ordinary and expert witnesses [35] unanimously stated that it is impossible to loosen or remove a nut without using hydraulic equipment. Hence, no matter how unusual and improper it may be to hit a tool inserted in the hole of a nut, such a manipulation is useless and has no impact on the torque value of the nuts.  Also, Wärtsilä does not recommend using hydraulic tools during crankcase inspections and to check the tightness of the nuts and TDI acted along this recommendation. [ 68 ] Secondly, the three problems which Wärtsilä raises in connection with the overhaul of units 1L and 2R in June 2009 are immaterial to the issue of interest here: did the TDI employees loosen the nut on the big end stud of unit 5L? [ 69 ] Admittedly, the TDI employees were initially unable to loosen the nuts.  As they were using the instructions for a line engine, and not for a V engine, they were applying insufficient hydraulic pressure (750 bars instead of 900).  In any event, as a result of that mistake, they were unable to loosen the nuts. [ 70 ] Also, the TDI maintenance crew has, in the course of this overhaul, damaged a small stud.  In planning the work, they also overlooked the fact that the second replacement kit of bearing shells which they had on board was fit for the old engine, and thus 1 mm oversized. [ 71 ] These facts also fall short of demonstrating that the TDI employees have or may have wrongfully loosen the nut on big end stud of unit 5L. [ 72 ] Actually, the overhaul performed on unit 1L and 2R was a significant operation. It required several days of work. The fact that the process was not uneventful and the nature of the problems which were encountered in the present case do not show that TDI employees lacked the skill and knowledge to properly maintain and repair the engine on the Camilla Desgagnés. [ 73 ] Moreover, all the work performed during this overhaul was done on units 1L and 2R. Any suggestion to the effect that the manipulations would have accidentally or mistakenly extended to unit 5L is speculative and fanciful. [ 74 ] The evidence on the maintenance procedures which TDI applied on board [36] also does not show that TDI inappropriately maintained the engine and would have inadvertently or negligently loosened the nut on the big end stud of unit 5L. [ 75 ] Also, a service bulletin confirms that studs which are appropriately pre stressed during the initial assembly may nevertheless loosen with time, even in the absence of faulty subsequent manipulations or improper maintenance by the buyer [37] . [ 76 ] Finally, TDI offered convincing expert evidence to show that the defect may very well have existed at the time of the sale. [ 77 ] More particularly, Mrs. Marina Banuta, engineer expert specialized in failure analysis and mechanical breakdowns [38] , states that the findings in this case are compatible with insufficient original tightening, at the time of the assembly of the crankshaft at Wärtsilä facility. [ 78 ] She highlights that the performance and resistance of materials, as well as the technological means of submitting such material to increasing numbers of stress cycles, have significantly progressed in the recent years or decades.  She convincingly explains that as a result, the scientific knowledge on failures occurring after 10 7 stress cycles is relatively recent and that the literature in this regard should therefore not be blindly relied on. [ 79 ] For instance, the existence of a surface fracture initiation site, as opposed to an internal initiation site, would not be incompatible with the failure occurring, such as in this case, after 2.17 x 10 8 stress cycles. She even worked on a case where the failure occurred after 10 7 stress cycles, and where the fracture initiation site was at the surface. [ 80 ] In any event internal inclusions, which Mr. Crosson claims [39] are a sine qua non condition to a finding of failure after10 7 stress cycles, were also found in the Camilla Desgagnés’ specimens which Mrs. Banuta reviewed. [40] [ 81 ] That being said, Mrs. Banuta also makes it clear that the fact that the big end stud of unit 5L complied with all applicable standards of quality, in spite of its several imperfections, is irrelevant. Such imperfections are not defects. However, the improper tightening of the nut on the big end stud of unit 5L, which is a defect, created a stress which turned these acceptable imperfections into the source of the eventual failure. [ 82 ] Mrs. Banuta also explains that the nut was probably not sufficiently tightened (improper tightening) initially.  She rejects the possibility that the nut was not tightened (lack of tightening) initially, as the failure would have occurred much more rapidly in such event. [ 83 ] Mrs. Banuta’s experience is vast, undisputable, highly reliable and relevant to the expert issues in this case.  Wärtsilä also participated in the elaboration of the testing protocol which she followed for the metallurgical investigation conducted after the failure of the engine on the Camilla Desgagnés.  Wärtsilä experts fully relied on the result of her investigations. [ 84 ] The Court holds, based on Mrs. Banuta’s testimony, that the clear-cut opinion of Mr. Crosson on the absence, in this case, of an internal initiation site and of the resulting conclusion that the failure cannot result from improper original tightening, must be considerably nuanced. [ 85 ] Also, the testimony of Mr. Poulson, the other expert of the Defence, should be considered with great caution.  He heavily relied on a staged presentation which Wärtsilä had organized for his benefit, on unverified and on partial information provided by Wärtsilä to make categorical statements which were systematically favourable to the Defence. Greater independence of the expert would probably have better served the Court. [ 86 ] There is thus no probative evidence, be it direct or by presumption, that TDI employees loosened the nut on the big stud of the 5L unit.  The presumption that the defect existed at the time of the sale still applies. [ 87 ] Furthermore, such presumption is reinforced by the flaws demonstrated in the procedure which Wärtsilä followed for the assembly of the crankshaft in November 2006. [ 88 ] More particularly, Wärtsilä had no quality control was in place in Zwolle to ensure that the assembly of the crankshaft was done in accordance with its instruction and with the applicable standards.  Also, no one inspected the bedplate and crankshaft at the end of the assembly. [ 89 ] Mr. Han Noorlander, senior service engineer, was responsible for the assembly of the crankshaft and bedplate at Wärtsilä facility in Zwolle in November 2006. Mr. President, mechanics, and Mr. Kleingeld, ingeneer, worked under Mr. Noorlander’s supervision for this project. [ 90 ] The entire work for this assembly lasted from November 13, 2006 to November 28, 2006. [ 91 ] In addition to supervising the work of Mr. Kleingeld and Mr. President, Mr. Noorlander himself worked directly on the assembly of the bedplate and crankshaft. He was the supervisor of his own work. [ 92 ] Mr. Noorlander himself did not inspect the bedplate and crankshaft at the end of the assembly.  Mr. Noorlander finished his work on November 24, 2006 and did not return to the Zwolle facility after. After the departure of Mr. Noorlander, Mr. Kleingeld nevertheless worked two additional days on the assembly, on November 27 and 28, 2006.  No one supervised his work. [ 93 ] The 12 connecting rods, which each have 2 big end studs such as the one that failed, were assembled on November 21 and 22, 2005 [41] by Mr. Noorlander and Mr. Kleingeld.  This represents 24 (12 x 2) big end studs to stretch and tension with nuts.  To that end, the use of hydraulic tolls along a strict step-by-step protocol [42] had to be applied for each of the 24 big studs. [ 94 ] No checklist of the procedure was kept or followed.  Wärtsilä’s service report, [43] which is the only document concerning the work performed on the assembly of the crankshaft and bedplate, does not (1) identify the parts used in the assembly, (2) describe how the parts were mounted, (3) identify the instructions which were followed to assemble the crankshaft, (4) identify which verifications were done in the course of the assembly, and (5) indentify which approval was done of the assembly after the work was completed. [ 95 ] The assembly of the connecting rod was done during the period of time where Mr. Noorlandar worked 10 hours a day for three consecutive days (with a two-hour drive in the morning and a two-hour drive at the end of the working day). [ 96 ] In this overall context, given the series of consecutive precise manipulations necessary to make in order to stretch and tension the big studs, a step may have been missed or overlooked when tensioning one of the big studs on unit 5L. [ 97 ] The service report also includes inaccuracies which illustrate, with all due respect for Mr. Noorlander, how lenient Wärtsilä was in the planning, supervision and conduct of this work. [ 98 ] This improper torque of the nut of the big stud on\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n96 ] In this overall context, given the series of consecutive precise manipulations necessary to make in order to stretch and tension the big studs, a step may have been missed or overlooked when tensioning one of the big studs on unit 5L. [ 97 ] The service report also includes inaccuracies which illustrate, with all due respect for Mr. Noorlander, how lenient Wärtsilä was in the planning, supervision and conduct of this work. [ 98 ] This improper torque of the nut of the big stud on unit 5L was therefore present at the time of the sale. 4.2.2.2 Are the contractual limitation clauses applicable? [ 99 ] The contract which the parties entered into incorporates Wärtsilä’s standard terms and conditions for spare parts. [44] Section 6.3 of such standard terms and conditions, as modified at TDI’s request, provide that the warranty runs for 6 months from the time the Camilla Desgagnés returned to service [45] . They also limit Wärtsilä’s liability to € 50,000 (CAN $ 79,000). [46] [ 100 ] However, these limitation provisions are not enforceable in the case at hand, as Wärtsilä did not rebut the presumption of knowledge of the existence of the defect. [ 101 ] As mentioned above, [47] Wärtsilä’s bad faith and knowledge of the existence of the defect at the time of the sale is presumed.  In this context, any contractual exclusion or limitation of its liability for latent defect is considered fraudulent.  In principle, Wärtsilä is thus not authorized to contractually limit its obligations in respect of latent defects. [ 102 ] Neither of the two defences which the manufacturer can raise in such instances [48] to claim the benefit of warranty limitation provisions was demonstrated. [ 103 ] No such demonstration was made. [ 104 ] Wärtsilä is thus liable for the defect in the crankshaft and bedplate assembly which it delivered to TDI in February 2007 and for all the related damages. [ 105 ] Quantum is admitted at $ 5,661,830.33. 4.3 Wärtsilä’s Cross-Demand [ 106 ] TDI has no obligation to pay for expenses which Wärtsilä incurred in relation to a latent defect which Wärtsilä presumably knew existed at the time it sold the bedplate and crankshaft assembly to TDI and eventually caused the complete loss of the material sold, in addition to other damages. [ 107 ] The Cross-Demand shall thus be dismissed. WHEREFORE THE COURT: [ 108 ] GRANTS Plaintiffs’ Motion; [ 109 ] ORDERS the Defendants jointly and severally to pay to the Plaintiffs the sum of $ 5,661,830.33 together with interest and additional indemnity from October 27, 2009; [ 110 ] THE WHOLE with costs, including the costs of expertise. __________________________________ MARIE-ANNE PAQUETTE, S.C.J. Me Danièle Dion Me Victor De Marco BRISSET BISHOP Attorney for the Plaintiffs Me George Pollack Me Vincent Boutet-Lehouillier DAVIS WARD PHILLIPS & VINEBERG LLP Attorney for the Defendants Hearing dates: September 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 21, 22, 24, 25, 28 and 29, 2015 TABLE OF CONTENTS OVERVIEW............................................................................................................................... 1 1. ADMISSIONS AND UNCHALLENGED EVIDENCE................................................. 2 2. POSITION OF THE PARTIES........................................................................................ 2 3. ISSUES IN DISPUTE...................................................................................................... 3 4. ANALYSIS......................................................................................................................... 3 4.1 Does Canadian maritime law or Quebec civil law govern the current dispute?          3 4.1.1 What is Canadian maritime law?............................................. 3 4.1.2 When does Canadian maritime law apply?........................... 4 4.1.3 Application to the present case................................................ 5 4.2 Based on such governing law, is Wärtsilä liable for the damages resulting from the engine breakdown?......................................................................................................................... 6 4.2.1 General principles...................................................................... 6 4.2.2 Application to the present case................................................ 9 4.2.2.1 Are the opening conditions of the legal warranty against latent defects met?                                                                                                        9 4.2.2.1.1 Evidence offered to rebut the presumption of existence of the defect at the time of the sale     9 4.2.2.1.2 The evidence nevertheless supports the presumption that the defect existed at the time of the sale....................................................................................................... 11 4.2.2.2 Are the contractual limitation clauses applicable?............. 16 4.3 ........ Wärtsilä’s Cross-Demand................................................................................. 17 WHEREFORE THE COURT:............................................................................................... 17 [1] Whitbread v. Walley , 1990 CanLII 33 (SCC) , [1990] 3 S.C.R. 1273, 1294-1296. [2] Admiralty Act, 1934 , 1934 (Can.), c. 31, s. 18. [3] ITO – Int’l Terminal Operators v Miida Electronics, 1986 CanLII 91 (SCC) , [1986] 1 S.C.R. 752, 770-771; British Columbia Marine Shipbuilders Ltd. v F.M. Yorke & Son Ltd., 1981 CanLII 182 (SCC) , [1981] 1 S.C.R. 363, par. 19 . [4] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 20; ITO – Int’l Terminal Operators v Miida Electronics, 1986 CanLII 91 (SCC) , [1986] 1 S.C.R. 752, 774. [5] 9171-7701 Québec inc. (Les Surplus JT) v. Her majesty the Queen , [2013] FC 832, par. 33, 44. [6] Whitbread v. Walley , 1990 CanLII 33 (SCC) , [1990] 3 S.C.R. 1273, 1295. [7] Marine Services v. Ryan Estate, [2013] 53, par. 61; Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 25. [8] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 24, 28; Monk Corp v. Island Fertilizers Ltd , 1991 CanLII 95 (SCC) , [1991] 1 S.C.R. 779, 795, 797, 800; ITO – Int’l Terminal Operators v Miida Electronics, 1986 CanLII 91 (SCC) , [1986] 1 S.C.R. 752, 774. [9] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 21; Ordon Estate v. Grail , 1998 CanLII 771 (SCC) , [1998] 3 S.C.R. 437, par. 73 .. [10] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 26; Monk Corp v. Island Fertilizers Ltd , 1991 CanLII 95 (SCC) , [1991] 1 S.C.R. 779, 797. [11] See par. [22] of the present judgment. [12] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, pars. 22, 28. [13] Exhibits P-2, P-15, P-19. [14] Article 1387 C.C.Q. [15] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461. [16] Entreprises d'électricité Rial inc. v. Lumen, division de Sonepar Canada inc. , 2010 QCCA 655 , pars. 31-44; Ferme avicole Héva inc. v. Coopérative fédérée de Québec, 2008 QCCA 1053 , pars. 73-75, 82-87; Fédération, compagnie d'assurances du Canada v. Joseph Élie ltée , 2008 QCCA 582 , pars. 23-27. [17] Article 1728 C.C.Q. [18] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 40-41, 44, 59, 60. [19] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461. [20] Articles 1728, 1733, C.C.Q.; ABB Inc. v Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 56, 106. [21] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 72, 73, 102. [22] Id. , pars. 69, 102, 105. [23] Id., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 42, 44. [24] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 69, 72. [25] Exhibits P-139, P-149, P-150, D-127A, D-127B. [26] Exhibit D-65 (P-25), P-77C. [27] Exhibit D-58. [28] Exhibit D-57. [29] Exhibits P-64, P-153, U-1. [30] Exhibit D-155. [31] Exhibit D-142. [32] This estimation is based on the calculation of the number of stress cycles which occur during an hour of operation of the engine at stake. Mr. Crosson’s calculations to convert the number of stress cycles into hours of operation is unchallenged. [33] Fédération, compagnie d'assurances du Canada v. Joseph Élie ltée , 2008 QCCA 582 , pars. 31, 36-37. [34] Mr. Ruslan Shylko, M. Patrick Chabot. [35] Mr. Ruslan Shylko, Mrs Marina Banuta, Mr. Poulson, Mr. Crosson. [36] Exhibits P-12, P-59, P-60, P-62, P-75, P-77, P-77A, P-133. [37] Exhibit-38B. [38] Exhibits P-38, P-38A. [39] Submission based, inter alia on scientific article by Mr. Claude Bathias,Claude BATHIAS and Paul C. PARIS, Gigacycle Fatigue in mechanical practice , New York, Marcel Dekker, p. 93, figure 4.4. [40] For instance, see Exhibit P-38, fig. 37. [41] Exhibits D-18, D-38. [42] Tension is to be applied the following way and in the following order for each of the 24 big studs: (1) 250 bars of pressure, (2) down to zero, (3) 900 bars of pressure, (4) down to zero, (5) 900 bars of pressure, (6) down to zero, (7) 900 bars of pressure, (8) down to zero. [43] Exhibit D-38. [44] Exhibit D-1. [45] Exhibits P-2, item E); D-3. [46] Exhibit D-133. [47] See par. [42] of the present judgment. [48] See par. [46] of the present judgment.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03007688745856285, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\", \"title\": \"Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc., 2015 QCCS 5514 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.03007688745856285, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\"}, \"token_count\": 10716, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Kalimidis c. Tao, 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII) | Kalimidis c. Tao | CanLII Kalimidis c. Tao, 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII) Date: 2018-06-07 File number: 500-32-153201-167 Citation: Kalimidis c. Tao, 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/hsqth > Kalimidis c. Tao 2018 QCCQ 3811 COURT OF QUÉBEC (Small Claims Division) CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32-153201-167 DATE: June 7, 2018 ______________________________________________________________________ BEFORE THE HONOURABLE DOMINIQUE GIBBENS, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ ATHANASIOS KALIMIDIS -and- helen kalimidis-kluger Plaintiffs v. tao tao -and- xinzhi he Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Plaintiffs claim $15,000 from Defendants, from whom they purchased a duplex in 2011. They claim that Defendants falsely represented that water infiltrations in the basement had been resolved by the installation of a French drain. The system installed, however, was not a French drain, and it did not resolve the infiltration issue. [ 2 ] The amount claimed represents the cost incurred by Plaintiffs to install a French drain ($8,761.23) and eliminate mold ($4,254.08), as well as damages ($1,984.69). [ 3 ] Defendants deny any liability towards Plaintiffs. They admit their mistake in describing the work completed in 2008 to remedy the water infiltration issue, but claim that the documentation provided to Plaintiff prior to the purchase of the property accurately described the work. They also argue that the claim must be dismissed because Plaintiffs did not notify them of the alleged defect or put them in default prior to completing corrective work on the property. FACTUAL CONTEXT [ 4 ] On February 26, 2011, Plaintiffs purchased from Defendants a duplex located on West Hill Avenue in Montréal (the “ Property ”) for a price of $408,000. [1] The Property, a row house with adjoining properties on both sides, includes a basement rental unit which had been occupied by the same tenant since 2004. [ 5 ] Prior to the sale, Defendants completed the standard form entitled Declarations by the seller of the immovable , [2] in which they stated that there had been water infiltrations in the past in the basement or elsewhere, but that these “[…] ha[d] been fixed with French drain ”. [3] Defendants also mentioned the installation of the French drain in 2008 as a major improvement to the Property. [4] [ 6 ] These statements were inaccurate, in that Defendants did not install a French drain in 2008. They retained 2896788 Canada Inc. (doing business under the name of BWN (“ BWN ”)) to “ waterproof subsoil masonry walls of the property ” by installing a drainage system on the inside of the Property, comprised of perforated pipes installed in the concrete floor of the basement (along the back and North walls) and connected to a sump pump. [5] This is different from a French drain, which is located on the outside perimeter of a property. [6] [ 7 ] The evidence shows that Defendants did not intentionally mislead Plaintiffs, as they believed that the system installed was a “French drain”. They provided the BWN contract, which was communicated to Plaintiffs’ real estate broker before Plaintiffs decided to purchase the Property. [7] [ 8 ] In 2012, when the basement-unit tenant moved out, Plaintiffs discovered what they believed to be mold on the walls in the washroom and kitchen of that unit. They believed at the time that this was the result of past water infiltrations, before the installation of a French drain. Humidity and what appeared to be mold were again noted the following year, when the subsequent tenant moved out. [ 9 ] In the fall of 2014, Plaintiffs retained a foundation expert, Les Goudrons du Québec (“ Goudrons ”), to determine the cause of the apparent mold and humidity. They were advised that contrary to what Defendants had stated, no French drain had been installed and water infiltrations were ongoing at the back of the Property. [ 10 ] In October 2014, Plaintiffs excavated the foundation at the back of the Property, installed a waterproofing membrane on the exterior of the foundation wall, and installed a French drain, at a total cost of $8,761.23. [8] [ 11 ] Afterwards, in November 2014, Plaintiffs notified Defendants of the absence of a French drain and ongoing water infiltration, summoning them to assess the damages themselves without mentioning that corrective work had already been completed. [9] [ 12 ] Shortly after receiving this letter of demand, Defendants went over to the Property and saw that corrective work had been carried out. After that, several exchanges took place between the parties. [10] Defendants provided Plaintiffs’ lawyer with a copy of the BWN contract [11] and BWN was approached on the basis of the conventional guarantee granted to Defendants. BWN denied liability, however, because it was not advised in a timely manner of the change of ownership as required by the contract. [ 13 ] Defendants were given the opportunity to assess the damage on the inside of the Property and they were provided with an estimate of the interior work required to fix the water infiltration damage. [12] The interior work was completed in March 2015 at a total cost of $4,254.08. [13] ANALYSIS a) Do Plaintiffs have a recourse based on the legal warranty of quality (warranty against latent defects)? [ 14 ] Plaintiffs’ claim calls into question, firstly, the legal warranty of quality to which sellers are bound pursuant to Articles 1726 and following of the Civil Code of Québec (the “ C.C.Q. ”). [ 15 ] Under these provisions, the seller must guarantee to the buyer that the property sold is free of latent defects that render it unfit for its intended purpose or significantly diminish its usefulness. The warranty does not extend to latent defects known to the buyer or to apparent defects (i.e., defects that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the assistance of an expert). [14] [ 16 ] A vendor is held to this warranty whether or not he is aware of the defect. If he is, his liability is greater: in addition to the cost of the work needed to correct the defect, the vendor is liable for the damages suffered by the purchaser. [15] [ 17 ] To succeed on a claim based on latent defects, a purchaser must prove, on a balance of probabilities, [16] that the property was, at the time of the sale, affected by a defect that was serious , hidden and unknown to the purchaser . [ 18 ] The purchaser must also demonstrate that he gave notice of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time of discovering it [17] and that the seller was given the opportunity to remedy the defect. [18] [ 19 ] In addition, the purchaser must place the seller in default of executing his obligation of warranty. [19] A letter of demand must be given before corrective work is carried out, unless it is possible to demonstrate that the seller was in default by the effect of the law. [20] [ 20 ] The Court concludes that these conditions are not met in the instant case, for the following reasons. [ 21 ] The absence of a French drain does not, in and of itself, constitute a latent defect that can give rise to liability under the legal warranty of quality. Many properties do not have French drains and have no water infiltration issues. [ 22 ] The evidence shows, however, that when Plaintiffs purchased the Property in 2011, water infiltration was ongoing through the back foundation wall. This constitutes a serious defect, which was hidden and unknown to Plaintiffs. Although the pre-purchase inspection revealed high levels of humidity in the basement, this problem was associated to the absence of a fan; [21] there were no visible signs of ongoing water infiltrations at the time. Moreover, Defendants’ representation that past infiltrations had been resolved by the installation of a French drain understandably reassured Plaintiffs that all was resolved. [ 23 ] Plaintiffs did not, however, give notice to the Defendants of the defect within a reasonable time of discovering it and, moreover, they proceeded to corrective work without giving them the opportunity of remedying the situation. This is fatal to their claim from the point of view of the warranty against hidden defects. [ 24 ] Notification of a defect within a reasonable time is intended to allow the seller to verify the existence and seriousness of the alleged defect and to correct it and it is a substantive condition of the implementation of the warranty. [22] Failure to give notice within a reasonable time – and before proceeding to corrective work – is fatal to a claim based on the warranty of quality, except where there is urgency or where the failure to give notice does not prejudice the seller’s rights. [23] [ 25 ] Plaintiffs realized or should have realized long before 2014 that there was a serious infiltration problem in the basement. Mr. Kalimidis discovered what he believed to be mold in the basement unit in 2012 and again in 2013. From then on, he could certainly suspect that there was a serious problem and should have given notice to Defendants without delay, as required by article 1739 C.C.Q.: 1739. A buyer who ascertains that the property is defective shall give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it. Where the defect appears gradually, the time begins to run on the day that the buyer could suspect the seriousness and extent of the defect . The seller may not invoke the tardiness of a notice from the buyer if he was aware of the defect or could not have been unaware of it. [Emphasis added] [ 26 ] Yet, Plaintiffs waited until November 2014 before giving notice to the Defendants and putting them in demand and by then, they had already installed a French drain. On the facts, there was no urgency to act and Defendants were not in default by the effect of the law. Defendants were therefore deprived of the opportunity of remedying the situation. [ 27 ] In view of the foregoing, the Court concludes that Plaintiffs have no recourse against Defendants based on the legal warranty of quality (warranty against latent defects). b) Do Plaintiffs have a recourse based on a breach of Defendants’ obligation of delivery given the inaccurate representation that a French drain had been installed? [ 28 ] Defendants’ potential liability must also be considered in light of their obligation of delivery as sellers as provided in Articles 1616 and 1717 C.C.Q. [ 29 ] Under these provisions, a seller must ensure that the property he or she delivers strictly complies with what was described or represented and what the purchaser agreed to. [24] If it does not, the seller is liable, irrespective of whether he or she was aware of the non-conformity. The purchaser can then obtain a reduction of the price and damages. [25] [ 30 ] It the instant case, Plaintiffs have established that the Property is not as it was described and represented by Defendants in that a French drain was not installed in 2008. This is a violation of Defendants’ obligation of delivery that can give rise to liability and provide a recourse against them. [ 31 ] To succeed on such a recourse, however, a purchaser must place the seller in default and give him the opportunity to remedy such default prior to proceeding to any corrective work. [ 32 ] Here, Plaintiffs did not place Defendants in default before installing the French drain. Their recourse must therefore fail. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the action; WITH LEGAL COSTS of $200 representing the judicial stamp on the Contestation. __________________________________ DOMINIQUE GIBBENS, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: February 26, 2018 [1] Exhibit D-2. [2] Exhibit D-1. [3] Exhibit D-1, clause D3.1. [4] Exhibit D-1, clause D15. [5] Contract between Defendants and BWN (Exhibit D-3). [6] Letter of Alain Ricard of Les Goudrons du Québec dated December 16, 2014 (Exhibit P-7). [7] Affidavit in lieu of testimony of Mrs. Marie-Claire LePessec dated November 17, 2011 and Exhibit D-3A. [8] Les Goudrons du Québec invoice (Exhibit P-3). [9] Letter of demand dated November 13, 2014 (Exhibit P-1). [10] Exhibit P-2. [11] Email of November 26, 2014 (Exhibit P-2, p. 2). [12] Email of December 31, 2014 (Exhibit P-2, p. 3). [13] Invoice of Dominovo inc. (Exhibit P-4). [14] Article 1726 C.C.Q. [15] Article 1728 C.C.Q. [16] As required by articles 2803-2804 C.C.Q. [17] Article 1739 C.C.Q. [18] Article 1590 C.C.Q. [19] Articles 1590 and 1594 C.C.Q. See also Claude Joyal inc. v. CNH Canada Ltd. , 2014 QCCA 588 , para. 40 to 45 . [20] Jeffrey EDWARDS , La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit Québécois , 2e Éd., Montréal, Wilson & Lafleur, 2008, para. 420, p. 203 to 205. [21] Exhibit P-8, p. 56. [22] Claude Joyal inc. v. CNH Canada Ltd. , 2014 QCCA 588 , para. 22 to 30 . [23] Id. , para. 31 to 36. See also Immeubles Bernadet inc. v. Kerzérho , 2015 QCCA 644 , para. 13 . [24] Pierre-Gabriel JOBIN et Michelle CUMYN, La vente , 4 th ed., Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2017, n o 107, at pages 139-140. See also Labrie v. Vanasse , 2005 QCCA 52 , para. 7-9 . [25] Munger v. Beaudoin , 2008 QCCQ 9315 , para. 64-65 ; Ouellette v. Giroux , 2009 QCCQ 3951 , para. 29 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02853124588727951, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\", \"title\": \"Kalimidis c. Tao, 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02853124588727951, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\"}, \"token_count\": 3353, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Waters c. Passmore, 2019 QCCS 3805 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2019 QCCS 3805 (CanLII) | Waters c. Passmore | CanLII Date: 2019-09-06 File number: 550-17-008706-150 Citation: Waters c. Passmore, 2019 QCCS 3805 (CanLII) Waters c. Passmore 2019 QCCS 3805 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF GATINEAU No.: 550-17-008706-150 DATE: September 6, 2019 ______________________________________________________________________ PRESIDING: THE HONOURABLE DOMINIQUE GOULET, J.S.C. ______________________________________________________________________ KAREN WATERS JONATHAN GOODMAN Plaintiffs v. VANESSA PASSMORE ROBERT WAUGH HENDRIK HYMANS (A TO Z HOME INSPECTIONS) ANN-MARIE BABIN THOMAS E. KEELY Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ Procedural Context [ 1 ] On November 24, 2015, the plaintiffs filed an application seeking $158,600.64 [1] from the defendants Passmore and Waugh arising from an action for the reduction of the purchase price of a residence, damages and latent defects. [ 2 ] The action also concerns pre-purchase inspector Hendrik Hymans. [ 3 ] On or about March 9, 2016, the defendants Passmore and Waugh made an assignment in bankruptcy. [ 4 ] Following the bankruptcy, on September 16, 2016, the plaintiffs amended their applications to add two new defendants, Ann-Marie Babin and Thomas E. Keely, who sold the property at issue to the defendants Passmore and Waugh in 2008. [2] [ 5 ] The application was also amended at that time to increase the amount claimed to $182,227.80. [3] [ 6 ] An application for authorization to continue the proceedings despite the bankruptcy was filed on September 21, 2016. That application was granted on November 23, 2016. [ 7 ] Pursuant to that application, the plaintiffs sought authorization to continue the proceedings in order to establish that, at the time of the sale, the bankrupt individuals had knowledge of the defects and that they made fraudulent misrepresentations so the debt would be declared non-dischargeable within the meaning of section 178 of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act . [4] [ 8 ] On or about April 17, 2017, the action was again amended to ask the Court to declare that the debt was not extinguished under section 178 of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act and to vary the quantum to $155,981.58. [5] [ 9 ] In or about April 2018, a partial settlement of the dispute was reached with the inspector pursuant to which $23,000 was paid to the plaintiffs. The agreement was made without any admission of liability. No amendment to the action was filed at the hearing to take this amount into consideration. [ 10 ] That is the procedural context. [ 11 ] The Court will now summarize the facts and then present the issues in dispute. Finally, the Court will conclude by setting out the positions of the parties. The final segment will also include the arguments of the parties. The facts [ 12 ] In or about June 2014, Mr. Goodman’s [6] employer informed the plaintiffs that they would be transferred to Ottawa in August 2014. [ 13 ] Faced with this short notice, they went to the Outaouais region in July 2014 to visit properties and buy one. They visited 12 to 15 residences during that period. [ 14 ] On July 4, 2014, [7] they made an offer to purchase the property located at 25 Xavier in Gatineau. [8] The residence was built in approximately 1962. [9] [ 15 ] The offer to purchase was conditional on, among other things, an inspection to be performed by a professional within 10 days. [10] [ 16 ] On July 6, 2014, the plaintiffs accompanied Mr. Hymans for the inspection. Subsequently, a report [11] was prepared. [ 17 ] The report provides a description of the residence and several recommendations, including repairing the damaged roof and chimneys. [12] [ 18 ] On the basis of this inspection, on July 10, 2014, the plaintiffs signed a document titled “notice and follow-up on fulfilment of conditions-immovable.” [13] [ 19 ] It is worth reproducing certain excerpts from this document, i.e., AV4.3, AV4.4 and AV4.5. AV4.3 IN ACCORDANCE WITH CLAUSE 8.1 OF THE PROMISE TO PURCHASE FORM IDENTIFIED ABOVE, NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN THAT: – The BUYER has had the IMMOVABLE inspected by a building inspector or a professional, he has read the inspection report and declares himself satisfied with the inspection. Consequently, this condition is fulfilled. AV4.4 IN ACCORDANCE WITH CLAUSE 9.1 OF THE PROMISE TO PURCHASE FORM IDENTIFIED ABOVE, NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN THAT: – The BUYER has examined and verified the documents listed under this clause and declares himself satisfied therewith. Consequently, this condition is fulfilled. AV5 OTHERS: -  CLAUSE 6.2 (FINANCING) THE CONDITION IS FULFILLED. ATTACH A COPY OF THE APPROVAL OF THE BANK. THE VENDOR HAS AGREED TO REPAIR A PORTION OF THE ROOF AS IDENTIFIED IN THE BUILDING INSPECTION REPORT AS WELL THE MASONRY OF BOTH CHIMNEYS ALSO AS IDENTIFIED IN THE BUILDING INSPECTION REPORT. THE WORK SHALL BE DONE BY A PROFESSIONNAL AT THE SELLER’S EXPENSE ANS SHALL PROVIDE A COPY OF PROOF OF SAID REPAIRS BEING COMPLETED WITH THE CONTACT INFORMATION FOR FUTURE REFERENCE PROVEDED. WORK SHALL BE COMPLETED PRIOR BUYER TAKING POSSESSION OF THE PROPERTY IF NOT COMPLETED A AMOUNT OF MONEY WILL BE HELD AT THE NOTARY UNTIL THE WORK IS COMPLETED. [ 20 ] In late July 2014, the plaintiffs received an email from their real estate agent along with photographs showing that the work requested had been performed. [ 21 ] It should be noted that the defendants Passmore and Waugh retained a licensed contractor to install a new membrane on the roof and a mason to repair the chimneys. [ 22 ] On August 8, 2014, the plaintiffs purchased the residence at issue in this dispute for $237,000. [ 23 ] It is worth mentioning that no discussions were ever held between the buyers and the sellers Passmore and Waugh. [ 24 ] The plaintiff Waters stated that after the purchase, she noticed the presence of mouse droppings. She installed traps and caught some twenty mice over time. It seems that this problem did not reoccur. In 2015, the plaintiff also noticed the presence of carpenter ants. [ 25 ] In the spring of 2015, the plaintiffs began landscaping work, in particular to change some of the patio slabs on the entrance walkway on the right side. [14] [ 26 ] While removing the patio slabs, the contractor noticed that the foundation made of concrete blocks had deteriorated. [ 27 ] The plaintiffs therefore retained an expert engineer, Pierre Beaupré. [ 28 ] The expert went on site and confirmed that foundation was in a state of deterioration. [ 29 ] The foundation was located under the demarcation line where the parging of the former walkway ended. [ 30 ] Using a bricklayer’s hammer, the expert noted that the front section on the right side of the building was damaged and that there were several deformations. [ 31 ] The expert also performed several hammering tests on the rest of the foundation’s perimeter and noted that the surface of the blocks was not crumbling, leading him to conclude that the problem detected was located in the section near the walkway. [ 32 ] Without restating the report in detail, since the Court will return to it later in its analysis, it should be noted that the expert considered the causes of this deterioration and concluded, further to spray tests performed on the roof, that water was flowing through the soffit directly into the excavation, i.e., the former walkway. [ 33 ] The expert stated that he was told by the plaintiff Waters that during the winter, the flow of water caused major accumulations of ice. [ 34 ] The expert said he was of the view that these accumulations of ice led to the use of calcium and de-icing products, which significantly affected the resistance of the concrete blocks. [ 35 ] He stated the following in summary with respect to the cause: L’application de produits de déverglaçage à cet endroit explique la détérioration localisée des fondations [15] . [ 36 ] In this context, on April 17, 2015, the plaintiffs sent the defendants Passmore and Waugh a formal notice of the defects noted. [ 37 ] It should be noted that upon receipt of the formal notice, the defendants Passmore and Waugh sent the defendants Babin and Keely a letter informing them of the defects alleged at that time. The plaintiffs did not send those defendants a formal notice. [ 38 ] In the fall of 2015, work was performed on the residence, and the plaintiffs allege that additional defects were discovered, affecting the plumbing and electricity. The plaintiffs also allege that they found mould. [ 39 ] As a result, according to them, significant work was required, hence the action in damages amended in 2017 to claim the amount of $155,981.58 in regard to an immovable that they purchased for $230,000. Issues [ 40 ] Because of the bankruptcy, the Court proposes to first consider the liability of the defendants Babin and Keely. To that end, it will be necessary to determine whether the latent defects existed at the time of the sale to the defendants Passmore and Waugh in 2008. [ 41 ] Next, it will be necessary to rule on the damages claimed. [ 42 ] As for the defendants Passmore and Waugh, it will be necessary to determine whether the conditions for the operation of section 178 of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act have been met. [ 43 ] This Court proposes the following questions: Regarding the defendants Babin and Keely [ 44 ] Has it been established on a balance of probabilities that there were latent defects within the meaning of article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec on September 2, 2008? If so, what is the appropriate quantum of damages? Regarding the defendants Passmore and Waugh [ 45 ] Are the conditions for the operation of s. 178(1) ( e ) of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act applicable such that the order of discharge does not release the   provable claim? Analysis and discussion Defendants Babin and Keely: [ 46 ] At the outset, it should be noted that it has been established since General Motors v. Kravitz [16] that the guarantee against latent defects follows the property sold. Moreover, articles 1442 and 1730 C.C.Q . codify this principle. [ 47 ] In this regard, the Court of Appeal stated the following in 2016: [17] [43]  Avec l’avènement du Code civil du Québec , en 1994, le législateur a codifié, aux articles 1442 et 1730 C.c.Q., la solution adoptée par la Cour suprême dans l’arrêt Kravitz pour la garantie contre les vices cachés [11] . En matière immobilière, le recours du sous-acquéreur contre un vendeur antérieur, consacré par la jurisprudence et la doctrine [12], est assuré par l’article 1442 C.c.Q. , comme il l’était auparavant, jusqu’à un certain point, en vertu de « la règle Kravitz » [13] . [44]  La garantie contre les vices cachés suit, en quelque sorte, le bien. Elle est l’accessoire de la chose vendue [14] . En somme, le sous-acquéreur, en acquérant la propriété de la chose, devient créancier de la garantie des vices cachés. [45]  Les principes énoncés par le juge Pratte, au nom de la Cour suprême, dans Kravitz , apportent des éléments pertinents de réflexion, même si cet arrêt porte sur le caractère solidaire des obligations auxquelles sont tenus les vendeurs professionnels et sur la résolution de la vente d’un bien mobilier de consommation et non d’un bien immobilier : L’on doit donc dire que le sous-acquéreur, en acquérant la propriété de la chose, devient le créancier de la garantie légale des vices cachés due par le premier vendeur à raison de la vente qu’il a consentie au premier acheteur. Cette créance, on le sait, a deux objets principaux: le premier est la résiliation de la vente alors que le deuxième est le paiement de dommages-intérêts. La créance transmise au sous-acquéreur comprend donc à la fois le droit à la résiliation de la vente consentie par le premier vendeur au premier acheteur et le droit aux dommages-intérêts. La créance est transmise au sous-acquéreur dans son intégralité […] [15] . [50] La garantie de qualité (art. 1522-1528 C.c.B.‑C. ; art. 1726 - 1731 C.c.Q. ) peut être invoquée par un sous-acquéreur contre un vendeur antérieur dans la mesure où le vice existait au moment où ce dernier était propriétaire du bien et que son auteur n’avait pas renoncé expressément à cette garantie. Il exerce les droits que lui a transmis son auteur avec l’immeuble. Le recours du sous-acquéreur est de nature contractuelle. Ce dernier y trouvera « un avantage dans les cas où son propre vendeur est introuvable, insolvable ou encore s’il n’est pas tenu à l’obligation de garantie » [18] ou encore, comme en l’espèce, si la société qui a vendu le bien est dissoute. [ 48 ] This last excerpt reveals that while a recourse against the previous seller exists, it must nevertheless satisfy criteria attached to the original sale, that is, the defect must have existed at the time of the sale, and the sale price at the time must also be taken into account. [ 49 ] Herein lies the first challenge in this case. Did the alleged defects exist in 2008? [ 50 ] Before embarking on a review of the evidence, it is worth describing the conditions attached to actions for latent defects. [ 51 ] First of all, pursuant to article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec , the seller must warrant the quality of the property sold. Art. 1726: The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. . The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert . [ 52 ] It is apparent in this article that there are certain elements attached to latent defects protected by the warranty of quality. [ 53 ] My colleague Jean-Jude Chabot J. summarized them well in a 2017 decision, [18] and the Court will reproduce his analysis in its entirety. [44] Comme le rappelle la Cour d'appel dans l'arrêt récent Leroux c. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 (CanLII) , pour donner ouverture à la garantie de qualité, le vice doit répondre à certaines exigences : [40] Pour se prévaloir de la garantie légale contre les vices cachés, quatre conditions doivent donc être respectées : (i) que le bien soit affecté d'un vice grave, l'intensité de cette gravité ayant été définie par la jurisprudence à partir des expressions « impropre à l'usage » et « diminuent tellement son utilité »; (ii) que le vice existait au moment de la vente; (iii) que le vice soit caché, qualité qui s'évalue objectivement et qui est accompagnée d'une obligation de s'informer; et (iv) que le vice soit inconnu de l'acheteur, qualité qui s'évalue subjectivement et dont le fardeau de preuve appartient au vendeur. [45]      Il s’agit d’une norme objective comme le souligne la Cour suprême du Canada dans l’arrêt ABB inc. c. Domtar , 2007 CSC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 R.C.S. 461 : […]  Cependant, quelle que soit la qualification du vice, il doit présenter quatre caractères, tous essentiels à la garantie : il doit être caché, suffisamment grave, existant au moment de la vente et inconnu de l’acheteur. 3.6.1.2  Caractère caché du vice Le caractère caché du vice s’...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 4\\nmore and Waugh to co-defendants Babin and Keely was valid. [ 151 ] What about the fall 2015 work, in regard to which the Court finds that the notices received by Passmore and Waugh were never transmitted to the co-defendants? Can we conclude, as the plaintiffs suggest, that notice to the defendants Passmore and Waugh is sufficient? [ 152 ] The Court does not think so. [ 153 ] Professors Jobin and Cumyn state the following in their book on sale: [34] Lorsque l’acheteur veut exercer ses droits contre un vendeur antérieur (par exemple le fabricant) selon les articles 1442 ou 1730, doit-il lui donner un préavis? Selon nous, la réponse est affirmative, en règle générale [35] . En effet, on ne voit pas de justification à une dispense de préavis ici, qui ferait perdre au vendeur antérieur la possibilité de vérifier s’il s’agit vraiment d’un vice couvert par la garantie et la chance de le réparer à meilleur compte. Une exception doit toutefois être admise, malgré sa sévérité, quand le vendeur antérieur et le vendeur immédiat sont solidairement tenus à la garantie : alors comme une mise en demeure (article 1599), le préavis donné à l’un des débiteurs produit ses effets sur le codébiteur solidaire, qui ne peut pas se plaindre de ne pas avoir reçu de préavis [36] . [ 154 ] In the Court’s view, while the professional seller may be held solidarily liable, the same cannot be said for ordinary sellers. The Court cannot see how the rules of solidarity could be applicable, and hence, how written notice given to Passmore and Waugh in October 2015 could be set up against the defendants Babin and Keely. [ 155 ] Solidarity between debtors is not presumed; it exists only where it is expressly stipulated by the parties or by law (art. 1525 C.C.Q.). [ 156 ] Nobody here is claiming that a contractual provision to that effect exists, so are we in a case provided for by law? [ 157 ] This is clearly not a situation involving the operation of an enterprise or an obligation to make reparation for injury caused to another in accordance with art. 1526 C.C.Q. [ 158 ] Also in Joyal , cited above, Dalphond J.A. stated the following on the nature of the debtors’ obligations under art. 1730 C.C.Q.: [49] Il convient de préciser que les vendeurs successifs, s’ils ne sont pas des vendeurs professionnels, sont débiteurs de l’acheteur, mais pas nécessairement au même titre. Ainsi, certains auteurs suggèrent que leur responsabilité est in solidum plutôt que solidaire (Jobin, par. 219). [50]  Par contre, si les vendeurs successifs sont des vendeurs professionnels et qu’il y a vice de qualité, l’ article 1730 C.c.Q. trouvera application. Cet article assimile le vendeur au fabricant et énonce que ce dernier, de même que les intermédiaires, sont désormais tenus à la même responsabilité que le vendeur quant aux vices de qualité. [51]  Dans leur traité La responsabilité civile , 7 e éd., vol. 2, Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2007, les auteurs Jean-Louis Baudouin et Patrice Deslauriers écrivent : 2-360 — Généralités — Que ce soit sous le régime de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur ou sous celui du Code civil , le fabricant est désormais tenu à la même responsabilité que le vendeur. Cette assimilation, conforme à l'évolution jurisprudentielle, est la rançon du rôle prépondérant que joue aujourd'hui le fabricant dans la mise en marché de ses propres produits. Elle obéit à une logique de protection accrue du consommateur, qui ne doit pas se retrouver uniquement face à un intermédiaire au statut juridique et à la solvabilité généralement plus incertains. Leur responsabilité solidaire vis-à-vis du consommateur ou de l'utilisateur est maintenant bien acceptée tant par la jurisprudence que par la loi. Seule la Convention des Nations Unies sur les contrats de vente internationale de marchandises n'amalgame pas vendeur et fabricant. [références omises] [ 159 ] Holding the professional seller liable for the property sold along with its buyer does not cause any problem and responds to a mindset of consumer protection. [37] This mindset has no place, however, in the presence of ordinary successive sellers who are not professionals. [ 160 ] If the plaintiffs wish to sue more than one seller, why should they be exempt from the requirement of providing written notice to one of them? [ 161 ] The obvious problem in this case results from the fact that the initial intention was not to sue Babin and Keely. It was only after the bankruptcy of Passmore and Waugh that the plaintiffs changed their minds. Why must Babin and Keely suffer from this strategic change? [ 162 ] The written notice serves the important purpose, among others, of allowing the sellers to assess the defect and if necessary, identify the steps that should be taken. [ 163 ] In Joseph c . Fortin , [38] the Court of Appeal stated the following in response to the appellants’ argument that the lack of notice had not caused any prejudice: [9]  Cependant, lorsque les appelants ont reçu dénonciation du vice de la part de leurs acheteurs, ils ne l’ont pas transmise aux défendeurs en garantie intimés. Leur propre dénonciation n’est survenue qu’une fois la décontamination et la remise en état des lieux complétée et a privé les intimés en garantie de l’opportunité de pouvoir même tenter de démontrer que le vice (la contamination) était subséquent à la vente de 2003. [10]  De toute façon, le fardeau de prouver l’antériorité du vice appartenait aux appelants, qui ne s’en sont pas déchargés. [ 164 ] In this case, the defendants Babin and Keely were deprived of a written notice concerning the fall 2015 work, and because the written notice is a condition for the operation of the remedy, the claim regarding that work must be dismissed. [ 165 ] However, at the risk of repetition and like in Joseph c. Fortin , cited above, the Court finds it useful to note that the evidence on a balance of probabilities does not allow us to establish that the latent defects existed in 2008, except for the beam and the joists that were cut ($2,400). [ 166 ] For these last elements, in addition to the absence of notice, it should be noted that the plaintiffs received $23,000 in a settlement with the inspector, which is more than sufficient to cover this portion of the claim. [ 167 ] In closing, it may seem paradoxical to question the lack of written notice in view of the conclusion that the evidence on a balance of probabilities does not support the claim for latent defects. [ 168 ] Indeed, the Court cannot conclude that there was any prejudice resulting from the absence of written notice if it finds that there were no latent defects in 2008. The defence has thus successfully rebutted the plaintiff’s evidence. [ 169 ] The Court understands this nuance but adds that it is only after a detailed examination of the mostly circumstantial evidence that it has arrived at this conclusion. [ 170 ] The fact remains that defendants found themselves in a way obliged to rely on circumstantial elements, whereas had they received a written notice, they would have had the benefit of direct evidence. [ 171 ] In the circumstances, the Court found it useful to make reference to the lack of written notice in order to demonstrate that even if it had found that latent defects did exist, the absence of one of the conditions for operation of the remedy, in this case the written notice concerning the fall 2015 work, would nevertheless have been fatal to the claim. [ 172 ] The Court will now address the situation of the other defendants, Passmore and Waugh. Defendants Passmore and Waugh [ 173 ] Are the conditions of section 178( 1 )( e ) of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act met such that the order of discharge does not release the provable claim? [ 174 ] Section 178(1) ( e ) of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act states: 178 (1) An order of discharge does not release the bankrupt from ( e ) any debt or liability resulting from obtaining property or services by false pretences or fraudulent misrepresentation, other than a debt or liability that arises from an equity claim ; [ 175 ] In Salesse (Syndic de) , [39] Catherine La Rosa J. set out the conditions for operation and identified a number of judgments concerning latent defects where the failure to give notice of the latent defects resulted in the operation of section 178(1) ( e ). [ 176 ] With respect to the conditions for operation of the remedy, La Rosa J. stated: [31] Ainsi, lors d’une demande introduite sur la base de l’article 178(1)e) LFI , les étapes suivantes doivent être franchies pour avoir gain de cause : a. Le demandeur doit prouver les fausses allégations; b. Une fois ces fausses allégations prouvées nait une présomption d’ intention frauduleuse qui détruit celle de la bonne foi; c. Le défendeur peut renverser cette présomption d’intention frauduleuse en fournissant des explications qui, au final, démontrent l’absence d’ intention frauduleuse [2]. [32] Il est important de mentionner que l’article 178 LFI doit recevoir une interprétation restrictive puisque la règle générale consiste en la libération du failli qui éteint toutes les dettes susceptibles de constituer une réclamation personnelle[3]. [ 177 ] The judgments identified by Justice La Rosa consists of situations where the latent defects were concealed or where there was voluntary failure to disclose a known fact. [ 178 ] What is the case here? [ 179 ] Did the defendants Passmore and Waugh make false statements, either directly or by voluntary concealment? [ 180 ] The Court does not think so. [ 181 ] Let us recall at the outset that the purchase of the house happened quickly because the buyers had very little time to relocate, and they visited a dozen houses over a few days. [ 182 ] Thus, there were never any meetings or even discussions between the buyers and the sellers. [ 183 ] The plaintiffs, of course aware of this situation, base the theory of their case on certain elements that could lead to the conclusion, in accordance with their position, that the sellers knew about the defects and tried to conceal them. [ 184 ] For example, there was a crack in the parging that had been patched by the defendant Passmore. [ 185 ] Next, they noted the presence of rocks in the mix to compensate for the depth of the crack. From this, they conclude that the sellers tried to hide the crack and mislead them. [ 186 ] Finally, they also assert that the residence was freshly painted, probably to hide marks inside. [ 187 ] The plaintiffs allege that there had to have been marks confirming the infiltration and therefore also the presence of mould. [ 188 ] Without repeating every element or each of the plaintiff’s suspicions, the Court will begin by stating at the outset that the plaintiffs did not discharge their burden of proof. [ 189 ] More specifically, it is worth mentionning that the parging work was explained by Ms. Passmore, and the Court believes her version. [ 190 ] She said that she did the parging work herself at the request of her real estate agent. The work was done for purely aesthetic reasons, with mixed results, as everyone agrees. [ 191 ] She says, however, that she never thought there was an infiltration problem. [ 192 ] The experts [40] heard confirmed that the crack in the parging was in no way indicative of the problem affecting the foundation. [ 193 ] The pre-purchase inspector in fact refers to it in his report. [41] [ 194 ] Even the plaintiff’s expert said that the deteriorations were located under the walkway and below ground level. [ 195 ] There is no doubt that it is a defect but that does not necessarily lead to the operation of section 178(1) ( e ) of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act . [ 196 ] Moreover, the fact that the residence had recently been painted does not lead to the conclusion of an intention to conceal the latent defects. [ 197 ] It does not seem unusual for sellers to try to improve the appearance of their residence by painting it. [ 198 ] What is more, the sellers even left cans of recently used paint on the premises so that the buyers would be aware of the new colours used. [ 199 ] Suffice it to say that this does not really suggest an intention to conceal. [ 200 ] As for the argument that there were likely marks or some other manifestation of mould, the Court adds that it does not find that there was mould present as described. [ 201 ] In addition, a particular situation cannot go unmentioned. [ 202 ] Between the purchase of the residence in 2008 and its sale to the plaintiffs, the Passmore–Waugh family went through a very sad period. [ 203 ] Their son was diagnosed with cancer. Without going into detail, it is worth noting that the type of cancer afflicting the child and the numerous treatments greatly affected his immune system. [ 204 ] At the hearing, his mother said that his frailty forced the family to be overly cautious and avoid all contact with germs. For that reason, she said, she would never have allowed her child to be in a mouldy environment. The Court believes her. [ 205 ] Another element corroborating the absence of knowledge of the problems by the defendants Passmore and Waugh results from the exhibits filed by the other defendants. [ 206 ] A review of the various discussions through Facebook [42] between the defendants in the months preceding the sale to the plaintiffs shows that Passmore and Waugh expressed their satisfaction of the residence acquired. Their move was not the result of any dissatisfaction. [ 207 ] In short, if the house was affected by all the defects alleged, and the sellers Passmore and Waugh were aware of it, the Court doubts they would express as much satisfaction. [ 208 ] In conclusion, the Court is of the view that the plaintiffs did not discharge their burden of proof and that section 178(1) ( e ) is not applicable in this case. [ 209 ] FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 210 ] DISMISSES the originating application against the defendants Babin and Keely; [ 211 ] WITH legal costs. [ 212 ] DISMISSES the application seeking the operation of section 178(1) ( e ) against the defendants Passmore and Waugh; [ 213 ] WITHOUT legal costs. ____________________ DOMINIQUE GOULET , J.S.C. Mtre Alexandra Roy Daoust Siroy Counsel for the plaintiffs Vanessa Passmore and Robert Waugh Representing themselves Mtre Benoit Duchesne Gowling WLG Counsel for the defendants Ann-Marie Babin and Thomas E. Keely Dates of hearing: March 25, 26, 27, 28 and 29 and June 21, 2019 [1] $107,381.25 + $51,219.39 = $158,600.64. [2] The sale price was $220,000, P-20. [3] $70,418.89 + $111,808.91 = $182,227.80. [4] That statement is set out in paragraph 26 of the application for authorization. [5] $80,805.03 + $75,176.55 = $155,981.58. [6] Mr. Goodman is in the military. [7] The offer P-1 states that it is made further to the July 3, 2014, visit (section 7.1). [8] P-1. [9] According to the inspector, see P-2. [10] P-1, section 8.1. [11] P-2. [12] P-2 at 4 and 5. [13] P-3. [14] P-28. [15] See report P-7 at 4 under the heading “cause of the defects.” [16] General Motors v. Kravitz 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , [1979] 1 S.C.R. 790. [17] Dupuy c . Leblanc , 2016 QCCA 1141 . [18] Vermette c . Boisvert , 2017 QCCS 3159 (CanLII) , aff’d 2019 QCCA 829 ; with respect to the conditions that must be met, see also the judgment rendered by Serge Gaudet J. in Pesant c. St-Louis , 2019 QCCS 1472 at paras. 47 to 54 . [19] P-28. [20] See in particular photograph AP-36, exhibit P-7. [21] See P-26 at 4 of 6. [22] Paragraph 20 of the re-amended originating application\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 5\\nS 3159 (CanLII) , aff’d 2019 QCCA 829 ; with respect to the conditions that must be met, see also the judgment rendered by Serge Gaudet J. in Pesant c. St-Louis , 2019 QCCS 1472 at paras. 47 to 54 . [19] P-28. [20] See in particular photograph AP-36, exhibit P-7. [21] See P-26 at 4 of 6. [22] Paragraph 20 of the re-amended originating application. [23] The Court notes that there is indeed a report prepared by Eskis Architect that was never introduced into evidence although the opportunity to do so was provided. Eskis is a company related to the contractor. [24] In September 2016. [25] P-15. [26] Except for the roof and 12.5% of the foundation. [27] See supra note 15. [28] La Cour d’’appel réitère ces principes dans une décision récente du 26 mars 2019; 6983499 Canada Inc. c. Gagné , 2019 QCCA 536 . [29] Except, however, for the beam and the joists. [30] See supra note 22. [31] Paragraph 18 a) of the re-amended originating application. [32] Claude Joyal Inc. c . CNH Canada Ltd et Axa Assurances Inc. , 2014 QCCA 588 . [33] Nadeau c. Mercedes Benz Canada Inc. 2017 QCCA 470 . [34] Pierre-Gabriel Jobin & Michelle Cumyn, La vente , 4th ed. (Montreal: Yvon Blais, 2017) at 250. [35] St-Germain c. Lévesque , EYB 2009-164515 (C.S.) ; Immeubles Rochefort inc. c. Charest , EYB 2009-153970 (C.Q) ; 7507836 Canada inc. c. Pouliot , 2017 QCCS 1759 , EYB 2017-279245. Contra : 9041-9086 Québec inc. c. Usinage Bernier , J.E. 2005-1995, REJB 2005-94177 (C.Q) . [36] Royal & Sunalliance c. Hewitt Equipement ltée , EYB 2008-148026 (C.S) ; Claude Joyal inc. c. CNH Canada Ltd , EYB 2014-234922 (C.A.) , inf. EYB2014-202477 (C.S). [37] See Jean-Louis Baudouin & Patrice Deslauriers, La responsabilité civile , 7th ed., vol. 2 (Cowansville, Qc.: Yvon Blais, 2007) at 2-360. [38] Joseph c. Fortin , 2018 QCCA 74 . [39] Salesse (Syndic de) , 2016 QCCS 3091 ; see also Poulin c. Anglehart , 2017 QCCS 1258 . [40] The defendants Passmore and Waugh called an expert to testify on this aspect. [41] P-2 at 8. [42] DBK-2.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.029083244502544403, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\", \"title\": \"Waters c. Passmore, 2019 QCCS 3805 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.029083244502544403, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\"}, \"token_count\": 8282, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Orlov c. Ali, 2023 QCCS 1790 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"--- BEGIN PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 0\\n2023 QCCS 1790 (CanLII) | Orlov c. Ali | CanLII Date: 2023-05-19 File number: 500-17-115483-219 Citation: Orlov c. Ali, 2023 QCCS 1790 (CanLII) Orlov c. Ali 2023 QCCS 1790 SUPERIOR COURT (Civil Division) CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL No.: 500-17‑115483‑219 DATE: May 19, 2023 _____________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE ANDRES C. GARIN, J.S.C. _____________________________________________________________________ INNA ORLOV and MICHAEL ORLOV Plaintiffs v. KHOSRO MOHAMMAD ALI and VÉRONIQUE MARIE Defendants _____________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT _____________________________________________________________________ OVERVIEW ................................................................................................................................... 2 CONTEXT ...................................................................................................................................... 3 1. The Purchase of the Property and the Inspection .................................................... 3 2. Issues Discovered by the Plaintiffs Upon Taking Possession of the Property .... 3 3. The Expert Evidence ..................................................................................................... 5 POINTS IN ISSUE ....................................................................................................................... 6 ANALYSIS ..................................................................................................................................... 7 1. The Defendants’ Objection to the Admission into Evidence of the Plaintiffs’ Photos 7 2. The Claims for Latent Defects ..................................................................................... 9 2.1 The Governing Principles ......................................................................................... 9 2.2 The Exterior Wall in the Area of the Rear Sliding Patio Door ........................... 11 2.3 The Basement Water Infiltration Issue ................................................................. 13 3. The Damages to which the Plaintiffs are Entitled as a result of the Latent Defects Affecting the Property ............................................................................................................................. 17 3.1 The Governing Principles ....................................................................................... 17 3.2 The Plaintiffs’ Claims ............................................................................................... 18 4. The Plaintiffs’ Other Claims for Compensation ...................................................... 23 4.1 The Damaged Front Door ...................................................................................... 23 4.2 The Removal of the Closet Shelving .................................................................... 25 4.3 The Defendants’ Failure to Remove Sundry Items from the Property ............ 27 LEGAL COSTS & EXPERT FEES .......................................................................................... 27 DISPOSITION ............................................................................................................................. 28 OVERVIEW [ 1 ] On July 28, 2020, the Plaintiffs, Inna Orlov and Michael Orlov, purchased a residential home on Crestwood Avenue in Montreal West (the Property ) from the Defendants, Khosro Mohammad Ali and Véronique Marie, for the sum of $785,000. [1] [ 2 ] The Plaintiffs claim that their new home is affected by latent defects. They also claim that the Defendants caused damage to the front door of the home and that, when vacating the premises, the latter both improperly removed certain fixtures and failed to remove unwanted objects from the Property. [ 3 ] In compensation for the alleged latent defects and the injury they claim to have suffered, the Plaintiffs seek damages in the amount of $108,366.23. [ 4 ] For the reasons that follow, I find that the Property was affected by latent defects that resulted in water infiltration in two areas of the home. These latent defects entitle the Plaintiffs to a price reduction in the amount of $30,527.05. The Plaintiffs are also entitled to $3,038.67 for damage to the front door, improper removal of certain fixtures and for failure to fully remove their personal belongings from the Property. CONTEXT 1. The Purchase of the Property and the Inspection [ 5 ] In the spring of 2020, the Plaintiffs were looking for a larger home for their family. They were interested in the Property and, after visiting it, decided to make an offer which was accepted on May 24, 2020. [2] [ 6 ] Prior to completing the purchase of the Property, which was built in 1957, the Plaintiffs had it inspected by Mr. Giuseppe Laurieri of Abitat Inspect Inc. On May 29, 2020, Mr. Laurieri conducted a visual, non-obtrusive, pre-purchase inspection of the Property. [ 7 ] Mr. Laurieri provided the Plaintiffs with a report (the Pre-Purchase Inspection Report ) setting out his observations and recommendations arising from his visual pre‑purchase inspection. [3] [ 8 ] Following receipt by the Plaintiffs of the Pre-Purchase Inspection Report, the parties negotiated a $5,000 price reduction “to account for the brick and masonry work to be performed on the chimney”. [4] [ 9 ] Moreover, the Defendants, who had disclosed a basement water infiltration issue in relation to a foundation crack in the laundry room, [5] had the crack in question repaired by the injection of epoxy resin. 2. Issues Discovered by the Plaintiffs Upon Taking Possession of the Property [ 10 ] The Plaintiffs took possession of their new home on or about July 31, 2020. They had been given the keys to the premises a few days earlier and visited the Property on July 27 or 28. On that occasion, Ms. Orlov noticed that the front door was noticeably dented. [6] No such dent was observed by the Plaintiffs or by their home inspector during previous visits. [ 11 ] Ms. Orlov also observed that the Defendants had left sundry unwanted items in the basement and the garage. [ 12 ] She further noticed that shelving affixed to the walls in several of the closets (main entrance hall and certain bedrooms) had been removed. [7] [ 13 ] The Plaintiffs, who intended to move into their new home one week after taking possession, had hired a contractor to paint the interior of the entire dwelling. One of the rooms at the rear of the house has a sliding patio door that leads to the backyard. The Plaintiffs’ contractor was having difficulty painting this area. The paint would not dry and the wall was noticeably wet. The Plaintiffs noted as well that the paint around the patio door had “bubbled”. [8] [ 14 ] Finding the situation unusual, the Plaintiffs’ contractor, Mr. Ilya Ruso, opened the area to explore. Significant water infiltration behind the brick siding was discovered. [ 15 ] The Plaintiffs also noticed a pervasive musty odour in the basement. Neither the Plaintiffs, nor their inspector, had detected any unusual smell during previous visits. [9] The basement odour raised concerns for Ms. Orlov. It reminded her of a smell she had previously experienced in a workplace that had a mold issue. [ 16 ] The Plaintiffs retained Mr. David Mitch of Axxonlab to conduct an air quality analysis in order to assess the level of mold spores in the air. Mr. Mitch’s report, which will be discussed further below, revealed elevated concentrations of mold spores in the basement. [ 17 ] The Plaintiffs’ contractor, Mr. Ruso, also found the smell in the basement to be abnormal. A particularly strong odour emanated from one area where he found the floor to be spongy or soft. Mr. Ruso obtained permission from the Plaintiffs to conduct some exploratory work. [ 18 ] When the flooring was removed, Mr. Ruso discovered that the area was wet. A decision was made to find the source of the moisture. The flooring and the wall panels were thus removed to expose the concrete slab and foundation. The removal of the flooring and wall panelling revealed a crack in the foundation and a hole in the slab. [ 19 ] On August 20, 2020, the Plaintiffs sent the Defendants a notice of defects and default. [10] They also had Mr. Ruso perform significant work to address a number of the issues discovered upon taking possession of the Property. They claim the cost of this work from the Defendants. They also advance claims for other heads of damages, including moral damages for inconvenience and compensation for their complaints in respect of the front door, the closets and the items abandoned on the premises. 3. The Expert Evidence [ 20 ] Two experts produced reports and were called to testify by the Plaintiffs. [ 21 ] The first was Mr. Laurieri, who was qualified without objection as an expert in residential building inspection. As noted, Mr. Laurieri conducted a pre-purchase inspection in May 2020. At the Plaintiffs’ request, he returned to the property on August 8, 2020. Following this visit, he produced a “Site Evaluation & Expertise Report” (the Expert Report ). [11] [ 22 ] Mr. Laurieri’s Expert Report deals with three issues that were not observed at the time of the pre‑purchase inspection: (1) water infiltration in the area of the sliding patio door at the rear of the house, (2) damage to the front door, and (3) damage to the closets. [ 23 ] With respect to water infiltration in the area of the sliding patio door, the Expert Report includes pictures showing water damage to the sliding door frame. Mr. Laurieri observes in his report (exact reproduction of the text): 1 – Signs of water infiltration as well as high moisture readings were noted along the top and upper side portions of the interior sliding door frame in the rear room in the lower ground floor area. 2 – The cause of the water infiltration appears to be from a pre-existing water infiltration from behind the exterior brick veneer wall directly above the said sliding patio door. Indications of previously attempted repairs to the brick veneer mortar was also noted. 3 – High moisture readings as well as active water infiltration was noted on the interior surface finishes at the affected areas. 4 – Removal of the interior finishing materials along the top and upper side portions of the interior sliding door frame indicated previous repairs to the affected area. [ 24 ] Mr. Laurieri then offers the following conclusions (exact reproduction of the text): 1 – The previously performed repairs to stop the water infiltration at the rear sliding door appear to be insufficient to resolve the water infiltration issue. The repairs to the exterior brick veneer do not appear to have been performed as per best standard practices. 2 – Given the type and configuration of the repair, that this situation appears to be a pre existing condition and was present prior to the purchase of the home. Indications of ongoing repairs to the interior finishes to dissimulate the water infiltration appears to have been made. This situation was not visible at the time of the visual pre purchase inspection. […] [ 25 ] Mr. Laurieri recommends: 1 – That the affected exterior brick veneer surfaces be evaluated to determine and identify the area of the water infiltration and that the proper and required repairs be performed by a qualified contractor to stop the source of the water infiltration. 2 – That the damaged interior surface materials be removed and that the affected materials be repaired / replaced and restored by a qualified contractor. 3 – That the damages to the sliding door and frame be evaluated and repaired / replaced as required by a qualified contractor. […] [ 26 ] With respect to the damage to the front door, the report notes that the door was not damaged at the time of the pre-purchase inspection. The report also notes that the removal of permanently anchored shelving had damaged the closets. [12] [ 27 ] The Plaintiffs also adduced the evidence of Mr. David Mitch, who was recognized as an expert in air quality control. Mr. Mitch attended at the residence on July 31, 2020, to test for the presence of mold. Mr. Mitch’s report discloses an elevated level of aspergillus/penicillium-like mold spores in the basement of the residence (i.e., a mold spore count of 3,780/m 3 ). [13] [ 28 ] Mr. Mitch explained that he was not a medical expert and could not opine on whether the quantity of spores detected in his analysis was harmful to human health. [ 29 ] In cross-examination, Mr. Mitch testified that he had frequently seen mold spore counts in the range that was detected in the basement and that he had also seen far more elevated spore counts (i.e., 10,000 /m 3 , 15,000/m 3 and 20,000/m 3 ). [14] POINTS IN ISSUE [ 30 ] The Plaintiffs’ claim raises the following issues: 1. Is the Property affected by one or more latent defects? 2. If so, what reduction in price are the Plaintiffs entitled to? 3. Are the Defendants liable for the damage to the front door? 4. Are the Defendants liable for the removal of shelving from the closets? 5. Are the Defendants liable for failing to remove sundry unwanted items from the Property? [ 31 ] A further point that I must rule on is the Defendants’ objection to the admission into evidence of a number of photos taken by Ms. Orlov and that were not communicated to the Defendants until the first day of trial. [ 32 ] I will begin my analysis with the Defendants’ objection to the photos. I will then examine the Plaintiffs’ claims for latent defects. Finally, I will address their various other claims of lesser monetary significance. ANALYSIS 1. The Defendants’ Objection to the Admission into Evidence of the Plaintiffs’ Photos [ 33 ] On the morning of the first day of the trial, the Plaintiffs provided the Defendants with a bundle of approximately 30 photographs taken by Ms. Orlov in August 2020 [15] . Given the tardy disclosure of these photographs, the Defendants objected to their admission into evidence. [ 34 ] Article 248 of the Code of Civil Procedure ( CCP ) provides that a party must provide the other party with the evidence it intends to use at trial at the latest with the declaration accompanying the request for setting the matter down for trial. If a party fails to do so, such evidence cannot be produced at trial without the court’s authorization: 248. A party in possession of evidence it intends to use at trial must send it to the other parties not later than with the declaration accompanying the request for setting down for trial. The party is dispensed from doing so if the evidence is an exhibit in support of a pleading or if the case protocol provides otherwise. In any other case, the evidence must be sent to the other parties within 30 days after the order to set down for trial is issued or the date of the trial is set, unless the court determines another time limit. A party that has failed to so disclose evidence cannot produce it at trial except with the authorization of the court. 248. La partie qui entend invoquer à l’instruction un élément de preuve en sa possession le communique aux autres parties au plus tard avec la déclaration qui accompagne la demande d’inscription. Elle en est dispensée s’il s’agit d’une pièce au soutien d’un acte de procédure ou si le protocole de l’instance en dispose autrement. Dans les autres cas, la communication est faite dans les 30 jours qui suivent l’ordonnance d’inscription ou la fixation de la date de l’instruction, à moins que le tribunal n’ait fixé un autre délai. La partie qui omet de communiquer ses éléments de preuve ne peut les produire lors de l’instruction si ce n’est qu’avec l’autorisation du tribunal. [ 35 ] Pursuant to article 250 CCP, exhibits must be filed with the court office at least 15 days prior to trial. 250. Unless they have already been filed with the court office for the purposes of the pre-trial conference, exhibits and other evidence must be filed by the parties at least 15 days before the scheduled trial date, or at least three days before that date if the trial is to be held within 15 days. However, in all instances, the court may require that exhibits and other evidence be delivered to it within the time it specifies. […] 250. À moins que les pièces et les autres éléments de preuve n’aient déjà été produits au greffe du tribunal en vue de la conférence préparatoire à l’instruction, les parties les produisent, au moins 15 jours avant la date fixée pour l’instruction; ce délai est d’au moins trois jours à l’avance si la date de l’instruction est fixée à moins de 15 jours. Cependant, dans tous les cas, le tribunal peut demander que les pièces et les autres éléments de preuve lui soient remis dans le délai qu’il indique. […] [ 36 ] Evidently, the Plaintiffs did not comply with these provisions. Under Quebec procedural law, the power to admit exhibits into evidence that have not been disclosed or filed within the requisite delays is discretionary. In this regard, Quebec courts are frequently lenient when it comes to the tardy disclosure and filing of exhibits. [ 37 ] That said, disclosure of the Plaintiffs’ photos to the Defendants on the morning of trial is belated in the extreme. Had the photos been disclosed earlier, the Defendants may well have defended the case differently or entered into settlement negotiations. [ 38 ] Although the existence of photos is mentioned in the Plaintiffs’ notice of defects and default of August 20, 2020, [16] no explanation was provided for their disclosure to the Defendants on the morning of trial. In the circumstances, I find that the extreme tardy disclosure of the photos was inconsistent with the Plaintiffs’ duty of cooperation and transparency under article 20 CCP. I have thus decided to sustain the Defendants’ objection. Accordingly, I have had no regard to the photos in these reasons and my judgment. 2. The Claims for Latent Defects [ 39 ] The Plaintiffs argue that the Property suffers from latent defects resulting in: (1) water infiltration in the area of the rear patio sliding door; and (2) water infiltration in the basement. [ 40 ] Prior to embarking on an analysis of these two issues, it is useful to set out the principles that govern a claim\\n\\n\\n--- END PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... 20 CCP. I have thus decided to sustain the Defendants’ objection. Accordingly, I have had no regard to the photos in these reasons and my judgment. 2. The Claims for Latent Defects [ 39 ] The Plaintiffs argue that the Property suffers from latent defects resulting in: (1) water infiltration in the area of the rear patio sliding door; and (2) water infiltration in the basement. [ 40 ] Prior to embarking on an analysis of these two issues, it is useful to set out the principles that govern a claim for latent defects. 2.1 The Governing Principles [ 41 ] The deed of sale provides that the Property was sold with legal warranty. [17] Legal warranty consists of the warranty of ownership and the warranty of quality. [18] The warranty of quality is codified at article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec ( CCQ ) and requires the seller to warrant that the property sold is free of latent defects: 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. 1726. Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l’acheteur que le bien et ses accessoires sont, lors de la vente, exempts de vices cachés qui le rendent impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acheté, ou n’aurait pas donné si haut prix, s’il les avait connus. Il n’est, cependant, pas tenu de garantir le vice caché connu de l’acheteur ni le vice apparent; est apparent le vice qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert. [ 42 ] A “defect” within the meaning of article 1726 can be material functional or conventional. [19] The notion of defect refers to a loss or deficit of use of the purchased property, as assessed in light of the buyer’s reasonable expectations. [20] The case law has identified four conditions for a defect to be “latent” thus engaging the legal warranty of quality: 1. the defect must be a serious one that renders the property unfit for its intended use or that diminishes its usefulness to such an extent that the buyer would not have purchased for the price paid; 2. the defect must have existed at the time of the sale of the property; 3. the defect must be hidden. The hidden nature of the defect is assessed objectively. Moreover, buyers have a duty to inform themselves; and 4. the defect must have been unknown to the buyer. This inquiry is subjective and the burden of proving the buyer’s knowledge of the defect falls on the seller. [21] [ 43 ] Frequently, the issue in dispute in latent defect cases will relate to the third element of the cause of action – the hidden or apparent nature of the defect at issue. [ 44 ] A hidden defect is one which the reasonably prudent and diligent buyer would not detect. [22] As explained by the Supreme Court in ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. , the standard to be applied “is whether a reasonable buyer in the same circumstances could have detected the defect at the time of the sale”. [23] [ 45 ] As previously noted, the above standard is an objective one and buyers are not obliged to have recourse to an expert. [24] They must perform a serious and attentive visual examination of the property to be purchased, but are not required to undertake invasive or destructive work. [25] [ 46 ] That said, when an attentive examination of the property reveals the existence of a potential problem, buyers must retain an expert and perform a more in‑depth inspection. [26] Failure to do so may well result in the defect being considered apparent. [ 47 ] The buyer who establishes that the purchased property is affected by a latent defect has a choice of recourse under article 1604 CCQ. [27] If the consequences of the defect are severe and reduce the usefulness of the property to such an extent that the buyer would not have completed the purchase, he or she may seek to have the sale set aside (formerly the redhibitory action). [ 48 ] On the other hand, if the buyer would have bought the property notwithstanding the defect, but only at a lower price, he or she may seek a reduction in the purchase price (formerly the estimatory or quanti minoris action). The principles governing the determination of the appropriate price reduction in the context of a latent defect claim are discussed below. [ 49 ] Moreover, if the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, the buyer can also obtain damages that do not relate directly to the property [28] . [ 50 ] In the present case, the Plaintiffs have brought an action in reduction of price based principally on the cost to repair the alleged latent defects. The Plaintiffs also seek a further award of damages for inconvenience and for cleaning costs. 2.2 The Exterior Wall in the Area of the Rear Sliding Patio Door [ 51 ] The evidence establishes the existence of a water infiltration problem in the area of the rear sliding patio door. In his Expert Report, Mr. Laurieri describes signs of water infiltration and high moisture meter readings in this area. A number of photos included in the Expert Report reveal damage to the sliding door frame resulting from water infiltration. [29] [ 52 ] For the reasons that follow, I find that the water infiltration problem in the rear sliding door area results from a latent defect affecting the impermeability of the exterior wall. [ 53 ] The Expert Report indicates that the “cause” of the water infiltration appears to be a pre-existing water infiltration from behind the exterior brick veneer wall directly above the sliding door. When Mr. Ruso opened the interior wall to identify the source of the humidity affecting the rear sliding door area, he discovered that there was no vapour barrier between the bricks and the interior gyprock and that the wood was dark from humidity. The absence of a vapour barrier is abnormal and the presence of water in this area demonstrates that the external wall was not built properly or in accordance with the rules of the trade at the time of construction. [ 54 ] The Expert Report and Mr. Ruso’s observations confirm that the construction of the exterior wall above the sliding door – whether original or as a result of subsequent repairs or renovations – was defective and allowed water to penetrate into the dwelling. It was not contested that repelling water is one of the key functions of an external wall and it was evidently not being performed as required, thereby resulting in damage to the interior. The defect affecting the exterior wall is serious in that it diminishes the usefulness of the Property. [ 55 ] Given the extent of the damage discovered, Mr. Laurieri opined that the water infiltration issue was pre-existing. Based on this evidence, and since the water infiltration issue affecting the rear exterior wall in the area of the sliding door was discovered very shortly after the Plaintiffs took possession of the Property, I find that this defect existed at the time of the sale. [ 56 ] In view of the foregoing, the key question is whether the defect was hidden or apparent. [ 57 ] As noted, the test in this regard queries whether a reasonably prudent and diligent buyer would have detected the defect. The evidence establishes that the Plaintiffs acted as reasonably prudent and diligent buyers. They retained the services of Mr. Laurieri, who performed a pre‑purchase inspection and prepared a report that was read by the Plaintiffs. This in itself indicates that the Plaintiffs met the requisite standard of prudence and diligence. [30] [ 58 ] Mr. Laurieri conducted a thorough visual inspection of the premises and nothing in the evidence suggests that it was in any way inadequate. While Mr. Laurieri’s inspection was non-invasive, that is perfectly consistent with what is required for a serious and attentive visual examination of the property. [ 59 ] Mr. Laurieri’s inspection did reveal that the exterior brick façade, including in the area of the rear sliding door, was “in generally poor condition”. [31] However, in his testimony, Mr. Laurieri explained that the issue with the brick siding observed during his pre-purchase inspection was not structural. He further testified that the poor condition of the brick exterior was not indicative of a water infiltration problem and that there were no visible signs of such a problem. The moisture meter used by Mr. Laurieri during the pre‑purchase inspection did not reveal any elevated humidity readings in the relevant area. Mr. Laurieri’s evidence on these points was uncontradicted. [ 60 ] In short, the evidence establishes that a serious and attentive examination of the property did not reveal a water infiltration issue in the area of the rear sliding door. Such examination did reveal the existence of a general issue with the exterior brick veneer. However, the evidence before me is that the issue thus observed is not indicative of the existence of a water infiltration problem either generally or in the specific area of the rear sliding door. [32] [ 61 ] Based on the evidence, I therefore find that the reasonably prudent and diligent buyer would not have detected the water infiltration problem affecting the rear sliding door area and that the source of such problem constituted a hidden defect. [ 62 ] The fourth and final condition to establish a claim for latent defects relates to the buyer’s knowledge of the defect before the sale. The Defendants have not suggested, nor attempted to discharge their burden of proving that the Plaintiffs were aware of the defect resulting in the water infiltration problem. [ 63 ] It follows that the Plaintiffs have satisfied the conditions for establishing a latent defect claim in respect of the water infiltration problem affecting the rear sliding door area. 2.3 The Basement Water Infiltration Issue [ 64 ] The Plaintiffs also submit that the basement of the Property is affected by a latent defect resulting in a water infiltration problem that they discovered only after taking possession of their new home. [ 65 ] The expert evidence relating to the basement consists of Mr. Mitch’s report and testimony. [33] This evidence simply discloses the existence of elevated concentrations of mold spores in the ambient basement air. As explained by Mr. Mitch, such concentrations, while not exceedingly high, are indicative of an underlying issue of water infiltration or moisture build-up requiring further investigation. [ 66 ] The most comprehensive evidence relating to the basement is the testimony of Mr. Ruso. Given the unpleasant musty smell in that area of the Property, he was asked to carry out exploratory work to determine the source of the odour. He observed that the laminate flooring was a bit soft, but was unable to detect any moisture without removing it. [ 67 ] When the laminate flooring was removed, Mr. Ruso observed the presence of water. He also noted that portions of the plywood subfloor were black from moisture and rotten. Further exploration, including removal of some of the paneling on the basement walls, led to the discovery of a hole in the concrete slab and a significant crack in the foundation wall. [ 68 ] The hole discovered by Mr. Ruso was approximately 10 inches wide and 10 inches deep and was situated in the slab adjacent to the foundation wall in the area of the chimney. As for the crack, it was situated in the front right corner of the foundation, and it extended up the foundation wall. Mr. Ruso observed that the water discovered under the laminate flooring originated from both the hole and this crack. [ 69 ] In his testimony, Mr. Mohammad Ali explained that he never had any water infiltration problem in this part of the basement over the 11 or so years that the Defendants owned the Property. The Defendants argue that the most probable explanation for the water under the basement floor is that there must have been an unusually severe rainfall at some point between the date they vacated the Property and the date that the Plaintiffs took possession. They submit that such an event likely resulted in flooding of the basement. [ 70 ] With respect, the explanation proffered by the Defendants is speculative and unsupported by any evidence. Based on the evidence adduced before me, I am compelled to conclude that the probable sources of the water observed on the basement floor are the hole in the slab and the foundation crack discovered by Mr. Ruso. [ 71 ] The Defendants also suggest that the water infiltration results from the age of the building, which was constructed in 1957. Beyond the mere fact that the home is about 70 years old, there is no evidence to support this theory. [ 72 ] In particular, there was no evidence adduced by the Defendants to show that the hole or the crack resulted from normal wear and tear. The preponderance of the evidence thus leads me to conclude that they both result from defective work performed at the time of construction. Indeed, had the home been properly built, there would not be a hole in the slab nor a crack in the foundation wall of the sort discovered by Mr. Ruso. [ 73 ] It is evident that a hole and a crack in the foundation that allow water to penetrate into the interior of the dwelling’s basement diminish and impair the normal usefulness of the Property. These defects are therefore serious. [ 74 ] I further find that the defects existed at the time of the sale of the Property to the Plaintiffs. Indeed, Mr. Ruso observed that the water he discovered had been there for some time and that portions of the subfloor were black and rotten. Moreover, the musty smell observed by the Plaintiffs upon taking possession of the Property, and the elevated mold concentrations observed by Mr. Mitch shortly after the purchase, all suggest that water had been infiltrating into the basement for some time prior to the sale. [ 75 ] I turn now to whether these defects were hidden or apparent. [ 76 ] With respect to the exterior of the Property, Mr. Laurieri’s Pre-Purchase Inspection Report notes that “The visible portions of the exterior poured concrete foundations ate [ sic ] in good and serviceable condition”. [34] On the other hand, Mr. Laurieri’s inspection of the basement interior did reveal some issues. He writes in his Pre-Purchase Inspection Report: Indications of water infiltration, high humidity as well as some cracking and spalling of the lower portions of the interior of the poured concrete foundation walls was [ sic ] noted. [35] [ 77 ] In his testimony, Mr. Laurieri stated that the above comment related to a visible foundation crack in a separate unfinished part of the basement. This visible crack, which was disclosed and repaired by the Defendants prior to the sale, [36] was not the source of the water infiltration discovered by Mr. Ruso in the finished portion of the basement. [ 78 ] Mr. Ruso explained that he had to remove the flooring and some of the basement panelling to reveal the hole and crack in issue. These defects in the slab and the foundation were thus covered by the basement flooring and the wall paneling and could only be discovered by invasive or destructive work. Defects that are concealed in such fashion are considered hidden. [37] [ 79 ] That said, once he discovered the crack, Mr. Ruso was able to observe, on careful examination, that it could also be seen from the exterior of the Property. Mr. Ruso explained that the portion of the crack visible from the outside was extremely narrow and fine and that such cracks are very common in foundations. [ 80 ] In view of its size, the prevalence of such fine cracks, and the fact that it was not noted by Mr. Laurieri, I find that the portion of the crack that was visible from the exterior does not constitute an indicium or sign that ought to have raised the Plaintiffs’ suspicions, acting as reasonable and prudent buyers, of a water infiltration problem. [ 81 ] On the other hand, when they visited the Property prior to making an offer to purchase, the Plaintiffs noted the presence of a dehumidifier in the basement area. Mr. Laurieri similarly observed that a dehumidifier was being used by the Defendants. [38] [ 82 ] Mr. Laurieri explained that 90% of humidity issues are caused by a building’s occupants, rather than by a structural problem. Mr. Laurieri’s evidence on this point was uncontradicted. In the circumstances, I find that the mere use of a dehumidifier in the basement of the Property did not suggest the existence of a water infiltration problem to a reasonably prudent and diligent buyer. In doing so, I note that the Defendants state that they were unaware of any water infiltration problem in that part of the basement and that they had used the space in question for many years without incident. [ 83 ] In short, a serious and attentive visual examination of the Property would not lead the reasonably prudent buyer to detect the existence of a defect affecting the basement. [39] Nor would it lead such a buyer to suspect a potential problem with the foundation wall or concrete slab requiring further investigation. [ 84 ] As noted by Côté J.A. writing for the Court of Appeal in Lahaie v. Laperrière : [51] La prudence et la diligence requises des acheteurs sont établies par leur conduite en exigeant la réparation par le vendeur des fissures apparentes et en requérant une inspection avant l'achat par un professionnel de la construction. Cette inspection a d'ailleurs révélé que la maison était en bon état et bien entretenue. [52] En utilisant les services d'un...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 3\\nThe evidence offers no assistance in assessing the cost of this more limited excavation. In the circumstances, I must again arbitrate and I will award a price reduction of $10,000 for excavation and repairing the crack. [ 124 ] In view of the foregoing, I find that the Plaintiffs are entitled to a price reduction of $24,013.05 in respect of the latent defects affecting the basement. 4. The Plaintiffs’ Other Claims for Compensation [ 125 ] I turn now to the Plaintiffs’ other claims for compensation that are unrelated to the latent defects affecting the Property. 4.1 The Damaged Front Door [ 126 ] Upon visiting the Property on July 27 or 28, the Plaintiffs noticed that the front door was dented. [56] Since no such dents were apparent in May, the Plaintiffs assume that the door was dented when the Defendants moved out their personal effects from the Property. [ 127 ] In his testimony, Mr. Mohammad Ali states that the Defendants did not hire movers and that they themselves transported their furniture and personal effects to their new residence. He explains that the items were mostly moved out through the garage and not the front door. He denies having damaged the door and asserts that it was intact when the Defendants vacated the Property. [ 128 ] In his report, Mr. Laurieri indicates that the damage to the front door was caused at the time of the move by the seller. [57] This statement is wholly speculative and I give it no weight. [58] [ 129 ] The evidence is inconclusive as to how the front door was damaged and by whom. However, based on the parties’ testimony, I find that, in all probability, the door was damaged at some point prior to the purchase of the Property on July 28, 2020. [ 130 ] In these circumstances, the “theory of risk” (“ théorie des risques ”) governs which of the parties bears the risk of the damage to the front door of the Property. Under the general law, the second paragraph of article 1456 CCQ imposes on the debtor of the obligation to deliver property [59] (i.e., the seller) the risk of loss of or damage to the property until it is delivered: 1456. […] The debtor of the obligation to deliver the property continues, however, to bear the risks attached to the property until it is delivered. 1456. […] Toutefois, tant que la délivrance du bien n’a pas été faite, le débiteur de l’obligation de délivrance continue d’assumer les risques y afférents. [ 131 ] Pursuant to article 1456 CCQ, the Defendants would thus bear the risk of damage to the Property prior to delivery of same to the Plaintiffs. However, this provision is not of public order [60] and, in this case, the parties chose to contract out of it. [ 132 ] The deed of sale provides that the risks are transferred to the buyers as of the date of its conclusion: TRANSFER OF RISK Notwithstanding paragraph 2 of article 1456 of the Civil Code of Québec , the Purchaser shall assume the risks attached to the immovable, in accordance with article 950 of the Civil Code of Québec , as of the date hereof. [61] [ 133 ] Article 950 CCQ codifies the principle that the risks related to the loss of property fall on the owner thereof ( res perit domino ): 950. The owner of the property assumes the risks of loss. 950. Le propriétaire du bien assume les risques de perte. [ 134 ] Accordingly, the risks of damage to or loss of the Property passed to the Plaintiffs on July 28, 2020, upon execution of the deed of sale, rather than on July 31, 2020, the date upon which they took possession. [ 135 ] In view of my finding that the damage to the front door occurred in all probability prior to July 28, 2020, such damage occurred while the Defendants were the owners of the Property. As a result, they bear the risk of the damage in question. [ 136 ] The damage to the front door of the Property is partial, and indeed relatively minor. In the circumstances, the Plaintiffs are bound to take the property in the state in which it was, but are entitled to a price reduction. [62] [ 137 ] The Plaintiffs claim $4,823.20 for replacing the front door. At the time of trial, the door had yet to be replaced and the amount claimed is based on an evaluation received by the Plaintiffs from their contractor. [ 138 ] There are two difficulties with the amount claimed by the Plaintiffs for the damage to the front door. The first is that while the dents to the door are obvious, it is unclear that the door itself needs to be replaced and can’t simply be repaired. In his Expert Report, Mr. Laurieri recommends replacing the door. This recommendation, however, is unaccompanied by any supporting analysis or explanation. The Defendants have not sought an estimate for fixing the door as opposed to replacing it. [ 139 ] The second difficulty is that there is no documentary evidence in support of the estimate of $4,823.20 for replacing the door. Presumably, a written estimate with a breakdown for parts and labour would not be difficult to obtain. In the absence of such a document, I am unable to assess whether the amount claimed for replacing the door is reasonable. [ 140 ] In the circumstances, the precise amount of the price reduction to which the Plaintiffs are entitled on account of the damage to the front door has not been established and I am left with no choice but to “arbitrate”. In the exercise of my discretion, I will grant an amount of $1,500 for the front door. 4.2 The Removal of the Closet Shelving [ 141 ] When the Plaintiffs first visited the Property, they observed what they believed to be “built-in” shelving in four of the closets. This shelving was removed by the Defendants when they moved out. [63] [ 142 ] In his testimony, Mr. Mohammad Ali recognizes that he made a mistake by removing the fixtures that had been affixed to the walls in two of the closets. As for the other two closets, he explains that he did not remove anything affixed to the walls. What he had removed were “cabinets” that he had placed inside the closets. Mr. Mohammad Ali states that these cabinets constitute furniture and not “fixtures”. [ 143 ] I accept Mr. Mohammad Ali’s testimony as to the nature of the items removed from the closets. After all, he had lived in the Property for approximately 12 years and is the witness best acquainted with the items in the Property when it was occupied by the Defendants. [ 144 ] Accordingly, I find that the Defendants were entitled to remove and take with them the cabinets that they had placed in two of the closets. These cabinets are furniture, not permanent fixtures, and are not included in the deed of sale. [ 145 ] However, as recognized by Mr. Mohammad Ali, the Defendants improperly removed permanently attached fixtures from the walls of two other closets. In doing so, the Defendants committed a contractual fault and are liable for the damage thereby caused to the Plaintiffs. [ 146 ] The Plaintiffs claim $2,246.76 for shelves they purchased at Ikea to replace the removed fixtures and $400 for assembly of the purchased items. [ 147 ] The Plaintiffs have produced receipts for the purchase of items amounting to $2,246.76. [64] However, on cross-examination it was revealed that the receipts include various items unrelated to the closets. [65] The cost of these unrelated purchases total $169.43, and this amount must be deducted from the Plaintiffs’ claim. [ 148 ] The total spent by the Plaintiffs on new closet fixtures is thus $2,077.33. This amount is for four closets. In view of my conclusion that the Defendants improperly removed fixtures from only two of the four closets, I will award the Plaintiffs half of the amount they spent on replacing fixtures. They are thus entitled to $1,038.67 for the shelving removed from the closets. I will also award them $200 for closet assembly, which also represents half the amount sought for four closets. [ 149 ] Mr. Mohammad Ali explains that he only paid about $150 for the fixtures he removed from the closets. That may be. On the other hand, the Defendants improperly removed these fixtures, which forced the Plaintiffs to act quickly in order to purchase replacement shelving required to make the closets usable. In the circumstances, the Defendants can hardly complain that the Plaintiffs overspent. [ 150 ] I will thus award the Plaintiffs the sum of $1,238.67 for the fixtures improperly removed from two closets by the Defendants. 4.3 The Defendants’ Failure to Remove Sundry Items from the Property [ 151 ] The Defendants failed to remove certain items from the Property when they moved out their personal belongings. The abandoned items included boxes and furniture left in the basement, an electric fireplace and even some tires. [ 152 ] Mr. Mohammad Ali admits to having left these items believing that the Plaintiffs might want them. [66] Excepting such items that were specifically included in the sale, I find that the Defendants were required to remove their personal belongings from the Property. Failure to do so constitutes a contractual fault. [ 153 ] The Plaintiffs claim $300 in compensation for the removal of the Defendants’ unwanted items. This amount is reasonable, and I will award it. LEGAL COSTS & EXPERT FEES [ 154 ] Legal costs will be awarded to the Plaintiffs in accordance with the general principle that the unsuccessful party bears the costs (article 340 CCP). An issue arises, however, in respect of the expert fees claimed by the Plaintiffs as legal costs. [ 155 ] With respect to Mr. Mitch, both his report and testimony were useful. The Plaintiffs’ claim $436.90 as expert fees for Mr. Mitch’s services. [67] The amount claimed is reasonable and will be awarded to the Plaintiffs as legal costs. [ 156 ] As for Mr. Laurieri, the situation is slightly different. His Expert Report was of some use, but it also addresses matters – the dent to the front door and the fixtures removed from the closets – that are not appropriate subjects for expert evidence. Moreover, Mr. Laurieri testified both as an expert and, in respect of his pre‑purchase inspection, as an ordinary witness. Only his fees for testifying as an expert may be claimed as legal costs. [ 157 ] In the circumstances, I will award the Plaintiffs $437.43 for Mr. Laurieri’s fees, which is half the amount claimed. [68] DISPOSITION FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 158 ] SUSTAINS the objection of the Defendants, Khosro Mohammad Ali and Véronique Marie, to Exhibit P‑13; [ 159 ] GRANTS in part the Introductory Application of the Plaintiffs, Inna Orlov and Michael Orlov; [ 160 ] CONDEMNS the Defendants, Khosro Mohammad Ali and Véronique Marie, to pay the Plaintiffs, Inna Orlov and Michael Orlov, the amount of $33,565.72 bearing interest at the legal rate from the date of service, February 2, 2021; [ 161 ] WITH legal costs including expert fees fixed at $874.33. __________________________________ ANDRES C. GARIN, J.S.C. Maître Asher Neudorfer Sarna Neudorfer s.e.n.c. Counsel for the Plaintiffs Maître Patrick Lamanna Aedifico Avocats inc. Counsel for the Defendants Hearing dates: March 2 and 3, 2023 [1] Exhibit P‑1. [2] Exhibit D‑6. [3] Exhibit P‑2. [4] Exhibit D‑3. This problem was noted in the Pre-Purchase Inspection Report (see Exhibit P‑2, p. 2 and 14). [5] Exhibit D‑10 (items D3.1 and D14). This problem was noted in the Pre-Purchase Inspection Report (see Exhibit P‑2, p. 2 and 27‑28). [6] Exhibit P‑10 (photos of front door). [7] Ibid. (closet interior photos). [8] Ibid. (photos of patio door frame). [9] They had noticed that a dehumidifier was running in the basement laundry room. Apparently, the Defendants had constantly used a dehumidifier in the basement over many years. [10] Exhibit P‑3. [11] Exhibit P‑10. [12] In cross-examination, Mr. Laurieri conceded that an easy fix would be to patch the holes in the closets. [13] Exhibit P‑9, p. 3 and 6; testimony of D. Mitch. [14] Mr. Mitch believed that the highest mold spore count he had ever observed was 50,000/m 3 . [15] Exhibit P‑13. [16] Exhibit P‑3. [17] Exhibit P‑1 (p. 2). [18] Article 1716 CCQ. [19] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. , 2007 SCC 50 , para. 48 [ ABB Inc. ]. As explained by LeBel and Deschamps JJ., “material defects” relate to the specific good and “functional defects” relate to the good’s design. Such defects are assessed by reference to the normal use to which buyers put the goods in question. A “conventional defect” is assessed in light of the particular use of the good intended by the buyer and communicated to the seller. [20] ABB Inc. , supra , para. 49 . [21] See Leroux v. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 , para. 40 [ Leroux ]; ABB Inc. , supra , paras. 50‑54 ; Verville v. 9146‑7308 Québec Inc. , 2008 QCCA 1593 , para. 37 [ Verville ]; Lahaie v. Laperrière , 2009 QCCA 1285 , para. 33 [ Lahaie ]. [22] Article 1726(2) CCQ; Leroux , supra , paras. 42‑46 ; Verville , supra , para. 38 ; Lahaie , supra , para. 49 ; Marcoux v. Picard , 2008 QCCA 259 , para. 20 [ Marcoux ]; St‑Louis v. Morin , 2006 QCCA 1643 , paras. 27‑28 [ St‑Louis ]. [23] ABB Inc. , supra , para. 42 ; see also para. 51. [24] St‑Louis , supra , para. 27 ; Lahaie , supra , para. 49 . [25] Leroux , supra , para. 44 ; Marcoux , supra , para. 21 ; Rivard v. Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 , para. 7 [ Rivard ]; Paulin v. Gauthier , 2014 QCCA 1897 , para. 4 [ Paulin ]; Thériault v. Martin , 2020 QCCA 1294 , para. 10 . [26] Rivard , supra , para. 7 ; Leroux , supra , para. 44‑46 ; St‑Louis , supra , paras. 38‑39 ; Paulin , supra , para. 4 . [27] Verville , supra , para. 56 ; Lahaie , supra , paras. 35‑38 . [28] Article 1728 CCQ. [29] See the photos in Exhibit P‑10. [30] St-Pierre v. Levy , 2022 QCCS 1066 , paras. 94‑95 . [31] Exhibit P‑2, p. 2 and 18‑19. [32] See by analogy: Crooks v. Nguyen , 2022 QCCS 55 , para. 81 . [33] Mr. Laurieri’s August 2020 Expert Report does not address the basement. [34] Exhibit P‑2, p. 27. [35] Ibid. [36] Exhibit D‑10 (items D3.1 and D14). [37] J. Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 nd Ed. (Montreal: Wilson & Lafleur, 2008) [ Edwards ], no. 385 (p. 181‑182). [38] At the time, no unpleasant musty odour was noted in the basement by either the Plaintiffs or by Mr. Laurieri. [39] With the exception, of course, of the visible crack detected by Mr. Laurieri. This crack was declared and repaired by the Defendants prior to the purchase. As noted, it is not the source of the water infiltration problem in the finished portion of the basement. [40] Lahaie , supra , paras. 51‑52 . See also Leroux , supra , para. 46 : “ On ne peut reprocher à l’acheteur qui fait appel à un inspecteur d’avoir manqué à son devoir de prudence et de diligence si l’inspecteur n’a pas décelé d’indice de vice que l’acheteur raisonnable ne pouvait pas lui-même déceler. C’est seulement lorsque cet inspecteur décèle un indice sérieux que l’acheteur doit pousser son étude ”. [41] Verville , supra , paras. 71 and 77 . [42] Ibid. , para. 67; Lahaie , supra , para. 92 . [43] Verville , supra , paras. 61‑62 ; Lahaie , supra , para. 91 . [44] Edwards, supra , no. 539 (p. 258). [45] Ibid . [46] P.‑G. Jobin and M. Cumyn, La vente , 3rd Ed. (Cowansville : Y\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 4\\nude ”. [41] Verville , supra , paras. 71 and 77 . [42] Ibid. , para. 67; Lahaie , supra , para. 92 . [43] Verville , supra , paras. 61‑62 ; Lahaie , supra , para. 91 . [44] Edwards, supra , no. 539 (p. 258). [45] Ibid . [46] P.‑G. Jobin and M. Cumyn, La vente , 3rd Ed. (Cowansville : Yvon Blais, 2007) no. 180 (p. 239-240); Verville , supra , para. 65 ; Lahaie , supra , paras. 97‑100 . [47] Tomlin v. Lelliott , 2016 QCCS 5530 , para. 137 . [48] Edwards, supra , no. 612 (p. 297). [49] Exhibit P‑7 [50] See Exhibit P‑6 for the descriptions. [51] Exhibit P‑2, p. 2 and 14; and Exhibit D‑3. [52] Exhibit P‑7. [53] Exhibit P‑7 [54] See Exhibit P‑6 for the descriptions. [55] Exhibit P‑7. [56] Photos of the dented front entrance door are found in Exhibit P‑10. [57] Exhibit P‑10. [58] In cross-examination, Mr. Laurieri recognized that he did not know how the damage to the front door was caused. [59] The obligation to deliver does not mean physical delivery in the sense of “ livraison ”. It is defined at article 1717 CCQ: 1717 . The obligation to deliver the property is fulfilled when the seller puts the buyer in possession of the property or consents to his taking possession of it and all hindrances are removed. 1717 . L’obligation de délivrer le bien est remplie lorsque le vendeur met l’acheteur en possession du bien ou consent à ce qu’il en prenne possession, tous obstacles étant écartés . Delivery of the property is distinct from transfer of ownership which, in principle, occurs instantaneously upon the conclusion of the contract of sale. See on these points : Kingsway, compagnie d'assurances générales v. Bombardier Produits récréatifs inc. , 2010 QCCA 1518 , paras. 25‑28 [ Kingsway ]. [60] Kingsway , supra , para. 39 . [61] Exhibit P‑1, p. 2. [62] J.‑L. Baudouin and P.‑G. Jobin, Les obligations, 7th Ed. (Cowansville (QC): Yvon Blais, 2013), by P.‑G. Jobin and N. Vézina, at No. 866 (p. 1072). [63] The photos in Exhibit P‑10 shows that fixtures had been unscrewed from the walls of two closets. [64] See Exhibit P‑7 ( Ikea receipts). [65] See Exhibit D‑14. [66] He recognizes that the tires were left at the Property by error. [67] Exhibit P‑14. [68] Exhibit P‑11.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02533269301056862, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\", \"title\": \"Orlov c. Ali, 2023 QCCS 1790 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02533269301056862, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\"}, \"token_count\": 13432, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Hannan c. Allcut, 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII) | Hannan c. Allcut | CanLII Hannan c. Allcut, 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII) Date: 2009-07-17 File number: 500-17-025251-052 Citation: Hannan c. Allcut, 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/24pbv > Hannan c. Allcut 2009 QCCS 3254 JM 2257 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL No: 500-17-025251-052 DATE: July 17, 2009 ______________________________________________________________________ IN THE PRESENCE OF: THE HONOURABLE GENEVIÈVE MARCOTTE, J.S.C. ______________________________________________________________________ MONICA HANNAN TIM ROZON Plaintiffs/Cross-Defendants v. STELLA MARGARET ALLCUT ESTATE OF ROBERT MUIR STELLA MARGARET ALLCUT , in her capacity as liquidator to the estate of Robert Muir Defendants/Cross-Plaintiffs ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ INTRODUCTION [ 1 ] Monica Hannan (\\\" Hannan \\\") and Tim Rozon (\\\" Rozon \\\") (collectively \\\" Buyers \\\") are claiming damages in the amount of $181,225.21 from the Estate of Robert Muir (\\\" Muir \\\") and Stella Margaret Allcut (\\\" Allcut \\\") (collectively \\\" Vendors \\\") as a result of latent defects affecting the foundation of the house, discovered in the year following the purchase of the house. [ 2 ] Vendors deny the existence of a latent defect given that the defects were visible at the time of purchase.  They assert that Buyers' legal action is an abuse of \\\" ester en justice \\\" which has forced them to incur significant attorney fees amounting to $58,622.87 [1] , which they are claiming in cross-demand. THE FACTS First visit and offer [ 3 ] On March 29, 2003, the Buyers became interested in the Vendors' property located in Beaconsfield, a few doors away from their home. [ 4 ] The house had only just been put up for sale a few days earlier.  At the time, Muir and Allcut, respectively 78 and 75 years old, had lived there for 40 years.  They were looking to sell their house to move into a seniors' residence, given that Allcut was confined to a wheelchair. [ 5 ] The Buyers, who were expecting a second child were and looking to purchase a larger home with the potential to build a playroom in the basement, found the home to fit their needs and budget. [ 6 ] Following a two-hour visit with their respective fathers during which they were assured by Muir that the unfinished basement was \\\"high and dry\\\" condition, the Buyers offered to purchase the home for the full asking price of $209,000. [2] [ 7 ] The next day, on March 30, 2003, the Vendors accepted the offer which was subject to a pre-purchase inspection. Pre-purchase Inspection [ 8 ] The Buyers hired Joe Rodrigues, a structural engineer (\\\" the Inspector \\\"), to conduct a pre-purchase inspection, which took place on April 1, 2003. [ 9 ] His inspection of the interior and exterior of the home, including the \\\"cluttered\\\" basement took two hours. [ 10 ] During the inspection, the Inspector noticed signs of settlement, on each side of the support beam, given the particular layout of the support walls, which were off-center from the main support beam . [ 11 ] The Inspector asked Muir some questions regarding signs of prior water infiltration on the basement floor near the back stairwell.  Although he admitted to having had water near the back stairwell a few years before, Muir claimed to have resolved the problem by installing a small drain measuring 2 inches by 4 inches and assured him that no other infiltration has occurred since. [ 12 ] The Inspector noticed cracks on the front masonry veneer.  He recommended that the Buyers seal these cracks to avoid further expansion. [ 13 ] At the end of the inspection, the Inspector provided the Buyers with his written report [3] , which he briefly reviewed with them on site. [ 14 ] His report acknowledges extensive signs of prior leakage near the rear basement stairwell and the probability of water infiltration.  It also confirms the presence of mold along the perimeter of the interior walls of the basement. [ 15 ] It mentions that the foundations are made of concrete blocks but that 95% of interior foundation walls were not visible at the time of inspection due to furniture , wall coverings and debris. [4] [ 16 ] The report refers as well to the vendor's statement that he never had water in the basement, except near the rear basement access door. [ 17 ] Insofar as the parget covering the exterior foundation walls is concerned, the report states that it is in excellent condition while there are cracks in the front masonry veneer which need to be sealed. [ 18 ] It also indicates that the level of settlement noticed is typical for homes of this era and location, but suggests that an engineering investigation be carried out. [5] Lifting of condition and transaction [ 19 ] Further to their discussion with the Inspector and their summary review of his inspection report, the Buyers declared themselves satisfied with the outcome of the inspection and lifted the condition on their offer. [ 20 ] While the sale transaction was scheduled to take place in June 2003, Muir gave the Buyers a set of keys to the house as soon as they moved out of the property in early May 2003, to enable them to plan their repair and renovation work.  However, the Buyers refrained from carrying out any work prior to the closing on June 19, 2003. [ 21 ] Before the signing of the deed of sale, members of the Vendors' family emptied the house of its contents, except for certain items which were left behind covering up the back wall of the basement, namely old doors, a large freezer, a stand-up shower and some shelves. [ 22 ] The house was sold with legal warranty and the Deed signed by the parties [6] incorporates by reference the Promise to purchase, as well as the conditions set out in the Listing. [7] After the purchase / Appearance of defect [ 23 ] After the closing, Rozon and his father removed the items left behind in the basement and cleaned its white Masonite walls. They noticed no signs of leaking water.  They installed a piano and a sofa in the basement. [ 24 ] In February 2004, Allcut phoned up Hannan asking whether any water infiltrations had occurred in the basement and suggesting that the Buyers \\\"give the sump pump a knock\\\". [ 25 ] On March 5, 2004, Hannan noticed water pouring in through the back wall onto the basement floor, as well as crumbling stone and cement. [8] [ 26 ] The same day, the Buyers contacted their real estate agent. On the following day, they removed the wall panelling and noticed that the foundation wall was bulging. [ 27 ] The real estate agent visited the house on March 6 th . She suggested that there may be a latent defect and advised the Buyers to contact an expert in foundation and that they contact a lawyer. [ 28 ] On March 9, 2004, Hannan called Allcut to let her know of the problems and offered them the opportunity to visit the house to make their own observations. [9] [ 29 ] Given their concern about the structural capacity of the foundation walls, the Buyers installed posts to reinforce the structural support of the bulging wall. [ 30 ] They also contacted a number of foundation specialists. [10] These specialists recommended that they first obtain an expert opinion in respect to the structural capacity of the house. Hiring of expert Hosseini [ 31 ] On March 16, 2004, Dr. Mohammed Hosseini (\\\" Hosseini \\\"), a geotechnical engineer, visited the house. [ 32 ] At the time, the concrete blocks were visible since the Masonite walls had been removed. [11] [ 33 ] The bottom of the back foundation wall was seriously disintegrated.  Several cracks in the blocks and around the windows appeared to have been repaired with grout. Hosseini was unable to determine when these repairs were done. [ 34 ] He noticed several cracks in the masonry veneer covering the front external walls of the house.  Wooden studs behind the Masonite walls were in an advanced stage of rot while others showed no signs of deterioration and appeared to have been replaced three or four years earlier. [ 35 ] On March 18, 2004, Hosseini conducted further testing, including material sampling, water resistance testing and measurements of differential movements along the foundation walls. [ 36 ] He issued a report dated March 30, 2004. [12] He concludes that there are signs of distortion of the building and that the concrete block foundation was seriously damaged, with the bottom of the back wall completely disintegrated. He finds the quality of the concrete at the base of the foundation walls to be mediocre. He recommends that all foundation walls be replaced. [ 37 ] In his report, Hosseini criticizes the Inspector for failing to further investigate, after he reported the existence of cracks in the front masonry veneer and water infiltrations near the back stairwell. [ 38 ] In light of his findings, Hosseini recommends that the foundation be replaced to restore the structural integrity of the house, and that a new French drain be installed. Notices and letters of demand [ 39 ] Further to Hosseini's report, Hannan called Allcut to inform her of the expert's findings. [ 40 ] Despite this call, Vendors made no attempts to visit the premises. [ 41 ] Further to their silence, the Buyers put the Inspector on notice by letter of attorney dated March 23, 2004. [13] [ 42 ] On March 25, 2004, the Buyers' attorney also sent a letter of demand to the Vendors [14] , accusing Muir of bad faith in failing to disclose the latent defect affecting the foundation wall. This letter marked the beginning of a long legal battle between the parties. [ 43 ] A second letter dated June 18, 2004 [15] was sent to the Inspector, based on Hosseini's comments regarding his failure to conduct a further investigation after noticing cracks in the front masonry wall. [ 44 ] In a letter dated July 5, 2004 [16] , the Inspector denied any wrongdoing and raised the fact that the Buyers failed to retain a structural engineer notwithstanding his suggestion that they do so. Visit by Vendors' expert Beauchamp [ 45 ] On August 11, 2004, shortly before the repairs began, the Vendors sent their expert, Gilles Beauchamp (\\\" Beauchamp \\\"), an architect with a Masters degree in material aging and deterioration, to visit the premises. [17] [ 46 ] He conducted a 45 minute visit, during which he did not question Hannan about the representations made by the Vendors at the time of the sale, the condition of the house at the time, nor about whether the uncovered walls were visible at the time of the inspection. [ 47 ] Following this visit, Beauchamp prepared a written report in which he denies the existence of a latent defect since 30% of the basement walls were visible at the time of purchase, and given that there were visible signs of mold and cracks in repaired concrete blocks, some of which were even displaced. [18] [ 48 ] Beauchamp concludes that punctual repairs may be carried out and that it is not necessary to replace the foundation walls. He insists on the added value brought to the property as a result of the replacement of the existing foundation with a new poured concrete one. He also underlines the fact that the recommended repair work entails the addition of a new French drain, an insulated slab on grade and the replacement of wall membranes. Performance of repair work [ 49 ] In September 2004, Hénault & Gosselin, who were the lowest of two contractors invited to bid, carried out the repair work. [19] [ 50 ] Given changes brought to municipal regulations regarding foundations, the Buyers were required to replace the concrete block foundation with a poured reinforced concrete foundation. [20] [ 51 ] Because of the increased height of the foundations and the need to install a new front porch, the Buyers were also required to move the house back a few feet in order to meet municipal requirements, adding an extra $6,000 plus taxes to the cost of repairs. [21] [ 52 ] Although, Hénault & Gosselin's work took three weeks, the Buyers spent three months outside the home, living with Hannan's parents, given that the repair work entailed not only lifting the house, excavating and pouring a new foundation, but also the removal and replacement of the old furnace, oil and water tanks, sum pump and all electrical, telephone wires and other plumbing components. [ 53 ] Following the lifting and relocation of the house, new cracks appeared around the house, on the parget and inside, around doors and wall/ceiling intersections and mouldings. The Buyers also noticed air infiltrations around window openings. [ 54 ] At the time of the hearing, these new cracks and air infiltration problems had not been repaired. [22] Hosseini's complementary report [ 55 ] Hosseini went back to visit the house a few months prior to the hearing.  He prepared a complementary report dated December 18, 2008. [23] [ 56 ] In this report, he examines the outcome of the repair work and comments on the findings and conclusions of the Beauchamp report. In addition, he reviews the bids prepared by the contractors for the repair work. Evidence at the hearing [ 57 ] While both Rozon and Hannan testified at the hearing, Muir and Allcut did not. [ 58 ] Unfortunately, by the time the case was heard, Muir had passed away and the Court must rely on Muir's Ad Futur Memoriam Examination conducted on May 8, 2006, which provides rather limited insight into the relevant facts of the case. [ 59 ] Allcut was not able to testify at the time of the trial either, due to her poor health. [24] The transcripts of her Examination after plea were produced into the Court record. [ 60 ] In his testimony, Muir admits to having had one prior water infiltration, which he corrected immediately, as per the following extract of his Examination: \\\"That was at the back door and I had an expert bring a back-hoe in, clean off all the soil and the stuff, go down, find the leak, the crack in the cement, fix it and put the stuff back, and that was…that must have been eight, ten years ago […] then years ago at that stage, now we're talking three years under that. That was the only infiltration.\\\" [25] [ 61 ] During her Examination after plea, Allcut confirmed a single occurrence of water infiltration, which would have been resolved 20 years prior to the sale. [26] She also admits that some cracks were repaired around the same time in the stucco walls prior to the installation of aluminium siding on the house. [27] ISSUES TO BE DECIDED 1. Are the damages to the back foundation wall latent defects? 2. In the affirmative, what damages should be awarded to the Buyers? 3. Does the Buyers' claim constitute an abuse of ester en justice justifying the reimbursement of extrajudicial fees? ANALYSIS 1. Are the damages to the back foundation wall latent defects? Position of the parties [ 62 ] According to the Buyers, the defect in the foundation wall constitutes a latent defect given that it was serious, hidden and unknown to the buyer at the time of purchase and that the Vendors failed to divulge the excavation and repair work carried out on this wall prior to the sale. [ 63 ] The Vendors acknowledge that the back wall constitutes a serious defect, which existed prior to the sale. They contend however that the Buyers and their expert ignored visible cracks in the front masonry wall and water infiltration signs, which were pointed out by the Inspector. [ 64 ] In essence, according to the Vendors, the Buyers knew of the defect and should have expected that repairs would have to be carried out on the concrete block foundations if they wanted to convert the basement into a waterproof area. [ 65 ] Pursuant to Article 1726 C.C.Q. [28] which deals with the vendor's obligation of warranty of quality, in order to constitute a latent defect giving rise to a claim in damages, the alleged defect must: i) be serious ii) be unknown to the buyer at the time of the purchase iii) pre-date the purchase iv) not have been apparent to a prudent and intelligent buyer at the time of purchase. [ 66 ] In ABB Inc. c. Domtar Inc . [29] , the Supreme Court repeated these four requirements, further insisting on the fact that the nature of the defect must be appreciated objectively. [ 67 ] The buyer is required to demonstrate that he conducted a serious and reasonable inspection, either on his own or with an expert. [30] [ 68 ] Where such an expert has been retained, the Court is justified in examining the his findings to determine whether the inspection was in fact serious and reasonable and whether visible signs of defects were brought to the buyer's attention. [31] [ 69 ] The Court enjoys some discretion in its appreciation of what constitutes a reasonable inspection, while also taking in consideration subjective criteria such as the technical abilities of the buyer, the nature and age of the property [32] , as well as the agreed price: a lower price might actually serve as an indication that the buyer should be more cautious or prudent in his inspection. [ 70 ] That being said, neither the buyer nor his expert are obliged to take unusual measures such as digging in the foundations or opening walls and floors to discover defects, unless there...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n... ) All work done to the foundation by Hénault & Gosselin (Exhibit P-11 ). $54,723.14 p) Landscaping work, by Greenteam (Exhibit P-22 ). $11,157.42 q) Landscaping work completed by \\\"Les Gazons Verkest\\\" (Exhibit P-33 ). $9,650.60 [ 123 ] The percentage used for the calculation also takes into account the hard costs related to the work performed and the fact that a straight linear calculation of 25% (for the repair of one of four walls) would not accurately reflect the total cost of repairs for a single wall. [ 124 ] The Court sets aside for the purpose of calculating a reasonable value of price reduction, the following items which are not directly related to the repair of the failed back wall, but are rather the consequence of Buyers' decision to remove and replace the entire foundation: Cost of building permit from the City of Montreal (Exhibit P-8 ) $159.00 Movers fees to move the belongings and piano from the basement to the first floor, invoice Transport (Exhibit P-9 ) $264.56 Notary fees of Mtre France Sauvé, required to get a loan from the Bank (Exhibit P‑10 ). $590.00 All electrical work, removing wiring and breaker panels in addition of the reinstallation having to be done at the completion of all the work, by RPE Electric Services (Exhibit P‑13 ) $1,058.23 All plumbing work by Plomberie Mario Bastien (Exhibit P-14 ). $1,248.71 Five and a half yards of stone dust for outdoor plumbing by Sources Nursery (Exhibit P-15 ). $183.18 Rental of a vibrating plate for outdoor plumbing stone packing at Location d'outils Simplex (Exhibit P-16 ). $45.91 Disconnecting and reconnecting phone lines by Bell (Exhibit P-17 ). $96.06 Replacement of furnace, lining, oil tank and reconnections by Huile Norco (Exhibit P‑18 ). $5,469.44 All other work related to plumbing, gypsum, installation by Denis Rozon (Exhibit P‑19 ). $2,141.89 Reconnection of wiring, by Hydro-Québec (Exhibit P-20 ). $189.79 Masonry work and parging required to the front of the house due to damage of the lifting of the home by CMS (Exhibit P-21 ). $10,237.23 All other plastering and painting relating to the shifting of the walls, by Rooney and Moffat Inc. (Exhibit P-23 ). $11,853.33 TOTAL: $33,537.33 [ 125 ] Indeed, the Court finds that all work related to the removal and replacement of the foundation, including all removal, disconnection, re-installation of electrical, telephone wires and plumbing components as well as all work required to repair cracks and parging further to the lifting of the house were not directly caused by the failed back wall but are rather the result of Buyers' decision to remove and replace the entire foundation. [ 126 ] Furthermore, the replacement of the foundation leading to the relocation of the house towards the back of their property and the replacement of the existing French drain, which was not proven to be defective, are not the direct result of the latent defect.  Nor is the cost incurred for the permit, which would not likely have been required for punctual repairs. [ 127 ] Furthermore, the notary fees pertaining to the additional financing required for the corresponding increase in the mortgage were not direct and foreseeable damages. [ 128 ] In terms of additional damages over and above the price reduction, the Court is of the opinion that the Buyers are entitled to claim same, based on Article 1728 C.C.Q.  and in light of the Court's earlier findings regarding Muir's failure to divulge the repair work conducted on the failed wall. [ 129 ] The Court finds that the Buyers did suffer some inconvenience as a result of the discovery of the latent defect and the repairs that had to be carried out, for which it grants a total amount of $7,000, being $3,500 to each Plaintiff. [ 130 ] The Court denies however the Buyers' claim for future repairs.  None had been incurred at the time of the hearing and there has been no demonstration on the balance of probabilities that any such damages are related to the failed back wall. [ 131 ] Moreover, given the Court's conclusion that the cracks found in the masonry veneer were not related to the failed back wall, the Court is unable to award any additional damages in relation to eventual repairs of the walls. [ 132 ] In any event, given that the cracks in the masonry were validly reported in the pre-purchase inspection report, the Court is of the opinion that they were visible at the time of purchase and do not constitute a latent defect giving rise to a claim. 3.         Does the Buyers' claim constitute an abuse of ester en justice justifying the reimbursement of extrajudicial fees? [ 133 ] In V iel c. Les Entreprises immobilières du terroir limitée [50] , the Court of Appeal set out the exceptional circumstances in which a party may be held to the reimbursement of extrajudicial fees incurred by the other party in the context of civil proceedings. There must be a real abuse of proceedings such that the lawsuit is the pursuit of a non-existent right or constitutes the multiplication of dilatory or useless proceedings. [ 134 ] Under the circumstances, in light of the Court's finding that there was a latent defect justifying a claim in damages, the cross-demand is unfounded and is dismissed accordingly. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 135 ] GRANTS the Plaintiffs' action in part; [ 136 ] CONDEMNS the Defendants to pay Plaintiffs an amount of $37,000 in damages together with interest and additional indemnity calculated since March 25, 2004; [ 137 ] DISMISSES Defendants' cross-demand; [ 138 ] WITH COSTS , including expert fees. __________________________________ GENEVIÈVE MARCOTTE, J.S.C. M e Ricardo Hrtschan Attorney of Plaintiffs/Cross-Defendants M e Luc Morin and M e Sébastien Lebel Fasken Martineau DuMoulin Attorneys of Defendants/Cross-Plaintiffs Dates of hearing: February 5, 6, 9, and 10 th 2009 [1] Exhibit D-10 . [2] As per Listing filed as Exhibit P-3 and Promise to purchase filed under Exhibit P-46 . [3] Exhibit P-1 . [4] See p. 6 of Report filed under Exhibit P-1 and photograph taken at time of visit, filed under Exhibit P‑24A . [5] See Report filed under Exhibit P-1 , p. 7, under the heading \\\"Structure\\\". [6] Exhibit P-2 . [7] See Exhibit P-46 at paragr. 6.1, which refers to the Listing (Exhibit P-3 ). [8] See photograph filed under Exhibit P-48 . [9] Exhibit P-50 . [10] As per list of business cards filed under Exhibit P-51 . [11] See Photo no. 12 filed under Exhibit P-4A . [12] Exhibit P-4A . [13] Exhibit D-4 . [14] Exhibit P-5 . [15] Exhibit D-5 . [16] Exhibit D-6 . [17] See curriculum vitae filed under Exhibit D-8 . [18] Report dated August 27, 2004, filed under Exhibit D-1 . [19] See photographs during construction, filed under Exhibit P-40 . [20] See Municipal regulations filed under exhibit P-45 , article 3.2.3. [21] See Contract filed under Exhibit P-6A. [22] As per photograph filed under Exhibit P-42 . [23] Report dated December 18, 2008, filed under Exhibit P-4B . [24] As per letter by Dr. Dignazio of Manoir Pierrefonds dated January 13, 2009, filed under Exhibit D-9 . [25] At pages 24 and 255 of the transcripts of the Examination of Robert Muir held on May 8, 2006, pursuant to Article 404 C.p.c. [26] Transcripts of Stella Margaret Allcott (sic) Examination of November 21, 2005, at pages 6-7. [27] Id , at page 10. [28] See Article 1726 C .C.Q. which reads as follows: \\\"1726.   The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance.\\\" [29] 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461. [30] Lavoie c. Comtois , 1999 CanLII 11787 (QC CS) , [2000] R.D.I. 36 (C.S.). [31] See St-Louis c. Morin , 2006 QCCA 1643 (C.A.). [32] See Dragon c. Crépeau-Lavallée , 2009 QCCS, 1132 (C.S). [33] See Supra , note 32. [34] See Jacpar inc. c. Bensakour et al., 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , EYB 1989-63151 (C.A.). [35] See photographs filed under Exhibit P-35 , photos 6, 7, 8, 9,11, as well as Exhibits P-40 and P-44 , photo 5. [36] See report filed under Exhibit P-1 at page 6. [37] Exhibit P-24B . [38] Based on the limitations set out in the Engineers Act Engineers Act , R.S.Q., c. I-9, article 2: \\\" 2. Works of the kinds hereinafter described constitute the field of practice of an engineer: (…) (e)    the foundations, framework and electrical and mechanical systems of buildings the cost of which exceeds $100,000 and of public buildings within the meaning of the Public Buildings Safety Act (chapter S-3 ).\\\" [39] Article 1604 C.C.Q. reads as follows: \\\"     1604.            Where the creditor does not avail himself of the right to force the specific performance of the contractual obligation of the debtor ins cases which admit of it, he is entitled either to the resolution of the contract, or to its resiliation in the case of a contract of successive performance. However and notwithstanding any stipulation to the contrary, he is not entitled to resolution or resiliation of the contract if the default of the debtor is of minor importance, unless, in the case of an obligation of successive performance, the default occurs repeatedly, but he is then entitled to a proportional reduction of his correlative obligation. All the relevant circumstances are taken into consideration in assessing the proportional reduction of the correlative obligation . If the obligation cannot be reduced, the creditor is entitled to damages only.\\\" (Our underlining) [40] Article 1728 C .C.Q. reads as follows: \\\"1728.   If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer.\\\" [41] Pierre-Gabriel JOBIN et Michelle CUMYN , La vente , 3 e éd. Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2007, at p.239-241; see also authors Jean-Louis BAUDOUIN and Patrice DESLAURIERS, La responsabilité civile , Volume I - Principes généraux, Cowansville, 7 e édi., Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2007, paragr. 438, 439, 1057, 1058. [42] (2008) 2008 QCCA 1593 (CanLII) , R.J.Q. 2025 (C.A.), at paragr. 61 and following. [43] 2009 QCCA 1285 , at paragr. 91 ; see also Chevalier c. Gariépy , 1987 CanLII 726 (QC CA) , [1987] R.L. 626 (C.A.) and Charrette c. Côté , J.E. 2006-2324 (C.A.) . [44] See Verville c. 9146-7308 Québec inc. , supra note 41, parag. 78; see also Lahaie c. Laperrière , supra note 42, paragr. 58 and following. [45] See Pierre-Gabriel JOBIN and Michelle CUMYN, supra note 40, at p. 24: \\\" Une même situation factuelle peut parfois donner ouverture soit à l'action en annulation ou en diminution de prix sur le fondement du dol, soit à l'action résolutoire ou en diminution de prix en raison d'un vice caché, ce qui n'est pas sans avoir causé une certaine confusion dans la jurisprudence (infra no 173). Il importe pourtant de bien distinguer ces deux recours, afin de déterminer si les conditions d'exercice de l'un ou de l'autre sont réunies. La violation, par le vendeur, de la garantie de qualité constitue une inexécution du contrat qui donne lieu à un recours de l'acheteur en résolution ou en diminution de prix, même si le vendeur ignorait l'existence du vice affectant la chose vendue; s'il connaissait le vice, le vendeur est de plus tenu au versement de dommages-intérêts (article 1728, infra no 164). C'est là une première différence très importante entre ce recours et le recours pour dol, puisque, dans ce dernier cas, il faut aussi démontrer que le vendeur avait l'intention de tromper l'acheteur. Une deuxième différence concerne le domaine du dol, qui est beaucoup plus étendu que celui des vices cachés : le dol peut porter sur autre chose que la qualité du bien vendu, comme on vient de le voir .\\\" [46] See 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461, at paragr.107 to 109 reproduced as follows: \\\"107.  The trial judge found that C.E. had breached its duty to inform, whereas in the Court of Appeal’s view, the issue related to the warranty against latent defects. The two concepts overlap, but it is important to distinguish them in order to identify the circumstances in which each rule will be applied. 108. Whereas the warranty against latent defects is expressly provided for in the C.C.L.C. and the C.C.Q., the duty to inform derives instead from the general principle of good faith ( Bank of Montreal v. Bail Ltée , 1992 CanLII 71 (SCC) , [1992] 2 S.C.R. 554, at p. 586; arts. 6, 7 and 1375 C.C.Q.) and the principle of free and informed consent. Furthermore, the scope of the general duty to inform is much broader than that of the disclosure of a latent defect. This duty encompasses any information that is of decisive importance for a party to a contract, as Gonthier J. stated in Bail (see pp. 586‑87). It is therefore easy to imagine a situation in which a seller would be in breach of the duty even though no latent defect exists. 109. Where a seller fails to discharge the duty to disclose a defect, on the other hand, it can probably be said at the same time that he or she has also breached the general duty to inform the buyer of a factor of decisive importance in respect of the good sold, namely the existence of a latent defect. The instant case is one example of this. If a party invokes the seller’s warranty against latent defects, the duty to inform is in a sense subsumed in the analysis of the seller’s liability for latent defects, and there is no need for the court to conduct a separate analysis on the seller’s duty to inform.  As a result, our analysis and conclusion regarding C.E.’s liability under the warranty against latent defects are sufficient to dispose of the case before the Court.\\\" [47] Exhibit P-6A and exhibit P-11 . [48] Exhibit P-45 , article 3.2.3. [49] See supra notes 40 to 43. [50] [2002] CanLII 41120 (Q.C. C.A.), 22 octobre 2002....\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n69 ] The Court enjoys some discretion in its appreciation of what constitutes a reasonable inspection, while also taking in consideration subjective criteria such as the technical abilities of the buyer, the nature and age of the property [32] , as well as the agreed price: a lower price might actually serve as an indication that the buyer should be more cautious or prudent in his inspection. [ 70 ] That being said, neither the buyer nor his expert are obliged to take unusual measures such as digging in the foundations or opening walls and floors to discover defects, unless there is a visible sign that should have raised suspicion. [ 71 ] The vendor is responsible for such defect which, had it been known by the purchaser, would have caused the buyer to refuse to go forward with the purchase or to pay a lower price for the property being purchased. [33] [ 72 ] Where the vendor fails to denounce or disclose the defect at the time of the sale, he has the burden of proving that the buyer was fully aware of the defect.  Otherwise, the vendor will have to demonstrate that the defect was apparent and that the buyer should have noticed the defect at the time of his visit or pre-purchase inspection. [ 73 ] False representations made by the vendor to reduce the concerns of the buyer regarding visible signs of defects may lead a Court to conclude that an otherwise apparent defect is in fact latent. [34] Analysis [ 74 ] Applying those principles to the case, the Court finds that the failed back foundation wall was affected by a latent defect for the following reasons. [ 75 ] Firstly, while there were some prior visible signs of water which should have alerted the Buyers to the risk of future water infiltration, the Court is unable to conclude that these signs should have led a prudent buyer to realize that the foundation wall would fail altogether and need to be repaired within the year that followed the purchase. [ 76 ] Secondly, the Buyers knew that they were purchasing a home built in 1952.  They could not legitimately expect its concrete block foundation to be completely waterproof. They should not, however, be held to expect the disintegration of the bottom portion of the back foundation wall, to the extent revealed by the photographs taken during the repairs. [35] [ 77 ] Thirdly, the price of the house alone could not serve as an indication that there was a serious problem affecting it, in the absence of evidence that the asking price was lower than the market. [ 78 ] Fourthly, by minimizing the extent of the work carried out to remedy prior problems to the back wall foundation and by failing to report on the nature and extent of work carried out to repair cracks in said walls or to replace the wooden studs supporting the walls in the basement, the Vendors falsely reassured the Buyers. Their silence denied the Buyers the opportunity to investigate further and ascertain the need for extensive repairs, or to allow them to reconsider their purchase. [ 79 ] Had the Vendors been more transparent about the extent of repairs carried out in relation to the back foundation wall, they would have transferred onto the Buyers the burden to conduct their own investigation, especially given the location of the repairs in relation to water infiltration signs. [ 80 ] Yet, the Court is of the opinion that the Buyers could not altogether ignore the signs of water infiltration as well as the risk of water in the basement despite the representations in the listing about the high and dry basement and Muir's statement of only a single occurrence of water in the basement. [ 81 ] For one, the Inspector admitted at the hearing that he normally avoids relying on the answers given by elderly people selling their home, given their limited capacity to recollect events or repairs. [ 82 ] Also, the Buyers knew that there was \\\" active leakage at rear basement stairwell \\\", \\\" evidence of extensive prior leakage \\\" and \\\" medium probability of infiltration \\\" as reported by the Inspector. [36] His photographs [37] showed signs of humidity and corrosion on the sump pump pipes. [ 83 ] Moreover, Rozon admitted that he saw rust at the base of the furnace during the pre-purchase inspection and that there was mold around the perimeters of the basement, which he did not consider significant enough to address in the year following the purchase. [ 84 ] Furthermore, the Buyers and their Inspector knew that the foundation walls were made of concrete blocks which, according to Hosseini and Beauchamp, were vulnerable to water infiltration and required more constant repair and maintenance than a poured concrete foundation. [ 85 ] As a result, the Buyers could not legitimately expect the concrete block foundation to be waterproof in a more than 50 year old house. [ 86 ] As for the cracks in the front masonry veneer, those noted by the Inspector in his report were not qualified as \\\"hairline\\\" cracks. While the Buyers and the Inspector made serious efforts to convince the Court that the cracks noted by Hosseini in March 2004 had only appeared in the year that followed the purchase, the Court affords little credibility to their versions, which contradict the inspection report and are self-serving. [ 87 ] In terms of credibility, the Court cannot ignore the Inspector's own admission at the hearing that upon being served with Buyers' letter of demand in March 2004, he was willing to do anything to defend himself for the purpose of avoiding liability regarding the content of his report. [ 88 ] Hosseini himself expressed some scepticism regarding his clients' claim that the cracks suddenly appeared in the veneer one year after the purchase. Beauchamp acknowledges that the cracks had in all likelihood been present in the veneer for a number of years. [ 89 ] While Hosseini was of the opinion that the cracks in the veneer should have led a prudent inspector to investigate further, Beauchamp, who testified under reserve of the objection raised by the Buyers' attorney regarding his ability to testify on structural integrity, a practice domain reserved to engineers [38] , denied that the cracks could be interpreted as signs of weakness of the structural integrity of the house. [ 90 ] Even setting aside Beauchamp's testimony on the absence of relationship between the cracks in the masonry and the failed back foundation wall, the Court is unable to find a relation between the cracks noticed in the front masonry veneer and the existence of a latent defect affecting the back foundation wall, given their difference in location. 2.         What is the extent of the damages that may be claimed by the Buyers in the context of this action? The position of the parties [ 91 ] The Buyers allege that if they had been made aware of the problems, they would not have paid such a high price for the property. [ 92 ] They are claiming the full amount of repairs incurred that is, the sum of $111,225.21, which includes the removal and replacement of a concrete block foundation with a poured concrete one, all ancillary work, the replacement of the existing French drain, the removal and replacement of the oil tank, furnace, electrical, plumbing and telephone components, landscaping, etc., the professional fees incurred by the Buyers to increase their mortgaged loan in order to pay the repairs. [ 93 ] Their claim is best summarized in the following table: a) Cost of Dr. Mohammad Hosseini's report (Exhibit P-7 ). $1,265.28 b) Cost of building permit from the City of Montreal (Exhibit P-8 ). $159.00 c) Movers fees to move the belongings and piano from the basement to the first floor, invoice Transport (Exhibit P-9 ). $264.56 d) Notary fees of Mtre France Sauvé, required to get a loan from the Bank (Exhibit P‑10 ). $590.00 e) All work done to the foundation by Hénault & Gosselin (Exhibit P-11 ). $54,723.14 f) All mason work having been done to the chimney, fireplace and sealing of all cracks, by Maçonnerie LPG Enr. (Exhibit P-12 ). $891.44 g) All electrical work, removing wiring and breaker panels in addition of the reinstallation having to be done at the completion of all the work, by RPE Electric Services (Exhibit P-13 ). $1,058.23 h) All plumbing work by Plomberie Mario Bastien (Exhibit P-14 ). $1,248.71 i) Five and a half yards of stone dust for outdoor plumbing by Sources Nursery (Exhibit P-15 ). $183.18 j) Rental of a vibrating plate for outdoor plumbing stone packing at Location d'outils Simplex (Exhibit P-16 ). $45.91 k) Disconnecting and reconnecting phone lines by Bell (Exhibit P-17 ). $96.06 l) Replacement of furnace, lining, oil tank and reconnections by Huile Norco (Exhibit P‑18 ). $5,469.44 m) All other work related to plumbing, gypsum, installation by Denis Rozon (Exhibit P‑19 ). $2,141.89 n) Reconnection of wiring, by Hydro-Québec (Exhibit P-20 ). $189.79 o) Masonry work and parging required to the front of the house due to damage of the lifting of the home by CMS (Exhibit P-21 ). $10,237.23 p) Landscaping work, by Greenteam (Exhibit P-22 ). $11,157.42 q) Landscaping work completed by \\\"Les Gazons Verkest\\\" (Exhibit P-33 ). $9,650.60 r) All other plastering and painting relating to the shifting of the walls, by Rooney and Moffat Inc. (Exhibit P-23 ). $11,853.33 TOTAL: $111,225.21 [ 94 ] The Buyers also claim $20,000 for inconveniences suffered by the entire family in relation to the latent defects and repairs, as well as an additional amount of $50,000 for anticipated damages: they suspect that they will discover further damages when they remove the masonry veneer, which they have been unable to investigate, given their limited financial means and the costs of the ongoing litigation. [ 95 ] As part of their $20,000 claim, Hannan claims to have suffered form a major depression from May 29 to September 3, 2006, following Muir's examination of May 8, 2006, in which he admitted that he had carried out excavation work near the failed back wall, while he refrained from disclosing it at the time of sale. This depression caused her to stop work for three months, with a $3,600 shortfall in revenues for the period of work. [ 96 ] The Vendors deny that any of these repairs were the result of a latent defect.  They contend that the work went beyond the required remedial work and brought an added value to the house, which they should not be held to pay for.  Moreover, they contest any prior knowledge of the defect or bad faith on their part which could lead to additional damages. The law [ 97 ] When faced with a latent defect, article 1604 C.C.Q. [39] provides the buyer with a choice: he may seek to annul the sale or demand a price reduction. [ 98 ] Where the buyer is able to demonstrate that the vendor knew of the existence of the latent defect, Article 1728 C.C.Q. [40] also entitles him to seek additional damages suffered over and above a reduction in price, on the basis that the defect was known to him or that he could not ignore it at the time of the sale. [ 99 ] By seeking a price reduction, the buyer chooses to keep the property but asks that the price be reduced to take into consideration the defect. [ 100 ] Québec authors Jobin and Cumyn agree the price reduction cannot be disproportionate to the value of the property or to the price paid for same. [41] [ 101 ] In Verville c. 9146-7308 Québec inc. [42] as well as more recently in Lahaie c. Laperrière [43] , the Court of Appeal reiterated that in claiming a price reduction the buyer cannot enrich himself to the detriment of the vendor. [ 102 ] In assessing the value of the reduction, the Court may rely on the costs of repairs but it must also take into account all appropriate circumstances to determine what price reduction would have been reasonably applied at the time of purchase. [ 103 ] The Court must consider not only the cost of the repairs incurred but the value of the property sold, as well as the components of the property (land and building), and the fact that the buyer chose to keep the property notwithstanding the defect rather than seek the annulment of the transaction altogether.  This serves as a sign that the property does retain some value for the buyer. [44] [ 104 ] Authors Jobin and Cumyn have distinguished the annulment of transaction and/or reduction in price, from the claim in damages, insisting in this case on the buyer's burden to demonstrate the vendor's knowledge of the defect. [45] [ 105 ] When deciding whether the vendor knew or could not have ignored the defect, the Court may consider whether the vendor had the duty to inform the buyer of certain circumstances which would have led the buyer to investigate further and whether the vendor did perform his duty. [ 106 ] Indeed, in ABB inc. c. Domtar inc. [46] , while the Supreme Court of Canada distinguished the warranty of quality from the duty to inform, it did acknowledge that the duty to inform was subsumed in the Court's analysis of the vendor's liability for latent detects. Analysis [ 107 ] Given the fact that the latent defect relates to the failed back wall rather than to the entire foundation, the Court is unable to accept the Buyers' entire claim as being a direct result of the latent defect. [ 108 ] The Buyers' expert Hosseini recognized that punctual repairs to the failed back wall could be done, he recommended that the entire foundation be replaced rather than the failed back wall alone because it had the benefit of ensuring a permanent waterproof foundation. [ 109 ] At Court, Hosseini also justified his recommendation for the full removal and replacement of the foundation by the fact that the price submitted for the work by Hénault & Gosselin was reasonably low. [47] [ 110 ] Along the same lines, Contractor Hénault & Gosselin's representative, Yvon Malenfant, acknowledged that punctual repairs could be done at a lower cost, although they would not have entitled the Buyers to the same warranty protection. [ 111 ] Yet, as a result of the decision to replace the entire foundation, a poured concrete foundation had to be installed to comply with municipal regulations. [48] [ 112 ] To enable the lifting of the house, the removal and replacement of the foundation, the Buyers were also forced to disconnect, remove, as well as reinstall all heating, electrical, telephone wires and plumbing components, including the aging furnace, oil tank and sump pump, which the Buyers were expecting to eventually replace. [ 113 ] Given the increased height of the standard poured concrete foundation, the Buyers were also required to move the house back a few feet on their lot, to allow for the construction of a higher front porch. [ 114 ] Overall, the decision to replace the entire foundation rather than just repair the failed back wall had an important impact on the price of the total work as well as on the inconveniences suffered by the Buyers given that it a caused the Buyers to move out of the house for the duration of the work.  While the replacement of the foundation itself only lasted three weeks, the Buyers moved out of their home for three months during which the new connections and installations were completed. [ 115 ] During this time, the Buyers stayed with Hannan's parents, thus suffering a variety of inconveniences mostly related to the distance between their house and Hannan's parents. [ 116 ] While the Buyers' decision to remove and replace the entire foundation may have been logical and warranted, knowing that the house was more than 50 years old and had a concrete block foundation that would remain vulnerable to humidity from this point on, the Court cannot hold the Vendors to pay for all of this. [ 117 ] Granting the Buyers' entire claim would lead to an unjust enrichment on their part which the Court is unable to sanction as per the doctrine and case law cited extensively above [49] , particularly given that the amount of their entire claim is nearly the price paid for the house, which they will continue to enjoy for the future. [ 118 ] Moreover, the Court is unable to accept the Buyers' argument that the work brought no added value to their house. The work provided them with a brand new waterproof foundation (which they could not expect at the time of purchase), a new insulated slab, and a higher basement. This gives them the ability to eventually install brick walls in replacement of the masonry walls. [ 119 ] What amount is the Court justified to award under the circumstances? [ 120 ] In terms of the claim for price reduction, the Vendors' assessment of the work required for the punctual repair of the failed back wall at $5,000 is insufficient. The Court is of the opinion that the Buyers are entitled to claim a larger sum, which the Court sets at $30,000. [ 121 ] In the Court's opinion, this sum represents a fair and reasonable reduction in the sale price in light of all appropriate circumstances, including the cost of repairs, value of the entire property, the total price paid at the time of purchase and the Buyers' choice of remedy. [ 122 ] This determination represents 40% of the following amounts incurred for the replacement of the foundation and the repair of the landscape work, totalling $75,531.16, being: e) All work done to the foundation by Hénault & Gosselin (Exhibit P-11 ). $54,723.14 p) Landscaping work, by Greenteam (Exhibit P-22 ). $11,157.42 q) Landscaping work completed by \\\"Les Gazons Verkest\\\" (Exhibit P-33 ). $9,650.60 [ 123 ] The percentage used for the calculation also takes into account the hard costs related to the work performed and the fact that a straight linear calculation of 25% (\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.022504456341266632, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\", \"title\": \"Hannan c. Allcut, 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.022504456341266632, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\"}, \"token_count\": 12281, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Brabant c. Shaw, 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII) | Brabant c. Shaw | CanLII Brabant c. Shaw, 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII) Date: 2009-05-26 File number: 505-32-023039-077 Citation: Brabant c. Shaw, 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/240nh > Brabant c. Shaw 2009 QCCQ 5455 COURT OF QUÉBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF LONGUEUIL Civil Division No: 505-32-023039-077 DATE: May 26, 2009 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE VIRGILE BUFFONI, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ ISABELLE BRABANT et MICHEL CLOUTIER-BRABANT Plaintiffs vs. MARY SHAW Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Plaintiffs claim from the defendant the sum of $5,751.25, alleging a latent defect. facts [ 2 ] Plaintiffs purchased the defendant's property on August 24, 2005. [ 3 ] On December 23, 2005, the plaintiffs discovered a leak in the basement, pouring water on the electrical installations. The plaintiffs phoned the defendant on January 12, 2006, to inform her of the problem and ask her to come to the house and evaluate the origin of the leak and assess the damage. The defendant denied any involvement or responsability in the situation and refused to discuss the matter further. [ 4 ] Due to the urgency of the situation, more particularly the fire hazard, the plaintiffs proceeded with the repairs. [ 5 ] On January 5, 2006, the plaintiffs asked Mr. O'Saughnessy, who previously had inspected the house prior to the sale, to evaluate the situation. He found that the leak was due to an infiltration of water in the bathroom wall under the window. The insulation material was black and moldy. Due to the extent of the damage, he concluded that the leak had existed for a long time, well before the sale. [ 6 ] The plaintiffs obtained three estimates. All three contractors estimated the cost of the repairs between $4,350 to $5,500, plus all applicable taxes. [ 7 ] The plaintiffs hired the contractor with the lowest estimate to proceed with the repairs. Upon opening the wall, the contractor discovered that the wall studs were also black and moldy and the floor underneath the bathtub had deteriorated and had to be replaced. The final cost of the repairs reached $5,000, plus taxes. [ 8 ] The defendant testified that the bathroom was renovated in 2003. She was also unaware of any defect affecting the house or any indication of a defect at the time of the sale. [ 9 ] Article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec provides that « ( T)he seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them .» [ 10 ] The legal warranty against latent defects applies to the seller even though the seller has no knowledge wathsoever of the defect in the thing sold. [1] The fact that the inspector could not detect the defect prior to the sale corroborates both plaintiffs' and defendant's testimony as to the absence of any apparent defect or indication of same. [ 11 ] The defect discovered by the plaintiff was latent, serious, existing at the time of the sale and unknown to the buyers. The defect rendered the house unfit for the use it was intented or seriously diminished its usefulness. [ 12 ] The Court concludes that the house sold by the defendant to the plaintiffs contained a latent defect at the time of the sale, according to Article 1726 C.c.Q ., which entitles the plaintiffs to a reduction of the sale price. [ 13 ] In the instance, the Court will reduce the sale price by the cost of the repairs, which are not contested. WHEREFORE, THE COURT : [ 14 ] CONDEMNS the defendant to pay to the plaintiffs the sum of $5,751.25, with interest at the legal rate and the additional indemnity provided for in Article 1619 of the Civil Code of Québec since August 31, 2006, with costs (151 $). __________________________________ VIRGILE BUFFONI, J.C.Q. [1] A.B.B. inc. c. Domtar inc ., [2007] 3 R.S.C. 461....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02186981774866581, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\", \"title\": \"Brabant c. Shaw, 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02186981774866581, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\"}, \"token_count\": 1075, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq50\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq50-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Bulgakov c. Location Claireview, 2018 QCCQ 50 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2018 QCCQ 50 (CanLII) | Bulgakov c. Location Claireview | CanLII Date: 2018-01-05 File number: 500-32-148622-154 Citation: Bulgakov c. Location Claireview, 2018 QCCQ 50 (CanLII) Bulgakov c. Location Claireview 2018 QCCQ 50 COURT OF QUEBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF montreal Civil Division No: 500-32-148622-154 DATE: January 5, 2018 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE Catherine pilon, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ Alexey bulgakov Plaintiff v. location claireview s.e.n.c. Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Alexey Bulgakov claims $7,217.82 from Location Claireview S.E.N.C. (“Claireview”) where he purchased a motor vehicle. He claims that the vehicle was affected by a latent defect at the time of purchase, and that it was later improperly repaired by Claireview. The claim is itemized as follows: • Refund of faulty repairs $ 1,211.10 ; • Repair costs to remedy the latent defect $4,741.69 ; • Stress, inconvenience, and loss of enjoyment of the vehicle $5,000 . [ 2 ] Claireview contests the claim. In its view, Mr. Bulgakov was aware of a potential issue with the vehicle prior to purchase, the repairs performed by Claireview were adequate, and the repairs undertaken by Mr Bulgakov to remedy the latent defect were performed without prior notice. [ 3 ] The case was first scheduled for hearing on January 23, 2017. Although he was duly notified of the hearing date, Mr. Bulgakov was absent on said date, and a judgment by default was rendered. Mr. Bulgakov has filed an application to revoke this judgment. Claireview contests this application. [ 4 ] The Court will first address the application for revocation of the judgment, and then, as the case may be, the original proceeding on the merits. The Application for Revocation [ 5 ] Mr. Bulgakov is a software developer employed by a software development company. He recently immigrated to Canada and is not yet familiar or comfortable with the ramifications of the justice system. [ 6 ] He admits that he was aware that this case was scheduled for hearing on January 23, 2017 at 1:30 pm. However, he was advised by his employer late during the day on January 22, 2017, that he was required to travel to Toronto the next day on urgent business. [ 7 ] Mr. Bulgakov advised his brother, a witness he had called, in the evening, that he would not be able to attend the hearing, but he did not advise the Court the next morning, either by phone or email. He testifies that he was unaware of the procedure to request a postponement. [ 8 ] Judgment by default dismissing the claim was rendered in his absence at the January 23, 2017 hearing. Mr. Bulgakov became aware of the judgment on March 3, 2017 and filed his application for revocation on April 3, 2017. [ 9 ] Claireview contests the application and argues that Mr. Bulgakov should have advised the Court that he could not be present and request a postponement. This would have prevented Claireview representatives and witnesses to travel to Court and saved them the waiting time for the judgment by default to be rendered. [ 10 ] The criteria for an application in revocation of a judgment are contained in article 345 of the Code of Civil Procedure (“CCP”): 345. A judgment may, on a party’s application, be revoked by the court that rendered it if letting the judgment stand would tend to bring the administration of justice into disrepute. The judgment may be revoked, for instance, if fraud was committed by another party, if the judgment was based on false exhibits or if the production of decisive exhibits was prevented by superior force or by the act or omission of another party. (… ) [ 11 ] Article 347 CCP further provides that the application for revocation must be served to all the parties to the proceedings within 30 days of knowledge of the judgment, and presented within six months of this judgment: 347. An application for revocation must be served on all parties to the proceeding within 30 days after the day on which the cause preventing the party from filing a defence ceased to exist, or after the day on which the party became aware of the judgment, evidence or fact that constitutes grounds for the revocation. In the case of a minor, the 30-day period only begins to run as of notification of the judgment after the person reaches full age. The application for revocation must be presented before the court within 30 days after service, as if it were an application in the course of a proceeding. It cannot be presented if more than six months have elapsed since the judgment. These are strict time limits. [ 12 ] Pursuant to article 345 CCP, the concept of bringing “the administration of justice into disrepute” forms an integral part of the assessment of the application for revocation. It grants the judge a broad discretion. This discretion must nevertheless be exercised in a context where revocation is an exception to the rule that judgments are final and binding. Serious grounds are, as a result, required to justify revocation of a judgment. [ 13 ] Additionally, the Court must determine if the allegations of the original proceeding are on their face valid. [ 14 ] Mr. Bulgakov had a justified and unforeseen reason which explains why he was unable to be present at the hearing. While he would have been better advised to inform the Court of his absence and request a postponement, which likely would have been granted, the Court cannot find that Mr. Bulgakov was grossly negligent or that he acted in bad faith. [ 15 ] Moreover, the allegations of the claim are serious. The Court must also consider this fact in its assessment of the merits of the application for revocation. [ 16 ] According to article 347 CCP, Mr. Bulgakov had 30 days from becoming aware of the judgment, on March 3, 2017, to file his application for revocation. This 30-day delay expired on April 2, 2017, a Sunday, thus extending the delay to the next day, i.e. April 3, 2017, when Mr. Bulgakov filed his application. The application for revocation was therefore filed within the delays of article 347 CCP. [ 17 ] In the Court’s view, given that Mr. Bulgakov could not attend the hearing for valid professional reasons, and that on its face the claim is serious, maintaining the judgment rendered by default would tend to bring the administration of justice into disrepute. The application for revocation is granted. The Original Application Questions at issue A. Was the Vehicle affected by a latent defect at the time of purchase? B. Were the repairs performed by Claireview faulty? C. Was prior notice of the defect required before the repairs were performed? D. What are the damages suffered by Mr. Bulgakov? Context [ 18 ] On February 20 th , 2015 Mr. Bulgakov purchased, a 2008 Range Rover (the “Vehicle”) from Claireview for $27,562.50. The odometer was then at 65,758 km. [ 19 ] The Vehicle was inspected by Claireview before the purchase and was declared good mechanical condition. [ 20 ] Two days after the sale, on February 22, 2015, a yellow light appeared on the dashboard with the message “Suspension Fault” and “Pressure is not rising quick enough”. [ 21 ] At the same time, the front of the vehicle was lowered and it was impossible to continue driving. [ 22 ] The same day, Mr. Bulgakov took a picture of the error message and sent it by email to Albert Bensimon, his sales representative at Claireview. Mr. Bulgakov wrote that he got “the dreaded suspension fault icon” on his dashboard. He asked for an appointment which was scheduled on February 24, 2015. [ 23 ] Mr. Bulgakov met with Mr. Bensimon and Mark Hastie, the Service Manager, and explained that the suspension fault message was intermittent and appeared every time after 20-30 minutes of continuous driving. [ 24 ] He was advised that Claireview could not identify the issue unless the fault message on the dashboard was permanently lit. Mr. Bulgakov was further told that it was safe to continue driving the Vehicle, as long as the light did not become permanent. [ 25 ] On May 13, 2015, while driving back to Montreal from Quebec City, at a speed of 100km/h, the suspension fault message appeared once again, this time with a red light, which meant a critical fault. The air suspension then automatically turned off, and the Vehicle started to bounce and became difficult to control. The computer set the speed limit to 45 km/h, the Vehicle adjusted itself and was driven back. [ 26 ] There was heavy traffic on the highway and Mr. Bulgakov and his passenger, his wife, were quite scared. [ 27 ] Mr. Bulgakov brought the Vehicle to Claireview on June 1 st , 2015, for an inspection. [ 28 ] On June 9, 2015, Mr. Hastie informed Mr. Bulgakov that there were of a few minor mechanical issues to fix in relation to replacement of one bushing, and that they had determined that the suspension fault was caused by the compressor which needed replacement at a cost of $1,400. The parties agreed that they would split these costs. [ 29 ] On June 12, 2015, Mr. Bulgakov recovered his Vehicle and paid $1,211.10 for the repairs, including the new compressor. [ 30 ] The next few days, the suspension fault message appeared once again. Mr. Bulgakov informed Claireview of this problem on June 15, 2015. [ 31 ] On June 22, 2015, Claireview conducted a new inspection. On June 30, 2015, Mr. Hastie wrote that they were not able to fix the suspension issue, and that Mr. Bulgakov needed to bring his vehicle to an official Land Rover dealership. [ 32 ] Mr. Bulgakov then brought the Vehicle to Jaguar Land Rover Laval (“Laval”), a Land Rover dealership. [ 33 ] On July 15, 2015, following a thorough inspection, Laval determined that: - The new compressor installed by Claireview was in fact refurbished and incompatible with the Vehicle model and needed replacement at a cost of $1,211.10, before tax and labour; - All bushings required replacement to eliminate the noise coming from the suspension. Laval also concluded that none of the four bushings had ever been replaced; - The front height adjustment sensors were defective and caused the suspension fault; - The Vehicle could not be driven until all repairs were performed. [ 34 ] Mr. Bulgakov was invoiced and paid Laval $4,741.69 for the repairs. The odometer indicated 69,619 km when the Vehicle was delivered back to him by Laval. [ 35 ] On July 22, 2015, Mr. Bulgakov sent Claireview a demand letter claiming all repair costs as well as $5,000 for moral damages. Analysis A. Was the Vehicle affected by a latent defect at the time of purchase? [ 36 ] Pursuant to article 1716 of the Civil Code of Quebec (the “CCQ”), the seller must deliver the property and warrant its quality [1] . [ 37 ] A claim for damages pursuant to the warranty of quality requires the buyer to prove a number of conditions contained in article 1726 CCQ : 1. The existence of a hidden defect at the time of the sale; 2. The hidden aspect of the defect, i.e. that it is not apparent; 3. The ignorance of the defect by the buyer; 4. The importance of the defect such that the buyer would not have purchased the property or had paid so high a price had he been aware of it. [ 38 ] In respect of the existence of the defect at the time of the sale, article 1729 CCQ provides: 1729. In a sale by a professional seller, a defect is presumed to have existed at the time of the sale if the property malfunctions or deteriorates prematurely in comparison with identical property or property of the same type; such a presumption is rebutted if the defect is due to improper use of the property by the buyer. [ 39 ] The issue with the suspension manifested itself two days after the sale. This creates a strong presumption that the problem existed at the time of the sale. As a professional seller, Claireview had the onus to prove that the malfunction of the Vehicle was caused by something other than a defect, for instance an improper use by Mr. Bulgakov. No such evidence was adduced and the Court finds that a defect existed at the time of the sale. [ 40 ] In terms of the hidden nature of the defect, Claireview itself inspected the Vehicle prior to the sale and did not note an issue with the suspension. In addition, Mr. Bulgakov brought the Vehicle to Claireview on three occasions, February 24, June 1, and June 30, 2015, for an inspection in relation to the suspension, and they were never in a position to identify the issue. This further supports that the defect with the suspension was hidden. [ 41 ] Claireview argues that Mr. Bulgakov was aware of the defect based on his February 22 email in which he mentions the “dreaded suspension fault icon”. The use of the word dreaded leads Claireview to believe that Mr. Bulgakov knew that his Vehicle had the potential for a suspension problem. However, Mr. Bulgakov testified at the hearing that his use of this word meant that the suspension fault was unexpected. The Court does not consider that the use of the word dreaded is conclusive of knowledge, particularly in the context where English is not Mr. Bulgakov’s first language. [ 42 ] Finally, it is evident to the Court that the defect was important. The suspension of a vehicle is not an accessory, and the incidents that Mr. Bulgakov suffered on the road because of the defective suspension could have led to serious accidents. [ 43 ] In light of the above, the Court finds that the Vehicle was affected by a hidden defect at the time of the Sale. B. Were the repairs performed by Claireview faulty? [ 44 ] Section 176 of the CPA provides that automobile repairs are guaranteed by the merchant for a period of three months or 5,000 km, whichever occurs first: 176. Repairs are guaranteed for three months or 5 000 kilometres, whichever occurs first. The guarantee takes effect upon the delivery of the automobile. [ 45 ] Both case law and legal authors agree that this is a guarantee of result which triggers a presumption of faulty repairs for which the merchant is liable. [ 46 ] The Vehicle was brought to Claireview on three occasions after the sale for verifications. Repairs were performed following the June 1 visit in an attempt to solve the suspension issue. The Vehicle was delivered to Mr. Bulgakov on June 12, and the suspension problem reoccurred a few days later, less than three months after the repairs. [ 47 ] The Vehicle was initially purchased from Claireview with an odometer at 65,758 km. The “kilometrage” of the Vehicle when it was delivered on June 12 following the repairs is not known, but the July 15 Laval invoice indicates 69,619 km, less than 5,000 km from purchase in February. It is therefore obviously safe to conclude that less 5,000 km were driven since June 12. [ 48 ] As a result of the above, the guarantee of section 176 of the CPA is applicable. Given that the Vehicle malfunctioned notwithstanding the repairs within the period covered by section 176 of the CPA, the repairs are deemed faulty and Claireview is liable. It is not necessary to address whether the compressor that was installed by Claireview was new or refurbished since, in any event, it was not useful in resolving the suspension issue. C. Was prior notice of the defect required before the repairs were performed? [ 49 ] Claireview blames Mr. Bulgakov for having had the repairs performed without giving prior notice. This argument is unfounded for two reasons. First, the objective of the notice is to allow the seller to inspect a vehicle to determine what causes the malfunction. In this case, Claireview had the Vehicle in its possession on three occasions before the Laval repairs. It had ample opportunity to inspect it and is not prejudiced by the absence of prior notice. Second, it is at the specific recommendation of Claireview that the Vehicle was taken to Laval to identify and resolve the suspension issue. D. What are the damages suffered by Mr. Bulgakov? [ 50 ] Mr. Bulgakov has proven that the amount of $4,741.69 to remedy the latent defect was justified. Claireview is liable to him for this amount. [ 51 ] Claireview is also liable for the cost of the ineffective June repairs of $1,211.10. [ 52 ] Mr. Bulgakov also claims damages of $5,000 for stress, inconvenience, and loss of enjoyment of the Vehicle. [ 53 ] Article 1728 CCQ provides that other than restoring the price or reducing it to take into account the impact of the latent defect on the value of the property, in this case the Laval repairs, the injury suffered by the buyer may also be repaired. Claireview, as a professional seller with deemed knowledge of the latent defect, is liable to Mr. Bulgakov for the injury he has suffered as a result of this defect. [ 54...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... for stress, inconvenience, and loss of enjoyment of the Vehicle. [ 53 ] Article 1728 CCQ provides that other than restoring the price or reducing it to take into account the impact of the latent defect on the value of the property, in this case the Laval repairs, the injury suffered by the buyer may also be repaired. Claireview, as a professional seller with deemed knowledge of the latent defect, is liable to Mr. Bulgakov for the injury he has suffered as a result of this defect. [ 54 ] Additionally, the failure to determine the issue with the suspension and the faulty repairs, which lasted close to five months, also caused injury to Mr. Bulgakov. [ 55 ] The Court finds that Mr. Bulgakov suffered injury due to the stress related to the unreliability of the Vehicle, as well as a loss of enjoyment of his Vehicle which was kept at Claireview premises on three occasions for several days. The Court assesses these damages at $500. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT : GRANTS the Application for revocation of the Plaintiff; GRANTS in part the Application of the Plaintiff; CONDEMNS the Defendant to pay the Plaintiff $6,452.79 with interest at the legal rate and the additional indemnity of article 1619 of the Civil Code of Quebe c since the demand letter of July 22, 2015; CONDEMNS the Defendant to pay the Plaintiff the Court costs of $200. __________________________________ CATHERINE PILON, j.c.q. Date of hearing: September 18, 2017 [1] The Consumer Protection Act (the “CPA”) contains a similar warranty of quality and of good working order for a contract of sale of a used automobile. Given the conclusions of the Court in respect of the existence of a hidden defect within the meaning of the CCQ, it is not necessary to examine the provisions of the CCQ on this part of the claim....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq50-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02490842528641224, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq50-en\", \"title\": \"Bulgakov c. Location Claireview, 2018 QCCQ 50 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq50-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02490842528641224, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq50-en\"}, \"token_count\": 4675, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq50-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2005canlii31818\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2005canlii31818-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Cooke v. Aslam, 2005 CanLII 31818 (QC CQ)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2005 CanLII 31818 (QC CQ) | Cooke v. Aslam | CanLII Cooke v. Aslam, 2005 CanLII 31818 (QC CQ) Date: 2005-07-21 File number: 500-32-082776-040 Citation: Cooke v. Aslam, 2005 CanLII 31818 (QC CQ), < https://canlii.ca/t/1lkmz > JP1736 COURT OF QUEBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL TOWN OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32-082776-040 DATE: July 21, 2005 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE MICHEL A. PINSONNAULT, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ BRENDA COOKE, 1450, Shevchenko, Apt. 1, LaSalle (Québec)  H8N 1P1 Plaintiff v. MOHAMMAD ASLAM, 4390, Kingston, Pierrefonds (Québec) H9A 2S9 Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] On March 30, 2004, Plaintiff, Brenda Cooke (“ Cooke ”), bought from Defendant, Mohammad Aslam (“ Aslam ”), a 1989 Toyota Corolla (the “ Toyota ”) for $750.00. [ 2 ] Within 10 days from the purchase, the Toyota stopped functioning completely.  In fact, the automatic transmission could only function in reverse mode at very low speed. [ 3 ] Two different inspections by mechanics revealed that the transmission was entirely finished and had to be replaced at a cost greater than the market value of the Toyota (equipped with a functioning transmission).  The mechanics recommended against the replacement as the Toyota’s market value was significantly less than the cost of replacing the transmission even with a used one. [ 4 ] Cooke immediately contacted Aslam telling him that the transmission was finished and that the Toyota could not operate without her investing a substantial amount of money.  Had she known that fact, she would have never agreed to purchase the vehicle.  She wants to cancel the sale forthwith. [ 5 ] Aslam refuses categorically on the basis that the Toyota was sold on an “ as is basis ” and that as she drove away with it, it became her sole responsibility and problem.  He will not respond to her letter of demand dated April 20, 2004. [ 6 ] Although the parties did not enter into any written contract of sale, Aslam nevertheless maintains that he sold the Toyota on an “ as is basis ”.  He is therefore not responsible for mechanical problems which may have occurred thereafter. [ 7 ] Concerning the possibility for a vendor to exclude or limit his liability stemming from the warranty against latent defects, article 1733 of the Civil code of Quebec (the “ Civil code ”) stipulates : 1733. A seller may not exclude or limit his liability unless he has disclosed the defects of which he was aware or could not have been unaware and which affect the right of ownership or the quality of the property . An exception may be made to this rule where a buyer buys property at his own risk from a seller who is not a professional seller . (emphasis added) [ 8 ] This particular issue has been examined by the courts on many occasions. [ 9 ] For example, it was decided that a similar expression « accepted as seen » ( accepté tel que vu ) inserted in a contract for the sale contract of used goods, does not constitute a waiver or an exclusion of the legal warranty against latent defects ( article 1726 of the Civil code ).  Such an expression must be interpreted restrictively and relates exclusively to apparent defects. [1] [ 10 ] In the present case, the Court is of the view that the expression « sold as is » or “ sold on an as is basis ” covers only the defects that were apparent at the time of the sale and does not have the same meaning than “ buying at his own risk ” stipulated in article 1733 . [ 11 ] Only the words “ buying at his own risk ” may effectively exclude “ a seller who is not a professional seller ” from his liability as to the legal warranty on latent defects. [2] [ 12 ] It was also decided that the use of the expression « tel que vu et examiné ( as seen and examined )” in a sale contract, does not justify selling an automobile that is inoperative. [3] [ 13 ] In the case Bourget vs Martel [4] , the Court of Appeal dealt with a similar situation : Celui qui achète une automobile usagée a le droit de s’attendre à recevoir une automobile qui fonctionne normalement et non pas une voiture sur laquelle il faudra dépenser quelques centaines de dollars pour la mettre en état de s’en servir.  Les mots « tel que vu et examiné » dans un contrat d’achat d’une automobile usagée ne sont pas suffisants pour justifier la livraison d’une voiture aussi défectueuse que celle qui, dans l’espèce, a été livrée au demandeur.  Certes, il eut été préférable que l’acheteur avant d’accepter une automobile usagée la fit examiner par un homme compétent, mais il ne faut pas en conclure que la vente ne doit pas être annulée parce que l’acheteur a eu trop confiance en son vendeur. [ 14 ] There is always a certain amount of risks involved when one buys a 15 year-old automobile and one may reasonably expect to incur additional repair costs as a result thereof.  However, when one purchases a used car, one can reasonably expect it to perform, during a reasonable period (which may vary given its age), what it was designed to do, namely transport people and merchandise under its own power and motivity. [ 15 ] In the present case, Cooke thought that she was purchasing an automobile that functioned.  She agreed to pay $750.00 to Aslam for an automobile that functioned, albeit the expected lifespan of the Toyota may have been relatively short given its advanced age.  However, Cooke was entitled to expect that the Toyota’s expected lifespan would be longer than 10 days. [ 16 ] In light of the fact that the Toyota became totally inoperative within 10 days from Cooke acquiring it due to a total failure of the automatic transmission, the Court can only come to the conclusion that the Toyota was affected, at the date of the sale, with a major latent defect within the meaning of article 1726 [5] of the Civil code .  In other words, the transmission problem which affected the Toyota, at the time, rendered it unfit for the use for which it was intended. [ 17 ] The fact that Aslam was apparently unaware of the existence of the transmission problem is not relevant, for the purposes hereof.  A latent defect does not have to be known to the seller to engage his liability.  However, with all due respect, the Court seriously doubts that Aslam was indeed unaware of such a major defect. [ 18 ] The Court therefore concludes that Cooke was sold an automobile that was unfit for the use for which it was intended and that the sale was made with the warranty against latent defects as Aslam failed to exclude his liability stemming from it, for the reasons stated above. [ 19 ] Had Cooke known the fact that the automatic transmission would have to be replaced within a few days, she would have never agreed to pay to Aslam $750.00 for an automobile that was practically worthless and useless, at the time, unless an amount of money exceeding significantly its market value was invested. [ 20 ] In light of the foregoing, Cooke is entitled to the cancellation of the sale of the Toyota and to recover the sale price ($750.00). [ 21 ] There remains one issue to resolve. [ 22 ] Cooke explained that as soon as she discovered that the Toyota was no longer driveable, she offered it back to Aslam who refused it.  She then had it stored at a garage station pending the resolution of this case. [ 23 ] Even at trial, Aslam refused the Toyota as it was no longer his responsibility and problem since he sold it to Cooke. [ 24 ] In any event, during the month of December 2004, the garage owner told her that the Toyota could no longer remain on storage on his site.  Having very limited means and given the absence of value of the Toyota and the steadfast refusal of Aslam to accept the automobile, she had no other choice but to send the Toyota to the scrap yard. [ 25 ] Under normal circumstances, when a sale is cancelled, the purchaser must return (or restitute) the good sold to the vendor. [ 26 ] On that specific issue, article 1699 of the Civil code stipulates : 1699. Restitution of prestations takes place where a person is bound by law to return to another person the property he has received, either unlawfully or by error, or under a juridical act which is subsequently annulled retroactively or under which the obligations become impossible to perform by reason of superior force . The court may, exceptionally, refuse restitution where it would have the effect of according an undue advantage to one party, whether the debtor or the creditor, unless it deems it sufficient, in that case, to modify the scope or mode of the restitution instead . (emphasis added) [ 27 ] On the basis of article 1699 of the Civil code , the Court is of the view that the exceptional circumstances herein justify the application of the provisions of its second paragraph, which enables the Court to dispense Cooke with the restitution of the Toyota. [ 28 ] In the present circumstances, Cooke was entitled to the cancellation of the sale made on March 30, 2004, having purchased an automobile that was useless and worthless at the time, given the state of its automatic transmission that had to be replaced at a cost greater than the market value of the Toyota if equipped with a functioning transmission. [ 29 ] The Court is of the view that Aslam knew or should have known about the state of the transmission which was about to surrender and had to be replaced. [ 30 ] In brief, Aslam knew or should have known that he sold to Cooke an automobile that was no longer fit to perform the use for which it was intended.  In other words, he sold an object that no longer served as an automobile, an object that no longer had a market value, given its state and condition; as, to render it operative again, one had to invest in a transmission at a cost greater than the actual market value for a functioning automobile of that age. [ 31 ] Under such circumstances, to prevent the cancellation of the sale due to Cooke’s impossibility to restitute the Toyota would confer upon Aslam an undue benefit as he knew or should have known that he was selling for $750.00 an automobile that had no real market value given its mechanical condition.  His refusal to accept the Toyota back was unreasonable under the present circumstances and forced Cooke to incur unnecessary costs during the present proceedings, which were out of proportion with the actual market value of the automobile. [ 32 ] Given the position adopted by Aslam and the absence of value of the Toyota, Cooke was justified to dispose of a worthless automobile during legal proceedings and cannot be held responsible for this. [ 33 ] The Court is of the view that Cooke’s impossibility to restitute the Toyota under the particular circumstances herein is fully justifiable and does not preclude her from cancelling the sale and recovering the sale price for an automobile, which no longer was an automobile, given its state and condition, and was therefore worthless. FOR THOSE REASONS, THE COURT: GRANTS Plaintiff's action, CONDEMNS the Defendant, Mohammad Aslam, to pay to Plaintiff, Brenda Cooke, the sum of $750.00 with interest thereon at the legal rate and the additional indemnity stipulated in article 1619 of the Civil Code of Québec from April 20, 2004, date of the letter of demand, THE WHOLE with costs of $63.00. __________________________________ MICHEL A. PINSONNAULT, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: July 20, 2005 [1] Forest vs. Fossberg , (C.Q., 2003-04-28), SOQUIJ AZ-50172355 Assurances générales des Caisses Desjardins inc. vs. Johri , (C.Q., 2001-12-07), SOQUIJ AZ-50111367, B.E. 2002BE-201 Lorrain vs. Auto Sans Limite inc. , (C.Q., 2001-04-24), SOQUIJ AZ-50187831 Artisans du paysage du Québec inc. vs. Roux paysagistes 2000 inc. , (C.Q., 2000-10-25), SOQUIJ AZ-50187683 Gagnon vs. Langlois , (C.Q., 2000-11-23), SOQUIJ AZ-50081296 Varin vs. Laprade , (C.Q., 1998-01-05), 1998 CanLII 11154 (QC CQ) , SOQUIJ AZ-98031103, J.E. 98-598, R.E.J.B. 1998-05724 Baron vs. Servant , (C.Q., 2002-08-14), 2002 CanLII 13657 (QC CQ) , SOQUIJ AZ-50143669, J.E. 2002-1890 Laforge vs. Bernier , (C.Q., 2001-07-18), SOQUIJ AZ-50187921 [2] Lamy vs. Vaillancourt , (C.Q., 1996-09-05), SOQUIJ AZ-50188137 [3] Beaudin vs. Ward , (C.Q., 1999-07-13), SOQUIJ AZ-50188410 [4] [1995] B.R. 659 [5] 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. (emphasis added)...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2005canlii31818-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.026631392538547516, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2005canlii31818-en\", \"title\": \"Cooke v. Aslam, 2005 CanLII 31818 (QC CQ)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2005canlii31818-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.026631392538547516, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2005canlii31818-en\"}, \"token_count\": 3267, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2005canlii31818-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette, 2009 QCCQ 472 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2009 QCCQ 472 (CanLII) | Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette | CanLII Date: 2009-02-03 File number: 550-32-015780-072 Citation: Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette, 2009 QCCQ 472 (CanLII) Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette 2009 QCCQ 472 COURT OF QUEBEC «Small Claims Division» CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF HULL TOWN OF GATINEAU Civil Division No: 550-32-015780-072 DATE: February 3 rd , 2009 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE ROSEMARIE MILLAR, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ ANTHONY GILCHRIST […] Kanata (Ontario) […] Plaintiff v. DENISE ALBERT BRUNETTE […] Gatineau (Québec)  […] Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] The plaintiff, Anthony Gilchrist, claims from the defendant, Denise Albert Brunette, the amount of 7 000,00 $ for latent defect (« vice caché ») discovered after buying a property from the defendant. [ 2 ] On July 28 th , 2006, the plaintiff purchased the defendant's residence located at […], in Gatineau. [ 3 ] The house, built approximately 50 years ago, was purchased for the amount of 166 000,00 $. [ 4 ] The plaintiff did visit the house a few times (at least 9 times according to the defendant which is denied by the plaintiff), before the purchase and, on one occasion, with a friend who had some experience in construction. [ 5 ] According to the defendant, the plaintiff was told that, in the year 2000, repairs had to be done on the foundation after a water infiltration (see invoice D-3). [ 6 ] According to the plaintiff, this information was given by the defendant only after the purchase when the plaintiff discovered water infiltration in the basement. [ 7 ] A few days after the purchase, the plaintiff took possession of the house and began some painting. [ 8 ] Shortly after, after an heavy rain, the plaintiff discovered water on the basement floor. [ 9 ] The plaintiff removed the « subfloor » in the basement and the panelling on the wall and discovered water leaking in 3 different places. [ 10 ] The plaintiff did not expected to find water infiltration in the house. [ 11 ] After talking to his notary, the plaintiff called the defendant and asked her to come in order to find a solution. [ 12 ] After series of communications and estimates, the matter could not be settle between the parties. [ 13 ] The plaintiff had to hire a contractor to repair the leaks and the cost was            11 964,75 $. [ 14 ] The plaintiff incured expenses for a total amount of 28 000,00 $ to repair the basement. [ 15 ] The plaintiff claims that the latent defect is covered by the legal warranty when he purchased the house. [ 16 ] According to the plaintiff, he did not know or see, before purchasing the house, that there was water infiltration. [ 17 ] To the knowledge of the defendant, except for the one in 2000, there was no other water infiltration in the basement. [ 18 ] The defendant, a widow, sold her house with the help of her son without a real estate agent. [ 19 ] According to the defendant's son the water infiltration which occured in 2000 and the repair done at the time were mentioned to the plaintiff as they were for all the visitors who came to see the house. [ 20 ] Mr. Daniel Levert, technologist in civil engineering for 30 years and home inspector for 15 years, visited the house on August 21 st , 2006, and prepared a report in which he concluded that there were obvious signs of wear and tear and deficiencies regarding drains and « hydrofuge barrier ». [ 21 ] According to Mr. Levert, the purchase's price of the house was below the market's value and the obvious signs of water infiltration's problem should prompt a diligent purchaser to obtain an expertise before buying such 50 years old house. ANALYSIS [ 22 ] Sections 1726 and 1728 of the Civil Code of Quebec state: \\\"Art. 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use of which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect;  an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. Art. 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer.\\\" [ 23 ] To win a claim for latent defect, the buyer needs to prove that the defect was harmful, serious, not apparent, that the defect existed before the sale and that it was unknown to the buyer. [ 24 ] The evidence presented by the defendant is to the effect that the plaintiff was informed that a water infiltration occured in 2000.  This evidence is denied by the plaintiff. [ 25 ] Nevertheless, even without this contradictory element, evidence presented before the court is to the effect that: 1. The house was 50 years old; 2. The price of the purchase was below the value of the market; 3. No inspection was done by an expert before the sale; 4. There were signs of apparent deficiencies related to water infiltration as it shows from the home inspector's report: « Lors de notre visite nous avons fait certaines observations au périmètre entier du sous-sol et dans la salle de lavage qui avait les murs non fini [sic] au moment de l'achat. Dans la salle de lavage il y avait des cernes d'eau au coin et de la peinture soulevé [sic] par des cristaux d'efflorescence venant du mur de la fondation.  De l'efflorescence était aussi visible au niveau du plancher de béton. L'efflorescence (dépôt de sels sur la surface) est un phénomène provoqué par un béton chargé d'eau, rejettant [sic] l'humidité dans l'air ambiant du sous-sol.  Cela peut contribuer à des excès d'humidité dommageables.  L'efflorescence témoigne souvent d'un manque d'étanchéité de l'hydrofuge appliqué du côté extérieur des fondations ou d'une déficience du drainage des fondations (ex.:  position incorrecte du drain perforé, obstruction partielle ou totale du conduit, etc.), ce qui occasionne une absorption anormale de l'eau souterraine par le béton.  L'imperméabilisant à fondation est un composé organique et il est normal qu'il se désagrège avec le temps. Compte tenu de l'âge de la construction, il est fort probable que la barrière hydrofuge de la fondation ne soit plus efficace.  La fondation est donc soumise à l'humidité du sol. Il y avait autre [sic] signes apparent [sic] d'une [sic] manque d'étanchéité de la fondation. Les cernes d'eau visibles dans le coin de la salle de lavage.  Une fissure apparente à la fondation sous le palier de l'entrée avant, endroit ou le propriétaire avait observé une infiltration de l'intérieur. Des cernes d'eau à la base du revêtement intérieur en panneaux préfinis ainsi que l'oxydation des clous et les moisissures au quart de rond sont aussi des indices apparents d'un problème d'imperméabilisation de la fondation. L'inspecteur ne peut se prononcer avec exactitude sur cette condition, car seul un sondage du côté extérieur (excavation partielle) pourrait nous informer sur la qualité du drainage des fondations. En conclusion il était sans doute évident qu'il y avait des signes visibles d'usure et de lacune au niveau de la barrière hydrofuge et des drains.  Ceci aurait du [sic], lors d'une inspection diligente des acheteurs, leurs [sic] incité [sic] d'avoir une expertise plus approfondie sur ce point avant de finaliser la transaction. » 5. These signs were visible without removing the walls or the « subfloor »; [ 26 ] The evidence does not show the existence of latent defect according to the Civil Code of Quebec . FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the action, without costs. __________________________________ ROSEMARIE MILLAR, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: January 6 th , 2009...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.024687640368938446, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\", \"title\": \"Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette, 2009 QCCQ 472 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.024687640368938446, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\"}, \"token_count\": 2035, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2015qccq11327\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2015qccq11327-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Dayan c. Amos, 2015 QCCQ 11327 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2015 QCCQ 11327 (CanLII) | Dayan c. Amos | CanLII Dayan c. Amos, 2015 QCCQ 11327 (CanLII) Date: 2015-10-15 File number: 500-32-140420-136 Citation: Dayan c. Amos, 2015 QCCQ 11327 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/gm43q > Dayan c. Amos 2015 QCCQ 11327 COURT OF QUEBEC (Small Claims Division) CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32-140420-136 DATE: October 15, 2015 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE DOMINIQUE GIBBENS, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ RosalinE DAYAN Plaintiff v. Simon Amos -and- aYALA CHRIQUI Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Plaintiff claims $7,000 from Defendants because the property she purchased from them (the “ Property ”) was affected by what she considers to be a latent defect. The amount claimed corresponds to the cost of the work that she carried out to correct the defect. [ 2 ] Defendants deny any liability towards Plaintiff. They claim that the defect was disclosed during the pre-purchase inspection of the Property and is therefore not a latent defect for which they are responsible. They also argue that Plaintiff’s claim must fail because she did not provide them with proper notice before completing the corrective work. questions in issue [ 3 ] The case raises the following questions: a) Is the defect at issue a latent defect giving rise to Defendants’ liability? b) Did Plaintiff provide Defendants with sufficient notice prior to proceeding with the corrective work and, if not, is this fatal to her claim? FACTUAL CONTEXT [ 4 ] Plaintiff purchased the Property from Defendants on February 27, 2013 for $825,000. [1] [ 5 ] The sale followed Plaintiff’s Promise to Purchase of January 30, 2013, which was accepted by Defendants, with counter-proposals, on February 3, 2013. [2] [ 6 ] The Promise to Purchase was conditional upon Plaintiff having the Property inspected by a building inspector [3] and Plaintiff retained AmeriSpec Home Inspection Service (“ AmeriSpec ”) for this purpose. [ 7 ] During the inspection, AmeriSpec noted damaged tiles at the bottom of one wall of the master bathroom shower stall and, in its written report of February 6, 2013, recommended that Plaintiff verify the condition of the wall components behind the damaged tiles and replace any affected materials. [4] [ 8 ] Without conducting any further investigations, Plaintiff declared herself satisfied with the inspection [5] and went ahead with the purchase. [ 9 ] After taking possession of the Property in early March 2013, Plaintiff commenced repairs to the master bedroom shower stall. After removing the damaged tiles, she found that a portion of the exterior wall behind the tiles was damp and showed signs of humidity and condensation. [ 10 ] AmeriSpec was brought in again and concluded that the shower may not have been built in accordance with the building code in that no vapour barrier was installed against the exterior wall, resulting in an accumulation of condensation and humidity. [6] [ 11 ] On April 19, 2013, Plaintiff, through her attorney, advised Defendants of the defect and called upon them to inspect the Property and take the required steps to repair or replace the affected shower wall within 14 days. [7] [ 12 ] On May 1, 2013, Defendants responded to the letter of demand through their attorney. They denied responsibility because Plaintiff was aware of the defect when she purchased the Property, but advised that they would be hiring an expert to inspect the Property. They requested that Plaintiff provide them with additional information and stated that no repair work should be undertaken without giving them prior notice. Finally, they asked that Plaintiff’s attorney withdraw from the matter because of a conflict of interest. [8] [ 13 ] A follow up letter was sent by Defendants’ attorney on May 20, 2013, [9] and on May 22, 2013, Plaintiff’s attorney advised that he was withdrawing and that Plaintiff would be acting on her own behalf going forward. [10] [ 14 ] Unbeknownst to Defendants’ attorney, Plaintiff had already sent a letter on May 8, 2013 asking Defendants to inspect the Property no later than May 25, 2013. [11] [ 15 ] This letter was sent by registered mail, but was left unclaimed and returned to Plaintiff on May 28, 2013. [12] Plaintiff immediately wrote to Defendants again, advising them that she would be proceeding to the corrective work on June 10, 2013 and that if they wished to inspect the Property they had to do so prior to that date. [13] [ 16 ] This letter was again sent by registered mail and returned unclaimed to Plaintiff. [14] At trial, Defendants explained that during this period, they frequently received registered mail for the previous resident of their new home and did not see the notices addressed to them. [ 17 ] Without otherwise attempting to contact Defendants, Plaintiff proceeded to the repair work in October 2013 at a total cost of $7,050.55. [15] She filed her claim on October 24, 2013. ANALYSIS a) Is the defect at issue a latent defect giving rise to Defendants’ liability? [ 18 ] Plaintiff’s claim is based on the legal warranty of quality to which sellers are bound by law. [16] [ 19 ] Under this warranty, the seller must guarantee to the buyer that the property sold is free of latent defects that render it unfit for its intended purpose or significantly diminishes its usefulness. The warranty does not extend, however, to defects known to the buyer or to apparent defects. A defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the assistance of an expert is an apparent defect. [17] [ 20 ] A buyer is expected to proceed to a careful inspection prior to purchasing a property and, if there are signs suggesting a possible defect, the buyer must proceed to further verifications. [18] If a buyer retains a building inspector to proceed to an inspection, he or she is expected to take due note of the inspector’s report and follow recommendations. [19] [ 21 ] In the instant case, the evidence shows that Plaintiff was made aware of a problem with the shower wall during the pre-purchase inspection of the Property. The visibly damaged tiles were brought to her attention by AmeriSpec, who specifically recommended that she verify the components behind the damaged tiles and replace any damaged materials. [20] [ 22 ] The Court is of the view that AmeriSpec’s comments were a clear indication of a potential defect affecting the wall components behind the tiles and that any prudent and diligent buyer would have perceived them as such. [ 23 ] Plaintiff’s decision to go ahead with the purchase of the Property without proceeding to any further investigations and without reserving her rights in relation to the shower wall precludes her from claiming that it was affected by a latent defect. [ 24 ] The defect at issue is therefore not a defect covered by the legal warranty of quality for which Defendants are liable. b) Did Plaintiff provide Defendants with sufficient notice prior to proceeding with the corrective work and, if not, is this fatal to her claim? [ 25 ] The Court also believes that Plaintiff did not provide proper notice to Defendants prior to proceeding with the corrective work and this is also fatal to her claim. [ 26 ] The law requires that a purchaser who discovers a latent defect give formal notice of the defect to the seller, in writing and within a reasonable delay after its discovery. [21] [ 27 ] The absence of such a notice, whose purpose is to allow the seller to verify the existence and nature of the alleged defect and proceed to corrective work if needed, is normally fatal to a claim to recover the cost of such work, except where it can be shown that the seller was not prejudiced. [22] [ 28 ] The buyer must also ensure that his seller is in default [23] prior to proceeding to any corrective work. [24] [ 29 ] Plaintiff’s letter of demand of April 16, 2013 [25] meets the above requirements and would have been sufficient if the letter had been left unanswered. [ 30 ] In response to the letter of demand, however, Defendants clearly expressed their intention to inspect the Property, requested information from Plaintiff and asked that no repair work be undertaken without advising them. Plaintiff could not ignore their response and proceed to the corrective work without providing them with further notice. [ 31 ] Although Plaintiff tried to do so through her letters of May 8 and May 28, 2013, [26] she knew that Defendants had not received these letters because they were returned to her unclaimed. She could have served a letter to Defendants or otherwise contacted them, but she chose not to. [ 32 ] In going ahead with the repair work without providing proper notice, Plaintiff effectively precluded Defendants from verifying the existence and nature of the alleged defect and potentially see to their repair. This is fatal to her claim. FOR THE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the action. WITH COSTS of $156 representing the judicial stamp on the Contestation. __________________________________ DOMINIQUE GIBBENS, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: July 10, 2015 [1] Deed of Sale (Exhibit P-6). [2] Promise to Purchase and counter-proposals (Exhibit D-1 en liasse). [3] Clause 8.1 of the Promise to Purchase (Exhibit D-1 en liasse). [4] Extract from AmeriSpec’s inspection report dated February 6, 2013 (Exhibit P-7). This is also stated in AmeriSpec’s letter of March 29, 2013 (Exhibit P-2). [5] Amendment AM 52668 (Exhibit D-1 en liasse). [6] AmeriSpec letter of March 29, 2013 (Exhibit P-2). [7] Letter of demand (Exhibit D-2). [8] Letter of May 1, 2013 (Exhibit D-3). [9] Email of May 20, 2013 (Exhibit D-4). [10] Letter of May 22, 2013 (Exhibit D-5). [11] Letter of May 8, 2013 (Exhibit P-1 en liasse). [12] Id. [13] Letter of May 28, 2013 (Exhibit P-1 en liasse). [14] Id. [15] Invoice of $6,668.55 (Exhibit P-3) and an additional amount of $382 in materials. [16] Articles 1726 and following of the Civil Code of Québec (the “ C.C.Q. ”). [17] Article 1726 C.C.Q. [18] Rouillard v. St-Martin , 2009 QCCA 2321 , at para. 5-6 . [19] Fortier v. Cadieux , 2011 QCCS 3966 , at para. 47 . [20] Letter of March 29, 2013 (Exhibit P-2). [21] Article 1739 C.C.Q. [22] Claude Joyal inc. v. CNH Canada Ltd. , 2014 QCCA 588 , at para. 22 to 36 . See also Immeubles Bernadet inc. v. Kerzérho , 2015 QCCA 644 , at para. 13 . [23] Articles 1594 to 1600 C.C.Q. This is done through a letter of demand, except where the seller is in default by the operation of law as set out in article 1597 C.C.Q. [24] Claude Joyal inc. v. CNH Canada Ltd. , 2014 QCCA 588 , at para. 40 to 46 . [25] Exhibit D-2. [26] Exhibit P-1 en liasse....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2015qccq11327-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.021988090127706528, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2015qccq11327-en\", \"title\": \"Dayan c. Amos, 2015 QCCQ 11327 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2015qccq11327-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.021988090127706528, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2015qccq11327-en\"}, \"token_count\": 2721, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2015qccq11327-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Pace c. Lintzeris, 2024 QCCQ 3813 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2024 QCCQ 3813 (CanLII) | Pace c. Lintzeris | CanLII Date: 2024-08-07 File number: 500-32-717183-224 Citation: Pace c. Lintzeris, 2024 QCCQ 3813 (CanLII) [NDLE : La traduction française non officielle de cette décision se trouve à la suite de la version originale.] Pace c. Lintzeris 2024 QCCQ 3813 COURT OF QUÉBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF MONTRÉAL “Civil Division” No: 500-32-717183-224 DATE: August 7, 2024 ______________________________________________________________________ PRESIDED BY THE HONOURABLE MÉLANIE DUGRÉ, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ FERNANDO PACE and ALEXANDRA NUNEZ-VILLOTA Plaintiffs v. SOPHIA LINTZERIS Defendant __________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Fernando Pace and Alexandra Nunez-Villota (the Buyers ) allege the existence of a latent defect in the property they bought from Sophia Lintzeris ( the Seller ) in June 2021. [ 2 ] The latent defect relates more specifically to the in-ground pool, which has been affected by a leak. The Buyers are claiming the cost of repairs. [1] [ 3 ] The Seller contends that the pool is excluded from the warranty of quality and that given its age (30 to 40 years), the Buyers could not expect a brand new pool. ISSUES [4] a)  Was the pool excluded from the inspection mandate? b) Is the pool excluded from the warranty of quality? c) Are the problems affecting the pool a latent defect under the warranty of quality? d) If so, what is the amount of damages to which the Buyers are entitled? [5] For the reasons explained below, the Court finds that the pool was excluded from the inspection mandate. However, the pool is covered by the warranty of quality and the problems affecting the pool meet the criteria of a latent defect. [6] In civil matters, the burden of proof is on Plaintiff’s shoulders, in accordance with the principle provided under article 2803 of the Civil Code of Québec ( C.C.Q. ), which states that “[a] person seeking to assert a right shall prove the facts on which his claim is based”. [7] The Court decides on the balance of probabilities, pursuant to article 2804 C.C.Q. , which states that “[evidence] is sufficient if it renders the existence of a fact more probable than its non-existence”. In other words, the Court must determine what is more probable and plausible, rather than improbable and implausible. [8] In addition, when the evidence adduced is not sufficiently convincing, or if the evidence is contradictory and it is impossible for the judge to identify the truth, the party upon whom the burden of proof lies will lose. [2] ANALYSIS a) Was the pool excluded from the inspection mandate? [9] Prior to the purchase of the house, the Buyers proceeded to an inspection in April 2021. [10] Obtaining an inspection report is not mandatory for the warranty of quality to apply. [11] On the other hand, a buyer who retains the services of an inspector still has a duty to examine the property based on the standard of the prudent and diligent buyer. [3] [12] The inspection report dated April 22, 2021 specifies under the Limitations and Conditions section that “[items] exterior [and/or] unattached to the building, such as […], swimming pools, […], are not covered in this inspection”. [4] [13] The Court finds that the swimming pool was not included in the mandate of the inspector. As a result, the analysis must be based exclusively on the criterion of the prudent and diligent buyer. b) Was the pool excluded from the warranty of quality ? [14] The Seller alleges that the warranty of quality does not apply to the pool. To support this argument, she relies on the Promise to Purchase form dated April 7, 2021, [5] which provides under section 11.5 that a few items, including washer/dryer, refrigerator, garage door opener and “[…] pool accessories” are “[…] sold without any legal warranty of quality, at the buyer’s own risk, but must be in working order at the time of delivery of the immovable”. [15] The inspection carried out on the pool in July 2021, after the Buyers noticed a water level problem, revealed the presence of a leak caused by a ruptured return pipe. [16] The Court is of the view that the return pipe is an integral part of the pool. It cannot be dissociated from the pool, nor can it be qualified as “accessory”. [17] Had the parties’ intent been to exclude the pool itself from the warranty, the provision to that effect should have been clearer. [18] With the current wording chosen by the parties, the Court concludes that the pool and its inherent components, such as the return pipe, are covered by the warranty of quality. c) Are the problems affecting the pool a latent defect under the warranty of quality? [19] The warranty of quality is owed by the seller to the buyer. [6] [20] Although the pool is covered by the warranty of quality, the reported problems must still meet the conditions of a latent defect to trigger the Seller’s liability. [21] To qualify as latent, a defect must meet the following criteria: [7] a) It must be serious, which means that it must interfere with a normal use of the good. The buyer must show that he would not have paid the same price, had he been aware of the defect. b) It must be unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale. c) It must be latent, which means that it cannot be identified by a prudent and diligent buyer, and it has not been reported by the seller. d) It must be anterior to the sale. [22] In addition, the buyer has the obligation to give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable delay of its discovery [8] , and to file his legal action within the three-year prescription delay. [9] [23] Evidence reveals the following facts. [24] The Buyers inspected the house in April. Although the snow had melted and the pool was partly visible, it was not in operation, and the water was dark. [25] The Buyers did not notice any obvious or visible issue with the pool. [26] Their inspector confirmed same. Even though the pool is excluded from the inspection mandate, the inspector did mention in his report that the “[…] pool appeared to be in good condition” and “[there] are no apparent cracks”. [10] [27] After they took possession of the house in June 2021, the Buyers noticed that there seemed to be a problem with the water level of the pool, which kept decreasing. [28] At that time, the Buyers were not living in the house due to renovation work, and they were planning to move in September. Yet, they came to the house every few days to supervise the work. [29] At the beginning of July, the water level was so low that the pump stopped working. [30] The Buyers questioned the Seller and were told that she had also encountered this issue, which was due to evaporation, according to her. [31] However, the inspection revealed the presence of a leak caused by the ruptured return pipe. [32] Some repairs were performed on July 22, 2021, for a total amount of $2,069.55. [11] [33] The Buyers reported the issue to the Seller, who agreed to pay $1,800 for the repair. [12] [34] The Seller acknowledges that no transaction was signed between the parties and that the Buyers, by accepting this payment, did not renounce to any future claims. [35] The Buyers noticed some improvements after the repair, but issues remained. They still had to add water to the pool on a regular basis, but every three or four days instead of daily. [36] In the fall of 2021, the Buyers obtained a full inspection report of the pool [13] and a new quote for further repair. [14] [37] Additional leaks were suspected in the skimmer and at the level of LED lights. [38] In January 2022, the Buyers sent the Seller a demand letter claiming an additional amount of $13,561.30. [15] [39] On January 24, 2022, the Seller answered the demand letter, in which she denied owing any further amount to the Buyers. [40] When assessing the criteria of a latent defect, the Court first notes that having to add water to the pool every few days to protect the pump is a serious issue, which interferes with the normal use of the pool. [41] Second, the evidence shows the Buyers were not aware of the problem at the time of the sale. [42] Third, the defect was latent, as no issue was noticeable either by the Buyers themselves or by their inspector in April 2021, when the pool was not in operation. There was no way for them to see or suspect the broken return pipe. [43] It was only when they took possession of the house, and after the pool had been started for the 2021 summer season, that the Buyers were able to notice that the water level was decreasing. [44] Finally, the defect was anterior to the sale, since the Seller admitted that she too had to add water to the pool from time to time. However, she believed this was due to evaporation and the fact that children were splashing water when playing in the pool. [45] The Seller also contends that, given the age of the pool (30 to 40 years), the Buyers could not expect it to be in perfect condition. [46] Caselaw does confirm that the warranty of quality does not extend to deterioration of a property caused by its wear and tear or obsolescence. The prudent and diligent buyer should, before purchasing older property and equipment, examine them carefully and anticipate certain repairs. [16] [47] The author Jeffrey Edwards also mentions: [17] Les détériorations dues à l’usure, au vieillissement ou à la vétusté ne constituent donc pas des vices, car, en raison de la révision à la baisse de l’usage attendu, elles n’occasionnent aucun déficit d’usage au sens de la garantie : l’usage protégé varie ainsi selon l’état de l’usure, du vieillissement et de la vétusté du bien au moment de la vente. [48] The Court finds that there is a distinction between expected wear and tear of a good and a defect affecting its normal functioning. [49] The photos contained in the inspection report show that the pool site and the pool itself are rather old and could benefit from improvement. [50] The Buyers are not complaining about this. [51] On the other hand, the Buyers could expect the pool to be functioning normally without having to continually add water to protect the pump. Normal wear and tear has nothing to do with the defect identified. [52] For all these reasons, the Court concludes that the Buyers have met their burden of proof to show that the conditions for a latent defect in the pool are met. d) If so, what is the amount of damages to which the Buyers are entitled? [53] The Buyers explained that they obtained a quote in December 2021 for $13,561.30. [18] [54] The Seller refused to assume the cost of additional repairs, and the Buyers decided to wait to carry out the work because they were planning to redo the concrete around the pool. [55] As a result, the situation remained the same during the summer of 2022, whereby the Buyers were regularly adding water to the pool to protect the pump. [56] The Buyers finally had the repairs done in June 2023. The cost was lower than expected and they paid $8,048.24. [19] [57] The Seller contends that the work carried out significantly improves the condition and value of the pool and goes beyond repairing the leak that caused the water level issues. [58] On this point, caselaw [20] establishes that plaintiffs cannot unduly enrich themselves to the detriment of their seller by purchasing a new good when the one purchased was heavily used or advanced in age. [59] The Court however disagrees with the Seller’s argument. [60] The invoice details the work carried out: replacement of the skimmer, replacement of LED lights, filling up of light niche and removal of cable anchors. [61] Most of these repairs addressed issues identified in October 2021 as being potential sources of the leak. [62] The Court believes that the work performed was meant to repair the defect on the pool which existed at the time of the sale and could not be identified or suspected by the Buyers. [63] The Court therefore concludes that the Buyers are entitled to the amount paid for the repairs. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: GRANTS Fernando Pace and Alexandra Nunez-Villota’s application; CONDEMNS Sophia Lintzeris to pay to Fernando Pace and Alexandra Nunez-Villota $8,048.25, plus interests at the legal rate and the additional indemnity under article 1619 C.C.Q. since the demand letter of January 13, 2022; CONDEMNS Sophia Lintzeris to pay to Fernando Pace and Alexandra Nunez-Villota the legal costs of $217 for the application. __________________________________ MÉLANIE DUGRÉ, J.C.Q. Hearing date: July 8, 2024 TRADUCTION NON OFFICIELLE RÉALISÉE PAR SOQUIJ, NON RÉVISÉE PAR LE JUGE Pace c. Lintzeris 2024 QCCQ 3813 COUR DU QUÉBEC Division des petites créances CANADA PROVINCE DE QUÉBEC DISTRICT DE MONTRÉAL « Chambre civile » N o : 500-32-717183-224 DATE : 7 août 2024 ______________________________________________________________________ SOUS LA PRÉSIDENCE DE L’HONORABLE MÉLANIE DUGRÉ, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ FERNANDO PACE et ALEXANDRA NUNEZ-VILLOTA Demandeurs c. SOPHIA LINTZERIS Défenderesse __________________________________________________________________ JUGEMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [1] Fernando Pace et Alexandra Nunez-Villota (les acheteurs) allèguent l’existence d’un vice caché dans la propriété qu’ils ont achetée de Sophia Lintzeris (la venderesse) en juin 2021. [2] Le vice caché concerne plus précisément la piscine creusée, qui a un problème de fuite. Les acheteurs réclament le coût des réparations [21] . [3] La venderesse prétend que la piscine est exclue de la garantie de qualité et que, compte tenu de l’âge de la piscine (de 30 à 40 ans), les acheteurs ne pouvaient s’attendre à ce que celle-ci soit à l’état neuf. QUESTIONS EN LITIGE [4] a) La piscine était-elle exclue du mandat d’inspection ? e) La piscine est-elle exclue de la garantie de qualité ? f) Les problèmes affectant la piscine constituent-ils un vice caché aux termes de la garantie de qualité ? g) Le cas échéant, à quel montant les acheteurs ont-ils droit à titre de dommages-intérêts ? [5] Pour les motifs qui suivent, la Cour conclut que la piscine était exclue du mandat d’inspection. Cependant, la piscine est couverte par la garantie de qualité et les problèmes affectant la piscine remplissent les critères d’un vice caché. [6] En matière civile, le fardeau de la preuve repose sur les épaules du demandeur, conformément au principe énoncé à l’ article 2803 du Code civil du Québe c (C.C.Q.), qui prévoit que « celui qui veut faire valoir un droit doit prouver les faits qui soutiennent sa prétention. » [7] La Cour décide selon la prépondérance des probabilités en vertu de l’article 2804 C.C.Q., qui prévoit que « la preuve qui rend l’existence d’un fait plus probable que son inexistence est suffisante » . Autrement dit, la Cour doit déterminer ce qui est le plus probable et vraisemblable, plutôt que ce qui est improbable et invraisemblable. [8] De plus, lorsque la preuve présentée n’est pas suffisamment convaincante, ou que les éléments de preuve sont contradictoires et ne permettent pas au juge de déterminer la vérité, la partie à qui incombe le fardeau de la preuve succombera [22] . ANALYSE e) La piscine était-elle exclue du mandat d’inspection ? [9] Avant l’achat de la maison, les acheteurs ont procédé à une inspection en avril 2021. [10] Pour que la garantie de qualité s’applique, il n’est pas nécessaire d’obtenir un rapport d’inspection. [11] Par ailleurs, un acheteur qui retient les services d’un inspecteur a tout de même l’obligation de procéder à l’examen de la propriété selon la norme de l’acheteur prudent et diligent [23] . [12] Le rapport d’inspection daté du 22 avril 2021 précise, à...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n... [29] Art. 2925 C.C.Q. [30] Supra note 4, p. 6. [31] Pièce P-6, facture de 1 800 $ plus taxes. [32] Pièce P-5. [33] Pièce P-3. [34] Pièce P-8. [35] Pièce P-7. [36] Naud c. Normand , 2007 QCCA 1814 ; Dinu c. Tremblay , 2018 QCCQ 8914 ; Auchinteck c. Gerkes , 2022 QCCS 2637 ; Yuan c. Lancione , 2023 QCCQ 7835 au par. 21 . [37] Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois, 2 e éd. (Montréal : Wilson & Lafleur, 2005), p. 155. [38] Supra note 14. [39] Supra note 4. [40] Artinian c. Wong , 2017 QCCQ 9806 au par. 63 ; Larente c. Lépine , 2019 QCCQ 336 au par. 33 ....\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n1. [10] Pour que la garantie de qualité s’applique, il n’est pas nécessaire d’obtenir un rapport d’inspection. [11] Par ailleurs, un acheteur qui retient les services d’un inspecteur a tout de même l’obligation de procéder à l’examen de la propriété selon la norme de l’acheteur prudent et diligent [23] . [12] Le rapport d’inspection daté du 22 avril 2021 précise, à la section Limites et conditions , que [ traduction ] « [les éléments] extérieurs [et/ou] non fixés au bâtiment, comme les […], piscines […], ne sont pas couverts dans cette inspection » [24] . [13] La Cour conclut que la piscine n’était pas incluse dans le mandat de l’inspecteur. L’analyse doit donc être fondée exclusivement sur le critère de l’acheteur prudent et diligent. f) La piscine était-elle exclue de la garantie de qualité ? [14] La venderesse allègue que la garantie de qualité ne s’applique pas à la piscine. Au soutien de cette prétention, elle s’appuie sur la promesse d’achat datée du 7 avril 2021 [25] , laquelle prévoit à son article 11.5 que certains éléments, y compris la machine à laver, la sécheuse, le réfrigérateur, l’ouvre-porte de garage et […] les accessoires de piscine sont [ traduction ] « […] vendus sans aucune garantie légale de qualité, aux risques et périls de l’acheteur, mais ils doivent être en bon état de marche au moment de la prise de possession de l’immeuble ». [15] L’inspection qui a été faite de la piscine en juillet 2021, après que les acheteurs ont remarqué un problème avec le niveau de l’eau, a révélé la présence d’une fuite causée par le bris d’un tuyau de retour. [16] La Cour est d’avis que le tuyau de retour fait partie intégrante de la piscine. Il ne peut être dissocié de la piscine ni être qualifié d’accessoire . [17] Si les parties avaient l’intention d’exclure la piscine de la garantie de qualité, la disposition à cet effet aurait dû être plus claire. [18] Compte tenu de la formulation que les parties ont choisie, la Cour conclut que la piscine et ses composantes inhérentes, comme le tuyau de retour, sont couvertes par la garantie de qualité. g) Les problèmes affectant la piscine constituent-ils un vice caché aux termes de la garantie de qualité ? [19] Le vendeur est tenu à la garantie de qualité envers l’acheteur [26] . [20] Même si la piscine est couverte par la garantie de qualité, la venderesse ne sera tenue responsable que si les problèmes signalés remplissent les conditions pour constituer un vice caché. [21] Pour être caché, un vice doit répondre aux critères suivants [27] : e) Il doit être grave, ce qui signifie qu’il doit empêcher l’usage normal du bien. L’acheteur doit montrer qu’il n’aurait pas payé le même prix s’il avait été au courant du vice . f) Il doit avoir été inconnu de l’acheteur au moment de la vente . g) Il doit être caché, ce qui signifie qu’il ne pouvait être décelé par un acheteur prudent et diligent et qu’il n’a pas été signalé par le vendeur. h) Il doit avoir existé au moment de la vente . [22] De plus, l’acheteur doit, par écrit, dénoncer le vice au vendeur dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte [28] et intenter son action dans un délai de trois ans [29] . [23] La preuve révèle les faits suivants. [24] Les acheteurs ont inspecté la maison en avril. Même si la neige avait fondu, la piscine n’était qu’en partie visible, elle n’était pas en marche et l’eau était sombre . [25] Les acheteurs n’ont remarqué aucun problème évident ou visible relativement à la piscine. [26] Cela a été confirmé par l’inspecteur. Même si la piscine était exclue du mandat d’inspection, l’inspecteur a mentionné que [ traduction ] « […] la piscine semble en bon état » et [ traduction ] « [qu’il] n’y a aucune fissure apparente » [30] . [27] Après avoir pris possession de la maison en juin 2021, les acheteurs ont remarqué qu’il semblait y avoir un problème avec le niveau de l’eau, qui baissait constamment dans la piscine. [28] À l’époque, ils n’habitaient pas dans la maison en raison des travaux de rénovation et ils prévoyaient d’y emménager en septembre. Ils venaient tout de même à la maison tous les quelques jours afin de superviser les travaux. [29] Au début de juillet, le niveau de l’eau était si bas que la pompe a cessé de fonctionner. [30] Les acheteurs ont questionné la venderesse, qui leur a dit qu’elle avait également fait face à ce problème causé, selon elle, par l’évaporation. [31] Cependant, une inspection a révélé la présence d’une fuite causée par le bris du tuyau de retour. [32] Une réparation, dont le coût s’est élevé à 2 069,55 $, a été effectuée le 22 juillet 2021 [31] . [33] Les acheteurs ont signalé le problème à la venderesse, qui a accepté de payer 1 800 $ pour la réparation [32] . [34] La venderesse reconnaît que les parties n’ont signé aucune transaction et que les acheteurs n’ont pas renoncé à toute autre réclamation future en acceptant ce paiement. [35] Les acheteurs ont remarqué une amélioration après la réparation, mais le problème a persisté. Ils devaient encore ajouter de l’eau régulièrement, tous les trois ou quatre jours plutôt que tous les jours . [36] À l’automne 2021, les acheteurs ont obtenu un rapport d’inspection complète de la piscine [33] et une nouvelle évaluation pour une autre réparation [34] . [37] On soupçonnait la présence d’autres fuites dans l’écumoire et au niveau des lumières DEL. [38] En janvier 2022, les acheteurs ont envoyé à la venderesse une mise en demeure lui réclamant une somme additionnelle de 13 561,30 $ [35] . [39] Le 24 janvier 2022, la venderesse a répondu à la mise en demeure en niant devoir une somme additionnelle aux acheteurs. [40] En évaluant les critères de vice caché, la Cour constate premièrement que le fait d’avoir à ajouter de l’eau dans une piscine tous les jours afin de protéger la pompe constitue un grave problème, qui nuit à l’usage normal de la piscine. [41] Deuxièmement, la preuve montre que les acheteurs n’étaient pas au courant du problème au moment de la vente. [42] Troisièmement, le vice était caché puisqu’aucun problème ne pouvait être décelé par les acheteurs ou leur inspecteur en avril 2021. La pompe n’étant pas en marche, il leur était impossible de constater ou de soupçonner le bris du tuyau de retour. [43] Ce n’est que lorsqu’ils ont pris possession de la maison, et après la mise en marche de la piscine pour l’été 2021, que les acheteurs ont pu remarquer que le niveau de l’eau baissait. [44] Enfin, le vice existait au moment de la vente, la venderesse ayant admis qu’elle aussi devait ajouter de l’eau dans la piscine de temps à autre. Cependant, elle croyait que cela était dû à l’évaporation et au fait que les enfants faisaient des éclaboussures en jouant dans la piscine. [45] La venderesse prétend également que, compte tenu de l’âge de la piscine (de 30 à 40 ans), les acheteurs ne pouvaient s’attendre à ce que celle-ci soit en parfait état. [46] La jurisprudence confirme que la garantie de qualité ne s’étend pas à la détérioration de la propriété causée par son usure ou sa vétusté. Avant d’acheter une propriété ou de l’équipement ayant un certain âge, l’acheteur prudent et diligent devrait les examiner soigneusement et prévoir certaines réparations [36] . [47] L’auteur Jeffrey Edwards [37] mentionne également que : Les détériorations dues à l’usure, au vieillissement ou à la vétusté ne constituent donc pas des vices, car, en raison de la révision à la baisse de l’usage attendu, elles n’occasionnent aucun déficit d’usage au sens de la garantie : l’usage protégé varie ainsi selon l’état de l’usure, du vieillissement et de la vétusté du bien au moment de la vente. [48] La Cour conclut qu’une distinction doit être faite entre l’usure prévisible d’un bien et un vice affectant son fonctionnement normal. [49] Les photos contenues dans le rapport d’inspection montrent que l’aménagement de la piscine et la piscine elle-même ont un certain âge et pourraient être améliorés. [50] Les acheteurs ne s’en plaignent pas. [51] Par ailleurs, les acheteurs pouvaient s’attendre à ce que la piscine fonctionne normalement, sans qu’il soit nécessaire d’y ajouter de l’eau constamment afin de protéger la pompe. L’usure normale n’a rien à voir avec le vice décelé . [52] Pour tous ces motifs, la Cour conclut que les acheteurs se sont déchargés de leur fardeau de prouver que les conditions pour conclure à un vice caché de la piscine sont remplies. h) Le cas échéant, à quel montant les acheteurs ont-ils droit à titre de dommages-intérêts ? [53] Les acheteurs ont expliqué qu’ils avaient obtenu, en décembre 2021, une évaluation s’élevant à 13 561,30 $ [38] . [54] La venderesse refuse d’assumer le coût de réparations supplémentaires et les acheteurs ont décidé d’attendre avant de faire les travaux parce qu’ils prévoient refaire le béton autour de la piscine. [55] Par conséquent, la situation était la même pendant l’été 2022, les acheteurs devant ajouter régulièrement de l’eau à la piscine afin de protéger la pompe. [56] Les acheteurs ont finalement fait faire des réparations en juin 2023. Le coût était moins élevé que prévu et le prix payé était de 8 048,24 $ [39] . [57] La venderesse prétend que les travaux exécutés améliorent de manière appréciable l’état et la valeur de la piscine et ne se limitent pas à la réparation de la fuite qui causait les problèmes de niveau de l’eau. [58] À ce sujet, la jurisprudence [40] établit que les demandeurs qui ont acheté un bien qui était vieux ou très usé ne peuvent s’enrichir injustement au détriment de leur vendeur en achetant un bien neuf. [59] Cependant, la Cour ne retient pas la prétention de la venderesse. [60] La facture précise les travaux qui ont été exécutés, soit le remplacement de l’écumoire, le remplacement des lumières DEL, le colmatage d’une niche de lumière et l’enlèvement de câbles d’ancrage. [61] Pour la plupart, ces travaux concernent des problèmes décelés en octobre 2021 comme des causes possibles de la fuite. [62] La Cour estime que les travaux exécutés visaient à réparer le vice qui affectait la piscine au moment de la vente et qui ne pouvait être décelé ni soupçonné par les acheteurs . [63] Par conséquent, la Cour conclut que les acheteurs ont droit à la somme qu’ils ont versée pour les réparations. POUR CES MOTIFS, LA COUR : ACCUEILLE la demande de Fernando Pace et de Alexandra Nunez-Villota ; CONDAMNE Sophia Lintzeris à verser à Fernando Pace et Alexandra Nunez-Villota 8 048,25 $ avec intérêt au taux légal et l’indemnité additionnelle prévue à l’article 1619 C.C.Q., à compter de la mise en demeure du 13 janvier 2022 ; CONDAMNE Sophia Lintzeris à verser à Fernando Pace et Alexandra Nunez-Villota des frais de justice de 217 $ pour la demande. __________________________________ MÉLANIE DUGRÉ, J.C.Q. Date d’audience : 8 juillet 2024 [1] Exhibit P-12, invoice for $8,048.25. [2] Léo Ducharme, Précis de la preuve , 6th ed. (Montréal: Wilson & Lafleur, 2005) at para. 146 . [3] Lamontagne v. Chiasson , 2017 QCCS 4651 at para. 60 . [4] Exhibit P-1 at 76. [5] Exhibit D-2. [6] Art. 1726 C.C.Q. [7] Leroux v. Gravant , 2016 QCCA 79 at para. 40 . [8] Art. 1739 C.c.Q. [9] Art. 2925 C.c.Q. [10] Supra note 4 at 6. [11] Exhibit P-6, invoice for $1,800 plus tax. [12] Exhibit P-5. [13] Exhibit P-3. [14] Exhibit P-8. [15] Exhibit P-7. [16] Naud v. Normand , 2007 QCCA 1814 ; Dinu v. Tremblay , 2018 QCCQ 8914 ; Auchinteck v. Gerkes , 2022 QCCS 2637 ; Yuan v. Lancione , 2023 QCCQ 7835 at para. 21 . [17] Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois, 2d ed. (Montréal: Wilson & Lafleur, 2005) at 155. [18] Supra note 14. [19] Supra note 4. [20] Artinian v. Wong , 2017 QCCQ 9806 at para. 63 ; Larente v. Lépine , 2019 QCCQ 336 at para. 33 . [21] Pièce P-12, facture de 8 048,25 $. [22] Léo Ducharme, Précis de la preuve , 6 e éd. (Montréal : Wilson & Lafleur, 2005) au par. 146 . [23] Lamontagne c. Chiasson , 2017 QCCS 4651 , au par. 60 . [24] Pièce P-1, p. 76. [25] Pièce D-2. [26] Art. 1726 C.C.Q. [27] Leroux c. Gravant , 2016 QCCA 79 au par. 40 . [28] Art. 1739 C.C.Q. [29] Art. 2925 C.C.Q. [30] Supra note 4, p. 6. [31] Pièce P-6, facture de 1 800 $ plus taxes. [32] Pièce P-5. [33] Pièce P-3. [34] Pièce P-8. [35] Pièce P-7. [36] Naud c. Normand , 2007 QCCA 1814 ; Dinu c. Trembl\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.023148149251937866, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\", \"title\": \"Pace c. Lintzeris, 2024 QCCQ 3813 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.023148149251937866, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\"}, \"token_count\": 7213, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau, 2019 QCCQ 1416 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2019 QCCQ 1416 (CanLII) | Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau | CanLII Date: 2019-02-28 File number: 455-22-004695-169 Citation: Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau, 2019 QCCQ 1416 (CanLII) Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau 2019 QCCQ 1416 COURT OF QUEBEC CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF BEDFORD TOWN OF COWANSVILLE Civil Division No: 455-22-004695-169 DATE: February 28, 2019 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE MARTIN TÉTREAULT, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ ALEXIS SPARLING and ANDREA SPARLING Plaintiffs v. DAVID MARCHAND-DUCHESNEAU And MAUDE TOUGAS Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Plaintiffs seek an order for reduction of the price sale and damages as a result of hidden defects and false representations by Defendants with respect to the sale of an immoveable property located in Mansonville (the “Property”). [ 2 ] The defects are: 1) absence of/or broken dispersal fields; 2) non-functional wells; 3) blocked french drain and 4) blocked gutters. [ 3 ] Defendants deny having provided false informations to Plaintiffs and submit that the defects are not covered by the warranty of quality provided by the Civil Code of Quebec (“CCQ”). CONTEXT [ 4 ] In the spring of 2013, Defendants decide to sale the Property that they had bought in 2007 [1] . [ 5 ] The Property, a 2.5 acres land on which a house was built in 1980, is located next to the Missisquoi North River [2] . [ 6 ] The listing mentions the following characteristics: “Système d’égoûts: Champ d’épuration, Fosse septique. Approvisionnement  eau: Puits artésien, Puits de surface [3] ” [ 7 ] The selling price is 165 000$. [ 8 ] On June 27, Plaintiffs visited the Property with Defendants’ real estate agent, Ms. Sandra Williams (“Ms Williams”). Defendants were not present. A Vendors’ Declaration signed by Defendants on March 12, 2013 was handed over at this visit [4] . [ 9 ] On the same day, Plaintiffs presented a Promise to Purchase to Defendants [5] . The offered price was 162 000$ and the sale was not conditional upon an inspection by a person chosen by the buyer. This offer was accepted by Defendants without any counter proposal. [ 10 ] On August 1, the parties signed the Deed of Sale [6] . Plaintiffs took possession the same day. [ 11 ] On August 4, Defendant David Marchand-Duchesneau (“Mr Marchand-Duchesneau”) came to the Property notably to explain Plaintiffs how to run the Water Supply System (“Water Supply System”). The residence was equipped with two pressure tanks located in the basement which served the surface and artesian wells. Mr Marchand-Duchesneau showed Plaintiffs how to switch from the surface to the artesian well. [ 12 ] On March 9, 2014, the surface well ran dry. [ 13 ] Eigth days later, the artesian well also ran dry. [ 14 ] On March 25, a plumber came to inspect the wells in order to solve the problem. He adjusted the pump of the Water Supply System and the water came back [7] . [ 15 ] At the end of June, the Plaintiffs Andrea Sparling (“Ms Sparling”) noticed water entering into the basement from behind the dry wall in the laundry room. [ 16 ] On July 2, a contractor came to inspect the basement. He suspected that the french drains were blocked and should be excavated. He also noticed the absence of dispersal field which accounted for a strong odor of raw sewage. [ 17 ] The next day, Plaintiffs advised Ms Williams of the situation and asked her to contact the Defendants. [ 18 ] On July 4, Mr Marchand-Duchesneu visited the Property and found that the exit pipe for the french drain was disconnected. He denied any liability regarding this situation. [ 19 ] On July 18, Ms Sparling sent a Letter of Demand to Defendants concerning the absence of a leeching field and the defects affecting the french drains [8] . [ 20 ] Upon receiving this letter, Defendants came back to the Property and identified the surface well which was beside the site chosen for the proposed dispersal field. Based upon this information, Plaintiffs suspended their plans for the construction of a dispersal field. [ 21 ] On September 14, Ms Sparling took a water sample from the surface well and sent it to be analysed. The conclusion of the analysis was that the sample did not conform to the Quebec Government’s drinking water recommendations and that the water should be boiled for one minute before drinking [9] . It also recommended that the well be disinfected. [ 22 ] On September 24, Ms Sparling sent another Letter of Demand to Defendants concerning the water, the wells, the french drains and the gutters [10] . [ 23 ] At the beginning of October, a contractor proceeded to the hydro-fracturation of the artesian well and to its reconstruction with all necessary accessories [11] . [ 24 ] On November 9, the gutters and downspouts were replaced [12] . [ 25 ] Between November 17 and 24, a new septic system was installed [13] . [ 26 ] On December 23, a Water Treatment System was installed [14] . [ 27 ] In July 2016, Plaintiffs replaced the french drains [15] . [ 28 ] On July 22, the Application for damages was served upon Defendants. ISSUES IN DISPUTE [ 29 ] 1. Are the defects affecting the Property covered by the legal warranty of quality? If so, what amount should be paid by the Defendants for these defects? [ 30 ] 2. Did Defendants make any false or misleading representations to Plaintiffs concerning the Property sold? If so, are they liable for the defects affecting the Property and what is the amount of damages owed to Plaintiffs? ANALYSIS THE LAW [ 31 ] Section 1726 of the CCQ describes the warranty of quality of the seller: “ 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. » [ 32 ] In their book on Contract Law in Quebec, Pr Campagnolo, Debruche and Grammond, summarize the elements to be proven under this section : « 665. Four elements are required to prove a latent defect, triggering the responsibility of the seller: (i) the seriousness of the defect; (ii) the anteriority of the defect to the sale; (iii) that the buyer was unaware of the defect; and (iv) and the occult (or hidden) nature of the defect. 1223 […] 667. Gravity, or the seriousness of the defect, is measured according to the consequences it entails, which must not be insignificant. In the context of the sale of a house, a window that does not close properly, for example, does not qualify. 1226 Only defects which meet the requirements of art. 1726 C.C.Q. give rise to the warranty, such that either the buyer would not have agreed to the sale had he known about the defect, or would not have paid as high a price. The case law provides many examples, ranging from defective roofs to rotten flooring and insulation, cracked foundations causing [Page 279] leaks, structural defects affecting the stability of buildings and infestations by insects and rodents. 1227 […] 668. The notion of anteriority to the sale is confirmed by the wording of art. 1726(1) C.C.Q., which states that the warranty applies to defects “at the time of sale.” It is important to note here that precedence to the sale does not mean that the defect had to manifest itself prior to the transfer of property. 1229 Rather, to trigger the warranty, it is sufficient that a defect was gestating at the time of the sale and only manifested itself once in the hands of the buyer. 669. In order not to give rise to the third condition of a latent defect – the buyer’s unawareness of the defect – it is common practice, stemming back to Roman Law, that the seller of a property fill out a declaration, informing the buyer of all defects affecting the property. 1230 Thus, the buyer may not invoke the warranty of quality against a seller who has revealed a defect in his declaration. Moreover, the entirety of the defect must be revealed. 1231 It is not enough for a seller to simply gloss over a problem, without getting into the details. This would not fulfill the seller’s duty to inform. 670. Finally, arguably most essential to the notion of latent defect, is the hidden nature of the defect. Despite common misconceptions about the necessity of an inspection by an expert (e.g., architect or engineer) and the consequences associated with the failure to engage an expert, the Civil Code states, at art. 1726(2) C.C.Q., that “an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert.” In other words, while it is common practice and not unwise to do so, there is no legal requirement that a buyer have a property professionally inspected upon purchase in order to take advantage of the warranty of quality. Rather, what is necessary, and continuously enforced by the courts, is that purchasers must be prudent and diligent in their dealings, taking care to carry out a careful examination of the property they wish to buy. The defect that is not noticed by the careful buyer is therefore a hidden defect. 1232 » [16] APPLICATION TO THIS CASE [ 33 ] Since Plaintiffs have raised different defects, we will adress them respectively. 1. Dispersal field [ 34 ] Initialy, Plaintiffs were pretending that the Property had no dispersal field. [ 35 ] However, after they received the proof that a septic tank and sweeping bed had been installed in 1988 [17] , they amended their proceeding to claim that it was not functional and non-conforming. [ 36 ] As mentioned at the trial by Mr Mike Mierzwinski, the contractor in excavation who was hired by Plaintiffs to install the Enviroseptic System, the old system was not operating correctly. The sewage was sent directly from the house to two culvert pipes with no bottom buried in the backyard [18] . Mr Mierzwinski added that the presence of green grass in this area was a proof of a leaking problem. [ 37 ] Under these circonstances, the Court comes to the conclusion that the dispersal field was affected by a serious defect which existed at the time of the sale and was unknown by Plaintiffs. [ 38 ] However, this defect cannot be qualified as hidden since it could have been perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer. [ 39 ] In a recent case, the Court of Appeal described the notion of « prudent and diligent buyer » [19] : « [7] Un acheteur prudent et diligent se doit de faire une inspection de l’immeuble qu’il se propose d’acquérir. Cet examen doit être attentif et sérieux [3] . En présence d’un indice de nature à soulever chez l’acheteur un soupçon quant à la présence de vices, il est tenu de pousser l’examen [4] . Il se doit de vérifier de façon satisfaisante ce qui est suspect [5] . Il n’est toutefois pas tenu d’ouvrir les murs et de creuser les fondations [6] ni de faire appel à un expert [7] . De plus, si le vendeur dénonce des problèmes existants, l’acheteur se doit d’en faire un examen approprié [8] . S’il n’a pas les connaissances nécessaires pour écarter la possibilité que le bien soit affecté d’un vice, l’acheteur doit obtenir une aide spécialisée. Dans ce cas, s’il omet de demander de l’aide, il agit de manière négligente et ne satisfait pas à la norme de l’acheteur prudent et diligent [9] . [ 8 ] L’âge du bien doit être tenu en compte. Plus le bien est âgé, plus s’intensifie l’obligation d’effectuer un examen approfondi. Ainsi, plus un vice se manifeste par des signes précurseurs, plus il doit être qualifié d’apparent. Si son existence n’est révélée que par des travaux exceptionnels, par exemple d’excavation ou de démolition, il s’agit d’un vice caché [10 ] » [20] [ 40 ] Here, Plaintiffs made an offer on the Property after a 30 to 60 minutes visit with Defendant’s real estate agent. Their offer was not conditional upon an inspection by a building inspector despite the fact that they had no knowledge in construction or renovation work or in septic system. Moreover, they knew that the house had been built in the 1980’s. [ 41 ] At the hearing, Ms Sparling explained that she had driven by the Property in 2008 and had loved the sight, the place and its location. Her visit in 2013 confirmed her feelings of 2008. Since the Declaration did not mention any problem with the septic system and she knew that the financial institution which would lend the money would rely on a evaluator before grantig the loan, she thought, mistakenly, that it was not necessary to proceed with an inspection by a building inspector. [ 42 ] Since Mr Sparling had never owned the Property, he relied on his mother for the sale and did not notice anything special when he visited the Property. At that time, the field in the back was overgrown with flowers so he did not notice any bad odors. It’s only after he cleared up the area after the sale that he started noticing the odors and the dampness in the area were the sewage was leaking. [ 43 ] In view of 1) the age of the house; 2) the mention on the Declaration that Defendants had no knowledge of the installation date of the septic system; and 3) their ignorance of such system, Plaintiffs should have conducted a more thorough inspection before buying. [ 44 ] As mentioned by Mr Mierzwinski, the mere presence of green grass in this area was a sign that the septic system had a leaking problem. [ 45 ] Plaintiffs’ claim under this item is therefore dismissed. [ 46 ] The Court adds that should it have granted this part of the claim, an important depreciation would have been applied since the system had been installed in 1988. 2. Water Supply System [ 47 ] Plaintiffs claim that insufficiency of water and the problems affecting its drinkability are hidden defects. Whether under the warranty of quality or because of false or misleading informations on their part, Defendants should be liable for the costs required to correct these problems. [ 48 ] These costs include tests, intervention by plumber, inspections, hydro-fracturation, installation of water treatment system and replacement of a hot water tank. [ 49 ] For the following reasons, the Court comes to the conclusion that this part of the claim should be dismissed. [ 50 ] As per the insufficiency of water, the claim refers to an incident that ocurred in March 2014, when the surface and the artesian wells ran dry. [ 51 ] At the hearing, the technician who inspected the wells, Mr Richard Giard, explained that the insufficiency of water was caused by a damaged pump. When he replaced it, the problem was solved. According to Mr Giard, it was fair wear and tear. [ 52 ] Though Defendants may not have been totally transparent when they mentionned in their Declaration that they never had any problem with the quantity of water, Mr Giard’s testimony confirms that the March 2014 incident was not related to the quantity of water in the wells. [ 53 ] Moreover, Plaintiffs never verified, prior to the sale, the age of the water supply system. [ 54 ] Since fair wear and tear is not covered by the warranty of quality, this part of the claim must be dismissed. [ 55 ] As per the quality problem, the water supply analysis done in September 2014 is clear : the water sample provided by Ms Sparling showed that it was unfit for consumption [21] . [ 56 ] Even if this type of defect is serious, the proof does not show that it existed at the time of the sale. [ 57 ] Defendants testified that they never had any problem with the drinkability of the water. [ 58 ] There is no proof that Plaintiffs had any problem prior to the reception of the Certificate of analysis in March 2014. [ 59 ] Under these circunstances, this part of the claim must also be dismissed. [ 60 ] As per the hot water tank, no proof was provided that the replacement of the hot water tank was caused by a latent defect. 3. French drains [ 61 ] According to Plaintiffs, Defendants should be liable for the replacement costs of the french drains on the Property. [ 62 ] As mentioned by Mr Mierzwinski in his testimony, a french drain was found while digging to replace the sewer pipe. The french...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n4. [ 59 ] Under these circunstances, this part of the claim must also be dismissed. [ 60 ] As per the hot water tank, no proof was provided that the replacement of the hot water tank was caused by a latent defect. 3. French drains [ 61 ] According to Plaintiffs, Defendants should be liable for the replacement costs of the french drains on the Property. [ 62 ] As mentioned by Mr Mierzwinski in his testimony, a french drain was found while digging to replace the sewer pipe. The french drain had an outlet and was not evacuating any water. He did not give is opinion as to the reason for the replacement of the french drain because he had not filed an expert’s report. [ 63 ] In fact, no expert report was filed by any party involved in this litigation. [ 64 ] Aside the fact that a part of the french drain was blocked, there is no proof as to the impact this problem had on the Property. [ 65 ] Therefore, even if this Court came to the conclusion that it was in fact a defect, there is no proof as to it’s seriousness. [ 66 ] Moreover, the Declaration mentions that there had been no modification to the french drains [22] . Since the house was built in the 1980’s, Plaintiffs should have been more careful on this aspect when they bought the Property. [ 67 ] Under these circonstance, this part of the claim is also dismissed. 4. The gutters [ 68 ] Plaintiffs alleged that the absence of gutters is a latent defect and that Defendants should be liable for the cost related to their installation. [ 69 ] The absence of gutters was clearly visible at the time Plaintiffs made their offer. Therefore, this defect can not be considered as hidden. [ 70 ] This part of the claim must also be dismissed [ 71 ] FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT : [ 72 ] DISMISSES Plaintiffs’ Re-amended originating application for damages resulting from hidden defects ; [ 73 ] WITH COSTS against Plaintiffs. __________________________________ Martin Tétreault, J.C.Q. Me Thomas A. Lavin Lavin Gosselin Avocats inc. Attorneys for Plaintiffs Me Joannie Tardif LB Avocats inc. Attorneys for Defendants [1] Exhibit PG-2. [2] Exhibit P-1. [3] Exhibit P-1. [4] Exhibit P-2 (the « Declaration »). [5] Exhibit P-3, p. 5 (the « Promise »). [6] Exhibit P-5. [7] Exhibit P-6. [8] Exhibit P-8. [9] Exhibit P-9. [10] Exhibits P-10. [11] Exhibit P-11. [12] Exhibit P-16. [13] Exhibit P-17. [14] Exhibit P-20. [15] Exhibit P-22. [16] Yan CAMPAGNOLO, Anne-Françoise DEBRUCHE & Sébastien GRAMMOND, Québec Contract Law , 2 e éd., Montréal, Wilson et Lafleur, 2016, par. 665, 667-670. [17] Exhibit P-23. [18] Exhibit P-24, pictures #5-13. [19] Rivard c. Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 . [20] Id., par. 7-8. [21] Exhibit P-9. [22] Declaration, s. D8.3.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.020350536331534386, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\", \"title\": \"Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau, 2019 QCCQ 1416 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.020350536331534386, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"latent defect warranty of quality seller knowledge Quebec civil code\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité du vendeur et vice caché connaissance du vendeur\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Garantie légale pour vice caché (défectuosité) et garantie de qualité en matière de vente au Québec : articulation avec la connaissance du vendeur, latitude de la clause d’exonération et critères du Code civil du Québec relatifs au « défaut latent ».\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Transaction de vente d’un bien meuble : le défaut n’apparaît qu’après livraison. L’acheteur découvre une défectuosité de qualité, allègue que le vendeur savait ou devait savoir, et demande annulation ou réduction du prix selon les remèdes usuels.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Charge de la preuve et conditions d’ouverture du recours en vente : temporalité de la découverte du défaut latent, exigence de preuve de la connaissance du vendeur, incidence sur la responsabilité et les sanctions (résolution, réduction du prix, dommages).\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens du vendeur pour faire échec au recours : absence de connaissance, défaut non « latent », défaut imputable à l’usure normale ou à un mauvais usage de l’acheteur; contestation du lien causal et de l’adéquation du remède demandé.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Le Tribunal examine une vente d’un bien présentant une détérioration progressive après la mise en service. L’acheteur soutient que le défaut était latent et que le vendeur en avait connaissance. Analyse des indices : historique d’entretien, représentations lors de la vente, délais de découverte et caractère non apparent à l’inspection. Application du régime de la responsabilité pour défaut de qualité/vice caché et détermination du remède proportionné, en tenant compte de la connaissance alléguée.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\"}, \"token_count\": 4838, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2024qccs788\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2024qccs788-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Promark Electronics Inc. c. Byoplanet International, 2024 QCCS 788 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"--- BEGIN PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 0\\n2024 QCCS 788 (CanLII) | Promark Electronics Inc. c. Byoplanet International | CanLII Date: 2024-03-12 File number: 500-17-118996-217 Citation: Promark Electronics Inc. c. Byoplanet International, 2024 QCCS 788 (CanLII) Promark Electronics Inc. c. Byoplanet International 2024 QCCS 788 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTRÉAL No.: 500-17-118996-217 DATE: March 12, 2024 _____________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE eleni YIANNAKIS, J.S.C. _____________________________________________________________________ PROMARK ELECTRONICS INC. Plaintiff v. BYOPLANET INTERNATIONAL LLC Defendant _____________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT _____________________________________________________________________ OVERVIEW [ 1 ] In its Originating Application, Plaintiff, Promark Electronics Inc. (“Promark”) is seeking $6,745,787.66 CAD [1] for unpaid products sold and delivered to Defendant, ByoPlanet International LLC (“ByoPlanet”) in 2020. The vast majority of the amounts owing are acknowledged by ByoPlanet in an agreement effective on May 21, 2021. Promark’s claim is in essence an action on account for breach of contract or for goods sold and delivered. [ 2 ] Despite this acknowledgement, ByoPlanet did not pay any of the amounts owing to Promark. Instead, as of August 25, 2021, for the first time, ByoPlanet invokes defects affecting Promark’s products. Constituting itself Cross-Plaintiff, ByoPlanet seeks the resolution of the sale for products it did not pay for and claims $10,915,031.83 CAD [2] . [ 3 ] For the reasons that follow, the Court grants Promark’s Originating Application and finds that the overwhelming factual and expert evidence reveals that there is no inherent defect in the products sold by Promark. In fact, the defect allegations are a pretext raised by ByoPlanet in order to avoid paying amounts that are clearly owing. Its Cross-Application is abusive under article 51 of the Code of Civil Procedure (“CCP”) since it is manifestly unfounded. FACTS Genesis of the Parties’ Relationship [ 4 ] Promark, a Quebec based company, develops, and manufactures electronic and electromechanical components [3] . It is a family-owned business founded in 1987. [ 5 ] The president Jarred Knecht (“Knecht”) [4] took over the company in 2016 with the help of his brother, Brandon Knecht, who is Vice President – Finance & Supply Chain. [ 6 ] ByoPlanet is a Florida limited liability company, with offices in Athens, Georgia. Until recently, ByoPlanet developed and manufactured disinfectant delivery systems for spraying various surfaces, including a rolling cart system equipped with a nozzle (the “Rolling Cart”) [5] . [ 7 ] Rick O’Shea (“O’Shea”) is the Chief Executive Officer of ByoPlanet. [ 8 ] Clorox Company (“Clorox”) is ByoPlanet’s partner [6] and principal client. Almost all of the products it manufactured, which at the time were mainly Rolling Carts, were sold to Clorox. Branded under the Clorox name as the “Clorox Total 360 Electrostatic Sprayer CLX1000” [7] , Clorox sold the Rolling Carts to third parties, such as Walmart, who in turn sold the products to end-users. [ 9 ] In light of the Covid-19 pandemic, in 2020, its sales grew tremendously. In order to keep up with the increasing demand, on April 25, 2020, ByoPlanet executed a Supply Agreement with Promark [8] . As per the Supply Agreement, Promark agreed to deliver a series of electronic and electromechanical components to ByoPlanet, which would be incorporated into the Rolling Cart. [ 10 ] The components consist of three assemblies which operate together (collectively the “Assemblies”) in order to provide the power supply to the Rolling Cart: a) the control panel assemblies, which include a power cord that is attached to the panel by a cable gland (the “Control Panels”); b) the electrostatic power supply assemblies (the “ESPS Power Supplies”); c) the sprayer control panel, which is the nozzle of the unit (the “ES-150 Sprayers”) [9] . [ 11 ] Promark is not ByoPlanet’s first, or only supplier of the Assemblies used in the production of the Rolling Cart units. Since 2013, ByoPlanet had been working with DK International Associates Inc. (“DK”), which was manufacturing the same Assemblies. However, with the emergence of the Covid-19 pandemic, demand for disinfectant products increased so significantly, that a second supplier—Promark, was needed to meet Clorox’s orders. [ 12 ] To that end, on October 9, 2020, Clorox loans ByoPlanet $6 M as a means to facilitate production and delivery of the units [10] . [ 13 ] In total, over 60,000 Assemblies are sold and delivered by Promark to ByoPlanet [11] . While the numbers presented by both parties differ, the products sold represent a total amount varying between $12,5 M USD and $14,5 M USD [12] , of which it is admitted that $5,982,311 USD, is outstanding as of May 2021 [13] . The Strategic Agreement and Acknowledgement of Debt [ 14 ] In January 2021, ByoPlanet is seeking to transition the Rolling Cart into a backpack system, known as the “Clorox Total 360 Propack” (the “Proton Backpack”) [14] , where the user could carry the disinfectant delivery system on his back, rather than roll the system on a cart. Some of the parts necessary for the backpack model are the same as the Rolling Cart, but others not, so a transition to this new product must be discussed and agreed upon. [ 15 ] Knecht begins discussions regarding the Proton Backpack with Peter Johansson (“Peter”), president, and Chief Operating Officer of ByoPlanet at the time. These discussions continue over several months and also involve Kyle Robinson, Director of Supply Chain and Charles (“Chuck”) Gilstrap (“Gilstrap”), Chief Financial Officer of ByoPlanet. [ 16 ] The parties also agree that the amounts owing to Promark at the time should be included in the new agreement that will be signed. To this end, between February and April 2021, the parties’ accounting teams have several exchanges to ensure that the accounts are properly reconciled as to the final amount owing to Promark [15] . [ 17 ] On May 21, 2021, the parties execute the Strategic Manufacturing and Supply Agreement (the “Strategic Agreement”) which is signed by Gilstrap for ByoPlanet and Knecht on behalf of Promark. The Strategic Agreement provides for the transition to the Proton Backpack and ByoPlanet’s formal acknowledgement that it owes $ 5,982,311 USD to Promark (the “Outstanding Amount”). In this regard, the Strategic Agreement lays out a payment plan, whereby ByoPlanet agrees to pay the Outstanding Amount in 29 installments [16] commencing on June 18, 2021, until December 31, 2021 [17] . [ 18 ] At the time of the execution of the Strategic Agreement, of the 60,000 Assemblies sold and delivered to ByoPlanet, 23,349 have already been incorporated into Rolling Carts sold to Clorox and are currently in the field [18] . ByoPlanet was fully paid by Clorox for these products [19] . The only existing log of returned items reveals that from June 2020 to April 2021, only 50 units were returned to Promark for various issues ranging from missing serial numbers, pump not rotating, and the unit won’t turn on. This represents approximately 2% of the units sold to Clorox [20] . These were all deemed minor issues, and the parties collaborated to resolve them. [ 19 ] Moreover, in March 2021, ByoPlanet starts receiving complaints notably from Walmart regarding damage to the power cord [21] . In this regard, ByoPlanet has some exchanges with Clorox, where it is found that user error could cause damage to the power cord, if the user pulls the Rolling Cart from the cord (a.k.a. the” Pigtail”) rather than from the handle [22] . It is agreed that a “strain relief hook” [23] and an installation video would be distributed to customers [24] . Thereafter, ByoPlanet’s internal report dated August 31, 2021, confirms that the incident rate drops significantly—a monthly chart from March to August reveals that there were less than five incidents reported regarding damage to the power cord in July and August 2021 [25] . Failed Attempt to Set Aside the Strategic Agreement [ 20 ] On July 20, 2021, Knecht writes to Gilstrap and O’Shea stating that they need to speak since the payment plan outlined in the Strategic Agreement has not been respected [26] . Indeed, the five first payments provided therein had not been made. Approximately 10 minutes later, O’Shea responds that “Peter” has been terminated and the Strategic Agreement had not been approved by the board [27] . Knecht answers that the Strategic Agreement had been negotiated with several members of the ByoPlanet team over many months and had been duly executed [28] . He expects ByoPlanet to abide by its obligations. [ 21 ] O’Shea’s response is baffling. “Peter” did not sign the Strategic Agreement on behalf of ByoPlanet, Chuck Gilstrap did. Moreover, O’Shea’s testimony on whether he actually sought approval from the board regarding the Strategic Agreement is contradictory. In his pre-trial examination, he claims that he did, “through a quick phone call” [29] . At trial, he contradicts his previous statement, maintaining that he only learned of the agreement sometime in June 2021. [ 22 ] O’Shea contradicts himself yet again when addressing the reasons for the first unpaid installments. In his pre- trial examination, he testifies that the first payment due on June 18, 2021, was not paid since “all payments were put on hold until further research was done on the Boards” [30] . However, at trial, he admits that the first payments should have been made, since he acknowledges that it was in August 2021 that ByoPlanet truly became aware of the alleged seriousness of the defect issue. [ 23 ] In actuality, none of ByoPlanet’s witnesses were able to offer any explanation as to why the five first payments provided under the Strategic Agreement (due on June 18, June 25, July 2, July 9, and July 16, 2021) totaling $ 400 000 USD were not paid. O’Shea contends that ByoPlanet had the liquidities at the time to cover them. However, ByoPlanet’s bank statements reveal a balance of $ 336,503.76 USD as of August 31, 2021, showing that cash flow availability was actually limited [31] . [ 24 ] Clearly, this was an attempt by O’Shea to set aside the Strategic Agreement. O’Shea testified that when he learned of the existence of this agreement around June 2021, he was “livid”. He felt that Peter, a friend since high school, who had participated in the negotiations, had betrayed him by going behind his back. [ 25 ] Following O’Shea’s failed attempt to set aside the Strategic Agreement, the parties agreed that an initial payment of $ 50 000 USD would be made to Promark on July 26 or 27, 2021 [32] . [ 26 ] Rather than make the payment, on July 28, 2021, ByoPlanet’s external council sends Promark an email asking to set up a call to “address your concerns” [33] . [ 27 ] On August 3, 2021, Promark sends a formal letter of demand signed by its attorneys asking for the payment of the Outstanding Amount and the termination of the Strategic Agreement [34] . This letter was never answered. “Defective Product Discovery” [ 28 ] Suddenly and unexpectedly, on August 25, 2021, Steve Cooper (“Cooper”), Vice-president of Research & Development at ByoPlanet, informs Promark in a short email, that it has discovered “serious issues” with the Control Panels [35] . [ 29 ] He states that ByoPlanet has received multiple complaints of power cord failures and that this results in an “inoperable machine and causes a safety hazard”. No supporting documentation is provided. Instead, Cooper indicates that “further analysis” is needed to determine whether a full repair and replacement of all the units will be required: (…) We have received multiple complaints of power cord failures that have been characterized by splitting of the outer insultation or broken wires inside the power cord. This results in an inoperable machine and a safety hazard. Our initial, ongoing investigation has shown that the panel-mounted fitting used to hold the power cord is excessively deforming the cord . (…) We are also seeing that when this condition is combined with cold weather there is a high incidence of failure of the cord’s insulated sheath or internal wires. (…) Based on inspections and testing , BYP concludes that the Control Panels supplied by Promark are defective and not fir for their intended use such that the manufacturing process employed by Promark will need to be revised accordingly. Further analysis and discussion is required to determine if a full repair and replacement of all existing units will be required. (…) [36] [Emphasis added] [ 30 ] Cooper testified that he fortuitously found out about the alleged defects in August 2021, when he glanced at a Control Panel lying on a desk while in the R&D department. He stated that he was in charge of the root cause analysis “by default” which is an indication of the less than thorough analysis carried out by ByoPlanet in this instance. [ 31 ] In fact, prior to sending this email, ByoPlanet conducted limited testing on the Control Panels, which did not reveal any inherent defect [37] . From the evidence filed, there is only one comprehensive report dated August 31, 2021, which outlines the results of ByoPlanet’s root cause analysis [38] . It is the same report that states that the reason for the power cord failures identified from March to August 2021 is user error and was solved with the addition of strain relief hooks [39] . [ 32 ] There is no evidence of a formal written notice provided to Clorox by ByoPlanet of this allegedly serious defect issue, despite a contractual obligation to do so [40] . The only piece of evidence put forward by ByoPlanet is Cooper’s meeting notes with Clorox for August 19, 2021, where one of the indicated discussion points is to “determine potential need for recall, or field repair of units” [41] . Cooper also spoke with Clorox of this issue on August 24, 2021, and understood that Clorox was not going to remove, recall of withdraw any of the Promark products [42] . [ 33 ] Finding the August 25 th email bizarre and suspicious in a context where important amounts are still outstanding, Promark does not respond. ByoPlanet never notifies Promark of any results of a “further analysis”, Promark is not afforded the opportunity to “repair or replace” the supposedly defective Control Panels, nor is it informed of Clorox’s position on this matter [43] . [ 34 ] Instead, and despite Clorox’s position, on September 9, 2021, Sean Corrigan (“Corrigan”), Director of Operations at ByoPlanet sends another email to Promark advising it that it will be: a) shipping back 32,618 of inventory of the Assemblies that have not been incorporated into any finished units; b) reworking 7,049 of Assemblies in inventory that have been incorporated into finished products but not sold; and c) reworking 25,138 [44] Assemblies currently in units in the field as they are returned [45] . [ 35 ] The total costs claimed by ByoPlanet, which include the purchase price of the Assemblies is approximately $ 6.8 M USD [46] . [ 36 ] However, even though ByoPlanet is asking for the “reimbursement” of the purchase price, in reality, ByoPlanet has not paid Promark for these Assemblies, or at least it admits that it has not paid for the majority thereof. While Promark contends that none of these Assemblies have been paid for, ByoPlanet admits that it did not pay for 20,320 of the 32,618 units held in inventory [47] . Termination of Clorox Relationship and Cash Flow Problems [ 37 ] During this same period and unbeknownst to Promark, ByoPlanet’s relationship with Clorox is also starting to deteriorate. On July 29, 2021, Clorox institutes proceedings against ByoPlanet alleging breach of contract and a default on the repayment of the $6M loan (the “Clorox Proceedings”) [48] . On December 20, 2021, Clorox and ByoPlanet agree to terminate their relationship (the “Wind Down Agreement”) [49] and settle the Clorox Proceedings [50] . [ 38 ] According to O’Shea’s testimony, the last order received from Clorox is in February 2021 for approximately 10,000 Rolling Cart units. Clorox paid for this order upfront and in full [51] . [ 39 ] There is no mention of any defective products in either the Clorox Proceedings or in the Wind Down Agreement. In fact, there is no evidence adduced at trial regarding any complaints or concerns from Clorox about defective units, returned products or any recall of the Rolling Carts by Clorox. [\\n\\n\\n--- END PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... to O’Shea’s testimony, the last order received from Clorox is in February 2021 for approximately 10,000 Rolling Cart units. Clorox paid for this order upfront and in full [51] . [ 39 ] There is no mention of any defective products in either the Clorox Proceedings or in the Wind Down Agreement. In fact, there is no evidence adduced at trial regarding any complaints or concerns from Clorox about defective units, returned products or any recall of the Rolling Carts by Clorox. [ 40 ] In addition to the Clorox Proceedings, again during the same period, on July 15, 2021, ByoPlanet is facing a lawsuit from one of its major suppliers Gast Manufacturing Inc. (“Gast”) for breach of contract and for unpaid invoices, claiming $ 1.8 M USD (the “Gast Proceedings”) [52] . The Gast Proceedings are settled on February 27, 2023 [53] . [ 41 ] As of the summer of 2021, ByoPlanet is experiencing serious cash flow problems. From September 9, 2021, to September 19, 2022, ByoPlanet borrows $ 6 M USD (the “Loan Agreement”) [54] from RPMOS LLC, a Florida limited liability company (“RPMOS”) [55] . However, despite numerous contradictions in O’Shea’s testimony on this issue [56] , the evidence reveals that RPMOS does not have a bank account and so the monies are actually advanced by its members [57] , O’Shea and John Barrett [58] . Based on O’Shea’s testimony, John Barrett holds 33 % equity and O’Shea 67 % in RPMOS. O’Shea is the only “Authorized Person” listed on RPMOS’ corporate registry [59] . [ 42 ] The Loan Agreement is secured by a Security Agreement dated September 9, 2021, pursuant to which ByoPlanet grants a security interest in almost all of its assets. RPMOS is essentially ByoPlanet’s only secured creditor [60] . [ 43 ] Following ByoPlanet’s failure to repay the loan, on February 17, 2023, RPMOS demands immediate repayment of the loan [61] . RPMOS executes a foreclosure on all of ByoPlanet’s assets, save for the claim in the Cross-Application and all of the inventory supplied by Promark which is still being held by ByoPlanet [62] . Good Salt Transaction [ 44 ] Thereafter, on May 10, 2023, RPMOS enters into an asset purchase agreement with Good Salt LLC (“Good Salt”) pursuant to which Good Salt, a company incorporated only five months earlier [63] , acquires all of ByoPlanet’s transferred assets (but none of its liabilities) for $ 250 000 USD (the “Good Salt Transaction”) [64] . [ 45 ] In his testimony, O’Shea confirms that ByoPlanet is no longer operating and has no employees, most of which have been hired by Good Salt [65] . [ 46 ] Essentially, Good Salt is the successor in interest to the vast majority of ByoPlanet’s assets. O’Shea is Good Salt’s president and RPMOS its agent [66] . Also, Good Salt, RPMOS and O’Shea all have the same registered address [67] . [ 47 ] On May 16, 2023, Good Salt enters into a share exchange and reorganization agreement with Plandai Biotechnology Inc. (“Plandai”), whereby Plandai is set to acquire all of the issued and outstanding interests in Good Salt (shares and assets) [68] . Plandai is a publicly traded company based in London, United-Kingdom. [ 48 ] On July 31, 2023, Plandai rescinds the agreement, thereby rendering it null and void [69] . PROCEDURAL HISTORY [ 49 ] On November 12, 2021, Promark files its Originating Application, which is modified on three occasions thereafter [70] , seeking the recovery of $ 5,419,173.89 USD broken down as follows: a) The Outstanding Amount, $ 5,982,311 USD as per section 7.4 of the Strategic Agreement; b) $ 749,362.89 USD for work in progress as per section 15 in fine of the Strategic Agreement [71] ; c) The whole net of a security deposit held by Promark of $ 1,312,500 USD. Total: $ 5,419,173.89 USD [ 50 ] On February 7, 2022, and then on September 9, 2022, ByoPlanet files its Summary Statement of Oral Grounds of Defence (“Defence”) and its Cross-Application, both of which are modified several times thereafter [72] . [ 51 ] ByoPlanet does not contest the amounts claimed, nor the validity of the Strategic Agreement and the Outstanding Amount. Rather, it invokes defects in Promark’s products and ultimately argues that it is justified not only to refuse to pay for the Assemblies but also claims in its Cross-Application $ 8,291,576.90 USD [73] , which consists of the purchase price and damages resulting from the alleged defects. [ 52 ] On December 15, 2022, the parties file a Request for Setting Down for Trial and Judgement by way of a Joint Declaration. On March 9, 2023, the parties set the dates for a 4-day trial which is held on December 11 to 14, 2023. [ 53 ] On April 18, 2023, upon learning of the Good Salt and Plandai transactions through press releases, Promark modifies its Originating Application and adds Good Salt as a new Defendant, which is contested by ByoPlanet. On November 2, 2023, Justice Daniel Urbas hears Promark’s Application for Authorization to Amend its Originating Application and renders his oral judgement on November 3, 2023. He allows the amendments and additional exhibits sought by Promark but refuses the addition of Good Salt as a Defendant. He also allows ByoPlanet to modify its proceedings to respond to the new allegations and exhibits as well as the conduct of a new pre-trial examination of O’Shea and the communication of undertakings. [ 54 ] At the outset of the trial, the parties argued an Amended Application for a Sealing Order dated November 21, 2023, and an Application for Confidentiality and Sealing Orders dated December 4, 2023, presented by ByoPlanet (collectively the “Confidentiality Application”). The Court took the Confidentiality Application under advisement and will address it in this judgement. PARTIES’ POSITIONS Promark’s Position [ 55 ] Promark argues that its Originating Application is straightforward. The Strategic Agreement contains an acknowledgement of debt, which is the equivalent to an extrajudicial admission. It is simply asking to be repaid the amounts owing as per the uncontested acknowledgement of debt and the Strategic Agreement. [ 56 ] Promark further contents that ByoPlanet is acting in bad faith in invoking alleged defects to the products sold and delivered, which is a pretext to avoid paying monies that are clearly owed. [ 57 ] From a purely legal point of view, Promark argues that ByoPlanet cannot ask for the resolution of the sale and the “reimbursement” of a purchase price for products it has not paid for. This is nonsensical and demonstrates that the Cross-Application is baseless. It is asking the Court to declare the Cross-Application abusive within the meaning of articles 51 and following of the CCP [74] . [ 58 ] On the issue of the alleged defects, it points out that there is not a shred of evidence that the 23,000 plus units that were sold to Clorox and are currently in the field have had any significant issues. Moreover, nearly three years later, there have been no safety issues or hazardous incidents reported, no log of items returned (save for the 50 units) [75] and no recall by Clorox. No evidence that the units are inoperable and need to be repaired or replaced. There are barely any written communications with Clorox relating to this issue [76] . [ 59 ] To add insult to injury, ByoPlanet’s own root cause analysis report dated August 31, 2021, reveals that the power wire failures are due to user error and to the Pigtail being overly pulled [77] . The report confirms that once cable pulls also called “strain relief hooks” [78] are distributed to customers, the incident rate drops significantly. Promark’s expert also corroborates this finding [79] . [ 60 ] Lastly, Promark argues that ByoPlanet’s bad faith is further evidenced when one analyzes the Good Salt Transaction, which reveals ByoPlanet’s ultimate objective to make itself judgement proof. ByoPlanet’s Position [ 61 ] ByoPlanet contends that although the timing of the discovery of the defects is not ideal, said defects exist, are confirmed by its engineering team [80] and by its experts [81] . It argues that its expert evidence shows that the gland connecting the cable to the Promark Control Panel is overtightened, a feature that is completely absent on the comparable DK panels. This defect creates an over compression and causes damage to the cable which could pose electrical safety hazards. Thus, Promark’s Control Panels are defective. Since the Assemblies all operate as a unit, all of Promark’s products are unusable. [ 62 ] It argues that Promark violated its obligation to warrant that the Control Panels it supplied were free from defects and could be used for their intended purpose under articles 1726 and 1729 of the Civil Code of Quebec (“CCQ”). As such, considering the defect of the Control Panels supplied by Promark, ByoPlanet is entitled to the resolution of their sale and to the restitution of the purchase price, or subsidiarily, it is entitled to invoke the exception of non-performance. ByoPlanet is also entitled to damages equivalent to the purchase price paid to Promark for the ESPS Power Supplies and the ES-150 Sprayers, which are being held by ByoPlanet as unusable inventory. [ 63 ] To the extent that ByoPlanet is ordered to pay Promark any of the amounts claimed in the Originating Application, ByoPlanet is entitled to seek judicial compensation between the said amount, on the one hand, and the purchase price of the Control Panels and damages, on the other hand, to be paid by Promark. [ 64 ] Finally, regarding the Good Salt Transaction, ByoPlanet argues that it is absurd to think that all of the steps taken leading up to this transaction were for the sole purpose of frustrating Promark, should it obtain a favorable judgement. It argues that ByoPlanet had cash flow issues that it attempted to resolve, but in the end was unsuccessful. ISSUES IN DISPUTE [ 65 ] The Court must decide the following issues: 1. Is Promark’s Originating Application well founded? 2. Are Promark’s products defective ? More specifically: a) Has ByoPlanet proven a loss of use of Promark’s products in order to establish a presumption of liability in its favor? b) If not, has ByoPlanet established that Promark’s products are otherwise defective? 3. If not, was ByoPlanet acting in bad faith? 4. Is ByoPlanet’s Cross-Application an abuse of process? 5. Is ByoPlanet’s Confidentiality Application well-founded? ANALYSIS 1. Is Promark’s Originating Application well founded? [ 66 ] The source of Promark’s claim is the Strategic Agreement. This agreement clearly provides that the amounts claimed are due by ByoPlanet. [ 67 ] Regarding the Outstanding Amount, the Court finds that the Strategic Agreement contains an acknowledgement of debt by ByoPlanet at section 7.4. An acknowledgement of debt constitutes an extra-judicial admission and serves as evidence against its author [82] , namely, ByoPlanet. It can only be revoked if an error vitiating consent is proven [83] . [ 68 ] Here, although O’Shea attempted to cancel the Strategic Agreement on July 20, 2021, in its proceedings and at trial, ByoPlanet did not contest the validity of the acknowledgement of debt, nor did it invoke any error which could have vitiated its consent [84] . The Outstanding Amount is due. [ 69 ] Regarding the amount of $ 749,362.89 USD for work in progress, section 15 in fine of the Strategic Agreement provides that the termination of the Agreement does not relieve the parties of its obligation to pay for any raw materials or work in progress (WIP) that remains unpaid. The invoice for the amount claimed was issued on June 18, 2021, prior to the termination of the Strategic Agreement, was uncontested by ByoPlanet and remains unpaid [85] . This amount is also due. [ 70 ] After deducting the security deposit held by Promark of $ 1,312,500 USD, the Court finds that Promark is entitled to the sum of $ 5,419,173.89 USD. Applying the conversion rate as of the date of the institution of the Originating Application, November 11, 2021, the amount owed by ByoPlanet to Promark is $ 6,745,787.66 CAD [86] . [ 71 ] Section 7.4 of the Strategic Agreement provides that all outstanding amounts will bear annual interest at a rate of 5% plus current prime. The annual prime rate of the Bank of Canada on November 11, 2021, was 2.45 %, so that the applicable conventional interest is 7.45 % per annum [87] . [ 72 ] As such, the Court finds that Promark is entitled to recover the sum of $6,745,787.66 CAD plus interest at a rate of 7.45 % per annum. [ 73 ] However, ByoPlanet argues that it is justified in refusing payment since Promark’s products are defective. [ 74 ] While Promark contests this assertion, its preliminary argument is that ByoPlanet is barred from presenting this claim, since the Supply Agreement was replaced by the Strategic Agreement, and therefore no longer has any effect. It argues that since the Supply Agreement was terminated, the warranty obligations attached to the Assemblies sold can no longer be invoked. [ 75 ] The Court does not agree. Even though the Supply Agreement was “updated “ [88] by the Strategic Agreement, Promark’s warranty obligations still survive under the Supply Agreement for the products already sold and delivered. One of the conditions to invoke warranty obligations is that the latent defect must have existed at the time of the sale [89] . As such, whether a contract was replaced or terminated has no impact on the continued existence of the warranty obligations [90] . [ 76 ] Nevertheless, for ByoPlanet’s argument to be successful, it must prove that Promark’s products are affected by a latent defect. 2. are promark’s products defective? 2.1 Legal Principles [ 77 ] Pursuant to articles 1716 and 1726 CCQ, the seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property, at the time of the sale is free of latent defects that render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or that so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price had they been aware of the defect. [ 78 ] A buyer seeking to invoke the warranty of quality must necessarily establish that the alleged defect (i) is latent, (ii) is sufficiently serious, (iii) existed at the time of the sale, and (iv) was unknown to the buyer [91] . [ 79 ] The buyer who discovers a latent defect must also comply with article 1739 CCQ and provide the seller with a timely notice outlining the discovered defects [92] .This is a substantive requirement, so that in order to succeed on its warranty claim, a buyer must not only prove the criteria outlined above but must also demonstrate that he provided timely notice of the latent defect [93] . [ 80 ] The starting point of this analysis is the existence of an actual defect [94] . The main purpose of the legal warranty aims to provide the buyer with the usefulness and full use of the sold property. The CCQ defines a defect as a flaw that renders the property unfit for its intended use [95] . [ 81 ] There are three main types of latent defects: (i) the material defect, which relates to a specific good that was for example broken upon delivery; (ii) the functional defect, which relates to the good’s design; and (iii) the conventional defect, which arises where the buyer has disclosed that the good is to be put to a particular use [96] . Regarding the functional defect, which is at issue here, such a defect will be established if the good cannot partially or totally perform its intended use regardless of the cause while applying an objective standard [97] . [ 82 ] In assessing whether the defect is sufficiently serious, the defect does not have to render the good completely unusable but simply has to reduce its usefulness significantly in relation to the legitimate expectations of a prudent and diligent [98] . [ 83 ] While the occurrence of an actual material prejudice is not required, it is necessary to prove the existence of a likely risk of prejudice or a deficit in the usefulness of the defective product: [88] A defect will be considered to be serious if it renders the good unfit for its intended use or so diminishes its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it at the price paid (arts. 1522 C.C.L.C. and 1726 C.C.Q.). An example frequently cited by the authors is where a house is at risk of water damage owing to a crack in its foundation. The crack does not actually have to cause such damage for a latent defect to exist; it is enough that the crack exists and that it is likely to lead to serious damage . [89] C.E. considers only the first aspect of the seriousness of the defect.  According to its interpretation, the boiler could not be considered to be defective unless there were absolutely no possibility of it working. The trial judge, too...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 5\\nDecember 22, 2023, abusive within the meaning of articles 51 and following of the Code of Civil Procedur e ; [ 205 ] DECLARES that pursuant to article 54 al. 2 of the Code of Civil Procedure , Promark Electronics Inc. may claim and prove its damages in a subsequent hearing; [ 206 ] ORDERS that the Share Exchange and Reorganisation Agreement between Plandai Biotechnology Inc. and Good Salt LLC dated May 16, 2023 [207] be the subject of a publication ban restricting the dissemination of information to only those persons consulting the court record for themselves and prohibit those individuals from spreading the information any further; [ 207 ] ORDERS that Exhibit P-53, U-7 consisting of the duplicate unredacted copy of ByoPlanet Internal LLC’s redacted bank statements filed under Exhibit D-32 be removed from the Court record; [ 208 ] THE WHOLE, with legal costs, including expert fees in the amount of $32,113.11 CAD. __________________________________ ELENI YIANNAKIS, j.S.C. Mtre Jason S. Novak Mtre Carolyn Booth Mtre Lara Assy Attorneys for Plaintiff Promark Electronics Inc. Mtre Erica Shadeed Mtre Alexander Little Mtre Abbie Buckman Mme Lauren Manoukian (Stagiaire en droit) Attorneys for Defendant ByoPlanet International LLC Hearing dates:         December 11, 12, 13 and 14, 2023 [1] $5,419,173.89 USD converted to CAD on November 11, 2021, the date of the Originating Application filed by Promark (see currency rate at Exhibit P-9). [2] $ 8,291,576.90 USD: see Amended Cross-Application dated December 22, 2023, par. 42 and Exhibit D-14 for the conversion rate as of September 7, 2022. [3] Exhibit P-1. [4] The use of individual’s first or last names in the present judgement is only for purposes of brevity and no disrespect is intended. [5] See Clorox Operator’s Manual for a picture of the Rolling Cart, Exhibi